hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4593","(4593) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr. He said: I heard Hilāl ibn Yasāf narrating from Abū Yaḥyā al-Aʿraj, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The prayer of the one who sits is half the prayer of the one who stands.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah. (Q) In the ḥadīth of Jarīr"," from Manṣūr"," there is a specification that the Prophet ﷺ prayed while sitting"," and that his statement — \""The prayer of the one who sits is half the prayer of the one who stands\"" — applies to others. This will be addressed"," Allah the Most High willing"," in the chapter of Khaṣāʾiṣ (Peculiarities) at the beginning of the Book of Marriage. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 735]"""
"4594","(4594) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would perform supererogatory prayer (yusabbiḥ) upon his mount"," facing whichever direction it turned"," and he would pray the witr upon it"," but he would not pray the obligatory prayers upon it.\""\n\nThe word \""qibal\"" (direction) is not in the ḥadīth of Abū Dāwūd.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah"," and I have narrated it through a higher chain previously. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī and Muslim 700]"""
"4595","(4595) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī informed us, Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, that ʿAbd Allāh would pray upon his camel, facing whichever direction it turned, and he informed them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that, and that ʿAbd Allāh would pray the witr upon his camel."
"4596","(4596) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us. He said: I read before Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever stands [in prayer] in Ramadan with faith and seeking reward"," his previous sins will be forgiven.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf"," from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 759–760]"""
"4597","(4597) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Sulayman al-Faqih narrated to us, 'Ubayd ibn 'Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: \Whoever stands (in prayer) during Ramadan with faith and seeking reward"," his previous sins will be forgiven.\"""""
"4598","(4598) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sab'i, and Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Muzahim al-Saffar al-Adib narrated to us — word for word — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Abu Salamah informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say regarding Ramadan: \Whoever stands (in prayer) during it with faith and seeking reward"," his previous sins will be forgiven.\""\n\nAnd Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr"," Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami"," Abu al-Hasan al-Sab'i"," and Abu Sa'id al-Adib narrated to us"," they said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us"," al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us"," Ibn Wahb narrated to us"," Malik informed me"," from Ibn Shihab"," from Abu Salamah and Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman"," from Abu Hurayrah"," from the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," the same meaning. And Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated it from al-Zuhri from Abu Salamah"," and he said: \""Whoever fasts Ramadan","\"" and he said: \""Whoever stands (in prayer) on Laylat al-Qadr.\"""""
"4599","(4599) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani narrated to us — dictation — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Qattan narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran in Baghdad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to encourage (people) to stand (in prayer) during Ramadan without commanding them with strict obligation, saying: \Whoever stands (in prayer) during Ramadan with faith and seeking reward"," his previous sins will be forgiven.\"" Then the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," passed away and the matter remained upon that.\n\nAhmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi added in his narration: during the caliphate of Abu Bakr and the beginning of the caliphate of 'Umar"," may Allah be pleased with them both."""
"4600","(4600) Malik also narrated it from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, with its meaning. And Ibn Shihab said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, passed away and the matter remained upon that, and the matter remained upon that during the beginning of the caliphate of Abu Bakr and the beginning of the caliphate of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both.\n\nAbu Ahmad al-Mihrajani narrated to us, Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Malik narrated it."
"4601","(4601) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas 'Abd Allah ibn al-Husayn al-Qadi in Marw narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, Malik narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Harashi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, prayed in the mosque one night, and people prayed with his prayer. Then he prayed the following night and the people increased. Then they gathered on the third or fourth night, but the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, did not come out to them. When morning came, he said: \I saw what you did"," and nothing prevented me from coming out to you except that I feared it would be made obligatory upon you.\"" And that was in Ramadan.\n\nThe wording of both their narrations is the same"," except that Ibn Abi Uways said: from 'A'ishah"," the wife of the Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him."""
"4602","(4602) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran al-'Adil in Baghdad narrated to us, Abu al-Husayn 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Ali ibn Mukram narrated to us, Abu Muhammad 'Ubayd ibn 'Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Yahya ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, informed him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came out one night in the depth of the night to pray in the mosque, and men prayed following his prayer. In the morning, the people spoke about it, and more of them gathered. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came out the second night, and they prayed with him. In the morning, the people spoke about it, and the people of the mosque increased on the third night. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came out and they prayed with his prayer. When the fourth night came, the mosque could not accommodate its people, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, did not come out to them. Some of them began saying \Prayer! Prayer!\"" but the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," did not come out to them until he came out for the Fajr prayer. When he completed the Fajr prayer"," he turned to the people and pronounced the tashahhud"," then said: \""As for what follows"," your affair was not hidden from me last night"," but I feared that it would be made obligatory upon you and you would be unable to bear it.\""\n\nThe Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," used to encourage them to stand (in prayer) during Ramadan without commanding them with strict obligation"," saying: \""Whoever stands (in prayer) during Ramadan with faith and seeking reward"," his previous sins will be forgiven.\"" Then the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," passed away and the matter remained upon that. Then the matter remained upon that during the caliphate of Abu Bakr and the beginning of the caliphate of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab"," may Allah be pleased with them both."""
"4603","(4603) 'Urwah said: And 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari informed me — and he was among the officials of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he used to work with 'Abd Allah ibn al-Aqram over the bayt al-mal of the Muslims — that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, went out one night in Ramadan, and 'Abd al-Rahman went out with him. They walked through the mosque and the people of the mosque were scattered groups: a man praying by himself, and a man praying with a group following his prayer. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \By Allah"," I think that if I were to gather them upon a single reciter"," it would be better.\"" So 'Umar ibn al-Khattab"," may Allah be pleased with him"," resolved to gather them upon a single reciter. He ordered Ubayy ibn Ka'b"," may Allah be pleased with him"," to lead them in Ramadan. Then 'Umar ibn al-Khattab"," may Allah be pleased with him"," went out and the people were praying behind their reciter"," and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari was with him. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: \""What an excellent innovation this is! And that which they sleep through is better than that which which they stand (in prayer).\"" He meant the latter part of the night"," and the people used to stand (in prayer) in the first part of it."""
"4604","(4604) Abu Ahmad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-'Adil narrated to us, Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Malik narrated from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari, who said: I went out with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, one night in Ramadan to the mosque, and the people were scattered groups: a man praying by himself, and a man praying with a group following his prayer. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \By Allah"," I think that if I were to gather these people upon a single reciter"," it would be better.\"" Then he resolved and gathered them upon Ubayy ibn Ka'b. He said: Then I went out with him another night"," and the people were praying behind their reciter. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: \""What an excellent innovation this is! And that which they sleep through is better than that which they stand (in prayer).\"" He meant the latter part of the night"," and the people used to stand (in prayer) in the first part of it."""
"4605","(4605) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Fanjuwayh al-Dinawari narrated to us, 'Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Shanbah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Imran narrated to us, Abu 'Ubayd Allah — meaning al-Makhzumi — narrated to us, Sufyan narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, gathered the people for standing (in prayer) during the month of Ramadan — the men upon Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and the women upon Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah."
"4606","(4606) Al-Husayn ibn Fanjuwayh narrated to us, Musa ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us, Ahmad ibn 'Isa ibn Mahan al-Razi in Baghdad narrated to us, Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated from Abu 'Abd Allah al-Thaqafi, 'Arfajah al-Thaqafi narrated to us, who said: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to command the people to stand (in prayer) during the month of Ramadan, and he would appoint an imam for the men and an imam for the women. 'Arfajah said: And I was the imam of the women."
"4607","(4607) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Qādim al-Marwazī and Aḥmad ibn Bashīr al-Marthadī informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, ʿImrān ibn Mūsā informed us — they said: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād informed us, Wahb informed us, Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed us, he said: I heard Abā al-Naḍr narrate from Busr ibn Saʿīd from Zayd ibn Thābit that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ constructed a chamber — he (the narrator) said: I think he said, \of palm-fibre matting\"" — in Ramaḍān"," and he prayed in it for several nights. In the narration of al-Marthadī: \""for two nights.\"" So the people prayed with his prayer. When he became aware of them"," he sat down"," then he came out to them and said: \""I have recognized what I have seen of your practice. O people"," pray in your homes"," for the most excellent prayer is that of a man in his home"," except the obligatory prayer.\"" In the narration of al-Marwazī and al-Marthadī"," it is from Sālim Abī al-Naḍr.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād"," and it was narrated by Muslim from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Bahz from Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 731]"""
"4608","(4608) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us, Abū Khalīfah informed us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us, Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr from Mujāhid from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: A man said to him: \Shall I pray behind the imām in Ramaḍān?\"" He — meaning Ibn ʿUmar — said: \""Do you not recite the Qurʾān?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Will you remain silent like a donkey? Pray in your home.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 7742]"""
"4609","(4609) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us, Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, Abū ʿĀmir Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir informed us, al-Walīd — he is Ibn Muslim — informed me, ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad narrated from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that he used to perform the night prayer in his home during the month of Ramaḍān. When the people dispersed from the mosque, he would take a vessel of water, then go out to the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would not leave it until he had prayed the Fajr prayer therein. [Ḥasan]"
"4610","(4610) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ṣanʿānī in Makkah informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Ṣanʿānī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from al-Thawrī from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥarashī from Jubayr ibn Nufayr from Abū Dharr, who said: We fasted Ramaḍān with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not lead us in night prayer during the month at all until it was the twenty-third night. He led us in prayer until about one-third of the night had passed. Then he did not lead us on the twenty-fourth night. He led us on the twenty-fifth night until about half the night had passed. We said: \O Messenger of Allah"," would you not lead us in supererogatory prayer for the remainder of the night?\"" He said: \""When a man stands in prayer with the imām until he completes it"," it is recorded for him the remainder of his night.\"" Then he did not lead us on the twenty-sixth night. He led us on the twenty-seventh night"," and he sent for his family"," and the people gathered until we feared that we would miss the falāḥ.\n\nHe (the narrator) said: \""I said: 'And what is the falāḥ?' He said: 'The suḥūr.'\""\n\nIt was narrated by Wahb from Dāwūd"," who said: \""The twenty-fourth night — the seventh of what remained.\"" And he said: \""The twenty-sixth night — the fifth of what remained"," and the twenty-eighth night — the third of what remained.\""\n\nAnd with the same meaning it was narrated by Hushaym ibn Bashīr and Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ and others from Dāwūd.\n\nAnd with the same meaning it was narrated by other than ʿAbd al-Razzāq from al-Thawrī"," and it was narrated by Ḥammād ibn Salamah from Dāwūd"," similar to the narration of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from al-Thawrī"," and likewise Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī from Dāwūd. The narration of Wahb and those who followed him is more correct"," and Allah knows best."""
"4611","(4611) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, it was recited to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Salmān and Bakr ibn Muḍar informed you from Ibn al-Hād that Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik al-Quraẓī narrated to him, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out one night in Ramaḍān and saw people in a corner of the mosque praying. He said: \What are these people doing?\"" A speaker said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," these are people who do not have the Qurʾān with them"," and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb is reciting"," and they are praying with his prayer.\"" He said: \""They have done well","\"" or he said: \""They have done rightly.\"" And he did not disapprove of that for them.\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us"," they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us"," al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us"," Ibn Wahb informed me"," Bakr ibn Muḍar and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Salmān al-Ḥajarī informed me"," and he mentioned it with the same meaning. Ibn Wahb said: \""And one of them adds a word or its equivalent over the other.\""\n\nThe Shaykh said: This is a ḥasan mursal.\n\nThaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik al-Quraẓī is from the first generation of the Tābiʿīn of the people of Madīnah. Ibn Mandah included him among the Companions"," and it is said that he saw the Prophet ﷺ"," and it is said that his age was the age of ʿAṭiyyah al-Quraẓī — they were both captured on the day of Qurayẓah and were not killed. He does not have companionship (ṣuḥbah). It has been narrated with a connected chain"," but it is weak. [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4612","(4612) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Hamdānī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me, Muslim ibn Khālid narrated from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from his father from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out and found people praying in a corner of the mosque in Ramaḍān. He said: \Who are these?\"" It was said: \""These are people who do not have the Qurʾān with them"," and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb is praying"," and they are praying with his prayer.\"" The Prophet ﷺ said: \""They have done rightly"," and how excellent is what they have done.\""\n\nAbū Dāwūd said: \""This ḥadīth is not strong.\"" (J) Muslim ibn Khālid is weak. [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4613","(4613) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Fanjawayh al-Dīnawarī informed us, al-Faḍl ibn al-Faḍl al-Kindī informed us, Ḥamzah ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUmar al-Baghdādī informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Turqufī informed us, Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-ʿAdanī informed us, al-Ḥakam ibn Abān narrated from ʿIkrimah, who said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: \We used to bring the boys from the Qurʾān school so that they would lead us in night prayer in the month of Ramaḍān"," and we would prepare for them fried meat and sweet bread.\"""""
"4614","(4614) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Bayhaqī informed us, my grandfather informed us, Ibn Abī Uways informed me, Mālik ibn Anas narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, he said: I read to Mālik from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd from Abū Salamah, who said: I asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, \How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ramaḍān?\"" She said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not exceed eleven rakʿahs in Ramaḍān or in any other month. He would pray four — do not ask about their beauty and length — then he would pray four — do not ask about their beauty and length — then he would pray three.\"" ʿĀʾishah said: I said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," do you sleep before you pray the Witr?\"" He said: \""O ʿĀʾishah"," my two eyes sleep"," but my heart does not sleep.\"" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. In the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī Uways: \""That he asked ʿĀʾishah"," the wife of the Prophet ﷺ.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways"," and it was narrated by Muslim from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1147]"""
"4615","(4615) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, Manṣūr ibn Abī Mazāḥim informed us, Abū Shaybah narrated from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \The Prophet ﷺ used to pray in the month of Ramaḍān"," not in congregation"," twenty rakʿahs and the Witr.\""\n\nAbū Shaybah Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUthmān al-ʿAbsī al-Kūfī is alone in narrating it. (J) And he is weak. [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4616","(4616) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us, Ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik narrated from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf, the nephew of al-Sāʾib, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded Ubayy ibn Kaʿb and Tamīm al-Dārī to lead the people in eleven rakʿahs. The reciter would recite in hundreds (of verses), until we would lean on staffs due to the length of the standing, and we would not leave except at the break of dawn. Thus it is in this narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 253]"
"4617","(4617) Abu 'Abd Allah — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Fanjuwayh al-Dinawari, in al-Damighan — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Sunni informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Baghawi narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd informed us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated from Yazid ibn Khusaifah, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, who said:\n\nThey used to perform the standing prayer (qiyam) in the month of Ramadan during the era of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, with twenty rak'ahs. He said: And they used to recite the hundreds (i.e., lengthy passages of two hundred verses), and they used to lean on their staffs during the era of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, due to the hardship of the prolonged standing. [Sahih]"
"4618","(4618) Abu Ahmad al-'Adl informed us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Yazid ibn Ruman, who said:\n\nThe people used to perform the standing prayer in the era of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in Ramadan with twenty-three rak'ahs.\n\nAnd it is possible to reconcile between the two narrations: they initially used to perform the standing prayer with eleven rak'ahs, then they performed twenty rak'ahs and concluded with three rak'ahs of Witr. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Malik 254]"
"4619","(4619) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us: Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us: Abu al-Khasib said:\n\nSuwayd ibn Ghafalah used to lead us in prayer in Ramadan, and he would pray five tarwihahs — twenty rak'ahs.\n\nAnd it has been narrated from Shutayr ibn Shakal, who was among the companions of 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to lead them in prayer in the month of Ramadan with twenty rak'ahs and would perform Witr with three. And in that there is strength for what has been stated. [Sahih]"
"4620","(4620) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan, in Baghdad, informed us: Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn 'Isa ibn 'Abdak al-Razi narrated to us: Abu 'Amir — 'Amr ibn Tamim — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us: Hammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:\n\nHe summoned the reciters (qurra') in Ramadan and commanded one of them to lead the people in twenty rak'ahs.\n\nHe said: And 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, would perform the Witr with them.\n\nAnd that has been narrated through another chain from 'Ali. As for the Tarawih, then in what has been mentioned there is [a basis]. [Da'if]"
"4621","(4621) Abu 'Abd Allah ibn Fanjuwayh al-Dinawari narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Sunni informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Bazzaz narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Yazid narrated to us: al-Hakam ibn Marwan al-Sulami informed us: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated from Abu Sa'd al-Baqqal, from Abu al-Hasnā', that:\n\n'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded a man to lead the people in five tarwihahs — twenty rak'ahs.\n\nAnd in this chain there is weakness. And Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
"4622","(4622) And Ibn Fanjuwayh informed us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Sunni narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Buzuri narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated from al-Rabi' ibn Suhaym al-Kahili, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said:\n\n'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to give us rest intervals in Ramadan — that is, between the two tarwihahs — approximately the time it would take a man to go from the mosque to Sal'.\n\nThus he said.\n\n(Q) And perhaps he intended the one who led them in the Tarawih prayer by the command of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. And Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
"4623","(4623) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari, in Tus, informed us: Abu Tahir al-Muhammadabadi narrated to us: al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Bashir al-Kufi narrated to us: al-Mu'afi ibn 'Imran narrated from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad al-Musili, from 'Ata', from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would pray four rak'ahs in the night, then he would take a rest (yatarawwah), then he would prolong the prayer until I felt mercy for him, so I said: May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah! Allah has forgiven you your earlier and later sins.\n\nHe said: \Shall I not be a grateful servant?\""\n\nAl-Mughirah ibn Ziyad is alone in narrating this. (J) And he is not strong. (Q) And his statement: \""then he would take a rest\"" — if it is established"," then it is a basis for the imam's rest interval in the Tarawih prayer. And Allah knows best. [Da'if]"""
"4624","(4624) Abu 'Abd Allah — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Fanjuwayh al-Dinawari, in al-Damighan — narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Narawayh narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah — Ibrahim ibn 'Arafah — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Shadhan narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us: Za'idah narrated to us: 'Asim al-Ahwal narrated from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, who said:\n\n'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, called for three reciters and had them recite. He commanded the fastest of them in recitation to recite thirty verses for the people, and he commanded the moderate among them to recite twenty-five verses, and he commanded the slowest of them to recite twenty verses.\n\nAnd likewise, al-Thawri narrated it from 'Asim. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Numayri in Akhbar al-Madinah 1184]"
"4625","(4625) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajanī informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, that he heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj say:\n\nI did not find the people except that they were cursing the disbelievers in Ramadan.\n\nHe said: And the reciter would lead them in Surah al-Baqarah in eight rak'ahs, and if he recited it in twelve rak'ahs, the people would consider that he had lightened the prayer. [Sahih. Malik 255]"
"4626","(4626) And through his chain, Malik narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, that he said: I heard my father say:\n\nWe used to depart from the standing prayer in Ramadan, and the servant would hasten with the food out of fear of the dawn (Fajr). [Sahih. Malik 256]"
"4627","(4627) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Bakr — informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd and Aḥmad ibn Jawwās al-Ḥanafī both said: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Burayd ibn Abī Maryam, from Abū al-Ḥawrāʾ, who said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me words that I recite in the Witr.\"" Ibn Jawwās said: \""In the Qunūt of the Witr: 'O Allah"," guide me among those whom You have granted guidance"," grant me well-being among those whom You have granted well-being"," take me as an ally among those whom You have taken as allies"," bless me in what You have given me"," and protect me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed"," You decree and none can decree upon him"," and indeed"," none is humiliated whom You have taken as an ally. Blessed are You"," our Lord"," and Exalted.'\"" [Weak. Previously mentioned.]"""
"4628","(4628) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Ḍabbī narrated to us; ʿAmr, meaning Ibn Marzūq, narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Burayd ibn Abī Maryam, from Abū al-Ḥawrāʾ, from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me: 'O Allah"," guide me among those whom You have granted guidance…'\"" He then mentioned the hadith"," and at the end of it he said: \""You recite these words in the Qunūt of the Witr.\"""""
"4629","(4629) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Hishām narrated to us, from Muḥammad, who is Ibn Sīrīn, from some of his companions, that Ubayy ibn Kaʿb used to lead them in prayer during Ramadan, and he would perform the Qunūt in the latter half of Ramadan. [Weak]"
"4630","(4630) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shujaʿ ibn Makhlad narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd informed us, from al-Ḥasan, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) gathered the people behind Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and he would lead them in prayer for twenty nights, and he would not perform the Qunūt with them except in the remaining half. When the last ten nights arrived, he would withdraw and pray in his home, and they would say: \Ubayy has absconded.\"" [Weak]"""
"4631","(4631) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to perform the Qunūt in the latter half of Ramadan. [Weak]"
"4632","(4632) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Bishr narrated to us; al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, who said: \ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib led us in prayer during the time of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with them both) for twenty nights"," then he stopped. Some of them said: 'He has freed himself for his own affairs.' Then Abū Ḥalīmah Muʿādh"," the Qāriʾ"," led them in prayer"," and he would perform the Qunūt.\"" [Weak]"""
"4633","(4633) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī — informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan — Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Tawbah al-Ṣūfī — informed us; Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Ḥātim al-Āmulī al-Najjār — narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Jumaḥī narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, that Ibn ʿUmar used not to perform the Qunūt in the Witr except in the second half of Ramadan. [Ḥasan]"
"4634","(4634) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Māsarjisī — informed us; Shaybān, meaning Ibn Farūkh al-Ubullī, narrated to us; Salām, meaning Ibn Miskin, who said: \Ibn Sīrīn used to dislike the Qunūt in the Witr except in the latter half of Ramadan.\"" [Ḥasan]"""
"4635","(4635) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Mihrajānī — informed us therein; Abū Saʿīd — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated to us, who said: \The Qunūt is in the latter half of Ramadan.\"" [Weak]"""
"4636","(4636) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd said: My father informed me, he said: \al-Awzāʿī was asked about the Qunūt in the month of Ramadan. He said: 'As for the mosques of the congregation"," they perform the Qunūt from the beginning of the month to its end. As for the people of Madinah"," they perform the Qunūt in the remaining half until its conclusion.'\"" And a musnad hadith has been narrated concerning it"," but it is weak; its chain of narration is not sound. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4637","(4637) Abū Sa'd al-Mālīnī informed us, that Abū Aḥmad ibn 'Adīyy informed us, that al-Ḥusayn ibn 'Abdullāh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, that Ayyūb al-Wazzān narrated to us, that Ghassān ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, that Abū 'Ātikah narrated from Anas: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to make qunūt in the latter half of Ramaḍān until its end.\n\nAbū Aḥmad said: Abū 'Ātikah is Ṭarīf ibn Salmān, also said to be ibn Sulaymān, a narrator of munkar (objectionable) ḥadīth. I heard Ibn Ḥammād mentioning him from al-Bukhārī. [Ḍa'īf (Weak)]"
"4638","(4638) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, that al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān al-'Āmirī informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Bashīr al-'Abdī narrated to us. (Ḥ) He said: And Abū al-Walīd informed me, that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Bashīr narrated to us, that Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Arūbah narrated to us, that Qatādah narrated, that Zurārah ibn Awfá narrated, that Sa'd ibn Hishām said: I set out to Ibn 'Abbās and asked him about the witr prayer. He said: \Shall I not direct you to the most knowledgeable of the people of the earth regarding the witr of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\"" I said: \""Who?\"" He said: \""'Ā'ishah"," may Allah be pleased with her. Go to her"," then ask her"," then inform me of what she replies to you.\"" He said: \""So I set out towards her and came to Ḥakīm ibn Aflaḥ and took him as my companion. We went to 'Ā'ishah"," sought permission"," and entered. She said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'Ḥakīm ibn Aflaḥ.' She said: 'Who is this with you?' I said: 'Sa'd ibn Hishām.' She said: 'And who is Hishām?' I said: 'Ibn 'Āmir.' She said: 'What a good man 'Āmir was! He was wounded on the day of Uḥud.' I said: 'O Mother of the Believers"," inform me about the character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' She said: 'Have you not read the Qur'ān?' I said: 'Indeed"," yes.' She said: 'For the character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the Qur'ān itself.' He said: \""I resolved to stand up"," but something occurred to me"," so I said: 'O Mother of the Believers"," inform me about the night prayer (qiyām) of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' She said: 'Have you not read: {Yā ayyuhā al-muzzammil} (O you who wraps himself in garments)?' I said: 'Indeed"," yes.' She said: 'For Allah"," the Most High"," obligated the night prayer at the beginning of this sūrah. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions stood in prayer for nearly a year until their feet swelled. Allah held back the concluding portion of it for twelve months in the heavens. Then Allah revealed the alleviation at the end of this sūrah"," and the night prayer became voluntary after having been obligatory.' He said: \""I resolved to stand up"," but the matter of the witr of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ occurred to me"," so I said: 'O Mother of the Believers"," inform me about the witr of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' She said: 'We would prepare his siwāk and his water for ablution. Then Allah would send him into whatever portion of the night He wished. He would use the siwāk and perform ablution"," then pray nine rak'āts"," sitting only at the eighth. He would supplicate his Lord and send blessings upon his Prophet"," then rise without making salām. Then he would pray the ninth rak'ah and sit. He would praise his Lord"," send blessings upon his Prophet"," and supplicate. Then he would make salām — a salām loud enough for us to hear. Then he would pray two rak'āts after making salām while seated. That makes eleven rak'āts"," O my son. When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ grew old and heavy"," he would make witr with seven rak'āts and pray two rak'āts after making salām"," O my son. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ"," when he prayed a prayer"," loved to persist in it. And when the night prayer overcame him"," he would pray twelve rak'āts during the day. I do not know that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ ever recited the entire Qur'ān in one night"," nor did he stand in prayer the entire night until morning"," nor did he ever fast an entire month completely other than Ramaḍān.'\"" He said: \""I went to Ibn 'Abbās and informed him of her ḥadīth"," and she said: 'She has spoken the truth.'\"" Sa'd's first affair — Ibn Bashīr said — meaning his first affair was that he divorced his wife"," then set out to Madīnah to sell his property there and invest it in weapons and cavalry"," then fight the Romans until he died. He met a group of his people who informed him that a group of them — six men — had intended to do that during the lifetime of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ"," and he forbade them from it.\n\nThe wording is from the ḥadīth of al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān.\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — Muslim 746]"""
"4639","(4639) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, that Abū Dāwūd informed us, that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Shabbūyah al-Marwazī informed me, that 'Alī ibn Ḥusayn narrated to me from his father, that Yazīd al-Naḥwī narrated, that 'Ikrimah narrated, that Ibn 'Abbās said regarding al-Muzzammil: {Qum al-layla illā qalīlan niṣfahu} (Stand the night except a little — half of it), it was abrogated by the verse within it: {'Alima an lan tuḥṣūhu fa-tāba 'alaykum fa-qra'ū mā tayassara min al-Qur'ān} (He knows that you will not be able to keep count, so He has turned to you; so recite what is easy of the Qur'ān) [al-Muzzammil: 20]. And nāshi'at al-layl (the hours of the night) — its beginning: their prayer was at the beginning of the night. He says: It is more fitting that you be able to keep count of what Allah has obligated upon you of the night prayer. That is because when a man sleeps, he does not know when he will awaken. And His statement {Aqwam qīlan} (Most upright in speech) [al-Muzzammil: 6] — it is more fitting that he understand the Qur'ān. And His statement {Inna laka fī al-nahāri sabḥan ṭawīlan} (Indeed, you have in the day much occupation) [al-Muzzammil: 7] — He says: much free time."
"4640","(4640) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqri' said: Abū al-'Abbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us, that al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān al-'Āmirī informed us, that Muḥammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Simāk — meaning al-Ḥanafī — who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbās saying: When the beginning of al-Muzzammil was revealed, they used to stand in prayer approximately as much as they would in the month of Ramaḍān, until its end was revealed. So between its beginning and its end was nearly a year. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic)]"
"4641","(4641) Abū al-Ḥusayn 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abdullāh ibn Bishrān al-'Adl in Baghdād informed us, that Abū 'Alī Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, that 'Abd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham informed us, that Abū al-Yamān al-Ḥakam ibn Nāfi' informed me, that Shu'ayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated, from al-Zuhrī, who said: 'Alī ibn Ḥusayn informed me that al-Ḥusayn ibn 'Alī informed him that 'Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib informed him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon him and Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, at night and said: \Do you not pray?\"" He said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," our souls are in the Hand of Allah. If He wishes to awaken us"," He awakens us.\"" He (the narrator) said: \""He departed when I said that and did not return with anything. Then I heard him as he was leaving"," striking his thigh and saying: {Wa kāna al-insānu akthara shay'in jadalan} (And man is"," of all things"," most quarrelsome) [al-Kahf: 54].\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Yamān"," and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of 'Uqayl from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic)]"""
"4642","(4642) Abū Muḥammad 'Abdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī in Baghdād informed us, that Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us, that 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us, that Ma'mar narrated, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: During the era of the Prophet ﷺ, when a man saw a dream, he would recount it to the Prophet ﷺ. He said: \I wished that I could see a dream and recount it to the Prophet ﷺ.\"" He said: \""And I was a young"," unmarried man"," and I used to sleep in the mosque.\"" He said: \""I saw as though two angels came to me"," and one of them said to the other: 'Take him to the Fire.'\"" He said: \""I began saying: 'I seek refuge in Allah from the Fire.'\"" He said: \""Then another angel met us and said to me: 'Do not be frightened.'\"" He said: \""They took me until we stood upon the Fire"," and lo"," it was folded up"," and it had two horns like the two wells.\"" He said: \""And I saw in it men whom I recognized.\"" He said: \""When I awoke in the morning"," I went to Ḥafṣah and recounted it to her"," and Ḥafṣah recounted it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'What an excellent man 'Abdullāh is"," were he to stand in prayer at night.'\"" Sālim said: \""After that"," he would not sleep at night except a little.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Maḥmūd and Isḥāq ibn Naṣr from 'Abd al-Razzāq"," and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Rāhawayh and 'Abd ibn Ḥumayd from 'Abd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — Muslim 2479]"""
"4643","(4643) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, that al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us, that 'Abdullāh ibn Wahb informed me, that Ibn Abī al-Zinād and Mālik ibn Anas narrated, from Abī al-Zinād, from 'Abd al-Raḥmān — al-A'raj — from Abī Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The devil ties three knots at the back of the head of any one of you when he sleeps. At each knot he strikes his place upon you"," saying: 'You have a long night ahead"," so sleep.' When he awakens and remembers Allah"," one knot is loosened. If he performs ablution"," one knot is loosened. If he prays"," one knot is loosened"," and he wakes up energetic and of good spirit. If he does not do that"," he wakes up foul-spirited and sluggish.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik"," and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Ibn 'Uyaynah from Abī al-Zinād. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — Muslim 7776]"""
"4644","(4644) Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' informed us, that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, that Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, that Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, that Abū al-Muthannā informed us, that Musaddad informed us, that Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd informed us, that Muḥammad ibn 'Ajlān narrated, from al-Qa'qā' ibn Ḥakīm, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \May Allah have mercy upon a man who stands at night and prays"," and awakens his wife. If she refuses"," he sprinkles water on her face. May Allah have mercy upon a woman who stands at night and prays"," and awakens her husband. If she refuses"," she sprinkles water on his face.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — Ibn Khuzaymah 1148]"""
"4645","(4645) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Alī al-Mihrajānī ibn al-Saqqā', and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-'Aṭṭār, and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn 'Alī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, that Muḥammad ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān al-'Āmirī — brother of al-Ḥasan — informed us, that 'Ubaydullāh ibn Mūsá informed us, that Shaybān narrated, from al-A'mash, from 'Alī ibn al-Aqmar, from al-Agharr Abī Muslim, from Abī Sa'īd and Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever awakens at night and awakens his wife"," and they both pray two rak'āts together"," they are recorded that night among those who remember Allah much and those who remember [Him].\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — Ibn Ḥibbān 2569]"""
"4646","(4646) And Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, that Abū Dāwūd informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us, that Sufyān narrated, from 'Alī ibn al-Aqmar, and he mentioned it but did not raise it (to the Prophet ﷺ), nor did he mention Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him; he placed it within the words of Abī Sa'īd.\n\nAbū Dāwūd said: Ibn Mahdī narrated it from Sufyān, and he said: \I think he mentioned Abī Hurayrah.\""\n\nAbū Dāwūd said: The ḥadīth of Sufyān is mawqūf (stopping at the Companion's level).\n\nThe shaykh said: And 'Īsā ibn Ja'far al-Rāzī narrated it from Sufyān"," raising it (marfū')"," similar to the ḥadīth of al-A'mash."""
"4647","(4647) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn ʿAwdh Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; ʿAwfu al-Aʿrābī narrated from Zurārah ibn Awfā, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Madīnah"," the people rushed toward him and said"," 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has arrived.' So I came among the people to look at his face. When I saw his face"," I recognized that his face was not the face of a liar. The first thing I heard from him was that he said: 'O people"," feed the food"," spread the greeting of peace"," maintain the ties of kinship"," and pray during the night while the people are asleep — you shall enter Paradise in peace.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tirmidhī 2485]"""
"4648","(4648) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Zāhid informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd, from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī, from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: \Upon you is the standing in prayer during the night"," for it was the practice of the righteous before you"," and it is a means of nearness for you to your Lord"," and an expiation for sins"," and a deterrent from wrongdoing.\"" Such is the wording of this narration. [Ḥasan. Ibn Khuzaymah 1135]"""
"4649","(4649) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw informed us; ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us; Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Khālid Abū ʿAbdullāh narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Thābit al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us; Makkī narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Khālid ibn Abī Khālid narrated from Yazīd ibn Rabīʿah, from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī, from Bilāl ibn Rabbāḥ, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: \Upon you is the standing in prayer during the night"," for it was the practice of the righteous before you"," and it is a means of nearness to Allah"," and an expiation for sins"," and a deterrent from wrongdoing"," and a means of driving disease away from the body.\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdullāh. And in the narration of al-Qaṭṭān: \""And indeed"," the standing in prayer during the night is a means of nearness to Allah"," the Most High.\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4650","(4650) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; Abū al-Ḍaḥr narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us; Abū al-Ḍaḥr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us; Bakr ibn Khunays narrated from Muḥammad al-Qurashī, from Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd, from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī, from Bilāl, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Upon you is the standing in prayer during the night"," for it was the practice of the righteous before you"," and indeed the standing in prayer during the night is a means of nearness to Allah"," the Most High"," and an expiation for sins"," and a deterrent from wrongdoing"," and a means of driving disease away from the body.\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4651","(4651) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Miṣʿar narrated from Zubayd, from Murrah al-Hamdānī, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: \The superiority of the prayer of the night over the prayer of the day is like the superiority of secret charity over public charity.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4735]"""
"4652","(4652) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Asfāṭī, meaning al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl, narrated to us; Ismāʿīl narrated from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated. He said: I read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abī ʿAbdullāh al-Agharr and from Abī Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Our Lord"," Mighty and Majestic"," descends to the lowest heaven every night when the last third of the night remains"," and He says: 'Who supplicates Me that I may answer him? Who asks Me that I may give him? Who seeks My forgiveness that I may forgive him?'\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And in the narration of Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and al-Qaʿnabī"," they did not mention the conjunction \""and\"" and they placed Abī Salamah before Abī ʿAbdullāh al-Agharr. al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī and Ibn Abī Uways"," and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1094]"""
"4653","(4653) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Muḥāḍir ibn al-Muwarrʿ narrated to us; Saʿd ibn Saʿīd narrated to me; Saʿīd ibn Murjānah narrated. He said: I heard Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Allah"," Mighty and Majestic"," descends to the lowest heaven at half of the night or at the last third of the night"," and He says: 'Who supplicates Me that I may answer him? Or who asks Me that I may give him?' Then He says: 'Who will lend to One who is neither impoverished nor unjust?'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir from Muḥāḍir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous]"""
"4654","(4654) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bishr ibn Maṭar narrated to us; al-Haytham ibn Khārijah narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated. He said: al-Awzāʿī, Mālik, Sufyān al-Thawrī, and al-Layth ibn Saʿd were asked about these aḥādīth that came regarding tashbīh (anthropomorphism). They said: \Pass them as they have come"," without asking 'how.'\""\n\nAnd Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Isḥāq ibn Aḥmad al-Fārisī narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Mihraqānī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd"," who is al-Ṭayālisī"," narrated. He said: Sufyān al-Thawrī"," Shuʿbah"," Ḥammād ibn Zayd"," Ḥammād ibn Salamah"," Sharīk"," and Abū ʿAwanah would not define"," nor draw resemblance"," nor make likenesses. They would narrate the ḥadīth and would not say \""how.\"" And when they were asked"," they would respond with the athar (narrated tradition). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr in al-Tamhīd 7/149]"""
"4655","(4655) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. He said: I heard Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī say: The ḥadīth of al-nuzūl (descent) has been established from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ through authentic chains, and there has come in the Revelation that which confirms it, and it is the words of the Most High: {And your Lord will come, and the angels, rank upon rank} [al-Fajr: 22]. And al-nuzūl (descent) and al-maʾīyīj (coming) are two attributes that are negated from Allah, the Most High, in the sense of physical movement and transition from one state to another. Rather, they are two of the attributes of Allah, the Most High, without drawing resemblance. Allah, the Most High, is exalted far above what the muʿaṭṭilah (those who deny His attributes) and the mushabbihah (those who liken Him to creation) say, an exalted transcendence.\n\nI say: And Abū Sulaymān al-Khaṭṭābī, may Allah have mercy upon him, used to say: \That which is rejected of this and similar aḥādīth is only rejected by one who measures such matters against what he observes of descent"," which is a lowering from above to below"," and a movement from the upper to the lower — and these are the attributes of bodies and corporeal forms. As for the descent of One over whom the attributes of bodies have no sway"," then such meanings are not conceivable in relation to Him. It is rather a report of His power"," His mercy toward His servants"," His compassion for them"," His answering of their supplications"," and His forgiveness of them. He does what He wills — no modality applies to His attributes"," and no quantity applies to His actions. Glorified is He — there is nothing like unto Him"," and He is the All-Hearing"," the All-Seeing.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4656","(4656) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Fārayābī narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated. And Abū Yaʿlā narrated to me; Abū Khaythamah narrated. They all said: Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated that he heard ʿAmr ibn Aws al-Thaqafī say: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: \The most beloved fasting to Allah is the fasting of Dāwūd — he would fast one day and break fast one day. And the most beloved prayer to Allah is the prayer of Dāwūd — he would sleep half of the night"," stand in prayer for its third"," and sleep for its sixth.\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Ḥumaydī. And others narrated from him"," from him. al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others"," and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Abū Khaythamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1079]"""
"4657","(4657) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us. Abū ʿAbdillāh said: \It was narrated to us","\"" and Abū Zakariyyā said: \""It was reported to us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us"," Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ reported to us"," Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn reported to us"," Misʿar reported to us"," from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm"," from Abū Salamah"," who said: ʿĀʾishah"," may Allah be pleased with her"," said: 'I never found the Prophet ﷺ at the end of the night except that he was asleep.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Misʿar"," and al-Bukhārī extracted it from the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd from his father."""
"4658","(4658) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ḥusayn ibn Yazīd al-Kūfī narrated to us, Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \Indeed"," Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," would awaken the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the night"," and dawn would not arrive until he had completed his portion (of night prayer).\""\n\n[Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1316]"""
"4659","(4659) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah reported to us, from al-Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from his father, from Masrūq, who said: \I asked ʿĀʾishah"," may Allah be pleased with her"," about the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and she said: 'The most beloved deed to him was that which was done consistently.' I said: 'At what time would he stand (for prayer)?' She said: 'When he heard the crowing of the rooster"," he would stand.'\""\n\nAbū Dāwūd said: \""Meaning the rooster.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1081]"""
"4660","(4660) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us, Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, al-Ashʿath ibn Sulaym reported to us, from his father, from Masrūq, who said: \I asked ʿĀʾishah"," may Allah be pleased with her"," about the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and she said: 'He loved that which was done consistently.' I said to her: 'At what time would he pray?' She said: 'When he heard the crowing of the rooster"," he would stand and pray.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah"," and both of them extracted it from the ḥadīth of Abū al-Aḥwaṣ from al-Ashʿath.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4661","(4661) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us, Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī reported to us, from Zāʾidah, from ʿAbd al-Malik — meaning Ibn ʿUmayr — from Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Ḥumayd al-Ḥimyarī, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: \A man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'Which prayer is most excellent after the obligatory prayer?' He said: 'Prayer in the middle of the night.' He said: 'Which fasting is most excellent after Ramadan?' He said: 'The month of Allah which you call al-Muḥarram.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah"," from Ḥusayn al-Juʿfī.\n\n(T) And similarly Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr"," and similarly Abū Bishr narrated it from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1163]"""
"4662","(4662) And ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqqī narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Jundub ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Bajalī, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \Among the most excellent prayers after the obligatory prayer is prayer in the middle of the night"," and indeed the most excellent fasting after the month of Ramadan is the month of Allah which you call al-Muḥarram.\""\n\nAḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us of it"," Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us"," Muḥammad ibn Muʿādh al-Marwazī narrated to us"," Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī narrated to us"," and ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated it.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4663","(4663) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī narrated to us, Ibn Wahb reported to us, Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me, Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir, Ḍamrah ibn Ḥabīb, and Nuʿaym ibn Ziyād informed me, from Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī, who said: ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, saying: \I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was encamped at ʿUkāẓ"," and I said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," is there any supplication that is closer (to acceptance) than another"," or any hour that we should seek or whose remembrance we should seek?' He said: 'Yes"," indeed the closest the Lord is to the servant is in the last part of the night. So if you can be among those who remember Allah in that hour"," then be so.'\""\n\n(T) And we have narrated previously from Abū Sallām"," from Abū Umāmah"," from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah"," who said: \""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," which part of the night is most heard (i.e."," in which supplication is most readily answered)?' He said: 'The last part of the night.'\""\n\n[Ḥasan. Aḥmad 4/385]"""
"4664","(4664) And ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdad informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq reported to us, from ʿAwf al-Aʿrābī, from Abū al-Khālid, from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, who said: \Abū Muslim narrated to me"," saying: 'I said to Abū Dharr"," may Allah be pleased with him: \""Which prayer of the night is most excellent?\"" He said: \""I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and he said: 'The middle of the night"," though few are those who do it.'\""\n\n[Ḥasan. Aḥmad 5/179]"""
"4665","(4665) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah reported to us, from Sulaymān ibn Abī Muslim, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stand at night to perform tahajjud...\""\n\n(H) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and the wording is his — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us"," Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us"," al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us"," Sufyān narrated to us"," Sulaymān al-Aḥwal — the maternal uncle of Ibn Abī Najīḥ — said: \""I heard Ṭāwūs say: 'I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: \""When the Prophet ﷺ would stand at night to perform tahajjud"," he would say: 'O Allah"," to You be all praise. You are the Light of the heavens and the earth and all that is in them. To You be all praise. You are the Sustainer of the heavens and the earth and all that is in them. To You be all praise. You are the King of the heavens and the earth and all that is in them. To You be all praise. You are the Truth"," and Your promise is the truth"," and Your word is the truth"," and meeting You is the truth"," and Paradise is true"," and the Fire is true"," and the Hour is true"," and Muḥammad ﷺ is true"," and the Prophets are true. O Allah"," to You I have submitted"," and in You I have believed"," and upon You I have relied"," and to You I have turned in repentance"," and with You I have disputed"," and to You I have referred for judgment. So forgive me what I have sent ahead and what I have left behind"," and what I have concealed and what I have made public. You are the One who brings forward and You are the One who delays. There is no deity except You.'\"" Or he said: \""There is no deity other than You.\"" Sufyān was uncertain.\n\nAl-Ḥumaydī said: Sufyān said: \""And ʿAbd al-Karīm Abū Umayyah added: 'And there is no power and no strength except with Allah.' But Sulaymān did not say it.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh and others"," and Muslim narrated it from ʿAmr al-Nāqid and others"," all of them from Ibn ʿUyaynah.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1069]"""
"4666","(4666) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdad informed us, Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Ṣaffār informed us in al-Muḥarram of the year forty-one and three hundred, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq reported to us, Ibn Jurayj reported to us, Sulaymān al-Aḥwal informed me, from Ṭāwūs, that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: \When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would perform tahajjud at night"," he would say: 'O Allah"," to You be all praise. You are the Light of the heavens and the earth. To You be all praise. You are the Sustainer of the heavens and the earth and all that is in them. You are the Truth"," and Your promise is the truth"," and Your word is the truth"," and meeting You is the truth. Paradise is true and the Fire is true"," and the Prophets are true. O Allah"," to You I have submitted"," and in You I have believed"," and upon You I have relied"," and to You I have turned in repentance"," and with You I have disputed"," and to You I have referred for judgment. So forgive me what I have sent ahead and what I have left behind"," and what I have concealed and what I have made public. You are my deity; there is no deity except You.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See above]"""
"4667","(4667) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ al-ʿAskarī and Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān al-Qaṣrī informed me, both saying: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us; al-Awzāʿī narrated to us; ʿUmayr ibn Hānīʾ narrated to me; Junādat Abī Umayyah narrated to me; ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit narrated to me, saying: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever wakes up during the night and says: 'There is no deity except Allah"," alone"," without partner; to Him belongs sovereignty"," and to Him belongs all praise"," and He is over all things capable. Glory be to Allah"," and praise be to Allah"," and there is no deity except Allah"," and Allah is the Greatest"," and there is no power and no strength except with Allah","' then says: 'O my Lord"," forgive me","' he will be forgiven; or if he supplicates"," his supplication will be answered; and if he resolves"," rises"," performs wuḍūʾ"," and prays"," his prayer will be accepted.\""\n\nNarrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1103]"""
"4668","(4668) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn Yūnus narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn Yūnus informed us; ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār informed me; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr informed me; Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf informed me, saying: I asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, \With what would the Prophet of Allah ﷺ begin the prayer when he stood up at night?\"" She said: \""When he stood up at night"," he would begin his prayer by saying: 'O Allah"," Lord of Jibrīl"," Mīkāʾīl"," and Isrāfīl"," Originator of the heavens and the earth"," Knower of the unseen and the seen"," You judge between Your servants in that over which they differ. Guide me to the truth in that over which they differed"," by Your permission. Indeed"," You guide whom You will to a straight path.'\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 770]"""
"4669","(4669) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿIṣmah ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAdl informed us; my father narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Hushaym informed us; Abū Ḥurrah informed us; from al-Ḥasan; from Saʿd ibn Hishām; from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up at night to pray"," he would begin his prayer with two light rakʿahs.\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 767]"""
"4670","(4670) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us; from Hishām; from Muḥammad; from Abū Hurayrah; from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \When one of you stands up at night"," let him begin his prayer with two light rakʿahs.\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr. And likewise narrated by Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar and others from Hishām ibn Ḥassān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 198]"""
"4671","(4671) Abū al-Ḥusayn Jāmiʿ ibn Aḥmad al-Wakīl informed us; Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us; ʿUthmān al-Dārimī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated to us; from Hishām ibn Ḥassān; from Ibn Sīrīn; from Abū Hurayrah: that the Prophet ﷺ would begin his night prayer with two light rakʿahs.\n\nAnd it was narrated by a group from Hishām, it being mawqūf (stopped) on Abū Hurayrah, among them Ḥammād ibn Salamah and Ḥammād ibn Zayd. And likewise narrated by Ayyūb and Ibn ʿAwn from Ibn Sīrīn. [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 6623]"
"4672","(4672) And it is narrated in the ḥadīth of Ayyūb from Ibn Sīrīn from Abū Hurayrah: \Then let him lengthen thereafter as he wishes.\""\n\nAbū ʾl-Rūdhbārī informed us of it; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Makhlad ibn Khālid narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Khālid narrated to us; from Rābāḥ; from Maʿmar; from Ayyūb; from Ibn Sīrīn; from Abū Hurayrah"," from his statement. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1324]"""
"4673","(4673) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Shaybānī narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated to us; from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, saying: I read to Mālik; from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī; from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: I asked ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, \How was the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ in Ramadan?\"" She said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not exceed eleven rakʿahs in Ramadan or in any other month. He would pray four — do not ask about their beauty and length — then he would pray four — do not ask about their beauty and length — then he would pray three.\"" ʿĀʾishah"," may Allah be pleased with her"," said: I said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," do you sleep before you perform the witr?\"" He said: \""O ʿĀʾishah"," my two eyes sleep"," but my heart does not sleep.\""\n\nNarrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī"," and narrated by Muslim from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1096]"""
"4674","(4674) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us by dictation; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī informed us in Makkah; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us; from Ibn Abī Labīd; from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; from ʿĀʾishah, who said: I asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: \His prayer at night in the month of Ramadan and in other months was thirteen rakʿahs"," including the two rakʿahs of Fajr.\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1089]"""
"4675","(4675) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Muḥammad ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad ibn Mahdī informed us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ḥanẓalah informed us; from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad; from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray at night thirteen rakʿahs"," including the witr and the two rakʿahs of Fajr.\""\n\nNarrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1089]"""
"4676","(4676) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Mihrān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī Sufyān informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad informed me; my father narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to me; Ḥanẓalah informed me; from al-Qāsim; from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray ten rakʿahs at night"," perform the witr with one rakʿah"," and pray two rakʿahs of Fajr — making that thirteen rakʿahs.\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 738]"""
"4677","(4677) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīb ibn Milḥān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us, al-Layth narrated —\n(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd informed us, al-Layth narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from 'Irāk ibn Mālik, from 'Urwah, that 'Ā'ishah informed him: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of Fajr.\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 737]"
"4678","(4678) Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Muzanī informed us, 'Alī ibn Muḥammad informed us, Abū al-Yamān informed us, Shu'ayb informed me from al-Zuhrī, 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, and 'Ā'ishah informed me: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray eleven rak'ahs — that was his prayer. He would prostrate for a length of time in which one of you would recite fifty verses before raising his head, and he would pray two rak'ahs before the prayer of Fajr, then he would lie down on his right side until the mu'adhdhin called for the prayer.\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 949]"
"4679","(4679) Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb al-Sūsī informed us, Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn 'Awf informed us, Abū al-Mughīrah informed us, al-Awzā'ī narrated from al-Zuhrī, from 'Urwah, from 'Ā'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray between the last 'Ishā' prayer and the break of Fajr eleven rak'ahs, making salām after every two rak'ahs, and performing Witr with one rak'ah. He would remain in prostration for a length of time in which one of you would recite fifty verses. When the mu'adhdhin fell silent, he would stand and pray two light rak'ahs, then lie down on his right side until the mu'adhdhin came to him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"4680","(4680) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, al-Qa'nabī narrated from Mālik, from Makhramah ibn Sulaymān, from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbās, that 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abbās informed him: He spent the night at Maymūnah's — the wife of the Prophet ﷺ — who was his maternal aunt. He said: I lay down across the width of the pillow, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his family lay down along its length. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept until when half the night had passed, or a little before that, or a little after that, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ woke up and sat, wiping the sleep from his face with his hand. Then he recited the last ten verses of Sūrat Āl 'Imrān. Then he stood up towards a hanging water-skin and performed wuḍū' from it, and he performed wuḍū' excellently. Then he stood up to pray. 'Abd Allāh said: I stood up and did as he had done, then I went and stood beside him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his right hand upon my head and took hold of my ear, twisting it. He prayed two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs — al-Qa'nabī said: six times — then he performed Witr, then he lay down until the mu'adhdhin came to him. He stood up and prayed two light rak'ahs, then he went out and prayed the Ṣubḥ prayer.\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qa'nabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 181]"
"4681","(4681) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibn Milḥān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us, al-Layth narrated from Khālid, meaning Ibn Yazīd, from Sa'īd ibn Abī Hilāl, from Makhramah ibn Sulaymān, that Kurayb informed him, saying: I asked Ibn 'Abbās about the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: Then he stood up and I stood beside him on his left side, and he placed me on his right side. Then he placed his hands upon my head and began touching my ear as though he was waking me. He prayed two light rak'ahs. I said: He recited Umm al-Qur'ān in each rak'ah, then made salām. Then he prayed two rak'ahs, then made salām, until he had prayed eleven rak'ahs with the Witr. Then he slept until he was in deep sleep, and I saw him snoring. Bilāl came to him and said: The prayer, O Messenger of Allah! He stood up and prayed two rak'ahs, and he led the people in prayer and did not perform wuḍū'.\n'Ā'ishah said: No Prophet's eyes sleep without his heart remaining awake, and if his heart were to sleep, his eyes would wake up. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1364]"
"4682","(4682) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Nūḥ ibn Ḥabīb and Yaḥyā ibn Mūsā both said: 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Ṭāwūs, from 'Ikrimah ibn Khālid, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: I spent the night at my maternal aunt Maymūnah's. The Prophet ﷺ stood up to pray at night, and he prayed thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of Fajr. I estimated his standing in each rak'ah to be approximately the length of the recitation of {Yā ayyuhā al-muzzammil}. Nūḥ did not say: \including the two rak'ahs of Fajr.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1365]"""
"4683","(4683) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismā'īl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, 'Abd Allāh, meaning al-Qa'nabī, narrated from Mālik, from 'Abd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, from his father, that 'Abd Allāh ibn Qays ibn Makhramah informed him, from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī, that he said: I will certainly observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tonight. He said: I lay down at his threshold or at his tent. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed two light rak'ahs, then he prayed two very long, very long, very long rak'ahs. Then he prayed two rak'ahs that were shorter than the two before them. Then he prayed two rak'ahs that were shorter than the two before them. Then he prayed two rak'ahs that were shorter than the two before them. Then he prayed two rak'ahs that were shorter than the two before them. Then he performed Witr. That was thirteen rak'ahs.\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd from Mālik. He added: Then he prayed two rak'ahs shorter than the two before them. And al-Qa'nabī said the same in another narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 765]"
"4684","(4684) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh and 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Ṣaydalānī both said: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us.\n(Ḥ) And 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Muslim informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us, Shu'bah narrated from Sulaymān al-A'mash, from Abū Wā'il, from 'Abd Allāh, who said: I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ one night, and he kept standing until I contemplated doing something evil. I said: What did you contemplate? He said: I contemplated sitting down and leaving the Prophet ﷺ.\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-A'mash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1084]"
"4685","(4685) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm al-Qanṭarī informed us, Abū Qilābah informed us, Abū 'Āṣim informed us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The best prayer is that with the longest qunūt (standing/devotion).\""\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abd ibn Ḥumayd from Abū 'Āṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 756]"""
"4686","(4686) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn al-Mu'ammal informed us, Abū 'Uthmān al-Baṣrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhāb informed us, Ya'lā ibn 'Ubayd narrated —\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdad informed us, Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, Ya'lā ibn 'Ubayd informed us, al-A'mash narrated from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir, who said: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ: Which prayer is best? He said: \The longest qunūt (standing/devotion).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"""
"4687","(4687) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Abū Mu'āwiya narrated from al-A'mash, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: \He was asked.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayba and others"," from Abū Mu'āwiya.\n\nAnd the ḥadīth of Ḥudhayfa from the Prophet ﷺ regarding his night prayer and his recitation therein—in one rak'a of which he would recite Sūrat al-Baqara"," Āl 'Imrān"," and Sūrat al-Nisā'—has already been mentioned. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited.]"""
"4688","(4688) Abū Muḥammad 'Abdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Sa'īd ibn al-A'rābī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Za'farānī narrated to us; Abū Mu'āwiya al-Ḍarīr narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Abū Mu'āwiya narrated from al-A'mash, from Shaqīq, who said: A man called Nahīk ibn Sinān came to 'Abdullāh and said, \O Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān"," how do you recite this verse: {min mā'in ghayri āsin} [Muḥammad: 15]—with a hamzah or with an alif?\"" He said"," \""Have you memorised the entire Qur'ān other than this?\"" He said"," \""Indeed"," I recite the Mufaṣṣal in a single rak'a.\"" 'Abdullāh said"," \""This is like the recitation of poetry. Indeed"," the best of prayer is [that with the most] rukū' and sujūd. People will recite the Qur'ān"," but it will not go beyond their throats. Rather"," when it is recited and it settles in the heart and becomes firmly rooted therein"," it benefits. Indeed"," I know the paired sūras that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite—two sūras in every rak'a.\"" Then he stood and left. 'Alqama came in"," so we said to him"," \""Ask him about the paired sūras that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in every rak'a.\"" He entered and asked him"," then came out and said"," \""Twenty sūras from the beginning of the Mufaṣṣal"," according to the arrangement of 'Abdullāh.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb"," from Abū Mu'āviya.\n\nAnd Wakī' said from al-A'mash: \""Indeed"," the best of prayer is [that with the most] rukū' and sujūd.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 822]"""
"4689","(4689) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū 'Amr ibn Abī Ja'far informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayba narrated to us; Wakī' narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated from Abī Wā'il, and he mentioned the ḥadīth, in which 'Abdullāh said: \Have you memorised the entire Qur'ān other than this?\"" He said"," \""Indeed"," I recite the Mufaṣṣal in a single rak'a.\"" 'Abdullāh said"," \""This is like the recitation of poetry. Indeed"," people will recite the Qur'ān"," but it will not go beyond their throats. Rather"," when it falls into the heart and becomes firmly rooted therein"," it benefits. Indeed"," the best of prayer is [that with the most] rukū' and sujūd. And indeed"," I know the paired sūras that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite together—two sūras in every rak'a.\"" Then 'Abdullāh stood and left"," and 'Alqama followed him. Then 'Alqama came out and said"," \""He has informed me of them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayba. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited.]"""
"4690","(4690) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū Ja'far al-Razzāz informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Ḥajjāj narrated, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me; 'Uthmān ibn Abī Sulaymān narrated from 'Alī al-Azdī from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr from 'Abdullāh ibn Ḥubshī that the Prophet ﷺ was asked: \Which deed is best?\"" He said: \""Faith (īmān) in which there is no doubt"," jihād in which there is no treachery"," and an accepted ḥajj (ḥajj mabrūr).\"" It was said: \""Which prayer is best?\"" He said: \""Lengthy standing (qiyām).\"" And he mentioned the ḥadīth. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1325]"""
"4691","(4691) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Sa'īd ibn al-A'rābī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Za'farānī narrated to us; Shabāba narrated to us; Isrā'īl narrated from Abī Isḥāq from 'Alqama and al-Aswad from Ibn Mas'ūd: A man came to him and said, \Indeed"," I recite the Mufaṣṣal in a single rak'a.\"" He said"," \""This is like the recitation of poetry and like the scattering of inferior dates. Rather"," the Prophet ﷺ used to recite paired sūras—two sūras in a rak'a: al-Raḥmān and al-Najm in one rak'a; al-Qāri'a and al-Ḥāqqa in one rak'a; al-Ṭūr and al-Dhāriyāt in one rak'a; Idhā waqa'at and al-Nūn in one rak'a; 'Amma yatasā'alūn and al-Mursalāt in one rak'a; al-Dukhān and Idhā al-shamsu kuwwirat—that is"," in one rak'a.\"" [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd 1396]"""
"4692","(4692) Someone other than him added from Isrā'īl in this ḥadīth: \Sa'ala sā'il and al-Nāzi'āt in one rak'a"," and Waylun lil-muṭaffifīn and 'Abasa in one rak'a.\"" Then he mentioned 'Amma yatasā'alūn and what comes after it.\n\nAbū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī narrated it to us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; 'Abbād ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ismā'īl ibn Ja'far narrated from Isrā'īl"," and he mentioned it with the addition. [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd 1396]"""
"4693","(4693) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salama informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; 'Īsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated from Shaqīq from 'Abdullāh, and he mentioned the ḥadīth with the same meaning as the ḥadīth of Wakī', except that he said: \Indeed"," the best of prayer is [that with the most] rukū' and sujūd. Indeed"," I know the paired sūras that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite—two in every rak'a: twenty sūras in ten rak'āt.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 822]"""
"4694","(4694) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; al-'Abbās al-Dūrī narrated to us; Rūḥ ibn Ḥarb al-Simṣār, Abū Ḥātim, narrated to us; Marwān ibn Mu'āwiya informed us; 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal informed us; Ibn Sīrīn said: \Ibn 'Umar used to recite ten sūras in every rak'a.\""\n\n'Āṣim said: \""I mentioned that to Abū al-'Āliya"," and he said: 'And I used to recite twenty sūras in every rak'a. However"," someone who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me"," saying: \""Every sūra has its share of rukū' and sujūd.\""'\""\n\n'Abd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād followed him"," narrating from 'Āṣim in the ḥadīth of Abū al-'Āliya. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 5/65]"""
"4695","(4695) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; 'Uthmān ibn 'Umar narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wāḥid narrated to us; 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal narrated from Abū al-'Āliya, who said: \Someone who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me"," saying: 'Every sūra has its share of rukū' and sujūd.'\"" Anas said to him"," \""Who informed you?\"" He said"," \""I remember"," and I remember the place where he informed me.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4696","(4696) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-'Abbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us; Asad ibn Mūsā informed us; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Mukhāriq, who said: \I passed by Abū Dharr at al-Rabadha while I was performing ḥajj. I entered his house and found him praying"," making the qiyām brief—approximately the length of his recitation of {Innā a'ṭaynāka al-kawthar} and {Idhā jā'a naṣru Llāh}—but making rukū' and sujūd abundant. When he finished the prayer"," I said"," 'O Abū Dharr"," I saw you making the qiyām brief and making rukū' and sujūd abundant.' He said"," 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \""There is no servant who prostrates to Allah a single prostration or bows to Allah a single rukū' except that Allah removes thereby a sin from him and raises him thereby a degree.\""'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Aḥmad 5/147]"""
"4697","(4697) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Muʿāwiya, that is Ibn Ṣāliḥ, narrated to me, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith, from Zayd ibn Arṭāʾah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar saw a young man who was praying and had lengthened his prayer and drawn it out excessively. He said: \Does anyone know this man?\"" A man said: \""I do.\"" ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: \""If I knew him"," I would command him to lengthen the rukūʿ and the sujūd"," for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'When a servant stands to pray"," his sins are brought and placed upon his head and his shoulders; every time he bows or prostrates"," they fall away from him.'\"" [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Ibn Ḥibbān 1734]"""
"4698","(4698) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Warkānī narrated to us: Ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to me, from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr, the freed slave of al-Muṭṭalib, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \The recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was at such a volume that whoever was in the ḥujrah could hear it while he was in the house.\""\n\nSaʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated it from Ibn Abī al-Zinād and said in its text: \""His recitation could be heard by whoever was behind the ḥujrah while he was in the house.\"" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1327]"""
"4699","(4699) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Khālid, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from Makhramah ibn Sulaymān, that Kurayb informed him, saying: \I asked Ibn ʿAbbās: 'How was the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?' He said: 'He would recite in one of his chambers"," and his recitation would be heard by whoever was outside.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī in al-Kubrā 499]"""
"4700","(4700) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm al-Qanṭarī in Baghdād narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq al-Sāliḥīnī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rabāḥ, from Abū Qatādah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) passed by Abū Bakr while he was praying, lowering his voice, and he passed by ʿUmar while he was praying, raising his voice. When they both gathered before the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), he said to Abū Bakr: \O Abū Bakr"," I passed by you while you were praying"," lowering your voice.\"" He said: \""I was heard by the One to whom I was speaking privately.\"" Then he said: \""O ʿUmar"," I passed by you while you were raising your voice.\"" He said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," I intended thereby to awaken the drowsy one.\"" So he said to Abū Bakr: \""Raise your voice a little.\"" And he said to ʿUmar: \""Lower your voice a little.\"" [Abū Dāwūd 1329]"""
"4701","(4701) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ḥammād narrated to us, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), mentioning it in a mursal narration up to his words: \He said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," I intended to awaken the drowsome one and to drive away Satan.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1329]"""
"4702","(4702) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Ḥuṣayn ibn Yaḥyā al-Rāzī narrated to us: Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), with this story, but he did not mention: \He said to Abū Bakr: 'Raise a little","' nor to ʿUmar: 'Lower a little.'\""\n\nHe said: \""I heard you"," O Bilāl"," reciting from this sūrah and from this sūrah.\"" He said: \""Fine speech"," which Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," gathers some of it to some.\"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \""All of you have hit the mark.\"" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1330]"""
"4703","(4703) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb al-ʿAdl narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā both said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us: Maʿmar informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) performed iʿtikāf in the mosque and heard them raising their voices in recitation while he was in his tent. He drew back the curtain and said: 'Verily"," each of you is privately conversing with his Lord; so let some of you not harm others"," and let some of you not raise their voices over others in recitation during the prayer.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1332]"""
"4704","(4704) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī, from Abū Ḥāzim al-Tammār, from al-Bayāḍī, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came out to the people while they were praying and their voices had risen in recitation. He said: \The one who prays is privately conversing with his Lord; let him see what he converses with Him by"," and let some of you not raise your voices over others in recitation.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 177]"""
"4705","(4705) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Saʿd al-Zuhrī, Abū Ibrāhīm, informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān al-Juʿfī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah: \The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) heard a man reciting at night in the mosque and said: 'May Allah have mercy on him; he has reminded me of such-and-such verses which I had forgotten from such-and-such a sūrah.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bishr ibn Ādam from ʿAlī ibn Mushir"," and both he and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Usāmah from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2512]"""
"4706","(4706) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, that a man stood at night and recited, raising his voice with the Qurʾān. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \May Allah have mercy on so-and-so; how many verses he has reminded me of tonight which I had omitted.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"""
"4707","(4707) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā): that the Prophet (ﷺ) heard a man reciting during the night and said: \May Allah have mercy on him. He has reminded me of such-and-such verses which I had dropped from such-and-such sūrah.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Abī Rajāʾ"," from Abū Usāmah; and Muslim narrated it from Abū Kurayb and others"," from Abū Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"""
"4708","(4708) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī and ʿImrān ibn Mūsā narrated to us, saying: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Umawī narrated to us: Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: \If you had seen me while I was listening to your recitation last night! You have been given a melodious voice from the voices of the family of Dāwūd.\"" He (Abū Mūsā) said: Had I known"," I would have adorned it further for you.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Dāwūd ibn Rushayd"," except that he did not mention the statement of Abū Mūsā. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in abridged form from the ḥadīth of Burayd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Burdah"," from his grandfather. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4761.]"""
"4709","(4709) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Mahrī informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿUmar ibn Mālik and Ḥaywah narrated to me, from Ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Allah has not given permission to anything as He has given permission to a Prophet with a beautiful voice who recites the Qurʾān aloud.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Wahb"," from his uncle. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4735.]"""
"4710","(4710) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlānī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb informed me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me that ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qays narrated to him: that he asked ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā): \How was the night recitation of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)? Did he recite aloud or softly?\"" She said: \""He would do all of that. Sometimes he would recite aloud"," and sometimes he would recite softly.\"" He said: \""All praise is due to Allah"," Who has made the matter flexible.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ by virtue of other chains. Tirmidhī 449.]"""
"4711","(4711) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us: ʿImrān ibn Zāʾidah ibn Nashīṭ narrated to us, from his father, from Abū Khālid al-Wālibī, who said: Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), when he stood at night (in prayer), would raise his voice at times and lower it at times, and he would mention that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to do that.\n\nSimilarly, Ibn al-Mubārak and ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated it from ʿImrān. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1328.]"
"4712","(4712) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us in Naysābūr, and Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUmar ibn Burhān, and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān, and others in Baghdād, said: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us, from Baḥīr ibn Saʿd al-Kilāʿī, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Kathīr ibn Murrah al-Ḥaḍramī, from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \The one who recites the Qurʾān aloud is like the one who gives charity openly"," and the one who recites the Qurʾān softly is like the one who gives charity in secret.\""\n\nSulaymān ibn Mūsā followed him (in narration) from Kathīr ibn Murrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1333.]"""
"4713","(4713) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Yaḥyā —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Mulaykah, from Yaʿlā ibn Mamlak: that he asked Umm Salamah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) about the recitation of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and his night prayer. She said: \What concern do you have with his prayer? He would pray"," then sleep for as long as he had prayed"," then pray for as long as he had slept"," then sleep for as long as he had prayed"," until morning came.\"" And she described his recitation"," and she described it as an explanatory recitation"," letter by letter. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1466.]"""
"4714","(4714) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Ḥammād narrated to us, from Abū Jamrah, who said: I said to Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā): \Indeed"," I am a quick reciter; I rush through the Qurʾān.\"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: \""That I recite Sūrat al-Baqarah and recite it with measured deliberation is more beloved to me than that I recite the entire Qurʾān in haste.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4178.]"""
"4715","(4715) Abū Muḥammad informed us: Ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Shabbābah narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: Abū Jamrah narrated to us, who said: I said to Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā): \Indeed"," I am a man who recites quickly"," and sometimes I recite the Qurʾān in a night once or twice.\"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: \""That I recite a single sūrah is more pleasing to me than that I do the like of what you do. If you must do it"," then recite in a manner that your ears hear and your heart retains.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"""
"4716","(4716) Shabbābah narrated to us, from al-Mughīrah, from Abū Jamrah, from Ibrāhīm, who said: ʿAbdullāh (ibn Masʿūd, raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: \Recite the Qurʾān and stir the hearts with it. Let not the concern of any of you be merely reaching the end of the sūrah.\"" [Ḍaʿīf.]"""
"4717","(4717) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā Abū Bakr al-Ṭaḥḥānī in Kufah narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Ghannām narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Kulayb al-ʿĀmirī from Kharashah ibn al-Ḥurr from Abū Dharr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was praying one night, reciting a single verse repeatedly until Fajr, performing rukūʿ with it and sujūd with it: {If You punish them, indeed they are Your servants} [Al-Māʾidah: 118]. I said: \O Messenger of Allah"," you kept reciting this verse until morning.\"" He said: \""Indeed"," I asked my Lord for intercession for my Ummah"," and it shall be granted for anyone who associates nothing with Allah.\"" [Ḥasan. Aḥmad 14515]"""
"4718","(4718) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us — Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Qudāmah ibn ʿAbdullāh al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us — Jassrah bint Dijājah narrated to us, saying: I heard Abū Dharr say: The Prophet (ﷺ) stood with one verse until morning, repeating it, and the verse was: {If You punish them, indeed they are Your servants, and if You forgive them, indeed You are the Almighty, the Wise} [Al-Māʾidah: 118].\n\nIt was corroborated by Fulayt al-ʿĀmirī from Jassrah, who added: \He performed rukūʿ with it and sujūd with it.\"" [Ḥasan. Nasāʾī 1010]"""
"4719","(4719) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Miṣrī narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah narrated to us — al-Awzāʿī narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā al-Tinnīsī narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah narrated from al-Awzāʿī — Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me — ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Thawbān narrated to me — Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: \Do not be like so-and-so; he used to stand [in prayer] at night"," then he abandoned standing at night.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf from ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the Ḥadīth of Ibn Abī al-ʿIshrīn from al-Awzāʿī. Then he said: ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah corroborated it. And Ibn al-Mubārak and Mubashshir ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it from al-Awzāʿī"," but they did not mention ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakam in its isnād. And likewise al-Walīd ibn Yazīd narrated it from al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1101]"""
"4720","(4720) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me — my father narrated to me — Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays, who said: I heard Jundab say: The Prophet (ﷺ) fell ill and did not stand [in prayer] for one or two nights. Then a woman came and said: \O Muhammad"," I think your shayṭān has abandoned you!\"" So Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," revealed: {By the morning brightness"," and by the night when it covers with darkness"," your Lord has not forsaken you"," nor has He become displeased} [Al-Ḍuḥā: 1–3].\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym"," and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq from Abū Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1072]"""
"4721","(4721) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdad informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Naysābūrī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūnus narrated to us — Zuhayr narrated to us — al-Aswad narrated, saying: I heard Jundab say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill and did not stand [in prayer] for two or three nights. Then a woman came to him and said: \O Muhammad"," I hope your shayṭān has left you; I have not seen him near you for two or three nights.\"" So Allah"," the Exalted"," revealed: {By the morning brightness"," and by the night when it covers with darkness"," your Lord has not forsaken you"," nor has He become displeased} [Al-Ḍuḥā: 1–3].\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus"," and Muslim narrated it from another route from Zuhayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4722","(4722) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris, and Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Sabʿī all informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Yazīd ibn Khumayr from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mūsā al-Naṣrī, who said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said to me: \Do not abandon standing [in prayer] at night"," for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never abandoned it. And when he became ill — or she said: became lazy — he would pray sitting.\""\n\nThus said Shuʿbah from Yazīd ibn Khumayr. And Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qays — and that is more correct. [Abū Dāwūd 1307]"""
"4723","(4723) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh narrated from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from a man who was with him, whom he was pleased with, who informed him that ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers, informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \There is no person who has a [habit of] prayer at night"," and then sleep overcomes him"," except that Allah records for him the reward of his prayer"," and his sleep is charity upon him.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Mālik 255]"""
"4724","(4724) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Rajāʾ ibn al-Sindī narrated to us — Abū Kurayb and Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Masrūqī both narrated to us — al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Juʿfī narrated to us — Zāʾidah narrated from Sulaymān from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit from ʿAbdah ibn Abī Lubābah from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah from Abū al-Dardāʾ, narrating that it reached the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \Whoever comes to his bed intending to rise and pray at night"," and his eyes overcome him until morning"," what he intended is recorded for him"," and his sleep is charity upon him from his Lord.\"" [Munkar. Nasāʾī 1778]"""
"4725","(4725) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr narrated to us — Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us — Zāʾidah narrated, mentioning it with its isnād as the statement of Abū al-Dardāʾ.\n\nAnd Jarīr narrated it from Sulaymān al-Aʿmash from Ḥabīb from ʿAbdah ibn Abī Lubābah from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh from Abū al-Dardāʾ, as a mawqūf [narration].\n\nAnd al-Thawrī narrated it from ʿAbdah from Zirr, or from Suwayd, from Abū al-Dardāʾ, or from Abū Dharr, as a mawqūf [narration]."
"4726","(4726) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir from Abū Wāʾil from ʿAbdullāh, who said: A man was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and it was said: \O Messenger of Allah"," he kept sleeping until morning; he did not stand for prayer.\"" He said: \""That is a man in whose ears the shayṭān has urinated — or he said: in his ear.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad"," and Muslim narrated it from the Ḥadīth of Jarīr from Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1039]"""
"4727","(4727) Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Mu'adhdhin — informed us; Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Baghdādī — narrated to us; Abū Ismāʿīl — Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī — narrated to us; Ayyūb ibn Sulaymān ibn Bilāl — narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Uways — narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said:\n\n\The Shayṭān ties three knots at the back of the head of any one of you when he sleeps. Upon each knot he strikes its place"," saying: 'You have a long night ahead"," so sleep.' When the person wakes and remembers Allah"," one knot is loosened. When he performs wuḍūʾ"," the second knot is loosened. When he prays"," the third knot is loosened"," and he begins the morning energetic and in good spirits. If he does not do so"," he begins the morning sluggish and lazy.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways"," from his brother Abū Bakr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1091]"""
"4728","(4728) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, and Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam — informed us; Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ — narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, that the Prophet ﷺ said:\n\n\When any one of you becomes drowsy during his prayer"," he should sleep until the drowsiness leaves him. For when one of you prays while drowsy"," he may go to seek forgiveness and end up cursing himself.\""\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ"," and Abū Naṣr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī"," and Abū Saʿīd — Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl — said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿUmarī"," and Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Jumaḥī"," and Mālik ibn Anas — informed me"," from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah — and they mentioned the hadith with its like.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf"," and it was narrated by Muslim from Qutaybah"," all of them from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 209]"""
"4729","(4729) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī — informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq — informed us; al-Thawrī — narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:\n\n\When any one of you becomes drowsy while praying"," he should sleep upon his bed"," for he does not know whether he is supplicating against himself or for himself.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 422]"""
"4730","(4730) And al-Sayyid Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him — narrated to us by dictation; Abū al-Qāsim — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq — informed us; Maʿmar — narrated to us, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:\n\n\When any one of you stands up at night and the Qurʾān becomes heavy upon his tongue"," and he does not know what he is saying"," then he should lie down.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ"," from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 788]"""
"4731","(4731) Abū Naṣr — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz, in Ṭabarān — narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī, in Ṭūs, in the year twenty-six and three hundred — informed us; Abū Bakr — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Naḥḥājī, in Makkah — narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah — narrated to us, from Ziyād ibn ʿIlāqah, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood in prayer until his feet became swollen. It was said: \O Messenger of Allah"," has Allah not forgiven you your past and future sins?\"" He said: \""Shall I not then be a grateful servant?\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl"," and it was narrated by Muslim from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others"," all of them from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1078]"""
"4732","(4732) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī — informed us; Abū Naṣr — Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Marwazī — narrated to us; Maḥmūd ibn Ādam al-Marwazī — narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah — narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abū al-ʿAbbās — who is al-Sāʾib ibn Farrūkh, the poet — who heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr say:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: \Has it not been conveyed to me that you fast during the day and stand in prayer at night?\"" I said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Do not do so"," for if you do that"," your eyes will become sunken and your soul will become exhausted. Your eyes have a right over you"," and your soul has a right over you"," and your family has a right over you. So fast and break your fast"," and stand in prayer and sleep.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī"," from Ibn ʿUyaynah"," and it was narrated by Muslim from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah"," from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1102]"""
"4733","(4733) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, from his original copy — informed us; Abū ʿUthmān — ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī — narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — informed us; Khālid ibn Makhlad — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar — narrated to us; Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl — narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said:\n\nYou would not wish to see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying at night except that you would see him, and you would not wish to see him sleeping except that you would see him.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Uwaysī, from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, more complete than that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1871]"
"4734","(4734) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, from his original copy — informed him; Abū al-Faḍl — ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Naysābūrī — narrated to us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī — narrated to me; Ḥumayd — said:\n\nAnas was asked about the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ and his voluntary fasting. He said: He would fast from the month until we would say that he did not intend to break fast from it at all, and he would break fast from the month until we would say that he did not intend to fast from it at all. And we would not wish to see him praying at night except that we would see him, and we would not wish to see him sleeping except that we would see him.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous]"
"4735","(4735) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī — narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī — narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us; Sufyān — narrated to me; Ashʿath ibn Sulaym — narrated to me, from his father, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet ﷺ:\n\nThat he loved constancy in deeds. I said: At which part of the night would he stand in prayer? She said: When the rooster crowed.\n\nIt was narrated by both of them [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] from the hadith of Abī al-Aḥwaṣ and others, from Ashʿath, in the Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its extraction is referenced in hadith 4659]"
"4736","(4736) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Murādī — narrated to us; Ibn Wahb — narrated to us, from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, who said:\n\nʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that al-Ḥawlāʾ bint Tuwayt ibn Ḥabīb ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā passed by her while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with her. She said: \This is al-Ḥawlāʾ bint Tuwayt"," and they claim that she does not sleep at night.\"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""Why does she not sleep at night?\"" Then he said: \""Take from deeds what you are able"," for by Allah"," Allah will not tire until you tire.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Murādī and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 785]"""
"4737","(4737) Imam Abū al-Ṭayyib: Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān, via dictation, and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, and Abū Ṭāhir: Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmash al-Faqīh, and Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl, all reciting to him, said: Abū al-ʿAbbās: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that ʿĀʾishah: There was a woman from Banī Asad with her. The Prophet (ﷺ) entered and said: \Who is this?\"" She said: \""So-and-so; she does not sleep at night.\"" She mentioned her prayer. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \""Desist! Upon you is what you are able to bear. By Allah"," Allah does not tire until you tire.\"" She said: \""The most beloved of religion to him is that upon which its practitioner is constant.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān and others"," from Hishām"," and they said: from his father"," from ʿĀʾishah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 43]"""
"4738","(4738) Abū ʿAmr: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Abū Bakr al-Fāriyābī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us; my father narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah: that the Prophet (ﷺ) entered upon her and she said: \This is so-and-so; she mentions her prayer.\"" He said: \""Desist! For indeed Allah does not tire until you tire"," and the most beloved of religion is that upon which one is constant.\""\n\nShaykh Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī said: His (peace be upon him) statement: \""For indeed Allah does not tire until you tire\"" — some of them said: He does not tire of reward until you tire of action. Allah"," Mighty and Majestic"," is not described with weariness (malāl)"," but the speech was brought forth in the manner of lexical correspondence (muḥādhāh)"," word for word"," and that is permissible in the speech of the Arabs. Similarly"," the statement of Allah"," Mighty and Majestic: {And the recompense of an evil is an evil the like thereof} [al-Shūrā: 40] — the first \""evil\"" (sayyiʾah)"," which is a sin"," is met with recompense using the word \""evil","\"" whereas retribution (qiṣāṣ) is justice and not an evil. Likewise"," His statement"," Exalted is He: {So retaliate upon him with the like of that which he has committed against you} [al-Baqarah: 194] — his retaliation is not oppression or transgression"," but it was expressed in wording corresponding to \""aggression\"" (iʿtidāʾ)"," while the meaning is not aggression. Similarly"," His statement: \""For indeed Allah does not tire until you tire\"" — it was brought forth in correspondence with the wording \""until you tire","\"" while the meaning is: He does not cut off from them the reward of their deeds so long as they do not tire and abandon them. And Allah knows best."""
"4739","(4739) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū Maʿmar narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) entered the mosque and there was a rope stretched between two pillars. He said: \What is this?\"" They said: \""This rope is for Zaynab; she prays"," and when she becomes weary"," she holds onto it.\"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""Untie it. Let each of you pray while he has energy"," and when he becomes fatigued"," let him sit.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Maʿmar"," and Muslim narrated it from Shaybān ibn Farūkh"," from ʿAbd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1099]"""
"4740","(4740) Abū Bakr: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Aṣbahānī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \There is none among you whose deeds will save him.\"" They said: \""Not even you"," O Messenger of Allah?\"" He said: \""Not even me"," unless Allah envelops me from it with His mercy. Be steadfast and follow a middle course"," or: draw near and take rest"," and go forth in the morning and take a portion of the night journey"," and be moderate"," be moderate"," and you will reach your goal.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam"," from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5349]"""
"4741","(4741) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Baḥr narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, from Maʿn ibn Muḥammad al-Ghifārī, who said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqbūrī narrating from Abū Hurayrah that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \Indeed this religion is ease"," and no one will be harsh with this religion but it will overcome him. So be steadfast and follow a middle course"," and give glad tidings"," and seek help through the early morning and the late afternoon"," and a portion of the night journey.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Muṭahhar"," from ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 39]"""
"4742","(4742) Abū Muḥammad: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Ashhal ibn Ḥātim narrated to us; ʿUyaynah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, from his father, who said: Buraydah said: I set out and saw the Prophet (ﷺ), and I thought he had a need. I would approach him and then withdraw from him. He called me and took my hand. He saw a man performing excessive bowing and prostration and said: \Do you see him? Do you see him?\"" He said: \""He released my hand from his and said: 'Upon you is a moderate way"," upon you is a moderate way.'\"" And he struck one of his hands upon the other: \""For indeed whoever is harsh with this religion"," it will overcome him.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than Bukhārī. Aḥmad 5/350]"""
"4743","(4743) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū al-Ḥasan: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Isḥāq al-Bazzāz in Baghdad informed us, saying: Abū Muḥammad: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī narrated to us; Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat narrated to us; Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū ʿUqayl: Yaḥyā ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Suqah, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said: \Indeed this religion is firm (matīn)"," so penetrate into it with gentleness"," and do not make the worship of Allah hateful to yourself"," for the one who exhausts himself without purpose neither traverses the land nor preserves his mount.\""\n\nThus Abū ʿUqayl narrated it. It has also been said: from Muḥammad ibn Suqah"," from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir"," from ʿĀʾishah; and it has been said: from him"," from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir"," from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," as a mursal narration; and it has been said: from him"," other than that. It has also been narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr"," from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Munkar. Ibn al-Mubārak in al-Zuhd 1178]"""
"4744","(4744) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him; Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us; al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us; Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from a freed slave of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said: \Indeed this religion is firm (matīn)"," so penetrate into it with gentleness"," and do not make the worship of your Lord hateful to yourself"," for the one who exhausts himself without purpose neither completes his journey nor preserves his mount. So work as one who thinks he will never die"," and be cautious as one who fears he may die tomorrow.\"" [Munkar]"""
"4745","(4745) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr and Mālik ibn al-Ḥārith, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \Moderation in the Sunnah is better than striving in innovation.\"" This is mawqūf.\n\nIt has also been narrated from al-Ḥasan"," from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," as a mursal narration"," with additional wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 1/184]"""
"4746","(4746) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ṭāhir: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Saʿdī informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the mosque and saw a rope stretched between two pillars. He said: \What is this rope?\"" They said: \""For so-and-so; she prays"," and when she is overcome"," she holds onto it.\"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""Let her pray as long as she is alert"," and when she fears she may be overcome"," let her sleep.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously narrated under no. 4739]"""
"4747","(4747) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah from Anas ibn Mālik that he said regarding this verse: {They used to sleep but little of the night} [al-Dhāriyāt: 17] — He said: \They used to remain awake between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ"," praying between the two.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1321]"""
"4748","(4748) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated from Saʿīd from Qatādah from Anas regarding the words of the Most High: {They used to sleep but little of the night} [al-Dhāriyāt: 17] — He said: \They used to pray between the two — between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\"" Yaḥyā added in his narration: {And their sides forsake their beds} [al-Sajdah: 16]. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1322]"""
"4749","(4749) And Abū ʿAlī informed us: Abū Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Abū Kāmil informed us: Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ informed us: Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah from Anas ibn Mālik regarding this verse: {Their sides forsake their beds} [al-Sajdah: 16] up to {and they spend}. He said: \They used to remain awake between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ"," praying.\"" He said: And al-Ḥasan used to say: \""It is qiyām al-layl (standing in prayer at night).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4750","(4750) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: al-Ashyab informed us: Ibn Lahīʿah informed us. And Abū ʿUqayl — Zuhrah ibn Maʿbad — said: I heard Ibn al-Munkadir and Abū Ḥāzim both saying regarding the verse: {Their sides forsake their beds} [al-Sajdah: 16]: \It is what is between Maghrib and the prayer of ʿIshāʾ — the prayer of the Awwābīn (those who constantly return to Allah).\"" [Narrated by Ibn Naṣr in Qiyām al-Layl"," as cited in al-Dur al-Manthūr 6/546]"""
"4751","(4751) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated from Hārūn from ʿĪsā ibn Muḥammad from Ibn Abī Mulaykah: Ibn al-Zubayr was asked about nāshiʾat al-layl (the rising at night), and he said: \The beginning of the night"," after Maghrib.\"" And I asked Ibn ʿAbbās"," and he said the same. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī in his Tafsīr 12/282]"""
"4752","(4752) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Nāfiʿ informed us: ʿUmārah ibn Zādhān narrated from Thābit from Anas regarding His words: {nāshiʾat al-layl} [al-Muzzammil: 6] — He said: \It is between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5926]"""
"4753","(4753) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Nāfiʿ said: Al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim used to pray between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. He said: And al-Ḥasan mentioned that Ṭāwūs did not consider it to be anything (i.e., he did not regard it as part of night prayer). Aḥmad said: And it has been narrated to us from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī that he used to count it among the prayers of the night. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5926]"
"4754","(4754) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us: al-Mubārak ibn Faḍālah narrated from al-Ḥasan who said: \Every prayer performed after the last ʿIshāʾ is from nāshiʾat al-layl (the rising at night).\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn al-Jaʿd 3212]"""
"4755","(4755) And it has been narrated to us from Abī Mijlaz that he said: \Al-Nāshiʾah is what is after ʿIshāʾ until morning.\"" Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍarawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated from Sulaymān al-Taymī from Abī Mijlaz"," and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Naṣr in Qiyām al-Layl"," as cited in al-Dur al-Manthūr 8/317]"""
"4756","(4756) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us: Ādam informed us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn who said: \Nāshiʾat al-layl is standing in prayer between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\"""""
"4757","(4757) Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us, Ibrāhīm informed us, Ādam informed us, Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding His statement {Indeed, the hours of the night} [al-Muzzammil: 6], he said: It means the standing (qiyām) of the night, and nashiʾah is an Abyssinian term — when a man stands up they say nashāʾ.\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us previously from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that he said: Nashiʾat al-layl (the rising of the night) refers to its beginning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 2/549]"
"4758","(4758) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from al-Taymī, who said: We were in the gathering of Abū ʿUthmān when a man came upon us and narrated to us from ʿUbayd, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he was asked about the prayer of the Prophet (ﷺ), and he mentioned the prayer between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\n\nAnd it has been narrated in this chapter from Umm Mubashshir al-Anṣāriyyah in a marfūʿ (elevated) narration. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4759","(4759) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Abū Sinān narrated from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Badr from Abū al-Shaʿthāʾ al-Muḥāribī, who said: I was in an army in which Salmān was present. Salmān said: Take care of these animals whose provision Allah has guaranteed — be gentle with them in travel and give them their sustenance. And hold fast to the prayer between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ, for it lightens for you a portion of your night's prayer and protects you from idle talk.\n\n[Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Tārīkh al-Kabīr 6/507]"
"4760","(4760) Abū al-Fawāris — al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Fawāris — informed us, selected by his brother Abū al-Fatḥ al-Ḥāfiẓ (may Allah have mercy on him). Abū ʿAlī — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Isḥāq — informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us, Abū Nuʿaym informed us, Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr from Ibrāhīm from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd from Abū Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever recites the two verses from the end of Sūrat al-Baqarah in a night"," they will suffice him.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym"," and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3786]"""
"4761","(4761) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarāʾifī informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us: I heard ʿAlī, meaning Ibn al-Madīnī, say: Sufyān said: Ibn Shubrumah said: \I considered how much of the Qurʾān would suffice a man"," and I did not find any sūrah with less than three verses. So I said: One should not recite less than three verses.\""\n\nSufyān said: And we were informed by Manṣūr from Ibrāhīm from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd from Abū Masʿūd"," tracing it back to the Prophet (ﷺ): \""Whoever recites two verses from Sūrat al-Baqarah in a night"," they will suffice him.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4762","(4762) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated what he read to Mālik, and Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah from his father from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī: A man heard a man reciting {Say: He is Allah, the One} [al-Ikhlāṣ], repeating it. When morning came, he came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and mentioned that to him, as though the man considered it insignificant. Al-Qaʿnabī said: as though he regarded it lightly. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \By the One in Whose Hand is my soul"," indeed it equals one-third of the Qurʾān.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī and others. Al-Bukhārī said: And Abū Maʿmar — and he is Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm — said"," and mentioned the ḥadīth which… [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4726]"""
"4763","(4763) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Aṣbahānī informed us by dictation, Abū Saʿīd al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Baḥr al-Barri informed me, Abū Maʿmar al-Baghdādī al-Bazzāz in Qaṭīʿat al-Rabīʿ informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated from Mālik ibn Anas from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — and he is Ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah al-Anṣārī — from his father from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān informed me that a man stood during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and recited {Say: He is Allah, the One} [al-Ikhlāṣ], repeating it entirely and adding nothing to it. When morning came, a man said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): \O Messenger of Allah"," a man stood in the night reciting from the pre-dawn hours {Say: He is Allah"," the One. Allah"," the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born"," nor is there to Him any equivalent}"," repeating it and adding nothing to it.\"" It was as though the man regarded it as insignificant. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad"," indeed it equals one-third of the Qurʾān.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4764","(4764) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and among the last of those who narrated, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, he said: I read to Mālik from Nāfiʿ and ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿUmar: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about the night prayer, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The night prayer is in pairs (mathnā mathnā). When any of you fears the dawn"," let him pray one rakʿah which will make witr for what he has already prayed.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"""
"4765","(4765) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Khaṭīb al-Isfaraʾīnī informed us, Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us, al-Ḥumaydī informed us, Sufyān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār narrated from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: I heard a man asking the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was on the pulpit: \How should one of us pray at night?\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \""(Pray) in pairs (mathnā mathnā). When you fear the dawn"," then make witr with one rakʿah"," which will make witr for your past prayer.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4766","(4766) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī informed us, Sufyān informed us, al-Zuhrī narrated from Sālim from his father. He said: And ʿAmr narrated from Ṭāwūs from Ibn ʿUmar: A man asked the Prophet (ﷺ) about the night prayer, and he said: \(Pray) in pairs (mathnā mathnā). When you fear the dawn"," then make witr with one rakʿah.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4767","(4767) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah al-Muzani informed us — 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa informed us — Abu al-Yaman informed us — Shu'ayb, from al-Zuhri, informed us — Salim ibn 'Abd Allah informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: A man among the Muslims asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: \O Messenger of Allah"," how is the night prayer?\"" He ﷺ said: \""Two by two (rak'atan rak'atan); if you fear the dawn"," then perform Witr with one rak'ah.\"" 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar used to give salam after every two rak'ahs"," then perform Witr with one rak'ah.\n\nNarrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih. Bukhari 946]"""
"4768","(4768) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — Abu Muhammad al-Muzani informed us and mentioned it.\n\nAbu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — Ishaq ibn al-Hasan al-Harbi informed us — Abu Ma'mar informed us — 'Abd al-Warith informed us — Abu al-Tayyah, from Abu Mijlaz, from Ibn 'Umar, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The Witr is one rak'ah at the end of the night.\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Shayban"," from 'Abd al-Warith. [Sahih. Muslim 752]"""
"4769","(4769) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Hammam informed us — Qatadah, from Abu Mijlaz, said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas about the Witr, and he said: \I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: 'The Witr is one rak'ah at the end of the night.'\"" [Sahih. Previously mentioned in the one before it]"""
"4770","(4770) And with his isnad, from Abu Mijlaz, said: I asked Ibn 'Umar about the Witr, and he said: \I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: 'One rak'ah at the end of the night.'\"" Muslim extracted it in his Sahih from the hadith of Hammam ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Previously mentioned in the one before it]"""
"4771","(4771) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Hamid ibn Bilal informed us — Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Ahmasi informed us — Ibn Fudayl informed us — 'Asim al-Ahwal informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about the Witr while I was between the two of them. He ﷺ said: \The night prayer is two by two; when it is the end of the night"," perform Witr with one rak'ah"," then pray two rak'ahs before dawn\"" — meaning before the Fajr prayer.\n\n'Asim al-Ahwal said"," and Laqib ibn Humayd said something similar to this hadith"," except that he said: \""Hasten with one rak'ah at dawn.\"" [Sahih. Previously mentioned under no. 4764]"""
"4772","(4772) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi informed us — Abu Usamah informed us — al-Walid ibn Kathir informed me — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar informed us that Ibn 'Umar related to them: A man called out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in the mosque, saying: \O Messenger of Allah"," how should I make Witr in the night prayer?\"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""Whoever prays"," let him pray two by two; if he fears that dawn may reach him"," let him prostrate one prostration"," and it will make Witr for whatever he has prayed.\""\n\nNarrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Abu Kurayb and others"," from Abu Usamah. [Sahih. See the preceding]"""
"4773","(4773) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us — Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn informed us — Adam ibn Abi Iyas informed us — Shu'bah —\n\nAnd Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah informed us — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us — Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us — Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us — Shu'bah said: I heard 'Uqbah ibn Hurayth say: I heard Ibn 'Umar narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The night prayer is two by two; when you see that dawn will overtake you"," perform Witr with one rak'ah.\"" It was said to Ibn 'Umar: \""What is two by two?\"" He said: \""You give salam after every two rak'ahs\"" — this is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Bashshar.\n\nIn the narration of Adam: \""Perform Witr with one rak'ah.\"" A man said to Ibn 'Umar: \""What is two by two?\"" He said: \""Salam between every two rak'ahs.\"" And in his isnad"," from Shu'bah"," he said: I heard 'Uqbah ibn Hurayth say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said.\n\nNarrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Muthanna"," from Muhammad ibn Ja'far. [Sahih. Muslim 749]"""
"4774","(4774) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah informed us — Yahya ibn Yahya said: I read to Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray at night eleven rak'ahs, performing Witr with one of them. When he finished, he would lie on his right side until the mu'adhdhin would come to him, whereupon he would pray two light rak'ahs.\n\nNarrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Previously mentioned under no. 4679]"
"4775","(4775) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Bahr ibn Nasser said: It was read to Ibn Wahb — Ibn Abi Dhi'b, 'Amr ibn al-Harith, and Yazid ibn Zayd informed you that Ibn Shihab informed them, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray between finishing the 'Isha' prayer and Fajr eleven rak'ahs, giving salam after every two rak'ahs, performing Witr with one rak'ah, and prostrating for a duration equal to what any one of you would take to recite fifty verses before raising his head. When the mu'adhdhin fell silent from the Fajr prayer and the dawn became evident to him, he would stand and pray two light rak'ahs, then lie on his right side until the mu'adhdhin came for the Iqamah, whereupon he would go out with him.\n\nHe said: And some of them add to others in the story of the hadith.\n\nNarrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Harmalah, from Ibn Wahb, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith and Yunus ibn Yazid. [Sahih. Previously mentioned under no. 4678]"
"4776","(4776) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz informed us — Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak —\n\nAnd Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak informed us — Quraysh ibn Hayyan al-'Ijli informed us — Bakr ibn Wa'il, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The Witr is a right upon every Muslim. Whoever wishes to perform Witr with five"," let him do so; whoever wishes to perform Witr with three"," let him do so; and whoever wishes to perform Witr with one"," let him do so.\"" [Sahih in other than this chain. Abu Dawud 1422]"""
"4777","(4777) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazid informed me: my father informed me: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Indeed"," the Witr prayer is an obligation (haqq). Whoever wishes may perform Witr with five rak'ahs"," whoever wishes may perform it with three"," and whoever wishes may perform it with one.\"" [Sahih. See its predecessor.]"""
"4778","(4778) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi in Marw informed us: Sa'id ibn Mas'ud narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata' ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyub, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Perform Witr with five rak'ahs; if you are unable"," then with three; if you are unable"," then with one; and if you are unable"," then gesture with a nod.\"" [Sahih]"""
"4779","(4779) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn 'Asim al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Hisham narrated to us: Sa'id narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata' ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \The Witr prayer is an obligation (haqq). Whoever wishes may perform Witr with seven rak'ahs"," whoever wishes may perform it with three"," and whoever wishes may perform it with one. And whoever is overcome"," let him gesture with a nod.\"" These narrators are agreed upon the elevation (raf') of this hadith from al-Zuhri. Ma'mar ibn Rashid followed them in this"," from the narration of Wuhib from him. [Sahih in other than this chain]"""
"4780","(4780) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastawayh narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: al-Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us: Wuhib narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \The Witr prayer is an obligation (haqq). Whoever loves to perform Witr with five rak'ahs"," let him do so; whoever loves to perform it with three"," let him do so; and whoever loves to perform it with one"," let him do so. And whoever is unable"," let him gesture with a nod.\"" Hammad ibn Zayd and 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated it from Ma'mar"," as a mawquf narration traced to Abu Ayyub. Likewise"," a group narrated it from al-Zuhri as a mawquf narration traced to Abu Ayyub. Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me: 'Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Amruwayh narrated to us"," saying: I heard Muhammad ibn Yahya say: \""This hadith"," through the narrations of Yunus"," al-Zubaydi"," Ibn 'Uyaynah"," Shu'ayb"," Ibn Ishaq"," and 'Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar"," is more likely to be non-elevated (ghayr marfu'). Yet there remains a doubt in my soul regarding the narrations of the others alongside the narration of Wuhib from Ma'mar. And Allah knows best.\"" And it has been narrated to us from a group of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) that voluntary prayer or Witr was performed with a single rak'ah separated from what preceded it"," among them being 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). [Sahih]"""
"4781","(4781) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us: Abu al-Fadl Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Khamirawayh informed us. And Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated to us: Qabus ibn Abi Zabyan informed us that his father narrated to him, saying: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by in the Mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and performed one rak'ah, then departed. A man caught up with him and said: \O Commander of the Faithful"," you only performed one rak'ah!\"" He said: \""It is voluntary prayer (tatawwu'). Whoever wishes may add"," and whoever wishes may reduce.\"" Al-Shafi'i narrated it from some of his companions"," from Sufyan al-Thawri"," from Qabus. Among them was also 'Uthan ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him). [Da'if. 'Abd al-Razzaq 5136]"""
"4782","(4782) Abu Muhammad 'Abdillah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'faran narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Uthman, who said: \I stood behind the Maqam (of Ibrahim)"," intending that no one would take my place that night. Then a man began nudging me"," but I did not turn. Then he nudged me again"," so I turned"," and lo"," it was 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him). I stepped aside"," and he came forward and recited the entire Qur'an in one rak'ah.\"" [Sahih in other than this chain. Ibn Abi Shaybah 3700]"""
"4783","(4783) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bilal al-Bazzaz narrated to us: Abu al-Azhar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Fulayh narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Uthman, who said: \I said: 'I shall certainly secure the Maqam tonight.' So I arrived there first. While I was standing praying"," a man placed his hand on my back. I looked and saw 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him)"," who was the Commander at that time. I stepped aside"," and he stood"," opened the Qur'an"," and recited it in its entirety"," then performed ruku'"," sat"," recited the tashahhud"," and saluted in a single rak'ah without adding anything further. When he finished"," I said: 'O Commander of the Faithful"," you only prayed one rak'ah!' He said: 'It is my Witr.'\"" [Sahih in other than this chain]"""
"4784","(4784) Among them was Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him). Abu Sa'id Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Khatib informed us: Abu Bahr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Kawthar narrated to us: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: al-Humaydi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to me: Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from his uncle Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, who said: \It was said to Sa'd: 'You perform Witr with one rak'ah.' He said: 'Yes. Seven are more beloved to me than five"," five are more beloved to me than three"," and three are more beloved to me than one. But I lighten it for myself.'\"" [Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq 4647]"""
"4785","(4785) Yahya informed us: Muhammad narrated to us: Bishr narrated to us: al-Humaydi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: Yazid ibn Khusayfah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Shurahbil, who said: \I saw Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) praying the 'Isha' prayer"," then after it he prayed one rak'ah.\"" [Sahih. al-Bukhari 5995]"""
"4786","(4786) Yahya informed us: Muhammad narrated to us: Bishr narrated to us: al-Humaydi narrated to us: 'Abdillah ibn al-Harith narrated to us: Yunus ibn Yazid al-Ayli narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Abdillah ibn Tha'labah al-'Udri — and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) had wiped his face — who said: \I saw Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him): when he prayed the 'Isha'"," he performed Witr with one rak'ah.\"" Others besides Yunus added: \""Until he would rise in the middle of the night.\"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih"," saying: \""And al-Layth said"," from Yunus.\"" And he also narrated it from the hadith of Shu'ayb"," from al-Zuhri. [Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq 4646]"""
"4787","Among them is Tamim al-Dari, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Muhammad — Abdullah ibn Yahya ibn Abd al-Jabbar in Baghdad — informed us. Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us. Sa'dan ibn Nasr informed us. Abu Mu'awiya narrated from Asim al-Ahwal from Ibn Sirin from Tamim al-Dari: that he recited the entire Qur'an in a single rakah. [Sahih. Al-Tahawi 1/348]"
"4788","Among them is Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu al-Hasan al-Muqri informed us. Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us. Yusuf ibn Ya'qub informed us. Sulayman ibn Harb informed us. Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Asim al-Ahwal from Abu Mijlaz: that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari was between Makkah and Madinah. He prayed two rakahs of Isha, then stood and prayed one rakah, making it his Witr, and recited one hundred verses from Surah al-Nisa'. Then he said: \I would not fall short of placing my feet where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) placed his feet"," and of reciting what he recited.\"" [Sahih. Al-Nasa'i 1728]"""
"4789","Among them is Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us. Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us. al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us. al-Shafi'i informed us. Malik informed us.\n\n(And) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us. Abu Zakariyya al-Anbari and Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki — both said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Abdi informed us. Ibn Bukayr informed us. Malik narrated from Nafi': that Ibn Umar used to make salam after one and two rakahs in the Witr, to the extent that he would give instructions regarding some of his needs. And in the narration of al-Shafi'i: \in the Witr.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Malik. [Sahih. Malik 274]"""
"4790","Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — both informed us. Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us. Ahmad ibn Isa al-Tinnisi informed us. Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated from al-Awza'i. al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah al-Makhzumi informed me, saying: A man came to Abdullah ibn Umar and said: \How should I pray Witr?\"" He said: \""Pray Witr with one rakah.\"" He said: \""I fear that the people will say it is al-Butayra'.\"" He said: \""Do you want the Sunnah of Allah and His Messenger? This is the Sunnah of Allah and His Messenger.\"" [Da'if. Ibn Khuzaymah 1074]"""
"4791","Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us. Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us. al-Saghani, meaning Muhammad ibn Ishaq, informed us. Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Razi informed us. Salamah ibn al-Fadl al-Ansari informed us. Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Abu Mansur, the freedman of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, who said: I asked Abdullah ibn Umar about the Witr of the night. He said: \O my son"," do you know the Witr of the day?\"" I said: \""Yes"," it is al-Maghrib.\"" He said: \""You have spoken the truth. The Witr of the night is one rakah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded that.\"" I said: \""O Abu Abd al-Rahman"," the people say that it is al-Butayra'.\"" He said: \""O my son"," that is not al-Butayra'. Al-Butayra' is that a man prays a complete rakah with its ruku'"," sujud"," and standing"," then stands for the other and does not complete its ruku'"," sujud"," or standing. That is al-Butayra'.\"" Among them is Abdullah ibn Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib"," may Allah be pleased with them both. [Da'if]"""
"4792","Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us. Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Fadl informed us. Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us. I heard Ishaq ibn Ibrahim being asked about the Witr. He said: Mahrum ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Attar informed me. He said: 'Isl ibn Sufyan narrated from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, who said: I prayed al-Isha al-Akhirah beside Ibn 'Abbas. When he finished, he said: \Shall I not teach you the Witr?\"" I said: \""Yes.\"" He stood and prayed one rakah. [Da'if]"""
"4793","Abu Sa'id — Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Isfara'ini — informed us. Abu Bahr al-Barbahari informed us. Bishr ibn Musa informed us. al-Humaydi informed us. Sufyan narrated. Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid narrated. Kuraib narrated, saying: I saw Mu'awiyah pray al-Isha, then he performed Witr with one rakah. I mentioned that to Ibn 'Abbas, and he said: \He has done correctly.\"" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 3553]"""
"4794","Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki informed us. Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us. al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us. al-Shafi'i informed us. Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated from Ibn Jurayj. Utbah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith informed me that Kuraib, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, informed him: that he saw Mu'awiyah pray al-Isha, then he performed Witr with a single rakah without adding anything further. He informed Ibn 'Abbas, and he said: \He has done correctly"," O my son. None of us knows more than Mu'awiyah. The Witr is one"," or five"," or seven"," or more than that — the Witr is whatever one wishes.\"" Among them is Abu Ayyub — Khalid ibn Zayd al-Ansari"," may Allah be pleased with him. [Hasan. Narrated by al-Shafi'i 386]"""
"4795","Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us. Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us. Abu Yahya — Abd al-Karim ibn al-Haytham — informed us. Abu al-Yaman informed me. Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated from al-Zuhri. 'Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, then al-Junda'i, informed me that he heard Abu Ayyub, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), say: \Witr is an obligation. Whoever wishes to perform Witr with five"," let him do so. Whoever wishes to perform Witr with three"," let him do so. Whoever wishes to perform Witr with one"," let him do so. And whoever is unable to do more than gesture with his head"," let him do so.\"" [Sahih. Previously narrated under no. 4780]"""
"4796","Among them is Mu'adh ibn al-Harith, Abu Halimah al-Qari, may Allah be pleased with him. He witnessed al-Jisr alongside Abu Ubayd al-Thaqafi during the caliphate of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. It has been said that he had companionship (of the Prophet). Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us. Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us. Abd al-Karim ibn al-Haytham informed us. Abu al-Yaman informed me. Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah said: Nafi' said: Ibn Umar used to pray at night in sets of two rakahs, as much as was decreed for him. When he feared the dawn, he would pray one rakah and make it the last of his prayer. He would sit and make salam in the two rakahs after which the Witr was to follow, then he would say the takbir and pray the Witr. Nafi' said: I heard Mu'adh al-Qari doing the same.\n\n(T) It was followed by Isma'il ibn Umayyah and Ayyub ibn Musa, both narrating from Nafi', from both of them together. [Sahih. Muwatta' 251]"
"4797","Among them is Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, may Allah be pleased with him.\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʢUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Tamtām Muḥammad ibn Ghālib informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Bishr ibn Salm al-Bajalī informed us: al-Muʿāfā ibn ʿImrān narrated from ʿUthmān ibn al-Aswad from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, who said: Muʿāwiyah performed Witr after ʿIshāʾ with one rakʿah, and there was in his presence a freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās. So he went to Ibn ʿAbbās and informed him of that. He said: \Leave him"," for he was indeed a companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn Bishr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 4793]"""
"4798","Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū Manṣūr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Ḥalabī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Imām both said: Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr informed us: Nāfiʿ ibn ʿUmar informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū Bakr al-Fārābī informed us: ʿAlī ibn Naṣr informed us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Nāfiʿ ibn ʿUmar narrated from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, who said: It was said to Ibn ʿAbbās: \What do you say about Muʿāwiyah"," who performs Witr with only one rakʿah?\"" He said: \""He has done correctly. Indeed"," he is a man of deep understanding (faqīh).\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam from Nāfiʿ ibn ʿUmar al-Jumaḥī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 4793]"""
"4799","Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Hishām narrated from his father from ʿĀʾishah that the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the night consisted of thirteen rakʿahs. He would perform Witr with five, and he would not make salām during any of the five until he sat in the last (rakʿah) and then made salām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 737]"
"4800","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr and ʿAbdah—\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us: ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān—both said: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray thirteen rakʿahs, performing Witr from among them with five, and he would not sit during any of them until he sat in the last of them and then made salām. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā.\n\nAnd in the narration of Abū Bakr: The night prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consisted of thirteen rakʿahs; he would perform Witr from among them with five, and he would not sit during any of them except in the last of them.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"4801","Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad informed us: ʿAffān informed us: Hammām informed us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us. He said: My father informed me that ʿĀʾishah informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sleep, and when he awoke he would use the siwāk, then perform wuḍūʾ, then pray eight rakʿahs, sitting after every two rakʿahs and making salām, then he would perform Witr with five rakʿahs, not sitting except in the fifth, and not making salām except in the fifth.\n\nAnd thus a group narrated it from Hishām. And Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr followed him in this narration from ʿUrwah, except that he said: \Six rakʿahs"," two by two (mathnā mathnā).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 6/123]"""
"4802","Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥarrānī informed me: Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray thirteen rakʿahs, including his two rakʿahs before Fajr. He would pray six rakʿahs two by two, and perform Witr with five, not sitting between them except in the last of them. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 1359]"
"4803","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Faqīh in Bukhārā informed us: Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥabīb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Shaybān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Abān narrated from Qatādah from Zurārah ibn Awfā from Saʿd ibn Hishām from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform Witr with three (rakʿahs), not sitting except in the last of them. Thus is this narration.\n\nAnd we have narrated in the ḥadīth of Saʿd ibn Hishām regarding the Witr of the Prophet ﷺ that he performed Witr with nine, then with seven. And Allah knows best. [Ḥākim 1/447]"
"4804","Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ and Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Murādī both said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qays, who said: I said to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: \With how many (rakʿahs) did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform Witr?\"" She said: \""He used to perform Witr with four and three"," six and three"," eight and three"," and ten and three. He would not perform Witr with less than seven"," nor with more than thirteen.\"" Aḥmad added: \""And he would not perform Witr with the two rakʿahs before Fajr.\""\n\nI said: \""How did he perform Witr?\"" She said: \""He never abandoned it.\"" Aḥmad did not mention: \""six and three.\""\n\nThis may mean that he intended by it three (rakʿahs) without separating them by sitting or salām"," thus being in accordance with the meaning of the narration of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. And Allah knows best.\n\nAnd we have narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet ﷺ to the same meaning as the narration of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah regarding Witr with five rakʿahs. [Strong. Abū Dāwūd 13062]"""
"4805","Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from al-Ḥakam, who said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jubayr narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \I spent the night in the house of my aunt Maymūnah. The Prophet ﷺ prayed ʿIshāʾ"," then he came and prayed four rakʿahs"," then he slept. Then he stood"," so I came and stood on his left side"," but he moved me to his right side. Then he prayed five rakʿahs"," then he prayed two rakʿahs"," then he slept until I heard his snoring—or he said"," his breathing—then he went out for the prayer.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 117]"""
"4806","ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, meaning Ibn Muḥammad, informed me: ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn Suhayl narrated from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās that al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib had sent him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on an errand, and it was the night of Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, the aunt of Ibn ʿAbbās. So he entered upon her and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque.\n\nIbn ʿAbbās said: \I lay down in his chamber"," and I resolved within myself to count how many (rakʿahs) the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray. He came while I was lying in the chamber after the night had passed"," and he said: 'Sleep.' Then he took a blanket from Maymūnah"," covering himself with part of it while part of it was upon her. Then he stood and prayed two rakʿahs"," until he had prayed eight rakʿahs. Then he performed Witr with five"," not sitting between them. Then he sat and extolled Allah as is His due"," praising Him abundantly"," and the last of his words were: 'O Allah"," place light in my heart"," and place light in my hearing"," and place light in my sight"," and place light on my right"," and light on my left"," and place light before me"," and light behind me"," and increase me in light"," and increase me in light.'\"" [Ḥasan. al-Nasāʾī in al-Kubrā 406]"""
"4807","(4807) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi — informed us; Abu Ishaq al-Isfahani — narrated to us; Abu Ahmad ibn Faris — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari — narrated to us; Musa ibn Isma'il — narrated to us; Abd al-Wahid — narrated to us; Uthman ibn Hakim — narrated to us; he heard Uthman ibn Urwah narrate from Isma'il ibn Zayd ibn Thabit: that Zayd used to perform the Witr prayer with five rak'ahs and would not give the salam (concluding greeting) except in the fifth rak'ah. And Ubayy used to do the same. Thus I found it in the book — \Ubayy\"" as a restricted narration. [Hasan. Bukhari in Tarikh al-Kabir 1/355]"""
"4808","(4808) Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz — informed us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Samarqandi — narrated to us; Abu Abdillah — Muhammad ibn Nasr — narrated to us; Abu Ja'far al-Darimi — narrated to us; Hibban ibn Hilal — narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' — narrated to us; Habib al-Mu'allim — narrated to us. He said: it was said to al-Hasan: \Indeed"," Ibn Umar used to give the salam in the two rak'ahs of Witr.\"" He said: \""Umar was more learned than him; he would stand up in the third rak'ah with the takbir.\"" [Sahih. Hakim 1/447]"""
"4809","(4809) And Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz — informed us; Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' — narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Bajali — narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrahim and Sulayman ibn Harb — both said: narrated to us Jarir ibn Hazim from Qays ibn Sa'd from Ata': that he used to perform the Witr with three rak'ahs, not sitting in them, and would not recite the tashahhud except in the last of them. [Sahih. Hakim 1/447]"
"4810","(4810) Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz — informed us; Abu al-Fadl — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Muzakkī — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Bashshar al-Abdi — narrated to us; Ibn Abi Adi — narrated from Sa'id. And Abu Abdillah — informed us; Ahmad ibn Ja'far — narrated to us; Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal — narrated to us; my father — narrated to us; Yahya — narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah — narrated from Qatadah from Zurarah ibn Awfi from Sa'd ibn Hisham: that he divorced his wife, then set out to Madinah to sell his property there, and to spend it on weapons and mounts, and then to wage jihad against the Romans until he died. He met a group of his people, and they informed him that a group of his people — six of them — had intended to do the same during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). He said: \Is there not among you a good example?\"" He forbade them from that"," and he had them witness his taking her back in marriage. Then he returned to us and told us that he came to Ibn Abbas and asked him about the Witr prayer. He said: \""Shall I not inform you of the most knowledgeable of the people of the earth regarding the Witr of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Go to A'ishah and ask her"," then come back to me and inform me of her response to you.\"" He said: \""So I came to Hakim ibn Aflah and asked him to accompany me to her. He said: 'I will not approach her; I forbade her from saying anything about these two bay'ahs (pledges)"," and she refused regarding them except to proceed (firmly).' So I swore an oath"," and he came with me. We entered upon her"," and she said: 'Hakim?' and she recognized him. He said: 'Yes"," indeed.' She said: 'Who is this with you?' He said: 'Sa'd ibn Hisham.' She said: 'The son of Amir.' She invoked mercy upon him and said: 'What a good man Amir was.' I said: 'O Mother of the Believers"," inform me about the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).' She said: 'Do you not recite the Qur'an?' I said: 'Indeed"," yes.' She said: 'Then the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was the Qur'an.' I resolved to stand (to ask further)"," but the matter of the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) occurred to me. She said: 'Do you not recite this Surah — O you who wraps himself [al-Muzzammul]?' I said: 'Indeed"," yes.' She said: 'Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," obligated the standing of the night prayer at the beginning of this Surah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and his Companions stood for a year"," until their feet swelled. Then Allah held back its conclusion in the heavens for twelve months. Then Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," revealed the alleviation at the end of this Surah"," so the standing of the night prayer became voluntary after having been obligatory.' I resolved to stand (to ask further)"," but the matter of the Witr of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) occurred to me. I said: 'O Mother of the Believers"," inform me about the Witr of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).' She said: 'We would prepare his siwak and his water for ablution. Then Allah would send him (awakening) whenever He willed during the night. He would use the siwak"," then perform ablution"," then pray eight rak'ahs"," not sitting in them except at the eighth. He would sit and remember his Lord"," the Mighty and Majestic"," and supplicate and seek forgiveness. Then he would stand up without giving the salam. Then he would give the salam. Then he would pray the ninth rak'ah; he would sit and praise his Lord"," remember Him"," and supplicate. Then he would give the salam — a salam that he would make us hear. Then he would pray two rak'ahs while seated after giving the salam. That makes eleven rak'ahs"," O my son. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) grew old and put on flesh"," he performed the Witr with seven"," then prayed two rak'ahs while seated after giving the salam. That makes nine"," O my son. The Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him)"," when he prayed a prayer"," he loved to continue with it. And when sleep or pain or illness distracted him from the standing of the night"," he would pray twelve rak'ahs during the day. And I do not know that the Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him) ever recited the entire Qur'an in one night"," nor that he stood an entire night until morning"," nor that he fasted a complete month other than Ramadan.' I came to Ibn Abbas and narrated her hadith to him. He said: 'She spoke the truth. If only I could enter upon her"," I would come to her so that she could speak to me face to face.'\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna from Ibn Abi Adi from Sa'id. [Sahih. Muslim 746]"""
"4811","(4811) And Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz — informed us; Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Anbari — narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib — narrated to us; Abu Qudamah — narrated to us; Mu'adh ibn Hisham — narrated to us; my father — narrated from Qatadah from Zurarah ibn Awfi from Sa'd ibn Hisham, and he mentioned the hadith with a similar meaning. A'ishah said: \When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) grew old and put on flesh"," he prayed seven rak'ahs"," not sitting except in the sixth. He would praise Allah and supplicate his Lord. Then he would stand without giving the salam. Then he would sit in the seventh"," praise Allah and supplicate his Lord. Then he would give the salam — a salam that he would make us hear. Then he would pray two rak'ahs while seated. That makes nine"," O my son.\"" And he mentioned the hadith. Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna from Mu'adh ibn Hisham. [Sahih. See the preceding one.]"""
"4812","(4812) Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Abu Muhammad — al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Affan al-Amiri in Kufah — narrated to us; Ibn Numayr — narrated from al-A'mash from Malik ibn al-Harith from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid. He said: Abdillah said: \The Witr is three"," like the Witr of the day — the Maghrib.\"" This is a correct narration from Abdillah ibn Mas'ud as his own statement"," not raised to the Prophet (peace be upon him). And Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi al-Hawajib al-Kufi raised it from al-A'mash"," but he is weak"," and his narration contradicts the narration of the majority from al-A'mash. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tabari in al-Kabir 9420]"""
"4813","(4813) And Abu al-Qasim — Zayd ibn Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Alawi in Kufah — informed us; Abu Ja'far — Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Duhaum — narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Abdillah — narrated to us; Waki' — narrated from al-A'mash from some of his companions. He said: Abdillah said: \The Witr is seven or five"," and no less than three.\"" And it was narrated from al-A'mash from Ibrahim: \""Abdillah ibn Mas'ud said.\"" And it is disconnected (munqati') and stopped (mawquf). [Weak]"""
"4814","(4814) Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr — both said: narrated to us Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib — informed us; Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' — narrated to us; Sa'id — narrated from Qatadah from Zurarah ibn Awfi from Sa'd ibn Hisham from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would not give the salam in the two rak'ahs of the Witr.\"" This is how Abd al-Wahhab narrated it from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah. And Aban said from Qatadah: \""He would perform the Witr with three"," not sitting except in the last of them.\"" And the majority narrated it from Ibn Abi Arubah from Qatadah"," and Hammam ibn Yahya from Qatadah"," as was previously mentioned regarding his Witr of nine and then seven. And likewise Bahz ibn Hakim narrated it from Zurarah ibn Awfi. In the narration of Abd al-Wahhab"," it appears to be an abridgment of the hadith. And the narration of Aban is an error"," and Allah knows best. And there has come a report prohibiting the Witr with three rak'ahs resembling the prayer of Maghrib. [Hakim 1/446]"""
"4815","(4815) It was narrated to us by Abu Ali — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Ali al-Rudhbari — informed us; al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Tusi — informed us; Abu Hatim — Muhammad ibn Idris al-Razi — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salih al-Misri — narrated to us; Abdillah ibn Wahb — narrated from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Salih ibn Kaysan from Abdillah ibn al-Fadl from al-A'raj and Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayrah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \Do not perform the Witr with three"," making it resemble the prayer of Maghrib. Perform the Witr with seven or five.\"" [Sahih. Ibn Hibban 2429]"""
"4816","(4816) And Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Uthman — Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn Abdan — and Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami — all said: narrated to us Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Abu al-Hasan — Tahir ibn Amr ibn al-Rabi' ibn Tariq ibn Qurrath ibn Nahik ibn Mujahid al-Hilali in Egypt — narrated to us; my father — informed me; al-Layth ibn Sa'd — narrated from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Irak ibn Malik from Abu Hurayrah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \Do not perform the Witr with three"," making it resemble the Maghrib. But perform the Witr with five"," or seven"," or nine"," or eleven rak'ahs"," or more than that.\"" And Ibn Bukayr narrated it from al-Layth likewise. [Sahih in meaning. Hakim 1/446]"""
"4817","(4817) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Ali ibn Mukram al-Bazzaz in Baghdad informed us: 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated from 'Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \Do not perform the Witr with three [rak'ahs].\"" He then mentioned something similar as a mawquf [stopping at the Companion level]. [Hasan. Al-Tahawi 1/292]"""
"4818","(4818) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bashir al-Hariri narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn Salam narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: Abu Salamah informed me that he asked 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and she said: \He used to pray thirteen rak'ahs at night. He would pray nine rak'ahs standing"," performing the Witr among them"," and he would pray two rak'ahs sitting. When he wished to prostrate"," he would stand"," then bow and prostrate. He would do this after the Witr"," and he would pray two rak'ahs when he heard the call to the morning prayer.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Bashir al-Hariri"," and he also recorded it additionally from the hadith of Hisham and Shayban"," both from Yahya. [Sahih Muslim 738]"""
"4819","(4819) Al-Awza'i narrated from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from 'A'ishah: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed two rak'ahs after the Witr"," reciting in them while he was sitting. When he wished to bow"," he stood and then bowed.\""\n\nAbu 'Abd Allah informed us: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Susi narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us: al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid informed me: my father narrated to me: al-Awza'i narrated to me: Yahya narrated to me"," and he mentioned it. [Sahih. Muslim 731]"""
"4820","(4820) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to pray thirteen rak'ahs at night. He would perform the Witr with nine—or as he said—and he would pray two rak'ahs while sitting"," and [he would pray] the two rak'ahs of Fajr between the Adhan and the Iqamah.\"" [Sahih from other than him. Abu Dawud 1350]"""
"4821","(4821) And with his chain of narration, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Hammad narrated from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to perform the Witr with nine rak'ahs"," then he would perform the Witr with seven rak'ahs"," and he would pray two rak'ahs after the Witr while sitting"," reciting in them. When he wished to bow"," he stood"," then bowed and prostrated.\"" Abu Dawud said: Khalid ibn 'Abd Allah narrated both hadiths from Muhammad ibn 'Amr similarly. In it"," 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas said: \""O Umm [i.e."," 'A'ishah]"," how did he pray the two rak'ahs?\"" And he mentioned their meaning as Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us from Khalid.\n\nThe Sheikh said: \""And we have been narrated these two rak'ahs in the hadith of Sa'd ibn Hisham from 'A'ishah from the Prophet (peace be upon him). And in the narration of al-Hasan from Sa'd"," he recited in them {Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun} and {Idha Zulzilat}.\"" [Sahih from other than him. The meaning is in the one before it]"""
"4822","(4822) Abu 'Abd Allah informed us: al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Ghada'iri at Bab al-Sham narrated to us: Abu 'Amr 'Uthman ibn Ahmad al-Daqqaq dictated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Hammad ibn Mas'udah narrated to us: Maymun ibn Musa al-Muradi narrated from al-Hasan, from his mother, from Umm Salamah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs after the Witr while he was sitting.\""\n\nThis Maymun is a Basran [narrator]; there is no harm in him except that he used to practice tadlis [concealing defects in the chain]"," as Ahmad ibn Hanbal and others stated. And Allah knows best.\n\nIt was also narrated from Zakariyya ibn Hakim from al-Hasan"," but Hisham contradicted them both"," narrating it from al-Hasan from Sa'd ibn Hisham from 'A'ishah. Al-Bukhari said: \""And this is more correct.\"" [Sahih from other than him. Al-Tirmidhi 471]"""
"4823","(4823) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sadiq Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Saydalani said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu Qilabah narrated to us: 'Abd al-Samad, meaning Ibn 'Abd al-Warith, narrated to us: my father narrated from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah: \The Prophet (peace be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs after the Witr while he was sitting"," reciting in them {Idha Zulzilat} and {Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun}.\"" [Hasan. Ahmad 5/260]"""
"4824","(4824) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi dictated to us: Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Hamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Marwazi narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Hammad al-Amuli narrated to us: Yazid ibn 'Abd Rabbih narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated from 'Utaybah ibn Abi Hakim, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik: \The Prophet (peace be upon him) used to pray the two rak'ahs after the Witr while he was sitting"," reciting in the first rak'ah Umm al-Qur'an and {Idha Zulzilat}"," and in the second rak'ah {Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun}.\"" Abu Ghalib and 'Utaybah ibn Abi Hakim are not strong [narrators].\n\nAnd 'Umarah ibn Zadan narrated from Thabit from Anas regarding the Witr with nine"," then with seven"," and he prayed two rak'ahs while sitting"," except that he said: \""And he recited in them al-Rahman and al-Waqi'ah.\""\n\nAnas said: \""And we used to recite with the short surahs from the Mufassal—{Idha Zulzilat} and {Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun} and the like.\"" [Hasan. Al-Daraqutni 2/41]"""
"4825","(4825) Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us: Hajib ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Nasr al-Muqri' narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to me: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated from Sharayh ibn 'Ubayd, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Thawban, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: \We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) on a journey"," and he said: 'In this journey there is hardship and burden. When one of you performs the Witr"," let him pray two rak'ahs. If he awakens [later at night]"," [it is well]; otherwise"," they will suffice him [for the night].'\""\n\nImam Ahmad (may Allah"," the Most High"," have mercy upon him) said: \""It is possible that what is meant by it is two rak'ahs after the Witr"," and it is possible that he intended: when he wishes to perform the Witr"," let him pray two rak'ahs before the Witr.\"" [Strong. Al-Darimi 1594]"""
"4826","(4826) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us in al-Tarikh: Abu al-Tayyib Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Karabisiyy narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Shadil ibn 'Ali narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Bazi', our neighbour, narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated from 'Umarah ibn Zadan: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us from Anas ibn Malik, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to perform the Witr with nine rak'ahs. When he grew old and became heavy"," he performed the Witr with seven"," and he prayed two rak'ahs while sitting"," reciting in them al-Rahman and al-Waqi'ah.\""\n\nAnas said: \""And we used to recite with the short surahs from the Mufassal—{Idha Zulzilat} and {Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun} and the like","\"" and on one occasion he said: \""He recited in them.\"" 'Umarah ibn Zadan contradicted all the other narrators regarding the Prophet's (peace be upon him) recitation in them. [Hasan from other than him]"""
"4827","(4827) It was narrated by Abu Ghalib from Abu Umamah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform Witr with seven rak'ahs. When his body became heavy and fleshy, he performed Witr with three and then prayed two rak'ahs while seated, reciting in them {Idha Zulzilat} and {Qul Ya Ayyuhal Kafirun}.\n\nIt was related to us by Abu Sa'd al-Malini, who was informed by Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adiyy, who narrated from Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Muthanna, who narrated from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghayyath, who narrated from 'Umarah ibn Zadhan, who narrated to me from Abu Ghalib, and he mentioned it.\n\nAl-Bukhari, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say: \'Umarah ibn Zadhan is sometimes imprecise in his hadith.\"" [Hasan li-ghayrihi. Previously cited as no. 4823]"""
"4828","(4828) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us; Hajjib ibn Ahmad informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us; Yahya narrated to us; 'Ubaydullah narrated —\n\n(Chain variant:) And Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq informed us; Abu al-Muthanna narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yahya narrated from 'Ubaydullah from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \Make the last of your prayers at night the Witr.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad"," and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna from Yahya ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. Bukhari 953]"""
"4829","(4829) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us.\n\n(Chain variant:) And Abu al-Walid informed me; Ibrahim ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Harun ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us; Hajjaj said: Ibn Jurayj said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn 'Umar used to say: \Whoever prays at night"," let him make the last of his prayer the Witr before dawn. Thus the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Harun ibn 'Abdullah. [Sahih. Muslim 751]"""
"4830","(4830) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us; Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us; Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyub and Budayl ibn Maynarah narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq from Ibn 'Umar that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ while he was between the Prophet and the questioner. He said: \O Messenger of Allah"," how is the prayer of the night?\"" He said: \""Two by two. When you fear the dawn"," then pray one rak'ah and make the last of your prayer the Witr.\"" Then a man asked him at the head of the year while I was in that place near the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and I do not know whether it was that man or another man"," and he said to him the same.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani. [Sahih. Bukhari 461]"""
"4831","(4831) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Walid al-Faqih informed me; Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us; 'Ammar ibn Ruzayq narrated from Abu Ishaq from al-Aswad from 'Aishah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray at night until the last of his prayer was the Witr.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Muslim 740]"""
"4832","(4832) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Mu'mil ibn Hisham narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated from Mansur ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani from al-Aswad ibn Yazid that he entered upon 'Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at night. She said: \He used to pray thirteen rak'ahs at night. Then he prayed eleven rak'ahs and left two rak'ahs. Then he was taken when he was taken"," and he used to pray nine rak'ahs at night"," the last of his prayer at night being the Witr.\""\n\nMuhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah contradicted him from Mu'mil ibn Hisham"," saying from Abu Ishaq from Masruq. The narration of Abu Dawud is more authentic based on what preceded from the narration of 'Ammar ibn Ruzayq. [Da'if. Abu Dawud 1363]"""
"4833","(4833) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Sufyan informed us.\n\n(Chain variant:) And Abu 'Abdullah narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us; Isma'il ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yahya ibn Yahya informed us; Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from Abu Ya'fur from Muslim ibn Subayh from Masruq from 'Aishah, who said: \In every night the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Witr"," and his Witr ended at the time of sahar (pre-dawn).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya"," and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of al-A'mash from Muslim. [Sahih. Bukhari 951]"""
"4834","(4834) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi narrated to us by dictation; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Sharqi narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Yahya ibn Waththab from Masruq from 'Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \In every night the Prophet ﷺ performed Witr"," and his Witr extended to the last part of the night.\"" [Sahih li-ghayrihi]"""
"4835","(4835) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Walid informed me; Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Waki' narrated from Sufyan from Abu Hasin from Yahya ibn Waththab from Masruq from 'Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \In every night the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Witr — from the beginning of the night"," its middle"," and its end — and his Witr ended at the time of sahar (pre-dawn).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. See what preceded]"""
"4836","(4836) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us; al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Qays, who said: \I asked 'Aishah"," may Allah be pleased with her"," about the Witr of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\"" She said: \""Sometimes he performed Witr at the beginning of the night"," and sometimes he performed Witr at the end of it.\"" I said: \""How was his recitation — did he recite quietly or aloud?\"" She said: \""He would do all of that. Sometimes he recited quietly"," and sometimes he recited aloud. Sometimes he performed ghusl"," and sometimes he performed wudu' and then slept.\"""""
"4837","(4837) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Sufyān from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever fears that he will not wake up in the latter part of the night"," let him perform the witr prayer at the beginning of the night and then sleep. And whoever hopes to wake up in the latter part of the night"," let him perform the witr at the end of the night"," for the recitation in the latter part of the night is attended [by the angels]"," and that is more virtuous.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah and others from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1755]"""
"4838","(4838) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us: Salamah ibn Shabīb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Aʿyan narrated to us: Maʿqil ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, who said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: \Whoever among you fears that he will not rise in the latter part of the night"," let him perform witr and then sleep. And whoever is confident of rising in the night"," let him perform witr at the end of it"," for the recitation in the latter part of the night is attended [by the angels]"," and that is more virtuous.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Salamah ibn Shabīb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1755]"""
"4839","(4839) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq al-Saylaḥīnī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit from ʿAbdillāh ibn Rabāḥ from Abū Qatādah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): \When do you perform witr?\"" He said: \""I perform witr before I sleep.\"" And he said to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him): \""When do you perform witr?\"" He said: \""I sleep and then perform witr.\"" So he said to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): \""You have taken the course of caution (al-ḥazm) or firm resolve (al-wathīqah).\"" And he said to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him): \""You have taken the course of strength (al-quwwah).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Ḥibbān 2446]"""
"4840","(4840) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Abū Umayyah al-Ṭarsūsī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq narrated it with its meaning, except that he said: \He took the course of caution (al-ḥazm)","\"" and he did not express any doubt. [Strong. See the preceding ḥadīth.]"""
"4841","(4841) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Sulaym narrated from ʿUbaydillāh from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): \When do you perform witr?\"" He said: \""I perform witr and then sleep.\"" He said: \""You have taken the course of caution (al-ḥazm).\"" And he asked ʿUmar: \""When do you perform witr?\"" He said: \""I sleep and then rise in the night and perform witr.\"" He said: \""You have done the deed of the strong one (al-qawiyy).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it.]"""
"4842","(4842) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us by dictation: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Abū Maʿmar narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us: Abū al-Tayyāḥ narrated from Abū ʿUthmān from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \My beloved (ﷺ) advised me regarding three things: fasting three days of every month"," the two rakʿahs of Ḍuḥā"," and that I perform witr before I sleep.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1124]"""
"4843","(4843) And Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us: Shaybān ibn Farūkh narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: \Abū ʿUthmān"," meaning al-Nahdī"," narrated to me.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Maʿmar"," and Muslim narrated it from Shaybān ibn Farūkh."""
"4844","(4844) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Mulāzim ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Badr narrated from Qays ibn Ṭalq, who said: \Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī visited us on a day in Ramadan"," stayed with us until evening"," and broke his fast with us. Then he led us in prayer that night and performed witr with us. Then he descended to his mosque and prayed with his companions until only the witr remained"," so he put forward a man and said: 'Perform witr with your companions"," for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \""There are not two witr prayers in one night.\""'\"" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1439]"""
"4845","(4845) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū Badr narrated to us: Abū Sinān Saʿīd ibn Sinān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, that he asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab about the witr prayer. He said: \ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to perform witr at the beginning of the night. When he woke up"," he would annul his witr"," then pray"," and then perform witr at the end of his prayer in the latter part of the night. And ʿUmar used to perform witr at the end of the night. And the best of all of us was Abū Bakr"," who would perform witr at the beginning of the night and then add [more prayers] at the end of it.\"" He meant by this that he would pray in sets of two rakʿahs and would not annul his witr. [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 6706]"""
"4846","(4846) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Jaʿfar, narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Jamrah, who said: \I asked Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) about annulling the witr. He said: 'If you have performed witr at the beginning of the night"," do not perform witr at the end of it. And if you have performed witr at the end of it"," do not perform witr at the beginning of it.' And I asked ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAmr"," who was among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ)"," about annulling the witr. He said: 'If you have performed witr at the beginning of it"," do not perform witr at the end of it. And if you have performed witr at the end of it"," do not perform witr at the beginning of it.'\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAmr in al-Ṣaḥīḥ. He said: \""And he was among the Companions of the Tree"," among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3942]"""
"4847","(4847) Abū Aḥmad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl — informed us; Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī — narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us; Mālik — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from Abū Murrah, the freedman of ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib, that he asked Abū Hurayrah how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform the witr prayer. Abū Hurayrah remained silent. He asked him again, and he remained silent. He asked him a third time, and he said: \If you wish"," I shall inform you how I myself used to do it.\"" He said: \""Then inform me.\"" He said: \""When I prayed ʿIshāʾ"," I would pray five rakʿahs after it"," then I would sleep. If I rose during the night"," I would pray two rakʿahs at a time. And when morning came"," I would have my witr completed.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 250]"""
"4848","(4848) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Usayd ibn ʿĀṣim — narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ — narrated to us; from Sufyān — from al-Aʿmash — from ʿUmārah — from Abū ʿAṭiyyah — from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \That is the one who plays with his witr\"" — meaning the one who performs witr and then sleeps. When he rises"," he adds a single rakʿah to make it even"," then he prays — meaning then he repeats his witr.\"" [Ḥasan li-ghayrih. Ibn Abī Shaybah 6744]"""
"4849","(4849) Abū Saʿīd — Muḥammad ibn Mūsā — narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us; al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah, the Most High, have mercy on him, said: \Whoever performs witr at the beginning of the night should then pray two rakʿahs at a time until morning.\""\n\nAnd he mentioned the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUlayyah"," from Abū Hārūn al-Ghanawī"," from Ḥiṭṭān ibn ʿAbdillāh"," who said: ʿAlī"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: \""Witr is of three types. Whoever wishes may perform witr at the beginning of the night; then if he wakes up"," he may add a rakʿah to make it even and pray two rakʿahs at a time until morning"," then perform witr again — and he may do so. Or he may pray two rakʿahs at a time until morning. Or he may perform witr at the end of the night.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Kitāb al-Umm 1/259]"""
"4850","(4850) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad — narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh — narrated to us; my father — narrated to us; Shuʿbah — from Abū Hārūn al-Ghanawī, who said: I heard Ḥiṭṭān ibn ʿAbdillāh say: I heard ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, say: \Witr is of three types. Whoever wishes may perform witr at the beginning of the night; then if he prays"," he prays two rakʿahs at a time until morning. Whoever wishes may perform witr"," then if he prays"," he prays a single rakʿah to make his witr even"," then prays two rakʿahs at a time"," then performs witr again. And whoever wishes may not perform witr until it is the last of his prayer.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in his Musnad 1772]"""
"4851","(4851) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him — informed us; Abū Naṣr — Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Muṭawwiʿī — narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥammād al-Āmulī — narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam — narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us by dictation; Abū Jaʿfar — Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Baghdādī — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Sahmī — narrated to us; my father, ʿAmr ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Ṭāriq, and Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam — they said: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb — narrated to us; from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī — from ʿAmrah — from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the witr: in the first rakʿah"," 'Sabbiḥ isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā' (Glorify the name of your Lord"," the Most High); in the second"," 'Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn' (Say: O disbelievers); and in the third"," 'Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad' (Say: He is Allah"," the One)"," 'Qul aʿūdhu bi-Rabbi al-falaq' (Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the daybreak)"," and 'Qul aʿūdhu bi-Rabbi al-nās' (Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind).'\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdillāh. In the narration of al-ʿAlawī: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us; from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān"," and the rest carries the same meaning. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1424]"""
"4852","(4852) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Zāhid al-Aṣbahānī — narrated to us; Abū Ismāʿīl — Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Salamī — narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn ʿUfayr al-Miṣrī — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb — informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī — informed us; from ʿAmrah — from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the two rakʿahs after which he performed witr: 'Sabbiḥ isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā' and 'Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn.' And he used to recite in the witr: 'Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad","' 'Qul aʿūdhu bi-Rabbi al-falaq","' and 'Qul aʿūdhu bi-Rabbi al-nās.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih. See the preceding]"""
"4853","(4853) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb — narrated to us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī — narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb — from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, and he mentioned it similarly."
"4854","(4854) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us; Abū Zakariyyā al-ʿAnbarī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām — narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Jazarī — narrated to us; Khuṣayf — from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Jurayj, who said: \We asked ʿĀʾishah: 'With what did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the witr?' She said: 'He used to recite in the first rakʿah: \""Sabbiḥ isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā\""; in the second: \""Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn\""; and in the third: \""Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad\"" and the two Muʿawwidhatayn (Sūrat al-Falaq and Sūrat al-Nās).'\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4855","(4855) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr — narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Kathīr — from Ḥuṣayn — from Dharr — from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā — from his father: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform witr with \Sabbiḥ isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā","\"" \""Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn","\"" and \""Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih. al-Nasāʾī 1699]"""
"4856","(4856) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl — narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Mūsā — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Dashtakī — narrated to us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Rāzī — from al-Aʿmash — from Zubayd and Ṭalḥah — from Dharr — from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā — from his father — from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform witr with 'Sabbiḥ isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā","' 'Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn","' and 'Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad.'\""\n\nʿAlī said: \""And similarly it was narrated by Abū Ḥafṣ al-Abbār and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah and Muḥammad ibn Anas — from al-Aʿmash — from Zubayd and Ṭalḥah. And it was narrated by Abū ʿUbaydah ibn Maʿn — from al-Aʿmash — from Ṭalḥah alone.\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4857","(4857) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us; Yūnus narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform the Witr prayer with {Sabbisma Rabbika al-Aʿlā} [Sūrah al-Aʿlā]"," {Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn} [Sūrah al-Kāfirūn]"," and {Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad} [Sūrah al-Ikhlāṣ].\""\n\nIn the narration of Isrāʾīl it is: \""That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform the Witr.\"" Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah contradicted both of them and narrated it as such.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. al-Nasāʾī 1702]"""
"4858","(4858) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Abū Hurayrah, that he used to perform the Witr with three Sūrahs: {Sabbisma Rabbika al-Aʿlā}, {Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn}, and {Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad}. Ismāʿīl said: \Zuhayr stopped it (as a Mawqūf narration)"," while Isrāʾīl raised it (as a Marfūʿ narration).\"""""
"4859","(4859) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānʾ and Abū Manṣūr Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-ʿAtakī both said: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shaybah al-Ḥizāmī narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) taught me"," for my Witr prayer"," when I raise my head and nothing remains except the prostration"," to say:\n\n'Allāhumma ʾihdī fīman hadayt"," wa ʿāfinī fīman ʿāfayt"," wa tawallanī fīman tawallayt"," wa bārik lī fīmā ʾātayt"," wa qinī sharra mā qaḍayt"," innaka taqḍī wa lā yuqaḍḍay ʿalayk"," innahu lā yadhillu man wālayt"," tabārakta Rabbanā wa taʿālayt.'\n\n(O Allah"," guide me among those whom You have granted guidance"," grant me well-being among those whom You have granted well-being"," take me as an ally among those whom You have taken as an ally"," bless me in what You have given me"," and protect me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed"," You decree and none can decree against him. Indeed"," he is not humiliated whom You have taken as an ally. Blessed are You"," our Lord"," and Exalted.)\""\n\nAbū Bakr ibn Shaybah al-Ḥizāmī is alone with this wording.\n\nAnd we have narrated regarding the Qunūt in the Fajr prayer after the Rukūʿ what necessitates reliance upon it"," and the Qunūt of the Witr is analogous to it.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4860","(4860) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān said: al-Shāfiʿī said, narrating from Hushaym, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to perform the Qunūt in the Witr prayer after the Rukūʿ.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 6901]"
"4861","(4861) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath narrated to us; al-Musayyab ibn Wāḍiḥ narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah. Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān — al-Musayyab would sometimes say: from ʿAzrah, and sometimes he would not say it — narrated from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform the Witr with three (Rakʿahs)"," reciting therein {Sabbisma Rabbika al-Aʿlā}"," {Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn}"," and {Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad}. He would perform the Qunūt before the Rukūʿ"," and when he would say the Salām"," he would say: 'Subḥāna al-Malik al-Quddūs' twice"," saying them quietly"," and the third time he would raise his voice and prolong it.\""\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1427]"""
"4862","(4862) And Abū Bakr informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Khashram narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated from Fiṭr, from Zubayd, from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform the Witr with three (Sūrahs): {Sabbisma Rabbika al-Aʿlā}"," {Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn}"," and {Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad}. He would perform the Qunūt before the Rukūʿ. When he would say the Salām"," he would say: 'Subḥāna al-Malik al-Quddūs' three times"," prolonging his voice with it on the last one"," saying: 'Rabb al-Malāʾikah wa al-Rūḥ.'\""\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding]"""
"4863","(4863) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr said: Abū Dāwūd Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath al-Sijistānī said: \The Ḥadīth of Saʿīd from Qatādah — Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated it from Saʿīd"," from Qatādah"," from ʿAzrah"," from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā"," from his father"," from the Prophet (ﷺ) — and he did not mention the Qunūt"," nor did he mention Ubayy. Similarly"," ʿAbd al-Aʿlā and Muḥammad ibn Bishr al-ʿAbdī narrated it — and his hearing was in Kufah along with ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus — and they did not mention the Qunūt. Hishām al-Dastuwāʾī and Shuʿbah also narrated it from Qatādah"," and they did not mention the Qunūt. The Ḥadīth of Zubayd was narrated by Sulaymān al-Aʿmash"," Shuʿbah"," ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān"," and Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim — all of them from Zubayd — and none of them mentioned the Qunūt"," except what was narrated from Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth from Miʿsar from Zubayd"," for he said in his Ḥadīth: 'And he performed the Qunūt before the Rukūʿ.' But this is not well-known from the Ḥadīth of Ḥafṣ; it is feared that it is from Ḥafṣ from other than Miʿsar.\"" All of this is the statement of Abū Dāwūd"," and Abū Dāwūd weakened this addition. And Allah knows best.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4864","(4864) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Isfarāʾinī, Ibn al-Saqqāʾ in Naysābūr, narrated to us the Ḥadīth of Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyādah al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Miʿsar; Zubayd narrated to me; from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform the Witr with three Rakʿahs, not saying Salām in them until he would turn away (conclude). The first (Rakʿah) with {Sabbisma Rabbika al-Aʿlā}, the second with {Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn}, and the third with {Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad}. He performed the Qunūt before the Rukūʿ. When he turned away (concluded), he said: \Subḥāna Allāh al-Malik al-Quddūs\"" twice"," and he raised his voice on the third time.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Previously cited under no. 4861]"""
"4865","(4865) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us; Abān ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh narrated from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \I spent the night with the Prophet (ﷺ) to observe how he would perform the Qunūt in his Witr. He performed the Qunūt before the Rukūʿ. Then I sent my mother"," Umm ʿAbd"," and said: 'Spend the night with his wives and observe how he performs the Qunūt in his Witr.' She came to me and informed me that he performed the Qunūt before the Rukūʿ.\""\n\nSufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Abān ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh"," and the Ḥadīth revolves around him. And Abān is abandoned (Matrūk).\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. al-Dāraquṭnī 2/32]"""
"4866","(4866) And ʿAṭāʾ ibn Muslim narrated from ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Musayyab, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \The Prophet (ﷺ) performed the Witr with three (Rakʿahs) and performed the Qunūt therein before the Rukūʿ.\""\n\nAbū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us of it; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj informed us; Muṭayyin narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yūnus al-Raqqī narrated to us; ʿAṭāʾ ibn Muslim al-Ḥalabī narrated it. And ʿAṭāʾ ibn Muslim is alone with this. And he is weak.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym in al-Ḥilyah 5/62]"""
"4867","(4867) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us — al-Aswad ibn 'Amir Shadhan narrated to us — Sharik narrated from Layth — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad — from his father, who said: Ibn Mas'ud used to raise his hands in the qunut up to his chest. [Da'if]"
"4868","(4868) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us — Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Isbahani narrated to us — Abu 'Amir Musa ibn 'Amir narrated to us — al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to me — Ibn Lahi'ah narrated from Musa ibn Wardan, that he used to see Abu Hurayrah raising his hands in his qunut during the month of Ramadan. Al-Walid said: and 'Amir ibn Shibl al-Jurmi informed me, he said: I saw Abu Qilabah raising his hands in his qunut. [Da'if]"
"4869","(4869) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from Salamat ibn Kuhayl — and Zubayd narrated from Dharr — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza — from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the witr: \Glorify the name of your Lord"," the Most High\"" (Al-A'la 87:1) and \""Say: O you disbelievers\"" (Al-Kafirun 109:1) and \""Say: He is Allah"," the One\"" (Al-Ikhlas 112:1). When he gave the salam"," he would say: \""Glory be to the Sovereign"," the Holy\"" (Subhāna al-Malik al-Quddūs) three times"," raising his voice on the third. [Sahih. Ahmad 3/402]"""
"4870","(4870) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abi 'Ubaydah narrated to us — his father narrated from al-A'mash — from Talhah al-Ayami — from Dharr — from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza — from his father — from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the salam in the witr, he would say: \Glory be to the Sovereign"," the Holy\"" (Subhāna al-Malik al-Quddūs). [Da'if. Abu Dawud 1430]"""
"4871","(4871) Abu al-Hasan al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us — Hammad ibn Salamah narrated — from Hisham ibn 'Amr al-Fazari — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham — from 'Ali ibn Abi Talid, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate at the end of his witr, saying: \O Allah"," I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath"," and in Your pardon from Your punishment"," and I seek refuge in You from You. I cannot enumerate praises upon You; You are as You have praised Yourself.\"" [Sahih. Abu Dawud 1427]"""
"4872","(4872) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us — Hammad narrated, mentioning something similar to it. Abu Dawud said: Hisham is the earliest shaykh of Hammad. He said: And it reached me from Yahya ibn Ma'in that he said: No one narrated from him other than Hammad ibn Salamah. [This is another chain of narration for the preceding hadith]"
"4873","(4873) Abu 'Uthman Sa'id ibn al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Qurashi narrated to us — and he was with us performing Hajj in the Mosque of the Messenger ﷺ — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sayyar and Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Jawhari both said: Yahya ibn Ahmad ibn Ziyad Abu Mansur narrated to us — Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us — Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us — Yazid ibn Kayyan narrated from Abu Hazim — from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet ﷺ recited in the two rak'ahs before Fajr: \Say: O you disbelievers\"" (Al-Kafirun 109:1) and \""Say: He is Allah"," the One\"" (Al-Ikhlas 112:1). [Sahih. Muslim 726]"""
"4874","(4874) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Walid al-Faqih informed me — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abbad al-Makki narrated to us — Marwan narrated, mentioning something similar to it. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abbad. And it has also been narrated to us from 'A'ishah, Ibn Mas'ud, and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ. [This is another chain of narration for the preceding hadith]"
"4875","(4875) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us — 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah — (h) — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz said: Abu al-Walid al-Faqih informed me — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Abu Kurayb narrated to us — both said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us — 'Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to me — Sa'id ibn Yasar narrated that Ibn 'Abbas informed him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: in the first of them the verse in al-Baqarah: \Say we believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us\"" (Al-Baqarah 2:136) — the entire verse — and in the last of them: \""We believe in Allah and bear witness that we are Muslims\"" (Al 'Imran 3:52). Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah"," from Marwan. [Sahih. Muslim 727]"""
"4876","(4876) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Muqri' informed me — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us — Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated from 'Uthman ibn Hakim — from Sa'id ibn Yasar — from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: \Say we believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us\"" (Al-Baqarah 2:136) — the verse — and in the second: \""Come to a word that is fair between us and you\"" (Al 'Imran 3:52). Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. And Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah"," 'Isa ibn Yunus"," and 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated it from 'Uthman ibn Hakim with the same meaning as the narration of Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari. [Sahih. See the preceding narration]"""
"4877","(4877) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Khalaf ibn ʿAmr al-ʿUkbarī informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad informed me, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar ibn Mūsā said: I heard Abā al-Ghayth say: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite in the two rakʿahs before Fajr: in the first prostration, {Say, \We have believed in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what was revealed to Ibrāhīm"," Ismāʿīl"," Isḥāq"," and Yaʿqūb\""} [al-Baqarah: 136] to His words {and to Him we are Muslims} [al-Baqarah: 136]"," and in the second: {Our Lord"," we have believed in what You revealed and followed the Messenger"," so write us among the witnesses} [Āl ʿImrān: 53]. Thus he reported it without doubt.\n\nMuḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī with doubt regarding his words {Our Lord"," what You revealed} [Āl ʿImrān: 53]"," as he did not know whether this āyah was recited or {Indeed"," We have sent you with the truth as a bringer of good tidings and a warner"," and you will not be asked about the companions of the Blaze} [al-Baqarah: 119]. Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah also narrated it from al-Dārawardī similarly. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1256]"""
"4878","(4878) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī informed us, Abū Yaḥyā ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Masrrah informed us, Badal ibn al-Muḥabbir informed us, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Walīd ibn Maʿdān al-Ḍubaʿī informed us, ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah narrated from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: I cannot count how many times I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite in the two rakʿahs of Maghrib and the two rakʿahs of al-Ghadāh: {Say, \O disbelievers\""} and {Say"," \""He is Allah"," the One\""}. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this collection. Tirmidhī 431]"""
"4879","(4879) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ Salām narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) more than twenty times recite in the two rakʿahs after Maghrib and the two rakʿahs before Fajr: {Say, \O disbelievers\""} and {Say"," \""He is Allah"," the One\""}. (T) And thus Sufyān and Isrāʾīl narrated it from Abū Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this collection. Tirmidhī 417]"""
"4880","(4880) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, Abū al-Jawwāb informed us, ʿAmmār ibn Ruzayq narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Ibrāhīm from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet (ﷺ) something similar to that. Thus I found it in the tenth of the Amālī."
"4881","(4881) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us.\n\n(H) And Abū al-Walīd informed me, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, meaning al-Thaqafī, informed us, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me that he heard ʿAmrah narrate from ʿĀʾishah that she used to say:\n\n(H) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd ibn Zurārah — and he was the brother of ʿAmrah's father — from ʿAmrah, who said: I heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray the two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer, and he would make them so brief that I would say: Did he recite Umm al-Qurʾān in them?\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from another chain from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1118]"
"4882","(4882) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr informed us, Wakīʿ informed us, Ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father from ʿľʾishah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to make the two rakʿahs of Fajr brief.\n\nHe said: And Misʿar said from a man from the Anṣār from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would sometimes make the two rakʿahs of Fajr lengthy.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Wakīʿ, without the narration of Misʿar, and it is in fact munqaṭiʿ (disconnected).\n\nIsḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī narrated it from Wakīʿ from Sufyān from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"4883","(4883) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to make the two rakʿahs of Fajr brief.\n\n(T) And thus Aḥmad ibn Salamah and Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sarrāj narrated it from Isḥāq. The narration of others from Wakīʿ from Hishām is more accurate, and Allah knows best."
"4884","(4884) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Fāryābī informed us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā informed us, ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us, Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah from ʿľʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When Fajr rose, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would pray two brief rakʿahs, then lie down on his right side until the muʾadhdhin came to him.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Yūsuf from Maʿmar. And likewise al-Awzāʿī, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, Yūnus ibn Yazīd, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, and Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated it from al-Zuhrī. And Abū al-Aswad also narrated it from ʿUrwah from ʿľʾishah. Mālik ibn Anas opposed them and mentioned the lying down after the Witr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 600]"
"4885","(4885) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā informed us, Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from ʿUrwah from ʿľʾishah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray at night eleven rakʿahs, performing Witr with one of them. When he finished them, he would lie down on his right side until the muʾadhdhin came to him, then he would pray two brief rakʿahs.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Thus Mālik said it, and the majority (al-ʿadah) is more deserving of being preserved than the single narrator.\n\nIt is also possible that both are preserved, and Mālik transmitted one of them while the others transmitted the other. There is also disagreement regarding it from Ibn ʿAbbās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 736]"
"4886","(4886) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥaddād informed us, Salm ibn Ibrāhīm al-Warrāq informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Mūsā ibn Abī ʿľʾishah from a man from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (ﷺ), when he prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr, would lie down.\n\nOthers narrated it from Shuʿbah from Mūsā from Saʿīd from the Prophet (ﷺ) in a disconnected (munqaṭiʿ) manner, as is the case in these narrations.\n\nThe established ḥadīth from Kurayb from Ibn ʿAbbās has already passed, which indicates that his lying down was after the Witr. And that may carry the same possibility as the narration of Mālik, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this collection. Aḥmad 1/220]"
"4887","(4887) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad, Abū Kāmil, and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Mayṣarah said: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us; al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When one of you has prayed the two rakʿahs before Fajr"," let him lie down on his right side.\""\n\nMarwān ibn al-Ḥakam said to him: \""Does not one's walking to the mosque suffice him"," so that he may lie down on his right side?\"" ʿUbayd Allāh said in his hadith: \""No.\"" This reached Ibn ʿUmar"," and he said: \""Abū Hurayrah has imposed upon himself.\"" It was said to Ibn ʿUmar: \""Do you deny anything of what he says?\"" He said: \""No"," but he showed boldness and we showed caution.\"" This reached Abū Hurayrah"," and he said: \""What is my fault if I preserved [the hadith] and they forgot?\""\n\nThis may be understood as indicating permissibility. Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī narrated it from Abū Ṣāliḥ"," from Abū Hurayrah"," as a report of the action of the Prophet (ﷺ)"," not as a report of his statement. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1261]"""
"4888","(4888) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; my father narrated to me from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to me from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Sammān, who said: I heard Abū Hurayrah narrating to Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam while he was in al-Madīnah: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to separate between his two rakʿahs of Fajr and the Ṣubḥ prayer by lying down on his right side.\""\n\nThe Shaykh said: This is more likely to be the preserved version"," due to its agreement with the other narrations from ʿĀʾishah and Ibn ʿAbbās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Mājah 1199]"""
"4889","(4889) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; Sālim Abū al-Naḍr narrated from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray the two rakʿahs of Fajr. If I was awake"," he would speak to me; otherwise"," he would lie down until he rose for the prayer.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam from Sufyān"," and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān. Mālik ibn Anas narrated it outside al-Muwaṭṭaʾ from Sālim Abū al-Naḍr"," mentioning the hadith after the night prayer"," and mentioning his lying down after two rakʿahs before the two rakʿahs of Fajr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1108]"""
"4890","(4890) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm narrated to us; Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Sālim Abū al-Naḍr, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had completed his prayer at the end of the night"," he would look. If I was awake"," he would speak to me; if I was asleep"," he would wake me. Then he would pray the two rakʿahs"," then lie down until the muezzin came to inform him of the Ṣubḥ prayer. He would then pray two light rakʿahs"," and then go out for the prayer.\""\n\nThis differs from the narration of the group from Abū Salamah."""
"4891","(4891) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Makkī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ziyād ibn Saʿd, from Ibn Abī ʿAttāb, from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \The Prophet (ﷺ) used to pray at night"," then perform Witr"," then pray the two rakʿahs. If I was awake"," he would speak to me; otherwise"," he would lie down until the muezzin came.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced under no. 4889]"""
"4892","(4892) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; Ziyād ibn Saʿd al-Khurāsānī narrated from Ibn Abī ʿAttāb.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ziyād ibn Saʿd, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAttāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, similar to the hadith of Ibn ʿUyaynah from Abū al-Naḍr.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, and he said: \from Ibn Abī ʿAttāb.\"" Others besides Ibn ʿUyaynah say his name is Zayd ibn Abī ʿAttāb."""
"4893","(4893) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAlqamah narrated from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray his night prayer while I was lying across between him and the qiblah. When he intended to perform Witr"," he would nudge me with his foot. He would pray the two rakʿahs; if I was awake"," he would speak to me; otherwise"," he would lie down until he rose for the prayer.\""\n\nHe said: Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī said: \""Sufyān used to be uncertain about the hadith of Abū al-Naḍr and would be confused about it. He would sometimes be uncertain about the hadith of Ziyād and say: 'It gets mixed up for me.' Then he said more than once: 'The hadith of Abū al-Naḍr is such and such"," the hadith of Ziyād is such and such"," and the hadith of Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr is such and such","' as I have mentioned all of that.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"4894","(4894) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās al-ʿAnbarī and Ziyād ibn Yaḥyā said: Sahl ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Abū Makīn narrated to us; Abū al-Faḍl, a man from the Anṣār, narrated from Muslim ibn Abī Bakrah, from his father, who said: \I went out with the Prophet (ﷺ) for the Ṣubḥ prayer. He would not pass by a man without calling him to prayer or nudging him with his foot.\"" Ziyād said: \""Abū al-Fuḍayl narrated to us.\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4895","(4895) Abū Muḥammad Jānāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Jānāḥ al-Muḥāribī informed us in al-Kūfah; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Miʿsar narrated from Zayd al-ʿAmmī, from Abū al-Ṣiddīq al-Nājī, who said: \ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar saw some people who had lain down after the two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer. He said: 'Go back to them and ask them what compelled them to do what they have done.' I went to them and asked them"," and they said: 'We intend the Sunnah.' He said: 'Go back to them and inform them that it is an innovation (bidʿah).'\""\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī (may Allah"," the Most High"," have mercy upon him) indicated that the lying down transmitted in the previous reports was for the purpose of distinguishing between the supererogatory prayer and the obligatory prayer. Whether that distinction was achieved by lying down"," or by speaking"," or by moving from that place"," or by other means"," the lying down is not specifically required for that purpose. And Allah knows best."""
"4896","(4896) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl al-Muzakkī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk informed us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥunayyin, from Abū Murrah, the freedman of Umm Hānīʾ, from Abū al-Dardāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \My beloved (ﷺ) enjoined upon me three things which I will not abandon as long as I live: fasting three days of every month"," the Ḍuḥā prayer"," and that I should not sleep until I have performed Witr.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and others.\n\nMention of the authentic hadiths from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the number of the Ḍuḥā prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 722]"""
"4897","(4897) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī informed us in Kūfah; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Mūsā al-Qazzāz informed us; Muʿallā ibn Asad informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Mukhtār narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh al-Dānāj, from Abū Rāfiʿ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said:\n\n\My intimate friend"," Abū al-Qāsim (ﷺ)"," enjoined upon me three things: performing the Witr prayer before sleeping"," fasting three days of every month"," and the two rakʿahs of Ḍuḥā.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Maʿbad"," from Muʿallā ibn Asad"," and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū ʿUthmān"," from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1880]"""
"4898","(4898) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ informed us; Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated to us; Wāṣil, the freedman of Abū ʿUyaynah, narrated to us, from Yaḥyya ibn ʿUqayl, from Yaḥyya ibn Yaʿmar, from Abū al-Aswad al-Dīlī, from Abū Dharr, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\Upon every joint of any one of you"," charity is due every morning. Every declaration of 'Subḥān Allāh' is charity. Every declaration of 'Alḥamdulillāh' is charity. Every declaration of 'Lā ilāha illā Allāh' is charity. Every declaration of 'Allāhu Akbar' is charity. Enjoining good is charity. Forbidding evil is charity. And two rakʿahs that one prays of Ḍuḥā suffice for all of that.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ ibn Akhī Juwayriyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 720]"""
"4899","(4899) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Yaḥyya ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyya ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar informed us — all of them — from Qatādah, from Muʿādhah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said:\n\n\The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray the Ḍuḥā prayer as four rakʿahs"," and he would add whatever Allah willed.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿArūbah and Hishām al-Dastawāʾī"," from Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 719]"""
"4900","(4900) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Yazīd al-Rishk, who said: I heard Muʿādhah al-ʿAdawiyyah say:\n\n\I asked ʿĀʾishah: Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray Ḍuḥā?\"" She said: \""Yes"," four rakʿahs"," and he would add whatever Allah willed.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"4901","(4901) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī informed us in Makkah; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd al-Thaqafī narrated to us, from Burd ibn Sinān, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makḥūl, from Kathīr ibn Murrah al-Ḥaḍramī, from Qays al-Judhāmī, from Nuʿaym ibn Hammār al-Ghaṭafānī, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), from his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, who said:\n\n\O son of Ādam"," pray for Me four rakʿahs at the beginning of the day"," and I shall suffice you for its end.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 1289]"""
"4902","(4902) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbbās al-Asfaṭī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ādam narrated to us — both of them said: Shuʿbah narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Murrah narrated to us, who said: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā say:\n\n\No one has ever narrated to us that he saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) praying Ḍuḥā except Umm Hānīʾ"," for she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered her house on the day of the Conquest of Makkah"," performed ghusl"," and prayed eight rakʿahs. I have never seen a prayer lighter than it"," except that he completed the rukūʿ and sujūd properly.\""\n\nThe wording is that of Ādam. al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās and Abū al-Walīd"," and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 350]"""
"4903","(4903) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad al-Faqīh informed us in Naysābūr; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyya narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me; Yūnus informed me, from Ibn Shihāb; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that his father, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal, said:\n\n\I asked and was eager to find anyone among the people to inform me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed the voluntary prayer of Ḍuḥā. Umm Hānīʾ bint Abī Ṭālib informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came after the day had risen on the day of the Conquest. He ordered that a cloth be placed as a screen for him. He then performed ghusl"," stood"," and prayed eight rakʿahs. I do not know whether his standing in them was longer"," or his rukūʿ"," or his sujūd — all of it was approximately equal.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah"," except that he said: from Ibn Shihāb"," who said: Ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith informed me"," and that is because the Ṣaḥīḥ is that it was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith. Thus said al-Layth ibn Saʿd and others from Ibn Shihāb"," except that Ibn Wahb says ʿUbayd Allāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"4904","(4904) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal narrate from Umm Hānīʾ:\n\n\She saw the Prophet (ﷺ) pray Ḍuḥā as eight rakʿahs. She had not seen him pray before it or after it in a garment whose two ends were draped differently.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 1012]"""
"4905","(4905) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me, from Makhrah ibn Sulaymān, from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Umm Hānīʾ bint Abī Ṭālib:\n\n\The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," on the day of the Conquest"," prayed the voluntary prayer of Ḍuḥā as eight rakʿahs"," giving salām after every two rakʿahs.\""\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1290]"""
"4906","(4906) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us; Yaḥyya ibn Jaʿfar informed us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us, from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said:\n\n\I met Abū Dharr and said: 'O uncle"," impart some good to me.' He said: 'I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) just as you have asked me"," and he said: \""If you pray Ḍuḥā as two rakʿahs"," you will not be written among the heedless. If you pray it as four"," you will be written among the doers of good. If you pray it as six"," you will be written among those who are devoutly obedient. If you pray it as eight"," you will be written among the triumphant. If you pray it as ten"," no sin will be written for you that day. And if you pray it as twelve rakʿahs"," Allah will build for you a house in Paradise.\""'\""\n\nIt has also been narrated from another chain from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr"," from Abū Dharr"," and we have mentioned it in the book al-Jāmiʿ.\n\n[Munkar. Ibn Ḥibbān in al-Majrūḥīn 1/243]"""
"4907","(4907) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Murādī narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, from Zabbān ibn Fā'id, from Sahl ibn Muʿādh ibn Anas al-Juhanī, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever remains seated in his place of prayer after finishing the Fajr prayer until he performs the two rakʿahs of Ḍuḥā"," saying nothing but good"," his sins shall be forgiven even if they are more abundant than the foam of the sea.\"" [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd 1287]"""
"4908","(4908) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated, from al-Qāsim al-Shaybānī, that Zayd ibn Arqam saw people praying in the Mosque of Qubā' during the time of Ḍuḥā. He said: \Do they not know that prayer at other times is more excellent? Indeed"," the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Indeed"," the prayer of the Awwābīn is when the young camels feel the heat of the sun.'\"" And he said on another occasion: \""And people were praying.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others"," from Ismāʿīl. [Ḥasan. Muslim 748]"""
"4909","(4909) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hishām narrated, from al-Qāsim al-Shaybānī.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated, from al-Qāsim al-Shaybānī, from Zayd ibn Arqam, that he saw people sitting near a storyteller. When the sun rose, they hastened to the pillars to pray. Zayd ibn Arqam said: \Indeed"," the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The prayer of the Awwābīn is when the young camels feel the heat.'\"" Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbdillāh al-Dustawā'ī"," from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAwf al-Shaybānī. [Ḥasan for other than it. See the preceding.]"""
"4910","(4910) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ṣadaqah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith narrated, from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abī Umāmah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever walks to an obligatory prayer while in a state of purity"," his reward is like that of a pilgrim in iḥrām. And whoever walks to the supererogatory prayer of Ḍuḥā"," nothing prompting him except that"," his reward is like that of one who performs ʿUmrah. And a prayer performed after another prayer"," with no idle talk between them"," is recorded in ʿIlliyyīn.\"" [Ḥasan for other than it. Abū Dāwūd 558]"""
"4911","(4911) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated, from al-Zuhrī.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀ'ishah, who said: \I never saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) perform the supererogatory prayer of Ḍuḥā"," and yet I perform it.\""\n\nMaʿmar added in his narration: \""And the people have not introduced anything more beloved to me than it.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam"," from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb.\n\nAnd I hold—and Allah knows best—that what is meant is: \""I did not see him persist in the supererogatory prayer of Ḍuḥā"," and yet I persist in it.\"" And her statement"," \""And the people have not introduced anything","\"" refers to persistence in it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1123]"""
"4912","(4912) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated, from al-Jurayrī.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated, from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shiqīq, who said: I said to ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): \Did the Prophet (ﷺ) pray Ḍuḥā?\"" She said: \""No"," except when he returned from a journey.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Maṭar.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. In this narration is the confirmation of his performing it when he returned from a journey.\n\nAnd it has been narrated regarding this from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh and Kaʿb ibn Mālik"," from the Prophet (ﷺ).\n\nAnd we have narrated previously from Muʿādhah"," from ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her)"," that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to pray it as four rakʿahs"," and he would add as much as Allah willed.\n\nIn all of this there is evidence supporting the interpretation we have mentioned. And the reason for his not persisting in it has been established. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 717]"""
"4913","(4913) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; ʿAbbās al-Asfaṭī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl—meaning Ibn Abī Uways—narrated, from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated. He said: I read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: \I never saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray the supererogatory prayer of Ḍuḥā at all"," and yet I perform it. And indeed"," the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would abandon an action while he loved to perform it"," out of fear that the people might perform it and thus it would become obligatory upon them.\""\n\nThis is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously narrated under no. 4911]"""
"4914","(4914) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated, from Sufyān, from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, who said: We asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) about the voluntary prayers of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the day. He said to us: \Who among you has the capacity for that?\"" We said: \""Tell us about it; we will take from it what we can bear.\"" He said: \""When the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed Fajr"," he would wait until the sun rose to the equivalent of its position at ʿAṣr. Then he would stand and pray two rakʿahs"," separating them with salām upon the angels who are brought near"," the prophets"," and those who followed them among the believers and the Muslims. Then he would wait until the sun rose higher"," to the equivalent of its position at Ẓuhr. Then he would stand and pray four rakʿahs"," separating them with salām upon the angels who are brought near"," the prophets"," and those who followed them among the believers and the Muslims. Then he would wait until the sun passed its meridian. Then he would stand and pray four rakʿahs"," separating them with salām upon the angels who are brought near"," the prophets"," and those who followed them among the believers and the Muslims. Then he would pray two rakʿahs after Ẓuhr"," doing the same in them. Then he would pray four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr"," doing the same in them.\""\n\nAnd likewise Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān"," Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj"," Isrā'īl ibn Yūnus"," Abū ʿAwanah"," Abū al-Aḥwaṣ"," and Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated it from Abī Isḥāq. And Isrā'īl added in his narration: \""And he rarely persisted in it.\"" [Jayyid. Tirmidhī 598]"""
"4915","(4915) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed him; ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab in Wāsiṭ narrated to us; Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated, from Isrā'īl, from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, who said: I asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) about the voluntary prayers of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the day. He said: \Who among you has the capacity for that?\"" We said: \""We will take from it what we can bear.\"" He said: \""He would wait until the sun"," from the direction of the east"," was in the same position as it is from the direction of the west at the time of ʿAṣr. Then he would stand and pray two rakʿahs. Then he would wait until the sun rose and became fully visible"," and it was in the same position from the east as it is from the west at the time of Ẓuhr. Then he would stand and pray four rakʿahs"," separating between every two rakʿahs with salām upon the angels who are brought near"," the prophets"," and those who followed them among the believers and the Muslims. Then he would wait until the sun passed its meridian. Then he would pray four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr"," separating them in the same manner. Then he would pray Ẓuhr. Then he would pray two rakʿahs after it. Then he would pray four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr"," separating between every two rakʿahs in the same manner.\"" Then he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth similarly. \""These are sixteen rakʿahs of the voluntary prayers of the Prophet (ﷺ) during the day. And he rarely persisted in them.\"" This is narrated exclusively by ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him).\n\nAnd ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak used to weaken him and criticized this narration of his. And Allah knows best. [Jayyid. See the preceding.]"""
"4916","(4916) Al-Sayyid Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us by dictation. Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us by dictation to us from his memory in the year twenty-five and three hundred. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam al-ʿAbdī narrated to us. Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Qinbārī narrated to us. Al-Ḥakam ibn Abān narrated, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib: \O ʿAbbās"," O my uncle! Shall I not give you? Shall I not show you affection? Shall I not grant you? Shall I not do for you ten qualities? If you do them"," Allah will forgive your sins—the first and the last"," the old and the deliberate"," the mistaken"," the secret and the open. Ten qualities: that you pray four rakʿahs"," beginning with the takbīr"," then reciting the Opening of the Book and a sūrah. Then when you finish the sūrah"," while standing"," you say: 'Subḥānallāhi"," wa al-ḥamdu lillāhi"," wa lā ilāha illallāhu"," wallāhu Akbar' fifteen times. Then you bow and say it ten times while bowing. Then you raise your head and say it ten times while standing. Then you prostrate and say it ten times. Then you raise your head and say it ten times. Then you prostrate and say it ten times. Then you raise your head and say it ten times. That is seventy-five times in every rakʿah. If you can pray it once every day"," do so. If you cannot"," then once every Friday. If you cannot"," then once every month. If you cannot"," then once every year. If you cannot"," then once in your lifetime.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1297]"""
"4917","(4917) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam al-Naysābūrī mentioned it with its meaning, and he added the words \his minor and major sins\"" before his saying \""his secret and his open deeds","\"" as though it had fallen upon me or upon my teacher in the dictation. [Weak. Abū Dāwūd 1297]"""
"4918","(4918) And Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us — Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Abān informed me — from my father, from ʿIkrimah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \O ʿAbbās"," O uncle of the Messenger of Allah"," shall I not gift you?\"" He mentioned it with its meaning"," in a mursal form"," and likewise it was narrated by a group of well-known narrators from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ. [Weak]"""
"4919","(4919) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Muḥammad ibn Sufyān al-Ubullī informed us — Ḥabbān ibn Hilāl, Abū Ḥabīb, informed me — Mahdī ibn Maymūn informed us — ʿAmr ibn Mālik informed us — from Abū al-Jawzāʾ, who informed me — from a man who had been a companion of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: \Come to me tomorrow so that I may gift you and reward you and give you","\"" until I thought he would give me a gift. He said: \""When the day has passed"," stand and pray four rakʿahs.\"" He mentioned something similar"," then he said: \""Then raise your head — meaning from the second prostration — and sit upright"," and do not stand until you have glorified Allah (tasbīḥ) ten times"," praised Him (taḥmīd) ten times"," magnified Him (takbīr) ten times"," and declared His Oneness (tahlīl) ten times. Then do that in all four rakʿahs.\"" He said: \""For if you were the greatest sinner on earth"," it would be forgiven for you by that.\"" I said: \""Then if I am not able to pray it at that hour?\"" He said: \""Pray it during the night and the day.\"" Abū Dāwūd said: And al-Mustamir ibn al-Rayyān narrated it from Abū al-Jawzāʾ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr"," as a mawqūf (stopping at the Companion's level). The shaykh said: And Abū Janāb narrated it from Abū al-Jawzāʾ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr from the Prophet ﷺ as a marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet)"," except that he made the tasbīḥ fifteen times before the recitation"," and he placed what comes after the second prostration after the recitation. Abū Dāwūd said: And Rūḥ ibn al-Musayyib and Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Mālik al-Nukrī from Abū al-Jawzāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās as his own statement"," and in Rūḥ's ḥadīth he said: \""The ḥadīth of the Prophet ﷺ.\"" [Weak]"""
"4920","(4920) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Abū Tawbah al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ informed us — Muḥammad ibn Muhājir from ʿUrwah ibn Ruwaym, who said: An Anṣārī narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Jaʿfar with this ḥadīth, and he mentioned something similar. Then he said regarding the second prostration of the first rakʿah, as he said in the ḥadīth of Mahdī ibn Maymūn. [Weak]"
"4921","(4921) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us — al-Qaʿnabī informed us — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Mawālī — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us the istikhārah (prayer for guidance) in matters just as he would teach us a sūrah from the Qurʾān. He would say to us: \When one of you is concerned about a matter"," let him pray two rakʿahs other than the obligatory prayer"," then let him say: 'O Allah"," I seek Your guidance through Your knowledge"," and I seek ability through Your power"," and I ask You from Your immense bounty"," for You know and I do not know"," and You are able and I am not able"," and You are the Knower of the unseen. O Allah"," if You know this matter — which he names specifically by his intention — to be good for me in my religion"," my livelihood"," my final return"," and the outcome of my affair"," then decree it for me"," make it easy for me"," and bless it for me. O Allah"," and if You know it to be harmful for me in the same way"," then turn it away from me and turn me away from it"," and decree for me the good wherever it may be"," then make me content with it' — or he said: 'in my immediate and future affairs.'\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1109]"""
"4922","(4922) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us — he said: I read to Mālik ibn Anas — from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr — from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqī — from Abū Qatādah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When one of you enters the mosque"," let him pray two rakʿahs before he sits.\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 433]"""
"4923","(4923) Abū al-Qāsim Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣaqr ibn ʿAbd al-Mujīb in Baghdad informed us — Daʿlaj ibn Aḥmad ibn Daʿlaj al-Sijistānī informed us — Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Zuhayr ibn Abī Khāld al-Ḥalwānī in Ḥalwān informed us — Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Hind informed us — from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr — from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym, who was a man of dignity, that he heard Abū Qatādah saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When one of you enters the mosque"," let him not sit until he has prayed two rakʿahs.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"4924","(4924) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Aḥmad Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān in Marw informed us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Bartī informed us — al-Qaʿnabī informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd informed us — from Ibn Shihāb — from Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ — from ʿItbān ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him in his house, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not sit until he said: \Where would you like me to pray in your house?\"" He said: So I indicated to him a place. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the takbīr"," and we lined up behind him"," and he prayed two rakʿahs. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī similarly. Others added in it from Ibrāhīm: \""Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr came to me after the day had grown hot.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 414]"""
"4925","(4925) And Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us — Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us — al-Fāriyābī informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khālid informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd informed us — from Ibn Shihāb — from Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī — from ʿItbān ibn Mālik, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \My eyesight has failed"," and the flood comes and obstructs me between myself and the mosque of my people. If you would be so pleased"," O Allah's blessings upon you"," to come and pray in my house at a place I could take as a prayer space","\"" he said: \""I shall do so.\"" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr came to me the next day after the day had grown hot"," and he sought permission and I granted him permission. He did not sit until he said: \""Where would you like me to pray for you from your house?\"" I indicated to him the place in which I would like him to pray. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood"," said the takbīr"," and we lined up behind him"," and he prayed two rakʿahs for us. Al-Bukhārī extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm from his father"," at greater length"," and included these words. And Muslim extracted it from another route from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. In meaning"," in the one before it.]"""
"4926","(4926) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us — Abū al-Naḍr informed us — Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Hudbah informed us — Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah from Thābit, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us, and there was no one present except me, my mother, and my maternal aunt Umm Ḥarām. He said: \Stand"," so that I may pray with you"," and that is outside the time of the prayer.\"" A man from the people said to Thābit: \""Then where did he place Anas?\"" He said: \""To his right.\"" He said: Then he invoked for all the people of the house with every good of this world and the Hereafter. My mother said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," this is your little servant — invoke Allah for him.\"" So I invoked for him with every good. The last thing I invoked for him was: \""O Allah"," increase his wealth and his children"," and bless him therein.\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Abū al-Naḍr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 3328]"""
"4927","(4927) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā informed us, Abū al-Ashʿath and Yaʿqūb both said, Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated from Ayyūb from ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from his father from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \I spent the night at the house of my aunt Maymūnah. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) rose to pray during the night"," so I stood to pray with him. I stood to his left"," so he took hold of my head and placed me to his right.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah.\n\nAnd we have narrated regarding the standing in prayer during the month of Ramaḍān from ʿĀʾishah and others that which indicates the permissibility of performing supererogatory prayers in congregation. And from Abū Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that which indicates its recommendation. And from Ibn Masʿūd and Ḥudhayfah regarding their praying with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that which indicates the same. And we have narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) from his practice that which indicates the same. And with Allah lies success.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 117]"""
"4928","(4928) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-Muthannā informed us, Musaddad informed us, Ismāʿīl —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, and the wording is his, he said: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Abū Yaʿlā informed us, Abū Khaythamah informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm, meaning Ibn ʿUlayyah, narrated from Ayyūb from Abū Qilābah from Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, who said: \We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while we were young men of similar age"," and we stayed with him for twenty nights. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was merciful and gentle. He thought that we had become homesick for our families"," so he asked us about those of our families whom we had left behind"," and we informed him. He said: 'Return to your families"," stay among them"," teach them"," and command them. When the time for prayer comes"," let one of you give the adhān and let the eldest of you lead you in prayer.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad"," and Muslim narrated it from Abū Khaythamah Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 602]"""
"4929","(4929) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr informed us, Zāʾidah informed us, al-Sāʾib ibn Ḥubaysh al-Kilāʿī narrated from Maʿdān ibn Abī Ṭalḥah al-Yaʿmarī, who said: Abū al-Dardāʾ said to me: \Where is your dwelling?\"" I said: \""In a village near Ḥimṣ.\"" Abū al-Dardāʾ said: \""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'There are not three people in a village or in the desert among whom the prayer is not established except that Satan has gained mastery over them. So hold fast to the congregation"," for indeed the wolf devours the one that strays.'\""\n\nAl-Sāʾib said: By \""the congregation\"" he meant the congregation in prayer.\n\n[Ḥasan. Ibn Ḥibbān 2101]"""
"4930","(4930) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us among the last, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us —\n\n(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿAbdullāh, meaning al-Qaʿnabī, narrated from Mālik from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \By the One in whose Hand is my soul"," I have considered commanding that firewood be gathered"," then commanding that the prayer be announced with the adhān"," then commanding a man to lead the people in prayer"," then going to men and burning their houses over them. By the One in whose Hand is my soul"," if any one of them knew that he would find a fat bone or two good meaty bones"," he would attend the ʿIshāʾ prayer.\""\n\nThis is the wording of al-Shāfiʿī's ḥadīth.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf and Ibn Abī Uways from Mālik"," and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah from Abū al-Zinād.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 618]"""
"4931","(4931) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥbūr, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dahhān, said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Indeed"," the heaviest prayers upon the hypocrites are the ʿIshāʾ prayer and the Fajr prayer. If they knew what reward is in them"," they would come to them even if they had to crawl on their knees. And I have considered commanding that the prayer be established"," then commanding a man to lead the people in prayer"," then setting out with me with men carrying bundles of firewood"," then going to a people who do not attend the prayer and burning their houses over them with fire.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Abū Muʿāwiyah"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth from al-Aʿmash.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4932","(4932) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad"," I have considered commanding my young men to prepare bundles of firewood for me"," then commanding a man to lead the people in prayer"," then burning houses upon those inside them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 651]"""
"4933","(4933) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Bāghandī informed us, Abū Nuʿaym informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated from Yazīd al-Aṣamm from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \I have considered commanding that the prayer be established"," then commanding young men with bundles of firewood"," and burning the houses of a people who hear the call but do not come to the prayer.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4934","(4934) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān from Yazīd al-Aṣamm from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \I have considered commanding my young men to gather bundles of firewood"," then setting out and burning the houses of a people who do not attend the Jumuʿah.\"" He said: \""The Jumuʿah.\"" And similarly it was narrated from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd. And that which the rest of the narrations indicate is that he expressed \""al-Jumuʿah\"" to mean \""al-Jamāʿah\"" (the congregation). And Allah knows best.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Its chain of narration as previously cited]"""
"4935","(4935) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us, Zuhayr narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ from ʿAbdullāh from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said to a people who were staying away from the Jumuʿah: \I have considered commanding a man to lead the people in prayer"," then burning upon men who stay away from the Jumuʿah.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 652]"""
"4936","(4936) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, al-Nufaylī informed us, Abū al-Malīḥ informed us, Yazīd ibn Yazīd informed us, Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm informed me, he said: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \I have considered commanding my young men to gather bundles of firewood"," then coming to a people who pray in their houses while there is no illness preventing them"," and burning them over them.\""\n\nI said to Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm: \""O Abū ʿAwf"," did he mean the Jumuʿah or something else?\"" He said: \""May my two ears be deaf if I did not hear Abū Hurayrah narrate it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)"," and he did not mention the Jumuʿah or anything else.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in its chain. Abū Dāwūd 549]"""
"4937","(4937) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: Asid ibn 'Asim narrated to us, saying: al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Sufyan, who said: Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Abu al-Sha'tha', who said: We were with Abu Hurayrah in the mosque when the mu'adhdhin called the 'Asr prayer. A man then left [the mosque]. Abu Hurayrah said: \As for this one"," he has indeed disobeyed Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ).\"" [Sahih. Muslim 655]"""
"4938","(4938) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn Salman, the two jurists, narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: 'Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sulaym al-Muharibi, from his father, who said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) was sitting in the mosque when he saw a man leaving after the adhan. He said: \As for this one"," he has indeed disobeyed Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar"," from Sufyan. [Sahih. Muslim 655]"""
"4939","(4939) Abu Sa'id Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Isfara'ini narrated to us, saying: Abu Bahr al-Barbahari narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah al-Aslami narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \No one leaves the mosque after the call [to prayer] except a hypocrite"," unless a man leaves for his need while intending to return to the mosque.\"" [Da'if. Malik 385]"""
"4940","(4940) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: al-'Abbas al-Duri narrated to us, saying: Qarad Abu Nuh narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Adiyy ibn Thabit, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \Whoever hears the call [to prayer] and does not respond"," there is no prayer for him"," except due to [a valid] excuse.\""\n\nAnd similarly did Hushaym ibn Bashir narrate it from Shu'bah. And the group narrated it from Shu'bah"," as a mawquf narration from Ibn 'Abbas. And Magha' al-'Abdi narrated it from 'Adiyy ibn Thabit as a marfu' narration. And it was narrated from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari both as a musnad and a mawquf narration"," and the mawquf is more correct. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Abu Dawud 551]"""
"4941","(4941) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas, who is al-Asamm, narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, saying: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from 'Adiyy ibn Thabit al-Ansari, who said: 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: \Whoever hears the call [to prayer] and does not respond — he does neither intend any good nor is any good intended for him.\"" [Sahih li ghayrihi. 'Abd al-Razzaq 1917]"""
"4942","(4942) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, saying: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Za'idah narrated to us, saying: Abu Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, saying: 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque.\"" [Da'if. 'Abd al-Razzaq 1915]"""
"4943","(4943) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: Asid ibn 'Asim narrated to us, saying: al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Sufyan, who said: Abu Hayyan narrated to us, from his father, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque.\"" It was said to him: \""Who is the neighbor of the mosque?\"" He said: \""The one whom the mu'adhdhin can make hear [the call].\"" [Da'if. See the preceding]"""
"4944","(4944) And with this chain of narration, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \Whoever hears the call [to prayer] from among the neighbors of the mosque"," and he is in good health with no [valid] excuse"," and he does not respond"," then there is no prayer for him.\""\n\nAnd it has been narrated from another chain as a marfu' narration"," but it is da'if. [Da'if. al-Daraqutni 1/419]"""
"4945","(4945) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated it to us, saying: Abu al-Fadl Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari narrated to us, saying: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdillah ibn al-Asamm narrated to us, from his uncle Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque.\"" [Munkar. al-Hakim 1/373]"""
"4946","(4946) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Fadl Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari narrated to us, saying: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdillah ibn al-Asamm narrated to us, from his uncle Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: A blind man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: \I have no one to lead me to the prayer.\"" So he asked him [the Prophet] to grant him permission to pray at home"," and he permitted him. When he turned to leave"," he called him and said: \""Do you hear the call to prayer?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Then respond [by coming].\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and others. [Sahih. Muslim 255]"""
"4947","(4947) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad: Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Thabit al-Saydalani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to us: Bishr ibn Hatim al-Raqi narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah from Abu Ishaq from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil from Ka'b ibn Ujrah: that a blind man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, \I hear the call to prayer"," but perhaps I may not find a guide. Shall I establish a mosque in my house?\"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him"," \""Do you hear the call?\"" He said"," \""Yes.\"" He said"," \""When you hear the call"," then go out.\"" (Tirmidhi) Abu Abd al-Rahim contradicted him"," and he narrated it from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah from Adiyy ibn Thabit from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil. [Sahih li-ghayrihi]"""
"4948","(4948) Abu Abd al-Hafiz Abu Abd Allah informed us: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated from Asim from Abu Razin from Amr ibn Umm Maktum, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, \O Messenger of Allah"," I am old and blind"," my house is far away"," and I have a guide who does not accommodate me. Do you find for me a concession that I may pray in my house?\"" He said"," \""Do you hear the call?\"" He said"," \""Yes.\"" He said"," \""I find no concession for you.\"" [Sahih. See what precedes it]"""
"4949","(4949) And Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Asim from Abu Razin: that Ibn Umm Maktum asked the Prophet (ﷺ) — the hadith. And Abu Sinan narrated it from Amr ibn Murrah from Abu Razin from Abu Hurayrah. [Sahih. Previously cited as no. 4946]"
"4950","(4950) And Abu Abd al-Hafiz Abu Abd Allah informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ammar al-Mawsili narrated to us: Qasim ibn Yazid al-Jarmi narrated to us: Sufyan — (H) And Abu Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Harun ibn Zayd ibn Abi al-Zarqa narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ibn Umm Maktum, who said: \O Messenger of Allah"," Madinah has many venomous creatures and predatory beasts.\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said"," \""Do you hear 'Hayya ala al-salah"," hayya ala al-falah'? Then respond (hayya hala).\"" Abu Abd Allah said: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih said: In the matter of this blind man regarding attendance at the congregational prayer"," there is nothing that indicates that his attendance is obligatory. For he granted Utbah ibn Malik"," who was blind"," permission to be absent from it. This indicates that his statement \""I find no concession for you\"" means: I find no concession for you that would attain the virtue of one who attends it. The Shaykh said: What confirms this interpretation is what... [Strong. Abu Dawud 553]"""
"4951","(4951) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Alawi, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Hamshadh al-Adl narrated to us: Abu Bakr Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-Mutawwi'i narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Mubarakhi narrated to us: Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated from al-Ala' ibn al-Musayyab from his father from Ibn Umm Maktum, who said: I said, \O Messenger of Allah"," I have a guide who does not accommodate me for these two prayers.\"" He said"," \""Which two prayers?\"" I said"," \""Isha and Subh.\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said"," \""If the one who stays behind from them knew what reward is in them"," he would come to them even if he had to crawl on his knees.\"" The Shaykh said: They differed regarding the name of Ibn Umm Maktum; it is said he was Abd Allah"," and it is said he was Amr. [Hasan]"""
"4952","(4952) Abu al-Qasim Abd al-Rahman ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah al-Huzafi al-Harbi narrated in the mosque of al-Harbiyyah in Baghdad: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Ishaq ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: Abu al-Umais narrated: I heard Ali ibn al-Aqmar narrating from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: \Whoever would love to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim"," let him guard these prayers where the call is made for them. For Allah has prescribed for your Prophet (ﷺ) the ways of guidance"," and indeed they are among the ways of guidance. Were you to pray in your houses as this one who stays behind prays in his house"," you would abandon the Sunnah of your Prophet (ﷺ). And were you to abandon the Sunnah of your Prophet (ﷺ)"," you would go astray. There is no man who purifies himself and performs the purification well"," then sets out towards one of these mosques"," but that Allah records for him a good deed for every step he takes"," raises him thereby a degree"," and erases thereby a sin from him. And I have seen us"," and no one stayed behind from it except a hypocrite whose hypocrisy was known. And indeed a man would be brought"," supported between two men"," until he was made to stand in the row.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn. [Sahih. Muslim 654]"""
"4953","(4953) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr narrated among others: They said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Malik narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Between us and the hypocrites is the attendance of Isha and Subh — they are unable to perform them.\"" Or something similar to that. Al-Shafi'i said: It is as though what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) intended by his concern was to burn the houses upon a people"," that what he said was concerning a people who stayed behind from the prayer of Isha out of hypocrisy"," and Allah knows best. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. al-Shafi'i 214]"""
"4954","(4954) Abu Abd al-Hafiz Abu Abd Allah and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from Yahya ibn Said from Nafi' from Ibn Umar, who said: \When we missed a man in the prayer of Isha and Fajr"," we would think ill of him.\"" [Sahih li-ghayrihi. al-Tabari in al-Kabir 13085]"""
"4955","(4955) Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated among others, and Ahmad ibn al-Hasan, and Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Malik narrated — (H) And Abu Abd al-Hafiz Abu Abd Allah informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us — (H) And Abu Abd al-Hafiz Abu Abd Allah said: Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Khazrajardi narrated to us: Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Khazrajardi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated: I read to Malik from Nafi' from Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The congregational prayer is superior to the prayer of the individual by twenty-seven degrees.\"" And in the narration of al-Shafi'i: \""It surpasses the prayer of the individual.\"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik"," and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Bukhari 619]"""
"4956","(4956) And Abu al-Hasan al-Alawi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Sharqi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn Abd Allah narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah from Nafi' from Ibn Umar that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The congregational prayer surpasses the prayer of the individual by twenty-seven.\"" [Sahih. See what precedes it]"""
"4957","(4957) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \The congregational prayer is superior to the prayer of any one of you praying alone by twenty-five degrees.\""\n\nThus al-Rabīʿ narrated it from al-Shāfiʿī in the book of al-Imāmah. And al-Muzanī and Ḥarmalah narrated it from al-Shāfiʿī"," from Mālik"," from Ibn Shihāb"," from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab"," from Abū Hurayrah"," from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," and this is the well-known version from Mālik. Among the ḥuffāẓ"," some have claimed that al-Rabīʿ erred in his narration"," and some have claimed that Mālik ibn Anas narrated several aḥādīth in the Muwaṭṭāʾ that he narrated outside the Muwaṭṭāʾ with different chains of narration. This is among them"," for Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated it from Mālik in a manner similar to the narration of al-Rabīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 289]"""
"4958","(4958) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥīrī al-Thiqah al-Maʾmūn narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib and ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān both said: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Mālik informed us, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \The virtue of a man's prayer in congregation over his prayer alone is twenty-five degrees.\"" As for the ḥadīth of Mālik from Ibn Shihāb… [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4959","(4959) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah ibn Qaʿnab narrated to us, from Mālik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The congregational prayer is superior to the prayer of any one of you praying alone by twenty-five degrees.\""\n\nAbū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read it back to Mālik"," and he mentioned it similarly.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4960","(4960) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us: Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, dictating: Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us, from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \The prayer of the congregation surpasses the prayer of a man praying alone by twenty-five degrees. And the angels of the night and the angels of the day gather at the Fajr prayer.\""\n\nAbū Hurayrah said: \""Recite"," if you wish: {And the Qurʾān of Fajr — indeed"," the Qurʾān of Fajr is ever witnessed} [al-Isrāʾ: 78].\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Shaybah.\n\nAnd we have narrated it previously from the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah"," from al-Zuhrī"," from Saʿīd and Abū Salamah"," from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 621]"""
"4961","(4961) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās informed us in Makkah: Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad al-Jumaḥī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: Aflaḥ, meaning Ibn Ḥumayd, narrated to us, from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, from Salmān al-Agharr, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The congregational prayer equals twenty-five prayers of one praying alone.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī."""
"4962","(4962) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn al-Hād, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Khabbāb, from Abū Saʿīd, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \The congregational prayer surpasses the prayer of one praying alone by twenty-five degrees.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf"," from al-Layth ibn Saʿd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 619]"""
"4963","(4963) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, from Sufyān.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us: al-Thawrī narrated to us, from ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmrah, from ʿUthmān, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever prays ʿIshāʾ in congregation"," it is as if he stood in prayer for half the night. And whoever prays ʿIshāʾ and al-Ṣubḥ (Fajr) in congregation"," it is as if he stood in prayer for an entire night.\""\n\nThis is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.\n\nAnd in the narration of Abū Nuʿaym: \""And whoever prays Fajr in congregation"," it is as if he stood in prayer for an entire night.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ"," from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.\n\nAnd Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Asadī narrated it from al-Thawrī"," and he attributed the standing of an entire night to Fajr alone"," as Abū Nuʿaym narrated it. And ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād similarly narrated it from ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm.\n\nMuslim narrated all of this"," except that he referred both narrations — the narration of Abū Aḥmad and ʿAbd al-Razzāq from al-Thawrī — back to the narration of ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 656]"""
"4964","(4964) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Simsār informed us: Ḥāmid ibn Maḥmūd al-Muqriʾ informed us: Isḥāq ibn Sulaymān al-Rāzī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān informed us, from Abū Isḥāq al-Hamdānī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Baṣīr, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the ʿIshāʾ prayer, then he noticed some men were absent and said: \Is So-and-so present?\"" It was said: \""No.\"" Then he said: \""Is So-and-so present?\"" It was said: \""No.\"" Then he said: \""Is So-and-so present?\"" They said: \""No.\"" He said: \""Indeed"," these two prayers\"" — meaning the ʿIshāʾ and Fajr prayers — \""are among the heaviest of prayers upon the hypocrites. And if they knew what reward lies therein"," they would come to them even crawling. And indeed"," the prayer of a man with another man is more purifying than his prayer alone"," and his prayer with two men is more purifying than his prayer with one man"," and the more numerous they are"," the more beloved it is to Allah. And indeed"," the first row is like the rows of the angels.\""\n\nIt has also been narrated from Abū Isḥāq"," from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Baṣīr"," from his father"," from Ubayy; and from Abū Isḥāq"," from Abī Baṣīr"," from Ubayy; and other than that has also been said. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 554]"""
"4965","(4965) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us. He said: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī wrote to me: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus informed us, from Thawr.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq al-Ṭālaqānī narrated to us: al-Walīd narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfaraʾīnī informed us: Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Barbahārī informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir and Thawr ibn Yazīd, from Yūnus ibn Sayf al-Kulāʿī, from Qubāth ibn Ashyam, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The prayer of two men"," one of them leading the other as imām"," is more purifying before Allah than the prayer of four praying individually. And the prayer of four"," one of them leading them as imām"," is more purifying before Allah than the prayer of eight praying individually. And the prayer of eight"," one of them leading them as imām"," is more purifying before Allah than the prayer of one hundred praying individually.\""\n\nThis is the ḥadīth of al-Walīd ibn Muslim. And ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus said in his narration from Thawr"," from Yūnus"," from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād"," from Qubāth. And al-Bukhārī similarly narrated it in al-Tārīkh from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf"," from al-Walīd"," from Thawr"," from Yūnus"," from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād"," from Qubāth. [Ḍaʿīf. Ḥākim 3/725]"""
"4966","(4966) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The virtue of a man's prayer in congregation over his prayer in his home and his prayer in his marketplace is twenty-five degrees. And there is no man who performs wuḍūʾ and performs it well"," then comes to the mosque"," being drawn to it only by the prayer"," but that for every step he takes a degree is raised for him and a sin is erased from him"," until he enters the mosque. And when he enters the mosque"," he is considered to be in a state of prayer so long as he remains waiting for the prayer. And the angels send blessings upon any one of you so long as he remains in the place where he prayed: 'O Allah"," have mercy on him; O Allah"," forgive him' — so long as he does not harm anyone therein and so long as he does not break his wuḍūʾ therein.'\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. And Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah"," both from Abū Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 465]"""
"4967","(4967) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Naẓīf al-Miṣrī reported to us in Makkah: Abū al-Faḍl al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Rāfiqī narrated to us: Hilāl ibn al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Hilāl narrated to us: my father narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both reported to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Dawrī narrated to us: Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqqī narrated from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah from ʿAdī ibn Thābit from Abī Ḥāzim al-Ashjaʿī from Abī Hurayrah who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever performs ablution in his house"," then walks to one of the houses of Allah"," the Most High"," in order to fulfil an obligation from among the obligations of Allah"," his steps — one of them erases a sin"," and the other raises a degree.\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥāfiẓ and the qāḍī. In the narration of the Miṣrī"," it reads: \""He fulfils an obligation from among the obligations of Allah — his two steps"," one of them erases from him a sin"," and the other raises for him thereby a degree.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr from Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 666]"""
"4968","(4968) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf reported to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to me: al-Aswad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When one of you leaves his house for his mosque"," one foot writes a good deed and the other erases a sin.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Nasāʾī 705]"""
"4969","(4969) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ reported to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī narrated to us. He said: I heard Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar. And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to me: Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated from al-ʿAlāʾ from his father from Abī Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Shall I not direct you to that by which Allah erases sins and by which He raises degrees?\"" They said: \""Yes"," O Messenger of Allah.\"" He said: \""Performing ablution thoroughly despite adverse conditions"," taking many steps towards the mosques"," and waiting for one prayer after another — that is al-Ribāṭ (steadfastness).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 251]"""
"4970","(4970) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh reported to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn reported to us —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Fawāris reported to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn reported to us: Abū Ghassān Muḥammad ibn Muṭarrif narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abī Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ) who said: \Whoever goes to the mosque in the morning and in the evening"," Allah prepares for him a place in Paradise every time he goes in the morning and in the evening.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī"," and Muslim narrated it from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah"," both from Yazīd ibn Hārūn.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 631]"""
"4971","(4971) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān reported to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Asfāṭī, meaning al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl, narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from Yazīd ibn al-Hād —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd and Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated from Ibn al-Hād from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from Abī Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abī Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \What do you think if there were a river at the door of one of you in which he bathed five times every day — would any of his filth remain?\"" I think he said: they said: \""No filth of his would remain.\"" He said: \""That is the likeness of the five prayers by which Allah erases sins.\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdullāh.\n\nIn the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān it reads: \""Such are the five prayers — they remove sins.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah"," and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 505]"""
"4972","(4972) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh reported to us: Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd al-Baghdādī narrated to us in Makkah: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated — (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb reported to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abī Sufyān from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The likeness of the five prayers is like a flowing river that passes by the door of one of you"," in which he bathes five times every day.\""\n\nAl-Ḥasan said: \""And what would that leave of filth?\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah.\n\nIn the ḥadīth of Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd"," he incorporated into the ḥadīth: \""So what would remain of his filth?\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4973","(4973) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī reported to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Tawbah narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn Ḥumayd narrated from Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith from al-Qāsim, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abī Umāmah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever leaves his house in a state of purification for an obligatory prayer"," his reward is like the reward of the pilgrim in iḥrām. And whoever goes out for the forenoon prayer (taṣbīḥ al-ḍuḥā)"," seeking nothing but that"," his reward is like the reward of the one performing ʿumrah. And a prayer following upon a prayer"," with no idle talk between them — that is recorded in ʿIlliyyīn.\""\n\n[Ḥasan for other than it. Previously cited as no. 4910]"""
"4974","(4974) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb reported to me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith reported from Abī ʿUshānah that he heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juḥanī narrating from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: \When a man performs ablution and then proceeds to the mosque"," mindful of the prayer"," his scribe or his scribes record for him ten good deeds for every step he takes towards the mosque. And the one who sits awaiting the prayer is like the one standing in devotion"," and he is recorded among the worshippers from the time he leaves his house until he returns.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/157]"""
"4975","(4975) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥalabī al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith al-Shīrāzī narrated to us — and he was trustworthy, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Dāwūd used to praise him — Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad al-Tamīmī and Abū Ghassān al-Madanī narrated from Abī Ḥāzim from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Give glad tidings of complete illumination on the Day of Resurrection to those who walk in darkness to the mosques.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Ibn Khuzaymah 1499]"""
"4976","(4976) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān reported to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawiyah narrated to us: Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Sūsī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mathannī al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl al-Kaḥḥāl narrated something similar —\n\n(Ḥ) And the Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān reported to us by dictation: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Harawī reported to us: Abū al-Mathannī Muʿādh ibn al-Mathannī narrated to me: Dāwūd ibn Sulaymān, the muʾadhdhin of the mosque of Thābit al-Bunānī, narrated to me: my father Sulaymān ibn Muslim narrated from Thābit ibn Aslam al-Bunānī from Anas ibn Mālik from the Prophet (ﷺ) who said: \Give glad tidings of complete illumination on the Day of Resurrection to those who walk in the darkness of night to the mosques.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Ibn Mājah 781]"""
"4977","(4977) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Bāghandī informed us, al-Anṣārī informed us, Ismāʿīl al-Kaḥḥāl narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Aws from Buraydah al-Aslamī from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \Give glad tidings of complete light on the Day of Resurrection to those who walk in darkness to the mosques.\"" Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from the ḥadīth of al-Kaḥḥāl. [Ṣaḥīgh for others. Abū Dāwūd 4756]"""
"4978","(4978) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī informed us, Abū Usāmah informed us, Burayd ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated from his grandfather Abū Burdah from Abū Mūsā, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Indeed"," the person who receives the greatest reward in prayer is the one who has the farthest distance to walk to it"," and the one who waits for the prayer in order to perform it with the imām in congregation receives a greater reward than the one who prays it and then sleeps.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb"," and Muslim narrated it from Abū Kurayb and others from Abū Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 623]"""
"4979","(4979) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Daqīqī informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated from Abū ʿUthmān from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: There was a man—I do not know anyone among the people of Madīnah who prayed the qiblah farther from the mosque than he was—yet he used to attend the prayers with the Prophet ﷺ. It was said to him: \Why don't you buy a donkey and ride it during the scorching heat and the darkness?\"" He said: \""By Allah"," I would not like my house to be adjacent to the mosque.\"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed of that"," so he asked him. He said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," so that my footsteps may be recorded"," and my walking to my family"," and my coming and going\""—or as he said. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""Allah will grant you all of that"," and He will grant you what you have intended altogether\""—or as he said. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Sulaymān al-Taymī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 663]"""
"4980","(4980) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Saʿdī informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Anas ibn Mālik that Banū Salamah intended to relocate their homes closer to the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked that Madīnah be left deserted, so he said: \O Banū Salamah"," will you not count your footsteps?\"" They said: \""Yes","\"" and they remained where they were. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ḥumayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1788]"""
"4981","(4981) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us, al-Muʿtamir said: I heard Kahmas narrating from Abū Naḍrah from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: Banū Salamah intended to move closer to the mosque, and the lands were vacant. The Prophet ﷺ was informed of that, so he said: \O Banū Salamah"," stay in your homes"," for your footsteps will be recorded.\"" He said: \""So they remained and said: 'We are not pleased that we had relocated.'\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿĀṣim ibn al-Naḍr from al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"4982","(4982) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Musaddad informed us, Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn Abī Dhiʿb from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mihrān from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \The farther one is from the mosque"," the greater the reward.\"" [Ḥasan for others. Abū Dāwūd 556]"""
"4983","(4983) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī informed us, Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ informed me, al-Ḥārith, meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mihrān, the freedman of Abū Hurayrah, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The most beloved lands to Allah are its mosques"," and the most hated lands to Allah are its marketplaces.\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf and Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 671]"""
"4984","(4984) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās in Makkah informed us, Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Jumaḥī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭālaqānī informed us, Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib from Muḥārib ibn Dithār from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \O Messenger of Allah"," which of the lands is best?\"" He said: \""I do not know.\"" He said: \""Which of the lands is worst?\"" He said: \""I do not know.\"" Then Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came"," and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \""O Jibrīl"," which of the lands is best?\"" He said: \""I do not know.\"" He said: \""Which of the lands is worst?\"" He said: \""I do not know.\"" He said: \""Ask your Lord.\"" Jibrīl then recoiled with such a start that Muḥammad ﷺ nearly fainted from it. He said: \""I will not ask Him about anything.\"" Allah"," Glorified be He"," said to Jibrīl (peace be upon him): \""Muḥammad asked you which of the lands is best"," and you said"," 'I do not know","' and he asked you which of the lands is worst"," and you said"," 'I do not know.' So inform him that the best of lands are the mosques and the worst of lands are the marketplaces.\"" [Ḥasan for others. Ibn Ḥibbān 1599]"""
"4985","(4985) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik. (Ḥ) And Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī informed us, Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad ibn Bishr al-Isfarāyinī informed us, Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAqīl al-Bayhaqī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read to Mālik from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \One of you remains in prayer as long as the prayer detains him"," and nothing prevents him from returning to his family except the prayer.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah and others"," and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 627]"""
"4986","(4986) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, dictating from his book, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ informed us, Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥanafī informed us, Qurrah ibn Khālid said: We waited for al-Ḥasan, and he was delayed in coming. Then he came and said: \Our neighbors invited us.\"" Then he said: Anas said: \""We waited for the Prophet ﷺ one night until it was nearly half the night. He was informed of that"," so he came and prayed for us"," then he addressed us and said: 'Indeed"," the people have prayed and gone to sleep"," and you will remain in prayer as long as you wait for the prayer.'\"" Al-Ḥasan said: \""And a people will remain in goodness as long as they wait for goodness.\"" Qurrah said: \""This is from the ḥadīth of Anas from the Prophet ﷺ.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 811]"""
"4987","(4987) Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmash al-Faqīh narrated to us: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī informed us: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Marwazī narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥalīm ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Maymūn al-Ṣāʾigh in Marw informed us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Khubayb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Seven shall be shaded by Allah on the Day of Resurrection under His shade"," on a day when there shall be no shade except His shade: a just leader; a youth who grew up in the worship of Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic; a man who remembered Allah in solitude and his eyes overflowed with tears; a man whose heart was attached to the mosque; two men who loved one another for the sake of Allah; a man whom a woman of high status and beauty invited to herself"," but he said: 'I fear Allah'; and a man who gave charity in secret such that his left hand did not know what his right hand bestowed.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sallām from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak"," and Muslim narrated it through another chain from ʿUbayd Allāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 629]"""
"4988","(4988) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed you, and he said: And Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Aṣbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me from Darrāj, who narrated to him from Abū al-Haytham, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When you see a man frequenting the mosque"," then bear witness to his faith.\"" Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," said: \""Indeed"," the mosques of Allah are only maintained by one who believes in Allah and the Last Day\"" [al-Tawbah: 18].\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Tirmidhī 2617]"""
"4989","(4989) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Dallawayh al-Daqqāq narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar ibn Manīʿ narrated to us: Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us: Ṣāliḥ al-Murrī narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Indeed"," the inhabitants of the houses of Allah are the people of Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic.\""\n\nṢāliḥ al-Murrī is not strong (in narration). [Munkar. Abū Yaʿlā 3606]"""
"4990","(4990) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: Ṣadaqah ibn Khālid narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī ʿĀtikah al-Azdī narrated from ʿUmayr ibn Hānī al-ʿAnasī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever comes to the mosque for a purpose"," that is his share (of reward).\""\n\n[Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 472]"""
"4991","(4991) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā al-Muṭarriz narrated to us: Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Marwān narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, and his wording is this: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Aṣamm narrated from his uncle Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: A blind man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: \I have no one to lead me to the prayer.\"" He asked him to grant him permission to pray at home"," and he permitted him. When he turned to leave"," he called him and said: \""Do you hear the call to prayer?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Then respond (by coming to the mosque).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah"," Suwayd"," and Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Previously cited under number 4946]"""
"4992","(4992) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was the best of people in character. Sometimes the prayer would come upon him while he was in our house, and he would order the mat beneath him to be swept, then sprinkled with water, then he would stand and we would stand behind him and he would lead us in prayer. He said: Their mat was made of palm branches.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān and Abū al-Rabīʿ from ʿAbd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 659]"
"4993","(4993) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah narrated from Ḥumayd, from Anas, from Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the Maghrib prayer during his illness in his house, wrapped in a single garment, and he recited {Wa al-Mursalāt} [al-Mursalāt: 1]. He did not pray any prayer after that until he was taken (in death).\n\n[Strong (Qawī). Nasāʾī 985]"
"4994","(4994) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us: Mūsā ibn Dāwūd narrated to us, and he mentioned it.\n\n[This is another chain of narration for the preceding hadith.]"
"4995","(4995) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad and ʿAlqamah, who both said: We came to ʿAbd Allāh in his house. He said: \Did these people pray behind you?\"" We said: \""No.\"" He said: \""Stand and pray.\"" And he mentioned the hadith regarding his prayer with them.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb from Abū Muʿāwiyah. And the hadith of al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Anas on this matter has preceded in the chapter on the dislike of delaying the ʿAṣr prayer"," and we shall narrate"," if Allah the Most High wills"," the saying of the Prophet (ﷺ): \""A man should not lead prayer in his house except with his permission.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 534]"""
"4996","(4996) Abū Manṣūr ʿAbd al-Qāhir ibn Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah, and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥamdān al-Fārisī, among others, said: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Najayd al-Sulamī narrated to us: Abū Muslim narrated to us: al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Sulaymān narrated from Abū Naḍrah that Abū Saʿīd, a freedman or slave of the Anṣār, invited Abū Dharr, Ḥudhayfah, and Ibn Masʿūd. When the time for prayer came, Abū Dharr stepped forward to lead them in prayer. Ḥudhayfah said to him: \Step back"," O Abū Dharr.\"" Abū Dharr said: \""Is that so"," O Ibn Masʿūd"," or O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" So he stepped back. Sulaymān said"," meaning: A man has the greatest right over his own house.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf]"""
"4997","(4997) Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — in Baghdad, informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from Musa al-Saghir from Habib ibn Abi Thabit: He prepared a meal and invited Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, Ibrahim al-Taymi, Salamat ibn Kuhayl, Dharr, and a number of prominent reciters. He then instructed Ibrahim al-Taymi to deliver a sermon to them. Subsequently, the time for prayer arrived, and they prayed in congregation in their homes without going out to the mosque. Then the food was brought to them. [Sahih]"
"4998","(4998) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Ishaq informed us: Abu al-Muthanna narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us: Khalid narrated from Abu Qilabah from Malik ibn al-Huwairith that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When the time for prayer arrives"," give the adhan"," then give the iqamah"," and let the eldest among you lead you in prayer.\"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih; Bukhari 604]"""
"4999","(4999) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz also informed me: Abu al-Walid narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated from Khalid al-Hadha' from Abu Qilabah from Malik ibn al-Huwairith, who said: \I came to the Prophet ﷺ along with a companion of mine. When we intended to depart from him"," he said to us: 'When the time for prayer arrives"," give the adhan"," then give the iqamah"," and let the eldest among you lead you in prayer.'\"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih; Muslim 674]"""
"5000","(5000) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastawayh narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu Zayd — Sa'id ibn al-Rabi' and Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal — both narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated, he said: informed me —\n\n(And Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us: Abu Ishaq narrated, he said: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Basir narrating from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the morning prayer and then said: 'Is so-and-present?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Is so-and-so present?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Indeed"," these two prayers — meaning the 'Isha and the Fajr — are the most burdensome upon the hypocrites. Had they known what reward lies in them"," they would have come to them even crawling. The first row is like the row of the angels. Had you known its virtue"," you would have hastened to it. The prayer of a man with another man is more meritorious than his prayer alone"," and his prayer with two men is more meritorious than his prayer with one man. The more numerous the congregation"," the more beloved it is to Allah"," the Almighty and Majestic.'\""\n\nThus did a group narrate it from Shu'bah. And likewise did Sufyan al-Thawri"," Ibrahim ibn Tahman"," Isra'il ibn Yunus"," and a group narrate from Abu Ishaq. And Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated from Abu Ishaq from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Basir from his father from Ubayy. [Da'if; previously referenced as no. 4964]"""
"5001","(5001) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed him: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated to us: Abu Ishaq narrated to me: 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Basir from his father, who said: \I came to Madinah and met Ubayy ibn Ka'b.\"" And likewise did Khalid ibn Maymun and a group narrate from Abu Ishaq. [Da'if; previously referenced as no. 4964]"""
"5002","(5002) And 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Shu'bah from Abu Ishaq from Abi Basir from Ubayy ibn Ka'b — Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: informed us al-Hasan ibn Halim: informed us Abu al-Muwajjih: informed us 'Adban: informed us 'Abd Allah, and he mentioned it. And likewise did Jarir ibn Hazim and a group narrate from Abu Ishaq. And Abu al-Ahwas narrated from Abu Ishaq from al-'Ayzarith ibn Hurayth from Abi Basir."
"5003","(5003) Informed us Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us: Abu al-Ahwas narrated from Abu Ishaq from al-'Ayzar ibn Hurayth from Abi Basir, who said: \Ubayy said...\"" And Yahya ibn Sa'id and Khalid ibn al-Harith and Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated from Shu'bah from Abu Ishaq"," and they mentioned that Abu Ishaq heard from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Basir and from his father."""
"5004","(5004) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id and Khalid ibn al-Harith both narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Abu Ishaq from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Basir from his father, who said: Abu Ishaq said: \And I heard it from him and from his father. I heard Ubayy ibn Ka'b saying: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the morning prayer one day and mentioned the hadith.'\"" [Da'if; see what preceded it]"""
"5005","(5005) And Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Abu Ishaq from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Basir from his father, who said: Shu'bah said: Abu Ishaq said: \I heard it from him and from his father.\"" He said: \""I heard Ubayy ibn Ka'b saying: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ...'\"" and he mentioned a hadith similar to that of Sa'id ibn al-Rabi'."""
"5006","(5006) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih said: I heard al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati saying: I heard 'Ali ibn al-Madini saying: \Abu Basir and Ibn Abi Basir both heard the hadith from Ubayy ibn Ka'b together.\"" And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq said: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Madini saying: Muhammad ibn Yahya said"," in the narration of Khalid ibn al-Harith and Yahya ibn Sa'id"," it is an indication that these narrations are preserved — those who narrated from his father and those who did not — but as for the hadith of Abu al-Ahwas"," I know not how it is."""
"5007","(5007) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us —\n\nAND Muʿammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Aḥmad: Bakr ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣayrafī in Marw informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Khaythamah informed us — Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us — they both said: Wahb ibn Khālid narrated to us from Sulaymān al-Aswad from Abī al-Mutawakkil al-Nājī from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī that the Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying alone and said: \Is there no man who would give charity to this one and pray with him?\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 574]"""
"5008","(5008) Abū Aḥmad: al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlūshā al-Asadābādhī therein informed us — Abū Bakr: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥamdān, that is al-Qaṭīʿī, informed us — Abū ʿAlī: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us — Abū Zakariyyāʾ, meaning Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq, informed us — ʿUlaylah ibn Badr informed us from his father from his grandfather from Abī Mūsā al-Ashʿarī who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Two or more constitute a congregation (jamāʿah).\"" A group narrated it similarly from ʿUlaylah"," and he is al-Rabīʿ ibn Badr"," and he is weak (ḍaʿīf). And Allah knows best.\n\nIt has also been narrated from another weak chain. [Very Weak. Ibn Mājah 972]"""
"5009","(5009) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — Abū al-ʿAbbās: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥajarī al-Kūfī informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣalt informed us — Saʿīd ibn Zarbī informed us — Thābit informed us from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \A man has the greatest right to the front of his mount"," and a man has the greatest right to the front of his bed.\"" And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""Two are a congregation"," three are a congregation"," and whatever is more is a congregation.\"" [Weak]"""
"5010","(5010) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Naṣr: Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Faqīh in Bukhārā informed us — Abū ʿIṣmah: Sahl ibn al-Mutawakkil al-Bukhārī informed us — al-Qaʿnabī: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah informed us —\n\nAND Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah informed us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad informed us from Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Ṭaḥlāʾ, from Muḥsin ibn ʿAlī from ʿAwf ibn al-Ḥārith from Abī Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever performs his ablution and excels in it"," then goes out and finds that the people have already prayed"," Allah will grant him a reward equal to that of the one who prayed and attended it"," and that will not diminish anything from their reward.\"" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 564]"""
"5011","(5011) And Abū ʿAlī informed us — Abū Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh ibn ʿAbbād al-ʿAnbarī informed us — Abū ʿAwanah informed us from Yaʿlī ibn ʿAṭāʾ from Maʿbad ibn Hurmuz from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib who said: Death approached a man from the Anṣār, and he said: \I shall narrate to you a ḥadīth which I shall not narrate to you except out of hope for reward (iḥtisāban). I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: 'When one of you performs his ablution and excels in it"," then goes out for the prayer"," he does not raise his right foot except that Allah records a good deed for him"," and he does not place his left foot except that Allah erases a sin from him. Let him come close or go far. If he comes to the mosque and prays in congregation"," he is forgiven. If he comes to the mosque and they have prayed part of it and part remains"," he prays what he catches and completes what remains"," and it is thus. If he comes to the mosque and they have already prayed"," he completes the prayer"," and it is thus.'\"" [Ḥasan for other than its reason. Abū Dāwūd 563]"""
"5012","(5012) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us — Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us — Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī informed us — ʿAffān informed us — Wahb ibn Khālid informed us — Sulaymān al-Aswad informed us from Abī al-Mutawakkil al-Nājī from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī who said: A man entered the mosque after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed, and he said: \Is there no man who would give charity to this one and pray with him?\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 5007]"""
"5013","(5013) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said — Abū al-ʿAbbās: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed us from Saʿīd, that is Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Sulaymān al-Nājī from Abī al-Mutawakkil from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Who will trade (i.e."," give charity) upon this?\"" So a man stood and prayed with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 5007]"""
"5014","(5014) Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad informed us — Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī informed us — Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī informed us — Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us — Hushaym informed us — Khuṣayb ibn Zayd informed us regarding this report that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and prayed with him, having already prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. [Weak. Ibn Abī Shaybah 6660]"
"5015","(5015) Abū Saʿīd al-Isfīrāʾīnī informed us — Abū Baḥr al-Barbahārī informed us — Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us — al-Ḥumaydī informed us — Abū ʿAbd al-Ṣamad al-ʿAmmī informed us — al-Jaʿd, Abū ʿUthmān al-Yashkurī, who said: We prayed the morning prayer in the mosque of Banī Rifāʿah and sat down, and Anas ibn Mālik came with approximately twenty of his young companions. He said: \Have you prayed?\"" We said: \""Yes.\"" He ordered some of his young companions"," and he gave the adhān and the iqāmah"," then he led them in prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 7094]"""
"5016","(5016) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhb in Wāsiṭ informed us — Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us from Sufyān from Yūnus from Abī ʿUthmān who said: Anas came to us after we had prayed, and he gave the adhān and the iqāmah and prayed with his companions. And from Yūnus from al-Ḥasan that he disliked it.\n\nThe Shaykh said: The dislike of al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī is understood to apply to a situation in which the congregation would cause division among the people after they have already prayed. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 3418]"
"5017","(5017) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us orally: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: I read to Mālik from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The adhān for ṣalāh was called on a cold and windy night, and he said: \Pray in your dwellings.\"" Then he said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command the mu'adhdhin"," when it was a cold and rainy night"," to say: 'Pray in your dwellings.'\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā.\n\nAnd in the ḥadīth of al-Shāfiʿī"," he said: from Ibn ʿUmar that he called the adhān"," and the remainder is the same.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik"," and it was narrated by Muslim from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 635]"""
"5018","(5018) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ: that Ibn ʿUmar called the adhān for ṣalāh on a cold and windy night, then said at the end of his call: \Pray in your dwellings"," pray in your dwellings","\"" for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command the mu'adhdhin"," when it was a cold night"," or a rainy night"," or a windy night during a journey"," to say: \""Pray in your dwellings.\""\n\nBoth al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"5019","(5019) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us: my father narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that he called the adhān for ṣalāh on a cold, windy, and rainy night, and said at the end of his adhān: \Pray in your dwellings.\"" Then he said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command the mu'adhdhin"," when it was a cold night or a rainy night during a journey"," to say: 'Pray in your dwellings.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another chain from ʿUbayd Allāh.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the preceding.]"""
"5020","(5020) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us: Abū ʿAwn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us: ʿAbdān informed me: my father narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Ayyūb from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, during a journey on a dark and windy night, or a dark and cold night, or a dark and rainy night, had his caller announce: \Pray in your dwellings.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously narrated under no. 5017.]"""
"5021","(5021) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Nufaylī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would announce that in Madīnah on a rainy night and a cold morning.\n[Munkar. Abū Dāwūd 1064]"
"5022","(5022) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us: Abū Khaythamah narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū Khaythamah narrated from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey and it rained upon us, so he said: \Let whoever wishes among you pray in his dwelling.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 698]"""
"5023","(5023) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us: Khālid narrated from Abū al-Malīḥ from his father, who said: On the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, rain befell us that did not even wet the soles of our sandals, so the caller of the Prophet ﷺ announced: \Pray in your dwellings.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Nasāʾī 854]"""
"5024","(5024) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbīdah al-Bāhilī narrated to us: Abū al-Malīḥ al-Hudhalī narrated from his father, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a light rain befell us, so the caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced while we were on a journey: \Let whoever wishes to pray in his dwelling"," do so.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"5025","(5025) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to me: Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb from Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī: that ʿItbān ibn Mālik used to lead his people in prayer and he was blind. He said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \O Messenger of Allah"," darkness and flooding occur"," and I am a man of impaired sight. So pray"," O Messenger of Allah"," in my house at a place that I may take as a place of prayer.\"" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and said: \""Where would you like me to pray?\"" He pointed to a place in the house"," and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed there.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously narrated under no. 4924.]"""
"5026","(5026) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl and Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar both said: Abū Ḥazrah Yaʿqūb ibn Mujāhid narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAtīq from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \None of you should pray in the presence of food"," nor while resisting the two foul things—urination and defecation.\""\n\nIbn Abī Maryam said: and al-Dāwardī narrated to me from Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAtīq from his father from ʿĀʾishah something similar.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 560]"""
"5027","(5027) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym narrated to us: Qutaybah narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Abū Ḥazrah al-Qāṣṣ narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī ʿAtīq from ʿĀʾishah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \None of you should pray while food is present"," nor while he is suppressing the two foul urges (urination and defecation).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others from Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the hadith before it.]"""
"5028","(5028) Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: Muḥammad ibn Kunāsah al-Asadī narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam from the Prophet (ﷺ): \When the time for prayer arrives and a man intends to relieve himself"," let him begin with relieving himself.\""\n\nAnd Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us: they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh informed us: al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us: Isḥāq ibn ʿĪsā ibn al-Ṭabbāʿ informed us: Mālik from Hishām from his father from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam that he once led his companions in prayer"," then went to relieve himself"," and upon returning he said: \""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'When one of you feels the need to relieve himself"," let him attend to it before the prayer.'\"" This is the wording of al-Shāfiʿī's hadith. In the hadith of al-ʿAlawī"," it is stated: from the Prophet (ﷺ): \""When the time for prayer arrives and a man intends to relieve himself"," let him begin with relieving himself.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 378.]"""
"5029","(5029) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us: Zuhayr narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam that he went out on Ḥajj or ʿUmrah, and the people were with him and he was leading them in prayer. One day, he announced the prayer for Fajr, then said: \Let one of you come forward (to lead the prayer)","\"" and he himself went to relieve himself. He said: \""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'When one of you intends to go to relieve himself and the prayer has been established"," let him begin with relieving himself.'\""\n\nMālik also narrated it from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 88.]"""
"5030","(5030) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: he said: Abū Dāwūd said: This hadith was narrated by Wuhaib ibn Khālid, Shuʿayb ibn Isḥāq, and Abū Ḍamrah from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from a man who narrated to him from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam. However, the majority of those who narrated it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah said as Zuhayr said."
"5031","(5031) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us by dictation: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam informed us: Bahz ibn Asad informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Idrīs al-Awdī from his father from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: \None of you should pray while he senses something of impurity (urination or defecation).\""\n\nA group narrated it from Shuʿbah. And Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated it from Shuʿbah and stopped it (as a mawqūf narration). [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"""
"5032","(5032) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-Ghaḍāʾirī in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb al-Mukharramī informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When the time for prayer arrives and the evening meal is present"," begin with the evening meal.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5147.]"""
"5033","(5033) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Shabīb al-Fāmī, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Ṣaydalānī all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb informed me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith and Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When the evening meal is placed and the time for prayer arrives"," begin with it before the Maghrib prayer.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn al-Aylī from Ibn Wahb from ʿAmr alone. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the hadith before it.]"""
"5034","(5034) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk and Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm both said: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When the evening meal arrives"," begin with the evening meal before you pray the Maghrib prayer"," and do not rush on account of your evening meal.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the one before it.]"""
"5035","(5035) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawīyah informed us: Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: Muʿallā ibn Asad informed us: Wuhaib narrated from Ayyūb from Abī Qilābah from Anas ibn Mālik from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \When the evening meal is placed and the prayer is established"," begin with the evening meal.\"" And from Ayyūb from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet (ﷺ) with something similar.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muʿallā ibn Asad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 5032.]"""
"5036","(5036) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ṭūsī al-Faqīh, Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Shādhyākhī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, who narrated from ʿĀʾishah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When the evening meal is placed and the prayer is established"," begin with the evening meal.\"" These are the words of Ibn ʿIyāḍ.\n\nMuslim and al-Bukhārī both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5148.]"""
"5037","(5037) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Muqr'i informed me — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abbad al-Makki narrated to us — Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us from Ya'qub ibn Mujahid, Abu Hazrah, from Abu 'Atiq — and he was 'Abd Allah ibn Abi 'Atiq — who said: I and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad were conversing in the presence of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and al-Qasim was a man of beautiful voice, and he was the son of an *umm walad*. 'A'ishah said to him: What is the matter with you that you do not speak as this son of my brother speaks? Indeed, I know from where you have been brought. This one was nurtured by his mother, and you were nurtured by your mother. Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad became angry and showed resentment toward her. When he saw that the table of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, had been brought with food, he stood up. She said: Where are you going? He said: I wish to pray. She said: Sit down. He said: I wish to pray. She said: Sit down, you audacious one! Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: \There is no prayer when food is present"," nor while one is resisting the two foul things (urine and faeces).\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in *al-Sahih* from Muhammad ibn 'Abbad"," and he said: from Ibn Abi 'Atiq. [Sahih. Muslim 560]"""
"5038","(5038) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us — Abu Usamah narrated to us — 'Ubayd Allah narrated to us from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When the evening meal is placed before one of you and the prayer is commenced"," begin with the evening meal"," and do not hasten until you have finished it.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in *al-Sahih* from 'Ubayd ibn Isma'il from Abu Usamah"," and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Bukhari 642]"""
"5039","(5039) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Musaddad and Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated — Ahmad said: Yahya narrated to me from 'Ubayd Allah — he said: Nafi' narrated to me from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \When the evening meal is placed before one of you and the prayer is commenced"," let him not stand up until he has finished.\"" Musaddad added: And 'Abd Allah"," when his evening meal was placed before him or when it arrived"," would not stand up until he had finished"," even if he heard the *iqamah*"," even if he heard the recitation of the imam. [Sahih. Abu Dawud 3757]"""
"5040","(5040) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to me — al-Hasan ibn al-'Abbas ibn Mihran al-Jammal narrated to us — Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us — Hafs ibn Mayasarah narrated to us from Musa ibn 'Uqbah from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When one of you is eating"," let him not hasten until he has fulfilled his need from it"," even if the prayer is commenced.\""\n\nWith this wording"," Zuhyr ibn Mu'awiyah and Wahb ibn 'Uthman narrated it from Musa ibn 'Uqbah"," and al-Bukhari alluded to their narration. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Ibn Khuzaymah 936]"""
"5041","(5041) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — 'Abdus ibn al-Husayn al-Simsar narrated to us — Abu Hatim narrated to us — al-Ansari narrated to me — Humayd narrated to us, saying: We were in the presence of Anas, and the muezzin called the *adhan* for Maghrib while the evening meal had arrived. Anas said: Begin with the evening meal. So we ate with him, then we prayed, and his evening meal was light. [Sahih]"
"5042","(5042) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu al-Nadr Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Faqih narrated to us — 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Salih and Yahya ibn Bukayr, the two Egyptians, narrated to us that Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to them from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shihab — he said: Ja'far ibn 'Umayr ibn Umayyah informed me that his father, 'Amr ibn Umayyah, informed him that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cutting a piece of meat from the shoulder of a sheep in his hand. Then he was called to the prayer, so he put it down along with the knife with which he had been cutting it, then he stood up and prayed and did not perform *wudu'*.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in *al-Sahih* from Yahya ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhri. [Sahih. Bukhari 205]"
"5043","(5043) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us — Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Maymun narrated to us from Ja'far from his father from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not delay the prayer for food nor for anything else. [Munkar. Abu Dawud 3758]"
"5044","(5044) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — 'Ali ibn Muslim al-Tusi narrated to us — Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us — al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, who said: I was with my father during the time of Ibn al-Zubayr, beside 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, when 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr said: We heard that he (i.e., the Prophet) would begin with the evening meal before the prayer. 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: Woe to you! What was their evening meal? Do you think it was like the evening meal of your father? [Jayyid. Abu Dawud 3759]"
"5045","(5045) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-Hafiz narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — Abu Ma'mar narrated to us — 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not come out to us for three days, and the prayer was commenced. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, went to lead the people in prayer. The Prophet ﷺ lifted the curtain, and we never saw a sight more wonderful to us than when the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became visible to us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to Abu Bakr to come forward, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ let the curtain fall, and he was not reached until he passed away.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in *al-Sahih* from Abu Ma'mar, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from 'Abd al-Warith. [Sahih. Bukhari 649]"
"5046","(5046) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us — Abu al-Yaman informed us — Shu'ayb narrated to us from al-Zuhri — he said: Anas ibn Malik al-Ansari, who had followed the Prophet ﷺ, served him, and accompanied him, informed me that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, used to lead them in prayer during the illness of the Prophet ﷺ in which he passed away. When it was Monday, and they were in rows for the prayer, the Prophet ﷺ lifted the curtain of the chamber, looking at us while he stood, as though his face were a page of a *mushaf*, then he smiled and laughed. We were concerned that we might become distracted in the prayer out of joy at seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Bakr stepped back on his heels to join the row, thinking that the Prophet ﷺ was coming out for the prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then gestured to us with his hand to complete your prayers. Then the Prophet ﷺ entered and let the curtain fall, and he passed away that same day.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in *al-Sahih* from Abu al-Yaman, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhri. [See the hadith before it]"
"5047","(5047) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Qutaybah narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from Abū Janāb, from Maghrāʾ al-ʿAbdī, from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever hears the muezzin and is not prevented from following him by any excuse"," his prayer which he has performed shall not be accepted from him.\"" They asked: \""And what is the excuse?\"" He said: \""Fear or illness.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ"," as reported by others. Previously referenced as no. 4940]"""
"5048","(5048) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdīy informed us; Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Dīnār narrated to us; Abū Rajāʾ — namely Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd — narrated it with the same wording, except that he said: \They asked: 'What is his excuse?'\"" He said: \""Fear or illness.\"" And he said: \""That prayer which he has performed.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ"," as reported by others. Ibn ʿAdīy in al-Kāmil 7/213]"""
"5049","(5049) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Yaḥyá narrated — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us by dictation; Abū al-Muthannā and Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, and the narration is that of Abū al-Muthannā; Musaddad narrated to us; Yaḥyá narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā both said: Yaḥyá ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh informed me; Nāfiʿ narrated from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said during the expedition of Khaybar: \Whoever eats from this tree"," let him not come to the mosques.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and Ibn al-Muthannā. And in the ḥadīth of Aḥmad: \""Let him not approach the mosques.\"" And it does not contain \""during the expedition of Khaybar\"" in the narration of Musaddad"," and he added — meaning garlic — and said: \""Let him not come to our mosque.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad"," and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 561]"""
"5050","(5050) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh — narrated to us by dictation; Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Ibn Numayr narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever eats from this herb — meaning garlic — let him not approach our mosque until its smell departs.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. It was also narrated by Anas ibn Mālik"," Abū Hurayrah"," and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 564]"""
"5051","(5051) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb, who said: We said to Anas ibn Mālik: \What did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say about garlic?\"" He said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \""Whoever eats from this tree"," let him not come near us"," and let him not pray with us.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad and others"," and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUlayyah from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 818]"""
"5052","(5052) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq and Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyá — and this is his ḥadīth — all said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever eats from this tree — meaning garlic — let him not harm us in our mosque.\"" And in the ḥadīth of Ibn Rāfiʿ: \""Let him not approach our mosque"," and let him not harm us with the smell of garlic.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 562]"""
"5053","(5053) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad in Baghdād narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Yaḥyá ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to me; ʿAṭāʾ narrated from Jābir, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \Whoever eats from this tree — garlic.\"" Then he said after garlic: \""and onion and leek — let him not come near us in our mosque"," for the angels are harmed by that which harms the human beings.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyá"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 564]"""
"5054","(5054) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — it was read to him — and Abū Muḥammad ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Mahdī al-Qushayrī al-Aṣamm verbatim, both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyá ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Hishām al-Dastuwāʾī narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade [eating] onion and leek. But need overcame us, so we ate from them. The Prophet ﷺ said: \Whoever eats from this foul tree"," let him not approach our mosque"," for the angels are harmed by that which harms the human beings.\"" Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5055","(5055) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from al-Shaybānī, from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit, from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh, from Ḥudhayfah — I think — from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: \Whoever spits in the direction of the Qiblah"," his spit will be placed between his eyes on the Day of Resurrection"," and whoever eats from this foul herb"," let him not approach our mosque.\"" Three times."""
"5056","(5056) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ informed me that Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever eats garlic or onion"," let him keep away from us"," or let him keep away from our mosque"," or let him sit in his house.\"" And a cooking pot containing green vegetables from the garden was brought to him"," and he detected a smell from it. He was asked and was informed of what vegetables it contained. He said: \""Bring it close to some of his companions who were with him.\"" When he saw that he disliked eating it"," he said: \""Eat"," for I converse with those whom you do not converse with.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr"," and Muslim narrated it from Abī al-Ṭāhir and Ḥarmalah"," all from Ibn Wahb. Al-Bukhārī said: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ said from Ibn Wahb: \""A badr was brought\"" — and Ibn Wahb said: meaning a dish containing green vegetables. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 817]"""
"5057","(5057) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us — and he mentioned it [i.e., the previous hadith with this chain of narration]."
"5058","(5058) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Saʿib ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate from some food"," he would send the remainder to Abū Ayyūb.\"" He said: \""He once sent him a dish from which he had not eaten"," because it contained garlic. Abū Ayyūb came to him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," is it ḥarām?' He ﷺ said: 'No"," but I dislike it because of its smell.'\"" He said: \""Then I also dislike what you dislike.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2053]"""
"5059","(5059) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawādah, that Abū al-Tujayb, the freedman of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd, narrated to him, that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated to him: \Garlic"," onions"," and leeks were mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and it was said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," the strongest of all of these is garlic — will you declare it ḥarām?' He ﷺ said: 'Whoever eats it"," let him not approach this mosque until its smell departs from him.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 3823]"""
"5060","(5060) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Ismāʿīl informed us; al-Jurayrī informed us, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: \We had scarcely conquered Khaybar when we came upon that vegetable — meaning garlic — and we ate from it a great deal"," and the people were hungry. Then we went to the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ detected the smell and said: 'Whoever has eaten anything from this foul tree"," let him not come near us in the mosque.' The people said: 'It has been forbidden! It has been forbidden!' This reached the Prophet ﷺ"," and he said: 'O people"," it is not for me to forbid what Allah has made ḥalāl"," but it is a tree whose smell I dislike.'\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid"," from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 565]"""
"5061","(5061) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Abū Hilāl al-Rāsibī, Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah, and others narrated to us, from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, from Abū Burdah, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who said: \I ate garlic during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," then I came to the mosque having missed one rakʿah. I joined them in the prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ detected its smell and said: 'Whoever eats from this foul tree"," let him not approach our place of prayer until its smell departs.' I completed my prayer"," and when I said the salām"," I said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," I adjure you by Allah to give me your hand.' He offered me his hand"," and I placed it inside my sleeve until I reached his chest and found it bandaged. He said: 'Indeed"," you have an excuse — or: I see for you an excuse.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/252]"""
"5062","(5062) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn al-Mundhir al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Baḥīr ibn Saʿd narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā said: Abū Dāwūd informed us; and Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Baḥīr, from Khālid, from Abū Ziyād Ḥayyān ibn Salamah, that he asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about onions, and she said: \The last food that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate contained onions.\"" Thus it is in the book: Ḥayyān — and they said the correct version is Khayyār. The Shaykh said: And in the narration of ʿĪsā it is Khayyār"," and likewise al-Bukhārī said in al-Tārīkh from Ḥaywah: Khayyār. [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd 3829]"""
"5063","(5063) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Ḥarafī in Baghdād narrated to us; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to me; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sālim narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd ibn ʿĀmir al-Zubaydī narrated to us; Rāshid ibn Saʿd informed us that Abū Rāshid narrated to him, tracing it back to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: \The Prophet ﷺ ate onions roasted in a pot on the Friday before his death.\"" [Munkar. al-Ṭabarānī in Musnad al-Shāmiyyīn 1859]"""
"5064","(5064) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Maʿdān ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, who said: \ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," delivered a sermon on Friday and mentioned the hadith. In it he said: 'O people"," you eat from two trees which I consider nothing but foul — this onion and garlic. I used to see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when he detected their smell from a man"," order him to be taken out to al-Baqīʿ. So whoever among you must eat them"," let him extinguish their smell by cooking.'\"" Muslim extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī and others"," from Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 569]"""
"5065","(5065) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; al-Jarāḥ, Abū Wakīʿ, narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, from Sharīk, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: \Eating garlic was prohibited"," except when cooked.\"" Abū Dāwūd said: \""Sharīk is Ibn Ḥanbal.\"" [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd 3827]"""
"5066","(5066) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Khālid ibn Mayṣarah, Abū Ḥātim — and he used to reside in Makkah — narrated to us, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrath, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ: \Whoever eats from these two trees"," let him not approach our mosque. And if you must eat them"," extinguish their smell by cooking.\"""""
"5067","(5067) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn al-Juʿfī narrated to us from Zāʾidah, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill and said: \Command Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\"" ʿĀʾishah said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," Abū Bakr is a tender-hearted man; whenever he stands in your place"," he will not be able to lead the people in prayer.\"" He said: \""Command Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer"," for you are indeed the companions of Yūsuf.\"" He said: So Abū Bakr led the people in prayer during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr"," from al-Ḥusayn al-Juʿfī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 663]"""
"5068","(5068) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill and his illness grew severe. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah."
"5069","(5069) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb narrated to us; Sufyān—(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, who heard Anas ibn Mālik say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell from a horse and his right side was grazed. We visited him during his illness, and he prayed sitting, so we prayed sitting. When he completed the prayer, he said: \The Imam is appointed only to be followed. When he says the takbīr"," say the takbīr; when he bows"," bow; when he says 'Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah","' say 'Rabbanā wa laka al-ḥamd'; and when he prays sitting"," pray sitting"," all of you.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī"," and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others"," from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 371]"""
"5070","(5070) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) rode a horse and was thrown from it, and his right side was grazed. He prayed one of the prayers sitting, and we prayed behind him sitting. When he finished, he said: \The Imam is appointed only to be followed. When he prays standing"," pray standing; when he bows"," bow; when he raises"," raise; when he says 'Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah","' say 'Rabbanā wa laka al-ḥamd'; and when he prays sitting"," pray sitting"," all of you.\"" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"5071","(5071) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us—among the last narrations—they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us—(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, from Mālik, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed in his house sitting, and a group of people prayed behind him standing. He gestured to them to sit. When he finished, he said: \The Imam is appointed only to be followed. When he bows"," bow; when he raises"," raise; and when he prays sitting"," pray sitting.\"" al-Shāfiʿī added in his narration: \""And he was unwell"," so he prayed sitting.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and others"," from Mālik"," and Muslim narrated it from another route from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 656]"""
"5072","(5072) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Khālid al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us; Bishr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us, from his father, from Abū al-Zinād—(Ḥ) And Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Sawwār narrated to us; Qutaybah narrated to us; al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said—and in the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The Imam is appointed only to be followed"," so do not differ from him. When he says the takbīr"," say the takbīr; when he bows"," bow; when he says 'Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah","' say 'Allāhumma Rabbanā laka al-ḥamd'; when he prostrates"," prostrate; and when he prays sitting"," pray sitting"," all of you.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū al-Yamān"," from Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 678]"""
"5073","(5073) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us—(Ḥ) And Abū Jaʿfar al-ʿAzāʾimī narrated to us; Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us. They both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd al-Ruʾāsī informed us, from his father, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said the takbīr, and Abū Bakr said the takbīr behind him so that we could hear. He saw us standing and gestured to us to sit. When he completed the prayer, he said: \You were about to do what the Persians and Romans do for their leaders. Follow your Imams: if they pray standing"," pray standing; and if they pray sitting"," pray sitting.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl. Dāwūd added in his ḥadīth: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the Ẓuhr prayer"," and Abū Bakr was behind him. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said the takbīr"," Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said the takbīr so that we could hear. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā"," and he narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Layth"," from Abū al-Zubayr"," who said in it: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) complained of illness"," and we prayed behind him while he was sitting. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 413]"""
"5074","(5074) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; al-Aʿmash narrated to us, from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was thrown from his horse onto the trunk of a palm tree, and his ankle was dislocated. He sat in a house belonging to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). We came to visit him and found him praying a voluntary prayer sitting, while we were standing. Then we came to him again and found him praying an obligatory prayer sitting. We stood, and he gestured to us, so we sat. Then he said: \Follow the Imam: if he prays sitting"," pray sitting; and if he prays standing"," pray standing. And do not do as the Persians do with their leaders.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"5075","(5075) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Ḍubaʿī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us; Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to me, from a man, from al-Shaʿbī, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said—(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mubashshir narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rabīʿah narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Jābir, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \No one shall lead the people in prayer sitting after me.\"" ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar said: No one narrated it other than Jābir al-Juʿfī"," and he is abandoned (matrūk). The ḥadīth is mursal"," and no proof is established by it. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ḥārith in his Musnad 147]"""
"5076","(5076) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: The one who cited this ḥadīth as evidence knows that there is no proof in it, and that it is not established because it is mursal, and because it is from a man from whom people have abandoned narration."
"5077","(5070) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu al-Nadr, the faqih, informed me — 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us.\n\n(And) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us — Zā'idah ibn Qudāmah narrated to us — Mūsā ibn Abī 'Ā'ishah narrated from — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utban, who said: I entered upon 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and said: \Will you not narrate to me concerning the illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?\"" She said: \""Indeed"," yes.\"" Then she mentioned the hadith in its full length.\n\nWe have already cited it in the Book of Purification"," up to her statement: \""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sent for Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\"" The messenger came to him and said: \""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) commands you to lead the people in prayer.\"" Abu Bakr — and he was a man of tender heart — said: \""'Umar"," lead the people in prayer.\"" 'Umar said: \""You are more deserving of that.\"" So Abu Bakr led the people in prayer during those days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) found some relief in himself and came out"," supported between two men"," one of them being al-'Abbas"," for the noon prayer (Salat al-Duhr)"," while Abu Bakr was leading the people. When Abu Bakr saw him"," he moved to step back"," but the Prophet (peace be upon him) gestured to him not to step back"," and said to the two men: \""Seat me beside him.\"" So they seated him beside Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). She said: Abu Bakr began to pray following the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) while standing"," and the people prayed following the prayer of Abu Bakr"," while the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was sitting.\n\n'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah said: I then entered upon 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas and said: \""Shall I not present to you what 'Ā'ishah narrated to me concerning the illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?\"" He said: \""Present it.\"" So I presented her hadith to him"," and he did not reject anything from it"," except that he said: \""Did she name for you the other man who was with al-'Abbas?\"" I said: \""No.\"" He said: \""That was 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him).\""\n\nAl-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it from Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yunus.\n\nIt has also been narrated from Shu'bah"," from Mūsā ibn Abī 'Ā'ishah"," in this hadith"," that Abu Bakr led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was in the row behind him. The excellent context of Zā'idah ibn Qudāmah's narration indicates his precision in memorization"," and that others did not preserve it as he did. For this reason"," al-Bukhari and Muslim (may Allah have mercy on them both) recorded it in their two Sahihs"," to the exclusion of the narration of those who differed from him. And similarly"," it has been narrated from another source from Ibn 'Abbas. [Sahih. Bukhari 655]"""
"5078","(5078) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us — Hisham ibn 'Ali narrated to us — Ibn Raja' informed us — Israil narrated from — Abi Ishaq from — Arqam ibn Shurahbil, who said: I traveled with Ibn 'Abbas from Madinah to Syria, and I asked him. So he mentioned the hadith concerning the illness of the Prophet (peace be upon him), up to his statement: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) found some relief in himself"," so he came out"," being supported between two men. When the people sensed it"," they said 'Subhan Allah' [Glory be to Allah]. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) began to step back"," but he [the Prophet] gestured to him with his hand: 'Stay in your place.'\"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) then began [reciting] the Qur'an from where Abu Bakr had left off. Abu Bakr was standing and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was sitting. Abu Bakr followed the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) [in prayer]"," and the people followed Abu Bakr. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) finished the prayer"," he became very heavy [in condition]"," so he came out"," being supported between two men"," and his legs were dragging along the ground. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) passed away"," and he did not leave a testament. And with the same meaning"," it was narrated from another source from 'Ā'ishah. [Sahih. Ahmad 1/356]"""
"5079","(5079) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq, the faqih, informed us — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah informed us. And Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us — both of them said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah informed us.\n\n(And) 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Ka'bi informed me — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah and Waki' narrated from — al-A'mash from — Ibrahim from — al-Aswad from — 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) became severely ill, Bilal came to inform him of the prayer, and he said: \Instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\"" She said: I said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," Abu Bakr is a tender-hearted man. When he stands in your place"," the people will not be able to hear him. If only you would instruct 'Umar.\"" He said: \""Instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\"" She said: I said to Hafsah: \""Say to him: Abu Bakr is a tender-hearted man. When he stands in your place"," the people will not be able to hear him. If only you would instruct 'Umar.\"" So she said it to him"," and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \""You are indeed like the women of Yusuf [i.e."," you are given to gossip]. Instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\"" She said: They instructed Abu Bakr"," and he led the people in prayer. She said: When he entered into the prayer"," the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) found some relief in himself. She said: He stood up"," being supported between two men"," and his legs were dragging along the ground. She said: When he entered the mosque"," Abu Bakr sensed his presence and moved to step back. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) gestured to him: \""Stand in your place.\"" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came and sat to the left of Abu Bakr. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to lead the people in prayer while sitting"," and Abu Bakr was standing — Abu Bakr followed the prayer of the Prophet (peace be upon him)"," and the people followed the prayer of Abu Bakr. [This is the wording of the hadith of Yahya ibn Yahya.]\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah"," and al-Bukhari narrated it from Qutaybah from Abu Mu'awiyah. [Sahih. Bukhari 681]"""
"5080","(5080) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us — Abu Yahya al-Ruyanī narrated to us — Ibrahim, that is, ibn Musa al-Farra', informed us — 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated from — al-A'mash, and he mentioned it with his chain of narration from — 'Ā'ishah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) fell ill with the illness in which he died, he gave permission for the prayer [to be announced]. She mentioned her story, without mentioning her statement to Hafsah, up to her statement: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came out"," and sat praying"," with Abu Bakr beside him"," and Abu Bakr would make the people hear [the takbir].\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh from 'Isa"," and 'Ali ibn Mushir narrated from al-A'mash"," and he said in it: \""The Prophet (peace be upon him) was leading the people in prayer"," and Abu Bakr was making them hear the takbir.\"" [Sahih. See what precedes it]"""
"5081","(5081) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Bayan and al-'Awdi both said — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from — Hisham ibn 'Urwah from — his father from — 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was ill, so he instructed Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) then found some relief, so he came and sat beside Abu Bakr. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) led Abu Bakr [in prayer] while he was sitting, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people [in prayer] while he was standing. [Sahih. Its meaning is in what has been stated]"
"5082","(5082) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us — my father narrated to us — Hisham narrated from — his father from — 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) instructed Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer during his illness, and so he used to lead them in prayer. 'Urwah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) then found some relief in himself, so he came out — and there was Abu Bakr leading the people. When Abu Bakr saw him, he stepped back, but the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) gestured to him: \As you are [stay as you are].\"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) sat beside Abu Bakr"," at his side. Abu Bakr was praying following the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)"," and the people were praying following the prayer of Abu Bakr.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr"," and al-Bukhari narrated it from Zakariyya ibn Yahya from 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr.\n\nThese narrations are agreed upon that the Prophet (peace be upon him) was the imam"," and that Abu Bakr and the rest of the people followed him. And it has been narrated that Abu Bakr was the imam and that the Prophet (peace be upon him) prayed behind him. [Sahih. Bukhari 651]"""
"5083","(5083) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi informed us — Abu Hamid al-Sharqi informed us — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahid, companion of Tha'lab, informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd Allah al-Narshi informed us — both of them said: Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from — Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind from — Abi Wa'il from — Masruq from — 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed in the illness in which he died, sitting, behind Abu Bakr. [The wording of both their narrations is the same.] [Sahih. See what has been stated]"
"5084","(5084) Abu 'Abd Allah informed us — Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq, the faqih, informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from — al-A'mash from — Ibrahim from — al-Aswad from — 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \Among the people are those who say that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was the one foremost before the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in the row"," and among them are those who say that the Prophet (peace be upon him) was the foremost.\""\n\nThus narrated al-Tayalisi from Shu'bah from al-A'mash"," and the narration of the majority from al'A'mash has been previously established with confirmation and authenticity"," and the narration of Masruq was uniquely reported by Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind from Abi Wa'il from him"," and there was disagreement concerning it. [Sahih. Ibn Khuzaymah 1618]"""
"5085","(5085) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us — al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated from — his father — Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind narrated from — Abi Wa'il from — 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she mentioned the story of the illness of the Prophet (peace be upon him) and his instruction of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the prayer. At the end of it, she said: \When Abu Bakr sensed the coming of the Prophet (peace be upon him)"," he wanted to step back"," but he gestured to him to stay firm.\"" He said: \""Then the Prophet (peace be upon him) was brought and was placed beside Abu Bakr\"" — or she said: \""in the row.\""\n\nThis contradicts the narration of Shababah from Shu'bah"," in both the chain of narration and the text. And something close to this text has been narrated from Shababah from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Previously cited under number 5082]"""
"5086","(5086) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us from the original of his book — Abu al-'Abbas al-A'ṣam narrated to us — Abu Umayyah, that is, al-Ṭarsusi, narrated to us — Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind narrated, and he said: I heard Abi Wa'il narrating from Masruq from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer during the illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was in the row. And thus narrated Badal ibn al-Muhabbar from Shu'bah. [Sahih. See what has been stated, similarly]"
"5087","(5087) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Fākihī narrated to us; Ibn Abī Masarra narrated to us; Badl ibn al-Muḥabbir narrated to us, and he mentioned it in the same manner as the narration of al-Ṭarsūsī from Shabāba.\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from Anas that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed behind Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him).\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed behind Abū Bakr once, that does not preclude the possibility that Abū Bakr prayed behind him on another occasion.\n\nThe Shaykh said: Mūsā ibn ʿUqba, in his work on the Maghāzī, held the view that Abū Bakr led one rakʿa of the Fajr prayer on Monday—the day on which the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) passed away. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) felt some relief in himself, so he came out and prayed one rakʿa with Abū Bakr. When Abū Bakr pronounced the salām, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) stood and completed the second rakʿa. It is therefore possible that this is the prayer intended by those who reported that he prayed behind Abū Bakr during his illness. As for the prayer that Abū Bakr led during the Prophet's illness, it is the Ẓuhr prayer on Saturday or Sunday, as has been narrated from ʿĀʾisha and Ibn ʿAbbās regarding the clarification of the Ẓuhr prayer. Thus, there is no contradiction between the two reports, and the first ḥadīth remains valid as evidence. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding ḥadīth.]"
"5088","(5088) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us, dictating; Mūsā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Shayba narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs and ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān narrated to us from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: I was presented before the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of Uḥud, and I was fourteen years old, so he considered me too young. I was presented before him again on the day of the Khandaq, and I was fifteen years old, so he granted me permission.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Shayba, and al-Bukhārī reported it through another chain from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2521]"
"5089","(5089) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us from Khālid, from Abī al-Ḍuḥā, from ʿAlī, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \The pen has been lifted from three: from the sleeper until he awakens"," from the child until he reaches puberty"," and from the insane until he regains his reason.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 4398]"""
"5090","(5090) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, Abū al-Shaykh, informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr ibn Abī ʀāṣim narrated to us; Hudba narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salama narrated to us from Qatāda, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Ṣafiyya bint al-Ḥārith, from ʿĀʾisha, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Allah does not accept the prayer of a menstruating woman unless she is wearing a head-covering (khimār).\"" Ibn Abī ʀāṣim said: By \""menstruation\"" he meant \""puberty.\""\n\nThe Shaykh said: This serves as an indication that the obligation is directed toward her when she reaches puberty through menstruation. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 641]"""
"5091","(5091) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Ḥarmala ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Sabra narrated to us from his uncle ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Sabra, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Teach the child Ṣalāh at the age of seven"," and strike him for it at the age of ten.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 494]"""
"5092","(5092) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ informed us; Sahl ibn Mihrān al-Daqqāq narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us; Sawwār ibn Dāwūd, Abū Ḥamza, narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Command the children to pray at the age of seven"," and strike them for it at the age of ten"," and separate them in their beds.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain]"""
"5093","(5093) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Hishām ibn Saʿd informed you, saying: Muʿādh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Juhanī narrated to me, saying: We came to him, and he said to his wife: \When should the child pray?\"" She said: \""Yes. A man among us used to narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that he was asked about that"," and he said: 'When he knows his right from his left"," then command him to pray.'\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 497]"""
"5094","(5094) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Abū al-ʿUmays informed us from al-Qāsim, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: \Instruct your children to be diligent in Ṣalāh"," and accustom them to goodness"," for indeed goodness is a matter of habit.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 9156]"""
"5095","(5095) Abū Ḥāzim ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAbdawī informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzayma informed us; Jamīl ibn al-Ḥasan al-Jahḍamī narrated to us; Makhlad ibn Yazīd narrated to us; Abū al-ʿUmays narrated to us from ʿAlī ibn al-Aqmar, from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \Instruct your children to be diligent in Ṣalāh"," and teach them goodness"," for indeed goodness is a matter of habit.\""\n\nJaʿfar ibn ʿAwn contradicted him"," narrating it from Abī al-ʿUmays from al-Qāsim"," from ʿAbd Allāh"," as a mursal narration. [Ḥasan in other than this chain]"""
"5096","(5096) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; al-Aʿmash informed us from ʿUmāra, from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: \Instruct your children to be diligent in Ṣalāh.\"" [Ḥasan in other than this chain. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 8755]"""
"5097","(5097) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Simsār narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Māhān al-Dīnawarī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; ʿĀmir ibn Abī ʿĀmir al-Khazzāz narrated to us; Ayyūb ibn Mūsā narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \A father does not bestow upon his child any gift better than good manners (adab).\""\n\nAyyūb ibn Mūsā is the son of ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ.\n\nA group also narrated it from ʿĀmir"," and it is mursal. Al-Bukhārī said: the hearing of his grandfather from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) is not established. [Weak. Tirmidhī 1952]"""
"5098","(5098) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿUthmān al-Ḥāṭibī said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar saying to a man: \Discipline your son"," for you will be questioned about your child — what manners you instilled in him and what you taught him. And he will be questioned about his dutifulness to you and his obedience to you.\"" [Ḥasan. The author narrated it in al-Shuʿab 8662]"""
"5099","(5099) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Abū Burdah:\n\nSaʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: \When I have educated my child"," married him off"," and enabled him to perform Ḥajj"," I have fulfilled his right. And my right upon him remains.\"" [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 26319]"""
"5100","(5100) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Abū Burdah:\n\nJābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh said: Muʿādh used to pray ʿIshāʾ (or al-ʿAtamah) with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), then return and lead his people in that prayer among Banū Salamah. He said: One night the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) delayed the ʿIshāʾ, so Muʿādh prayed with him, then returned and led his people. He began reciting Sūrat al-Baqarah. A man stepped aside and prayed alone. They said to him: \Have you become a hypocrite?\"" He said: \""No. But I will go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and inform him.\"" So he went to him and said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," you delayed the ʿIshāʾ"," and Muʿādh prayed with you"," then returned and led us in prayer. He opened with Sūrat al-Baqarah. When I saw that"," I held back and prayed alone. We are people who work with our own hands"," drawing water for irrigation.\"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) turned to Muʿādh and said: \""Are you a causer of tribulation (fattān)"," O Muʿādh? Are you a causer of tribulation? Recite such-and-such a sūrah and such-and-such a sūrah.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 669]"""
"5101","(5101) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Muʿādh used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), then come and lead his people. He prayed one night the ʿIshāʾ with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), then came to his people and opened with Sūrat al-Baqarah. A man turned away, gave salām, then prayed alone and departed. He mentioned the rest of the hadith with the same meaning. None of the narrators in this hadith mentioned \and he gave salām\"" except Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5102","(5102) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; ʿĀrim narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh: that Muʿādh (may Allah be pleased with him) used to pray with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), then come to his people and lead them in prayer. This is the wording of ʿĀrim's hadith. In the hadith of Sulaymān: \then come to their companions and lead them in prayer.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿĀrim and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb"," and Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5103","(5103) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAmr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh: that Muʿādh ibn Jabal used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), then return and lead his people in prayer.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"5104","(5104) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Hushaym informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Manṣūr narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh: that Muʿādh used to pray the ʿIshāʾ with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), then depart and come to his people and lead them in that prayer.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Manṣūr is ibn Zādhān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded by no. 5100]"
"5105","(5105) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār; Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me: that Muʿādh used to pray the ʿIshāʾ with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), then go to his people and lead them in prayer. For him it was a supererogatory prayer (nafl), and for them it was an obligatory prayer (farīḍah). [Ḥ. Ṣaḥīḥ. Meaning as above]"
"5106","(5106) He said: And Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr and Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; they said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed me. He mentioned it with the same meaning, except that he said: \He would lead them in that prayer — for them it was an obligatory prayer (farīḍah)"," and for him it was a supererogatory prayer (nāfilah).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Meaning as above]"""
"5107","(5107) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us; from Ibn ʿAjlān, who said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Miqsam narrated to me, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: Muʿādh used to pray ʿIshāʾ with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then he would come to his people and lead them in that same prayer. [Sound, with the meaning fully conveyed.]"
"5108","(5108) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār — informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb — narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah — narrated to us; from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with his Companions — a group of them — two rakʿahs, then he said the salām, then he prayed with the other group two rakʿahs, then he said the salām.\n\nAnd likewise Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd narrated it from al-Ḥasan from Jābir, and its meaning is confirmed from the ḥadīth of Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Jābir. [Sound through other chains.]"
"5109","(5109) And Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī — informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī — informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim ibn Ḥayyān al-Ṭūsī — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān — narrated to us; Ashʿath — narrated to us; from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ prayed with these [people] two rakʿahs and with those [people] two rakʿahs, so it was four rakʿahs for the Prophet ﷺ and two rakʿahs each for them.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said: The latter of these two [sets of rakʿahs] for the Prophet ﷺ was a supererogatory prayer (nāfilah) and for the others it was an obligatory prayer (farīḍah). [Sound through other chains.]"
"5110","(5110) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us; al-Shāfiʿī — informed us; Muslim — narrated to us; from Ibn Jurayj, that ʿAṭāʾ used to miss ʿAtamah [i.e., ʿIshāʾ], so he would come while the people were standing [in prayer], and he would pray with them two rakʿahs, then he would add two more rakʿahs upon that. And he [Ibn Jurayj] saw him do that and he counted it as ʿAtamah [ʿIshāʾ].\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said: And Wahb ibn Munabbih, al-Ḥasan, and Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī used to say this: A group of people came to Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī intending to pray Ẓuhr, and they found that he had already prayed. They said: \We only came to pray with you.\"" He said: \""I will not disappoint you.\"" Then he stood up and prayed with them. Abū Qaṭan mentioned this from Abū Khaldah"," from Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: And something similar to this meaning is narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"," may Allah be pleased with him"," and from a man among the Anṣār"," and something close to it is narrated from Abū al-Dardāʾ and Ibn ʿAbbās. [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī cited it in al-Umm 1/305.]"""
"5111","(5111) And with his chain of narration, he said: al-Shāfiʿī informed us; ʿAbd al-Majīd informed us; from Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to Ṭāwūs: \I found the people standing [in prayer]"," so I made it the last ʿIshāʾ.\"" He said: \""You have done correctly.\"""""
"5112","(5112) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī — informed us; Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Khalīl al-Qaṭṭān — informed us; Abū al-Azhar al-Salīṭī — narrated to us; Marwān ibn Muḥammad — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah — narrated to us; al-Waḍīn ibn ʿAṭāʾ — narrated to us; from Maḥfūẓ ibn ʿAlqamah, from Ibn ʾĀʾidh, who said: A group of three men from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque while the people were in the ʿAṣr prayer, having already completed the Ẓuhr prayer. They prayed with the people. When they had finished, some of them said to others: \How did you handle it?\"" One of them said: \""I made it Ẓuhr"," then I prayed ʿAṣr.\"" Another said: \""I made it ʿAṣr"," then I prayed Ẓuhr.\"" And the third said: \""I made it a prayer for the mosque [i.e."," a greeting prayer]"," then I prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr.\"" And none of them found fault with another. [Ḥasan.]"""
"5113","(5113) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us; al-Shāfiʿī — informed us; ʿAbd al-Majīd — narrated to us; from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: If you catch the ʿAṣr prayer and have not yet prayed Ẓuhr, then make the one you caught with the imām [count as] Ẓuhr, and pray ʿAṣr after that. [Ḥasan. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 2259.]"
"5114","(5114) Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā — and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us; al-Shāfiʿī — narrated to us; Mālik — narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd — informed us; ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī — narrated to us; Ismāʿīl — narrated to us; Mālik — narrated to us; from Ibn Shihāb, from Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ, that ʿItbān ibn Mālik used to lead his people in prayer while he was blind. He said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \Darkness and rain occur"," and I am a man of impaired sight. So pray"," O Messenger of Allah"," in a place in my house that I may take it as a place of prayer.\"" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and said: \""Where would you like me to pray?\"" And he pointed to him to a place in the house"," and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed therein.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways"," and he said \""a place in the house","\"" except that he did not mention \""rain\"" and said \""the flood\"" (al-sayl) instead. And Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Sound. Previously cited under no. 4924.]"""
"5115","(5115) Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Ḥirafī — in Baghdād — narrated to us; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ghālib — narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī — narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū Aḥmad — Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān — in Marw — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Birtī — narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī — narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd — narrated to us; from Ibn Shihāb, from Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ, from ʿItbān ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him in his house, and he mentioned the ḥadīth. And in it he said: \And I saw ʿItbān leading his people"," Banū Sālim"," in their mosque while he was blind.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Birtī.\n\nAnd in the narration of Muḥammad ibn Ghālib"," from Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī"," that he saw ʿItbān ibn Mālik and mentioned it similarly. [Sound. Previously cited under no. 4924.]"""
"5116","(5116) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār — narrated to us; Ibn Milḥān — narrated to us; Yaḥyā — narrated to us; al-Layth — narrated to us; from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, that he said: Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī narrated to me that ʿItbān ibn Mālik — and he was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who witnessed Badr from among the Anṣār — came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \O Messenger of Allah"," my sight has become impaired"," and I pray with my people. When the rains come"," the valley that lies between me and them floods"," and I am unable to come to their mosque to lead them in prayer. I wish"," O Messenger of Allah"," that you would come and pray in my house","\"" and he mentioned the ḥadīth.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr. [Sound. Previously cited under no. 4924.]"""
"5117","(5117) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-ʿAnbarī, Abū ʿAbd Allāh, informed us — Ibn Mahdī informed us — ʿImrān al-Qaṭṭān narrated from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Prophet ﷺ appointed Ibn Umm Maktūm as his deputy to lead the people in prayer, and he was blind."
"5118","(5118) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Shuʿbah informed me — Abū al-Ṭayyāḥ said: I heard Anas saying that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Dharr: \Listen and obey"," even if it be an Ethiopian slave whose head resembles a raisin.\"" Al-Bukhārī reported it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 660]"""
"5119","(5119) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us — Shuʿbah informed me — Abū al-Ṭayyāḥ, from Anas ibn Mālik, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Listen and obey"," even if an Ethiopian slave is appointed over you"," whose head resembles a raisin.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 7142]"""
"5120","(5120) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us — Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Shuʿbah, from Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ṣāmit, from Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: He arrived at al-Rabadhah and the prayer had been commenced, and a slave was leading them in prayer. It was said: \This is Abū Dharr.\"" So he went to withdraw"," and Abū Dharr"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: \""My intimate friend ﷺ enjoined upon me three things: I hear and I obey"," even if it be an Ethiopian slave with severed limbs.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. And Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated it from Shuʿbah"," and said in the ḥadīth: \""And a slave was leading them in prayer"," and they said to Abū Dharr: 'Come forward","' but he refused. So the slave came forward and led them in prayer.\"" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Bakhtarī informed us — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh informed us — my father informed us — Shuʿbah narrated it. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 648]"""
"5121","(5121) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, from Ibn Jurayj — ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Mulaykah informed me: They used to come to ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, at the upper part of the valley — he (i.e., ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd Allāh) and ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and many others — and Abū ʿAmr, the mawlā of ʿĀʾishah, would lead them in prayer. And Abū ʿAmr was her slave at that time, not yet freed, and he was the imam of Banū Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr and ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ from other than him. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 3824]"
"5122","(5122) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj al-Ḥijāzī al-Ḥimṣī, in Ḥimṣ, in Ṣafar of the year two hundred and sixty-seven, informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ḥimyār ibn Unays informed us — Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that Abū ʿAmr Dhakwān was a slave of ʿĀʾishah, and she manumitted him, and he used to stand in prayer for her in the month of Ramaḍān, leading her in prayer while he was still a slave."
"5123","(5123) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — al-Qaʿnabī informed us — Anas, meaning Ibn ʿIyāḍ, said — and Abū Dāwūd also informed us — al-Haytham ibn Khālid al-Juhanī informed us — Ibn Numayr, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, said: When the first Muhājirūn arrived, they settled at al-ʿUṣbah before the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Sālim, the mawlā of Abū Ḥudhayfah, was leading them in prayer, and he was the most learned of them in Qurʾān. Al-Haytham added: And among them were ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir, from Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 660]"
"5124","(5124) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both said — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Baḥr ibn Naṣr said: It was read to Ibn Wahb — Ibn Jurayj informed you — that Nāfiʿ informed them that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar informed him, saying: Sālim, the mawlā of Abū Ḥudhayfah, used to lead the first Muhājirūn and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from the Anṣār in the Mosque of Qubāʾ, among whom were Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, Abū Salamah, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah. The Shaykh said: Thus he said in this and in what preceded it — \among them was Abū Bakr\"" — perhaps at another time"," for Abū Bakr"," may Allah be pleased with him"," only arrived with the Prophet ﷺ. And it is possible that his leading them in prayer was before his arrival and after it. And the statement of the narrator"," \""among them was Abū Bakr","\"" refers to after his arrival. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 3807]"""
"5125","(5125) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us — ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham informed us — Abū al-Yamān informed us — Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah, from al-Zuhrī, said: ʿĀmir ibn Wāthilah al-Laythī informed me that Nāfiʿ ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥārith al-Khuzāʿī met ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, at ʿUsfān, and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, had appointed him over the people of Makkah. He greeted ʿUmar, and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: \Whom have you appointed as your deputy over the people of the valley?\"" He said: \""I have appointed Ibn Abzā over them.\"" ʿUmar said: \""Who is Ibn Abzā?\"" Nāfiʿ said: \""A mawlā of ours.\"" ʿUmar said: \""You have appointed a mawlā over them?\"" He said: \""O Commander of the Faithful"," he is a reciter of the Book of Allah and knowledgeable in the laws of inheritance.\"" ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: \""Has not the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Indeed"," Allah raises by means of this Book certain people and by it He debases others'?\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq"," from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 817]"""
"5126","(5126) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Hishām, from Qatādah, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd, that the Prophet ﷺ said: \When there are three of them on a journey"," let one of them lead them in prayer"," and the most deserving of them to lead in prayer is the one who has read the most Qurʾān.\"" Muslim reported it from the ḥadīth of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī and others"," from Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 672]"""
"5127","(5127) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, among others, narrated to us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us. Al-Shāfiʿī informed us. ʿAbd al-Majīd reported from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAṭāʾ informed me, who said: I heard ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr say: A group of people gathered in the vicinity of Makkah — I think he said, in the upper part of the valley — during the Ḥajj. When the time for Ṣalāh arrived, a man from the family of Abū al-Sāʾib, who was of non-Arabic tongue, stepped forward. Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah pushed him back and led someone else in prayer instead. This reached ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, but he did not question him about it until he came to Madīnah. When he came to Madīnah, ʿUmar asked him about it. Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah said: Grant me respite, O Amīr al-Muʾminīn. The man was of non-Arabic tongue, and it was during the Ḥajj, so I feared that some of the pilgrims might hear his recitation and take note of his foreignness in recitation. ʿUmar said: \Did you take him there?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" ʿUmar said: \""You have done well.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 1/294]"""
"5128","(5128) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us. Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us. ʿUthmān ibn al-Haytham narrated to us. ʿAwf reported from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah, who said: Allah benefited me through a word I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after I had nearly joined the Companions of the Camel to fight alongside them. It reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the people of Persia had appointed the daughter of Kisrā as their ruler. He said: \A people shall never prosper who entrust their affairs to a woman.\"" [Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUthmān ibn al-Haytham. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4164]]"""
"5129","(5129) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us. They said: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī informed us. ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb narrated to us. Jarīr narrated to us. Suhayl reported from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The best rows of men are the first of them"," and the worst are the last of them. The best rows of women are the last of them"," and the worst are the first of them.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 440]"""
"5130","(5130) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Makhlad ibn Jaʿfar al-Daqqāq narrated to us. Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us. ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us. Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it with the same meaning. [Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Jarīr.]"
"5131","(5131) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us. Abū Jaʿfar — Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Daqīqī narrated to us. Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us. Al-Fuḍayl ibn Marzūq informed me. Al-Walīd ibn Bukayr informed me. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad reported from ʿAlī ibn Zayd, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon his minbar say — and he mentioned the hadith, in which is: \Beware! Let no woman lead a man in prayer.\"" This hadith has weakness in its chain of narration. It is also narrated through another weak chain from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālli"," may Allah be pleased with him"," as his own statement. This is the position of the seven jurists from among the Tābiʿīn and those after them. [Very weak. Ibn Mājah 108]"""
"5132","(5132) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us. Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us. Ibn Numayr reported from al-Aʿmash, from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ, from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj, who said: I heard Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The one who leads the people in prayer should be the one most proficient in the recitation of the Book of Allah. If they are equal in recitation — I think he said — then the one most knowledgeable of the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah"," then the one who emigrated earliest. If they are equal in emigration"," then the one eldest in age. And let no man lead another in prayer in the latter's domain of authority"," nor sit upon his seat of honour in his house"," except with his permission.\"" [Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 673]]"""
"5133","(5133) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Asad al-Harawī narrated to us. Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr narrated to us. Salām ibn Sulaymān narrated to us. ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd reported from Muḥammad ibn Wāsiʿ, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Appoint your best among you as your imams"," for they are your envoys between you and your Lord.\"" The chain of this hadith is weak. [Munkar. Ḥākim 3/246]"""
"5134","(5134) Abū Aḥmad al-Mahrjānī informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. Ibn Bukayr narrated to us. Mālik reported from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd that a man used to lead the people in prayer in al-ʿAqīq, so ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz sent to him and forbade him. Mālik said: He only forbade him because his father was not known. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 303]"
"5135","(5135) Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥirafī in Baghdād narrated to us. ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Kūfī narrated to us. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us. Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to me. Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, the brother of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, narrated to me. He said: I asked ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ: If the child of zinā falls ill, should I visit him? He said: Yes. I said: If he dies, should I pray over him? He said: Yes. I said: If he bears witness, is his testimony accepted? He said: Yes. I said: Can he lead in prayer? He said: Yes. [Weak]"
"5136","(5136) And with his chain, he said: Zayd narrated to us. Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me. Al-Safar ibn Nusayr al-Asadī informed us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The child of zinā is the worst of the third\"" — meaning that his parents submitted [to Allah] but he did not submit. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""He is the worst of the three.\"" This is a mursal hadith. And it has been narrated from ʿĀʾishah"," may Allah be pleased with her"," that she said: \""He bears no burden of his parents' sin.\"" Allah"," the Most High"," said: {No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another} [al-Anʿām: 164] — meaning the child of zinā. And from al-Shaʿbī"," al-Nakhaʿī"," and al-Zuhrī regarding the child of zinā: that he may lead in prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Abū Dāwūd 3963]"""
"5137","(5137) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from ʿAmr ibn Salamah — who was still alive at the time — that Ayyūb said: \Why do you not meet him and ask him?\"" Ayyūb said: \""So I met ʿAmr and he said: 'We were at a watering place frequented by people"," and the riders would pass by us"," so we would ask them: What is this matter? What has happened to the people? They would say: A prophet who claims that Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," has sent him"," and that Allah has revealed to him such and such.' So I would commit that speech to memory"," as though it were being instilled in my breast with a natural eagerness. The Arabs used to delay their acceptance of Islam until after the Conquest"," saying: 'Leave him to his people; if he prevails over them"," then he is indeed a true prophet.' When the event of the Conquest occurred"," every tribe hastened to embrace Islam. My father set out with the people of our clan to accept Islam. When he came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and stayed in his presence"," and then departed from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," we met him. When he saw us"," he said: 'By Allah"," I have come to you from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in truth. He commands you to do such and such"," and he forbids you from such and such.' And he said: 'Pray such a prayer at such a time"," and such a prayer at such a time. When the time for prayer arrives"," let one of you give the adhān"," and let the one among you who has memorized the most Qurʾān lead you in prayer.' So they looked among the people of our clan and found no one who had memorized more Qurʾān than I"," on account of what I had gathered from the riders. So they placed me before them"," and I was seven years old"," or six years old. I had a cloak on me that was small"," and when I prostrated"," it would ride up from me. A woman of the clan said: 'Will you not cover the backside of your reciter from us?' So they clothed me with a shirt from the woven cloth of al-Baḥrayn. I never rejoiced over anything as I rejoiced over that shirt.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4051]"""
"5138","(5138) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Malik — narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, ʿĀṣim narrated from ʿAmr ibn Salamah, who said: \When my people returned from the presence of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," he said to us: 'Let the one among you who has the most recitation of the Qurʾān lead you in prayer.'\"" He said: \""They called me and taught me the rukūʿ and the sujūd"," and I used to lead them in prayer while I was a young boy"," wearing a torn cloak.\"" They would say to my father: \""Will you not cover your son's backside from us?\"" It was narrated by Misʿar ibn Ḥabīb from ʿAmr ibn Salamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the one before it]"""
"5139","(5139) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Sinān narrated to us, Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, Misʿar ibn Ḥabīb al-Jarmī — who was a shrewd elder, lively of heart — narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Salamah, who said: \My people came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) after they had read the Qurʾān. When they had fulfilled their needs"," they asked him: 'Who should lead us in prayer?' He said: 'The one among you who has gathered the most of the Qurʾān"," or taken the most of the Qurʾān.'\"" He said: \""They returned to their people and asked them"," but they found no one who had gathered or taken more of the Qurʾān than I.\"" He said: \""So they placed me forward"," and I was a young boy"," and I used to lead them in prayer.\"" He said: \""I did not attend any gathering except that I was their imām.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the one before it]"""
"5140","(5140) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us, Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī informed us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who both said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: 'There is no deity but Allah.' When they say it"," they have protected from me their blood and their wealth"," except by its right"," and their reckoning is with Allah.\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 25]"""
"5141","(5141) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Muthannā al-ʿAnbarī informed us, Abū Ghassān Mālik ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid al-Mismaʿī informed us, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Wāqid ibn Muḥammad ibn Zayd, from his father, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \I have been commanded to fight the people until they bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah"," and they establish the prayer"," and they give the zakāh. When they do that"," they have protected from me their blood and their wealth"," and their reckoning is with Allah.\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ghassān al-Mismaʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the one before it]"""
"5142","(5142) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to me, Ḥumayd narrated that he heard Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \I have been commanded to fight the polytheists until they bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. When they bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah"," and they pray as we pray"," and they face our qiblah"," and they eat what we slaughter"," then their wealth and their blood are forbidden to us"," except by its right. For them is what is for the Muslim"," and upon them is what is upon the Muslim.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ"," saying: \""And Ibn Abī Maryam said…\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the one before it]"""
"5143","(5143) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shīrūyah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bazīʿ narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Zuraīʿ narrated to us, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us, Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī narrated from ʿUrwah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) lagged behind, and I lagged behind with him. When he had relieved himself, he said: \Do you have water?\"" So I brought him a vessel for purification. He washed his face and his hands"," then he went to pull out his arms from his jubbah"," but the sleeves of the jubbah were tight"," so he took his hands out of the jubbah and cast the jubbah over his shoulders"," and washed his arms. He wiped over his forelock and over his turban"," and over his khuffs. Then he mounted"," and I mounted. We came to the people while they were standing in prayer. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf was leading them in prayer"," and he had already performed one rakʿah with them. When he sensed the presence of the Prophet (ﷺ)"," he began to step back"," but the Prophet (ﷺ) gestured to him"," so he led them in prayer. When he gave the salām"," the Prophet (ﷺ) stood up"," and I stood up with him"," and we performed the rakʿah that we had missed.\"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bazīʿ"," thus. And it was narrated by Musaddad and Ḥumayd ibn Masʿadah and a group from Yazīd ibn Zuraʿī"," from Ḥumayd"," from Bakr"," from Ḥamzah ibn al-Mughīrah"," from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 274]"""
"5144","(5144) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm — both with the same meaning — narrated from Wuhaib, from Muṣʿab ibn Muḥammad, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \The imām is appointed only to be followed. So when he says the takbīr"," say the takbīr"," and do not say the takbīr until he says it. And when he bows"," bow"," and do not bow until he bows. And when he says: 'Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah","' say: 'Allāhumma Rabbanā laka al-ḥamd.' Muslim said: 'Wa laka al-ḥamd.' And when he prostrates"," prostrate"," and do not prostrate until he prostrates. And when he prays standing"," pray standing. And when he prays sitting"," pray sitting"," all of you together.\"" Abū Dāwūd said: \""Allāhumma Rabbanā laka al-ḥamd.\"" Some of my companions conveyed this to me from Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 689]"""
"5145","(5145) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Rajāʾ al-Adīb, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Sūsī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʦAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Abī Fudayk informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlawayh al-Abharī al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us, Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \When you hear the iqāmah"," walk"," and upon you should be tranquility. Whatever you catch"," pray it"," and whatever you miss"," make it up.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb"," and he said: \""And complete it.\"" Similarly"," Ḥuḥaym narrated from Ibn Abī Fudayk: \""And complete it.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 610]"""
"5146","(5146) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, known as Abū Shaykh, informed us, al-Marwazī — meaning Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān — narrated to us, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, al-Masʿūdī narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Muʿādh, who said: \They would come to the prayer when the Prophet (ﷺ) had already preceded them with part of the prayer. They would signal to them with their fingers — one"," two — how much he had prayed. Then Muʿādh came"," and the Prophet (ﷺ) had preceded him with part of the prayer. He joined the prayer and said: 'I did not find him in any state except that I joined him in it.' Then I made up what I had missed.\"" He said: \""Then he came another time when the Prophet (ﷺ) had preceded him with part of the prayer"," so he joined the prayer. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) completed the prayer"," Muʿādh stood up to make up what he had missed. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Muʿādh has set a precedent for you in this"," so do likewise.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. Abū Dāwūd 507]"""
"5147","(5147) Abu al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that 'Amr ibn Marzuq informed us that Shu'bah informed us from 'Amr ibn Murrah from Ibn Abi Layla, who said: \And our companions informed us"," saying: 'A man would come to pray and would be informed of what had preceded him from his prayer.' They stood with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among those standing"," bowing"," sitting"," and praying with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Mu'adh came"," and they gestured to him.\"" Shu'bah said: \""And I heard this from Husayn"," meaning from Ibn Abi Layla"," who said: Mu'adh said: 'I do not see him in any state except that I am in it.' He ﷺ said: 'Indeed"," Mu'adh has established a sunnah for you; do likewise.'\"""""
"5148","(5148) Ghandar narrated it from Shu'bah up to his words: \with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\"" Then he said: 'Amr ibn Murrah said"," and Husayn narrated it to me from Ibn Abi Layla"," until Mu'adh came. Shu'bah said: \""And I heard it from Husayn"," who said: 'I do not see him in any state' up to his words: 'do likewise.'\"" Abu al-Rudhbari informed him that Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed him that Abu Dawud informed him that Ibn al-Muthanna informed him that Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed him from Shu'bah"," and he mentioned it."""
"5149","(5149) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us that Abu al-Muthanna informed us that Musaddad informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Dawud informed us that al-A'mash informed us from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from 'A'ishah, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell ill with the illness in which he passed away"," Bilal came to give him the call to prayer. He ﷺ said: 'Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.' I said: 'Abu Bakr is a tender-hearted man; if he stands in your place"," he will weep and will not be able to recite.' He said it a third or a fourth time: 'You are the companions of Yusuf. Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.' Abu Bakr"," may Allah be pleased with him"," stood and led the people in prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out"," being supported between two men"," and it was as if I were looking at him"," his feet dragging on the ground. When Abu Bakr saw him"," he began to step back. He ﷺ gestured to him to continue praying. Abu Bakr stood"," and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat beside him"," praying"," while Abu Bakr was making the people hear [the recitation].\"""""
"5150","(5150) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us that Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Abi Qumash informed us that Ibn 'A'ishah informed us that Hammad ibn Salamah informed us from Ziyad al-A'lam from al-Hasan from Abi Bakrah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered into prayer"," then gestured to them to stay in their places. Then he entered his house and came out"," his head dripping with water. He entered into prayer and led them in it. When he completed the prayer"," he said: 'I am only human"," and I had been in a state of janabah.'\"""""
"5151","(5151) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Ahmad ibn Ja'far informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal informed me that my father informed me that Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Madani informed us that Warqa' informed us from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, who said: \I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey"," and we came to a watering place. He ﷺ said: 'Will you water [the animals]"," O Jabir?' I said: 'Yes.' He ﷺ dismounted"," and I watered [them]. He ﷺ then went to relieve himself"," and I placed water for his wudu'. He came"," performed wudu'"," and then stood to pray in a single garment"," wrapping its two ends [around him]. I stood behind him"," and he took me by my ear and placed me on his right.\"""""
"5152","(5152) Abu al-Rudhbari and Abu 'Abd Allah ibn Burhan and Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan and Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan and Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari informed us, they said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us that al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah informed us that Hushaym informed us from Abu Bishr from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: \I spent a night at the house of my maternal aunt Maymunah bint al-Harith"," may Allah be pleased with her. The Prophet ﷺ stood to pray at night. I stood on his left to pray following his prayer. He took hold of my hair"," or my head"," and placed me on his right.\"""""
"5153","(5153) Abu al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that Hisham ibn 'Ammar and Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn al-Fadl al-Sijistani informed us that Hatim, meaning Isma'il, informed us that Ya'qub ibn Mujahid Abu Hazrah informed us from 'Ubadah ibn al-Walid ibn 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, who said: \We came to Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah. He said: 'I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a military expedition. He stood to pray","' and he mentioned the hadith. He said: 'Then I came and stood on the left of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He took me by the hand and turned me until he placed me on his right. Then Ibn Sakhr came and stood on his left. He took both of us by the hands and placed us behind him.'\"""""
"5154","(5154) From Muhammad ibn 'Abad from Hatim ibn Isma'il, and he said in the hadith: \He took both of us by the hands and pushed us until he placed us behind him.\"" Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed him that Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed him that 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed him that Muhammad ibn 'Abad al-Makki informed him that Hatim ibn Isma'il informed him"," and he mentioned something similar to it."""
"5155","(5155) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Asfahani informed us by dictation that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us that Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Baghdadi informed us that Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr informed us that Shu'bah informed us from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mukhtar from Musa ibn Anas from Anas ibn Malik, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led me and a woman in prayer. He placed me on his right and the woman behind them both.\"""""
"5156","(5156) Abu al-Qasim Zayd ibn Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-'Alawi and Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nudhayr ibn Janah al-Qadi and Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Khushaysh al-Muqri' in Kufah informed us, they said: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duheym informed us that Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Hunayn informed us that Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi informed us that Shu'bah informed us from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mukhtar from Musa ibn Anas from his father, who said: \The Prophet ﷺ led him and a woman in prayer. He said: 'He placed me on his right and the woman behind us.'\"""""
"5157","(5157) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us that Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated from Thabit from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us, and there was no one present except myself, my mother, and my maternal aunt Umm Haram. He said: \Stand"," so that I may lead you in prayer.\"" He then led us in prayer at a time other than the prescribed time of prayer. A man said to Thabit: \""Where did he place Anas?\"" He said: \""He placed him to his right.\"" When he completed his prayer"," he supplicated for us"," the people of the house"," with every good of this world and the Hereafter. My mother said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," your little servant — invoke Allah for him.\"" So he supplicated for me with every good. The last of what he supplicated for me was: \""O Allah"," increase his wealth and his children"," and bless him therein.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah. [Sahih. Muslim 660]"""
"5158","(5158) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us that al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awza'i who said: al-Zuhri narrated to me that Mahmud ibn al-Rabi' said: \I clearly remember a mouthful that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) spat from a bucket in our house.\"" Mahmud said: \""Then 'Itban ibn Malik narrated to me"," saying: I said"," 'O Messenger of Allah"," my sight has become weak","' meaning"," 'and when the rains become heavy and the valley floods"," it comes between me and the prayer in the mosque of my people. If you were to come and pray in my house at a place that I could take as a place of prayer.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Yes.' He said: 'The next morning"," the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to me"," and Abu Bakr was with him. They sought permission"," and I granted them permission. He did not sit until he said: 'Where would you like me to pray in your house?' I pointed to a corner. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stepped forward"," and we stood in rows behind him. He led us in two rak'ahs"," and we detained the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for a dish of porridge that we had prepared for him.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih. Previously cited under no. 5114]"""
"5159","(5159) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastuyah narrated to us that Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us that Ibn Qa'nab and Ibn Bukayr narrated from Malik from Ibn Shihab from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah from his father who said: \I came upon 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) at al-Hijrah and found him praying voluntary prayers. I stood behind him. He drew me near until he placed me to his right. When someone came to relieve him"," I stepped back"," and we stood in rows behind him.\""\n\nAnd it has been narrated from 'Umar and 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when they were three in number"," the two would stand behind the imam. [Sahih. Malik 360]"""
"5160","(5160) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki and others said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us that al-Shafi'i narrated to us that Malik narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu al-Qasim Zayd ibn Abi Hashim al-'Alawi in Kufah informed us that Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duham narrated to us that Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Hunay narrated to us that al-Qa'nabi narrated from Malik.\n\n(And) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that 'Ali ibn Hamshadh narrated to us that Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn narrated to us that Yahya ibn Yahya narrated — he said: I read to Malik — from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah from Anas ibn Malik: \His grandmother Mulaykah invited the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to a meal she had prepared. He ate from it and then said: 'Stand"," so that I may lead you in prayer.' Anas said: 'I went to our mat that had turned black from long use"," and I sprinkled it with water. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood upon it"," and I and the orphan stood in a row behind him"," and the old woman stood behind us. He led us in two rak'ahs and then departed.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf and others from Malik. And Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Bukhari 373]"""
"5161","(5161) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us that Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us that Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu al-Walid narrated to us that Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn Nasr and 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad said: Yahya ibn Habib ibn 'Arabi narrated to us that Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us that Khalid al-Hadha' narrated from Abu Ma'shar from Ibrahim from 'Alqamah from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Let those among you who are of mature judgment and understanding stand closest to me"," then those next to them\"" — three times — \""and beware of the tumult of the markets.\"" The wording is that of the hadith of Yahya.\n\nAnd in the narration of Muhammad with his chain from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," he said: \""Let those among you who are of mature judgment and understanding stand closest to me"," then those next to them"," then those next to them. And do not differ"," lest your hearts differ. And beware of the tumult of the markets.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Habib ibn 'Arabi. [Sahih. Muslim 432]"""
"5162","(5162) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Ahmad al-Mahrajani said: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us that Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us that al-A'mash narrated from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr from Abu Ma'mar from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to touch our shoulders during prayer and say: 'Do not differ"," lest your hearts differ. Let those among you who are of mature judgment and understanding stand closest to me"," then those next to them"," then those next to them.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it through various routes from al-A'mash. [Sahih. Muslim 432]"""
"5163","(5163) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi informed us that Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah al-Sa'di informed us that Yazid ibn Harun informed us that Humayd al-Tawil narrated from Anas ibn Malik: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) loved that the Muhajirun and the Ansar stand closest to him in prayer so that they could learn from him.\"" [Sahih. Ahmad 3/205]"""
"5164","(5164) Yazid ibn Zurai' also narrated it, saying: Humayd narrated to us, who said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to us, who said: \The Prophet (ﷺ)…\"" and he mentioned it with the same meaning.\n\nAbu al-Hasan al-Muqri informed us that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us that Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us that Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated it.\n\n[No translation provided]"""
"5165","(5165) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Qattan narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us that 'Ayyash ibn al-Walid narrated to us that 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us that Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us that Budayl narrated to us that Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm who said: Abu Malik al-Ash'ari said: \Shall I not inform you of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (750)?\"" He said: \""He established the prayer and arranged the rows — meaning the men — and arranged the boys behind them"," then he led them in prayer. He said: 'When he prostrated"," he said the takbir"," and when he raised his head"," he said the takbir"," and when he stood up from the two rak'ahs"," he said the takbir"," and he gave salutation to his right and to his left.' Then he said: 'This is the prayer.'\"" 'Abd al-A'la said: \""I do not think he said anything other than 'the prayer of the Prophet (ﷺ).'\"" [Da'if. Abu Dawud 677]"""
"5166","(5166) And Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf informed us that Abu Muhammad Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Nusayr al-Khawwas in Baghdad narrated to us that al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Mus'ab al-Kufi narrated to us that Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Ansari narrated to us that Mus'ab ibn Mahan narrated to us that Sufyan al-Thawri narrated from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Malik al-Ash'ari who said: \The Prophet (ﷺ) would have the men stand closest to him in prayer"," then the boys"," then the women.\"" This chain of narration is weak"," and the first narration is stronger. And Allah knows best. [Da'if. Narrated by al-Harith in his Musnad 151]"""
"5167","(5167) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī in Makkah informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; al-Qaʿnabī informed us; al-Dāwardī informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad informed us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The best rows of men are the first of them"," and the worst are the last of them. And the best rows of women are the last of them"," and the worst are the first of them.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 440]"""
"5168","(5168) And Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Naẓīr al-Miṣrī in Makkah informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Mawt informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; Sufyān al-Thawrī informed us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Mahrajānī informed us there; Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī informed us; Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us; Abū ʿĀṣim informed us, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The best rows of men are the first of them"," and the worst are the last of them. And the best rows of women are the last of them"," and the worst are the first of them.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5169","(5169) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Nūḥ ibn Qays informed me; ʿAmr ibn Mālik al-Nukarī informed me, from Abū al-Jazāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A woman used to pray behind the Prophet (ﷺ) who was the most beautiful of people. There were some men in the last rows of the men, and they would look at her. He said: One of them would look at her from beneath his armpit, and one of them would move forward to the first row so that he would not see her. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed this verse: {And indeed, We know the foremost among you, and indeed, We know the laggards.} [Al-Ḥijr: 24] [Munkar. Tirmidhī 3122]"
"5170","(5170) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed me; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū ʿUmar Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar informed us; Nūḥ ibn Qays informed us, and he mentioned it with his chain of narration, except that he said: A woman of the most beautiful of people used to pray behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Some of the people would move forward in the first row so that they would not see her, and some of them would lag behind until they were in the last row. When they went into rukūʿ, they would look from beneath their armpits and spread their hands away from their sides. So Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed concerning her: {And indeed, We know the foremost among you, and indeed, We know the laggards.} [Al-Ḥijr: 24] [Munkar. See the preceding]"
"5171","(5171) And Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbaydillāh al-Ḥirafī in Baghdād informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm al-Simmārī informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, from his father, who said: I and ʿAlqamah entered upon ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd in al-Hājirah. When the sun declined, he stood for the prayer. I and my companion stood behind him. He took hold of my hand and the hand of my companion and placed us to his right and his left. He stood between us and said: \This is how the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to do when they were three.\"" Then he led us in prayer. When he finished"," he said: \""There will be imāms who will delay the prayer from its prescribed times. Do not wait for them with it"," and make the prayer with them as supererogatory (nāfilah).\""\n\nAnd this is subject to the possibility that it was abrogated there"," and we draw evidence for its abrogation from what has preceded from the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh and Anas ibn Mālik"," and what we have narrated from ʿUmar and ʿAlī (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā) and the generality.\n\nAnd we have narrated from Abū Dharr that which indicates that what Ibn Masʿūd witnessed from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in that regard was witnessed other than in a congregational prayer"," and that each one of them was praying for himself. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 534]"""
"5172","(5172) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfarāyinī informed us; Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Barbadhārī informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us; al-Ḥumaydī informed us; Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī informed us; Qudāmah ibn ʿAbdillāh Abū Rawḥ informed us; Jastrah bint Dijājah informed me, from Abū Dharr, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood one night at such and such a place and prayed therein the last ʿIshāʾ. When the people saw that they had remained with him in his place of prayer, he turned to his dwelling until the eyes were weary and his place was empty. He stood there alone. Abū Dharr said: I came forward and stood behind him, and he gestured to his right. Then ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd came and stood behind him and behind me. He said: He gestured to him with his left. We stood thus, and he joined between the index finger, the middle finger, and the one next to the little finger. Each one of us was praying for himself.\n\nAl-Ḥumaydī said: Ibn Masʿūd went to this thinking that the Prophet (ﷺ) was leading them in prayer. When Abū Dharr said, \Each one of us was praying for himself","\"" his statement made it clear that he knew from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he had not led them in prayer"," and that he was the one who had begun the prayer with him at its opening takbīr"," while Ibn Masʿūd was the one who came and entered"," whose intention preceded at its opening takbīr."""
"5173","(5173) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Hishām ibn Ḥassān informed us. He said: I mentioned that to Ibn Sīrīn — meaning what Ibn Masʿūd did — and Ibn Sīrīn said: \The mosque was narrow.\"" [Ḥasan]"""
"5174","(5174) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī in Naysābūr informed us, and Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us orally; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us. They both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us; Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq informed us; ʿAbd al-Malik informed us, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that he came to his maternal aunt Maymūnah. He said: The Prophet (ﷺ) got up at night to a water-skin and performed wuḍūʾ, then stood and prayed. He said: I stood and performed wuḍūʾ, then stood to his left. He turned me from behind him until he placed me to his right.\n\nMuslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr, from his father, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān.\n\nAnd Qays ibn Saʿd narrated it from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: He took hold of me from behind his back and placed me to his right. And with its meaning Ibn Jurayj narrated it from ʿAṭāʾ. And in it is an indication of the prohibition of the maʾmūm advancing ahead of the imām, since he turned him from behind him and did not turn him from in front of him. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5175","(5175) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar informed us, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Straighten the row in prayer"," for straightening the row is part of the excellence of prayer.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad"," and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ"," both from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 689]"""
"5176","(5176) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us; Shuʿbah informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bazzāz in Ṭabarān informed us; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us orally; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us; Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī informed us; Shuʿbah informed us, from Qatādah, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Straighten your rows"," for straightening the row is part of the completeness of prayer.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd"," and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 433]"""
"5177","(5177) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Straighten your rows"," for I see you behind my back.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from Abu Ma'mar"," from 'Abd al-Warith. [Sahih. Bukhari 686]"""
"5178","(5178) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to me; Harun ibn 'Abd al-Samad al-Rukhiyy narrated to us; Shayban ibn Farukh narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated it with his chain of narration, except that he said: \Complete the rows.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Shayban ibn Farukh.\n\nAnd Humayd narrated it from Anas and added therein: \""And press closely together.\"" This has already been mentioned in the chapter on the description of the prayer. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"""
"5179","(5179) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Aban narrated from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \Draw your rows close together"," bring them near to one another"," and align the necks. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul"," I indeed see the Shaytan entering through the gaps in the row as though he were a small black lamb.\""\n\n[Sahih. Abu Dawud 667]"""
"5180","(5180) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; Abu Muslim narrated to us; Abu al-Walid and Sulayman ibn Harb both said: Shu'bah narrated to us; 'Amr — meaning ibn Murrah — informed me. He said: I heard Salim — meaning ibn Abi al-Ja'd — say: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \You must straighten your rows in your prayer"," or Allah will surely cause discord among your faces.\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from Abu al-Walid"," and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ghundar. [Sahih. Bukhari 685]"""
"5181","(5181) Abu al-Hasan al-'Alawiyy informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Dilluyah al-Daqqaq narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to me; my father narrated to me; Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said: I heard him say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to straighten the rows. He saw a man whose chest was protruding from the row. He said: \You must straighten your rows"," or Allah will surely cause discord among your faces on the Day of Resurrection.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it through another chain from Simak. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"""
"5182","(5182) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Abu al-Ahwaz narrated from Simak, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to straighten the rows just as arrows are straightened. One day he saw a man whose chest was protruding from the row. I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) saying: \You must straighten your rows"," or Allah will surely cause discord among your faces.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Same meaning as above.]"""
"5183","(5183) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Waki' narrated from Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah, from Abu al-Qasim al-Jadali, who said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned his face toward the people and said: \Straighten your rows\"" — three times — \""By Allah"," you must straighten your rows"," or Allah will surely cause discord among your hearts.\"" He said: I saw each man pressing his shoulder against his companion's shoulder"," his knee against his companion's knee"," and his ankle against his companion's ankle.\n\n[Sahih in meaning through other narrations. Abu Dawud 662]"""
"5184","(5184) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Muhammad Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Muzani narrated to us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us; Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us; Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Ubayd Allah al-Nakha'i, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awsajah, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Press closely together in the row"," so that the young of the small black lambs do not pass through you.\"" It was said: \""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," what are the young of the small black lambs?\"" He said: \""Black"," hairless sheep that are found in the land of Yemen.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Hafs ibn Ghiyath from al-Hasan ibn 'Ubayd Allah"," and he said: \""like the young of the small black lambs.\"" [Sahih in meaning through other narrations. Al-Hakim 1/337]"""
"5185","(5185) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us; 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us; my father narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ubayd Allah narrated from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awsajah, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, from the Prophet (ﷺ): He commanded them to press the rows closely together so that the young of the small black lambs would not pass through them, and the young of the small black lambs are black, hairless sheep found in Yemen.\n\n[Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5186","(5186) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqiyy narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated. (H) And Qutaybah narrated to us; al-Layth narrated; and the hadith of Ibn Wahb is more complete. From Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar. Qutaybah said: from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Abu Shajarah — Kathir ibn Murrah — and he did not mention Ibn 'Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Straighten the rows"," align the shoulders"," close the gaps"," and be gentle with the hands of your brothers. Do not leave gaps for the Shaytan. Whoever connects a row"," Allah will connect him"," and whoever severs a row"," Allah — the Mighty and Majestic — will sever him.\""\n\nAbu Dawud said: 'Isa did not say \""with the hands of your brothers.\"" [Strong. Abu Dawud 666]"""
"5187","(5187) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me — and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū al-Qāsim al-Sarrāj informed us by dictation — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb: Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī informed you from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \Indeed"," Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the rows.\""\n\n[Ḥasan. Ibn Mājah 995]"""
"5188","(5188) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn Bashshār narrated to us; Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Yaḥyá ibn Thawbān narrated to us, saying: my uncle ʿUmārah ibn Thawbān informed me from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The best of you are those whose shoulders are the softest during Ṣalāh.\""\n\nIt was also narrated by Zayd ibn Aslam from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal hadith. [Ḥasan. Ḥasan in light of other narrations. Abū Dāwūd 672]"""
"5189","(5189) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; al-Aʿmash narrated to us from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ from Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to the rows and said: \Will you not form rows as the angels form rows before their Lord?\"" They said: \""How do the angels form rows before their Lord?\"" He said: \""They complete the front rows and they stand shoulder to shoulder in the row.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from the hadith of Abū Muʿāwiyah from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 435]"""
"5190","(5190) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated to us; Qatādah narrated to us from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Complete the first row"," then the second; and if there is any shortfall"," let it be in the back row.\"" And he used to say: \""The best rows of men are the first of them"," and the best rows of women are the last of them.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 671]"""
"5191","(5191) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyá ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, saying: Saʿīd — meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — was asked about the virtue of the front row, so he informed us from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Complete the front row"," then the one after it; and whatever shortfall there is"," let it be in the back row.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in light of other narrations]"""
"5192","(5192) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī and Muḥammad ibn Shādhān narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; Abū Quṭn ʿAmr ibn al-Haytham narrated to us from Shuʿbah, from Qatādah, from Khilās ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Rāfiʿ, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \If they knew — or if you knew — what is in the first row"," there would be nothing but drawing lots.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 439]"""
"5193","(5193) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Baṣīr, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the Fajr prayer. When he gave the salām, he looked at the faces of the people and said: \Is so-and-so present?\"" They said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""There is no ṣalāh more burdensome upon the hypocrites than these two prayers\"" — meaning Fajr and ʿIshāʾ. \""If they knew what is in them"," they would come to them even if they had to crawl on their knees. And the first row is like the row of the angels. If you knew what is in it"," you would race to it. And a man's ṣalāh with another man is more purifying than his ṣalāh alone"," and his ṣalāh with two men is more purifying than his ṣalāh with one man; and the more numerous it is"," the more beloved it is to Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic.\""\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Previously referenced under no. 5000]"""
"5194","(5194) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū ʿUtbah narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated to us from Buḥayr ibn Saʿd, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from al-ʿIrāḍ ibn Sāriyah, from the Prophet (ﷺ): that he used to send blessings upon the first row three times and upon the one next to it once.\n\nIt was also narrated by Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī from Khālid from al-ʿIrāḍ without mentioning Jubayr ibn Nufayr in his isnād.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/124]"
"5195","(5195) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hishām al-Dastawāʾī narrated to us from Yaḥyá ibn Abī Kathīr, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from al-ʿIrāḍ ibn Sāriyah: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sought forgiveness for the front row three times and for the second row once.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in light of other narrations. al-Ṭayālisī 1163]"
"5196","(5196) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us from Mālik ibn Miḥwal, from Ṭalḥah — and Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us from Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif — who said: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwsajah narrating from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: When we stood for ṣalāh, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would come to us and touch our shoulders and chests, saying: \Do not differ"," lest your hearts differ. Indeed"," Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row\"" — or he said: \""upon the foremost rows.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭayālisī 741]"""
"5197","(5197) Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī in al-Kūfah informed us — Abū Ja'far ibn Duḥaym informed us — Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us — 'Ubayd Allāh ibn Mūsā and Abū Nu'aym narrated to us — from Abī al-Ash'bah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us — Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr ibn 'Abd al-Wahhāb informed us — Shaybān ibn Farrūkh narrated to us — Abū al-Ash'bah narrated — from Abū Naḍrah — from Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, observed a tendency to lag behind among his Companions, so he said to them: \Come forward and follow my lead"," and let those who come after you follow your lead. A people who continually lag behind will be kept behind by Allah.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 438]"""
"5198","(5198) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Ma'īn narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzāq narrated — from 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammār — from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr — from Abū Salamah — from 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \A people who continually lag behind from the first row will be kept behind by Allah"," the Almighty and Majestic"," in the Fire.\"" [Ḍa'īf — Abū Dāwūd 679]"""
"5199","(5199) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — 'Uthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Mu'āwiyah ibn Hishām narrated to us — Sufyān narrated — from Usāmah ibn Zayd — from 'Uthmān ibn 'Urwah — from 'Urwah — from 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \Verily"," Allah and His angels send blessings upon the right sides of the rows.\"" Thus he said"," but what is preserved with this chain from the Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him"," is: \""Verily"," Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the rows.\"" [Munkar — Abū Dāwūd 676]"""
"5200","(5200) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān informed him — Abū al-Qāsim al-Ṭabarānī informed us — Ḥafṣ ibn 'Umar narrated to us — Qabīṣah narrated to us — Sufyān narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me — 'Alī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us — Yazīd ibn al-Haytham narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth narrated to us — al-Ashja'ī narrated — from Sufyān — from Usāmah ibn Zayd — from 'Uthmān ibn 'Urwah — from his father — from 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said — and he mentioned it.\n\nAnd thus did Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrate it from Sufyān.\n\nAnd al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated it from Sufyān — from Usāmah ibn Zayd — from 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Urwah — from 'Urwah — from 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \Verily"," Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the rows.\"" [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi]"""
"5201","(5201) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr informed him, both saying: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us — Usayd ibn 'Āṣim narrated to us — al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its like.\n\nAnd thus did 'Abd al-Razzāq and 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Walīd al-'Adanī narrate it from Sufyān. Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān said to me: Abū al-Qāsim al-Ṭabarānī said: Both chains of narration are ṣaḥīḥ.\n\nThe Shaykh — meaning both chains — but as for the text, then Mu'āwiyah ibn Hishām is singular with the first text, and I do not consider it preserved, for 'Abd Allāh ibn Wahb and 'Abd al-Wahhāb ibn 'Aṭā' narrated it from Usāmah ibn Zayd in a manner similar to the narration of the majority regarding the text."
"5202","(5202) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us — Abū Qilābah narrated to us — 'Abd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn al-Faḍl al-'Atakī narrated to us — 'Imrān ibn Khālid al-Khuzā'ī narrated — from al-'Alā' ibn 'Alī — from his father — from Abū Barzah, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to me: \If you are able"," stand behind the imam; otherwise"," stand to his right.\"" He said: \""Thus Abū Bakr and 'Umar used to stand behind the Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him.\"""""
"5203","(5203) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ja'far ibn Musāfir narrated to us — al-Imām ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Bashīr ibn Khallād narrated — from his mother, who said: She entered upon Muḥammad ibn Ka'b al-Quraẓī and heard him say: Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \Center the imam and fill the gaps.\"" [Ḍa'īf — Abū Dāwūd 681]"""
"5204","(5204) Abū al-Qāsim ibn Abī Hāshim al-'Alawī in al-Kūfah and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī in Naysābūr both said: Abū Ja'far ibn Duḥaym informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayn informed us — Qabīṣah ibn 'Uqbah narrated — from Sufyān.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to us — Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us — Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Abī al-Naḍr al-Marwazī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us — Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated — all three saying: Sufyān narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Hānī' narrated — from 'Abd al-Ḥamīd ibn Maḥmūd, who said: We were with Anas ibn Mālik in the row, and they pushed us until we were thrown between the pillars. He moved back, and when he had finished praying, he said: \We used to guard against this during the time of the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd 673]"""
"5205","(5205) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us — 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Hārūn Abū Muslim narrated to us — Qatādah narrated — from Mu'āwiyah ibn Qurrah — from his father, who said: During the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, we were driven away so that we would not stand between the pillars in prayer. [Ḥasan — Ibn Mājah 1002]"
"5206","(5206) Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Ja'far, reading it to him, narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahhāb ibn 'Aṭā' informed us — Shu'bah narrated — from Abī Isḥāq — from Ma'dī Karib — from Ibn Mas'ūd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: \Do not form rows between the pillars.\""\n\nAnd Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Abī Isḥāq"," and said in his text: \""Do not form rows between the pillars (al-asāṭīn).\""\n\nAnd this — Allah knows best — is because the pillar stands between them and the continuity of the row. If a person is alone or they do not go beyond the space between two pillars"," then there is not dislike"," Allah willing"," according to what we have narrated in the firmly established ḥadīth from Ibn 'Umar"," may Allah be pleased with both of them"," who said: \""I asked Bilāl where the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," prayed — meaning inside the Ka'bah — and he said: 'Between the two front pillars.'\"" [Ḍa'īf — Ibn Abī Shaybah 7500]"""
"5207","(5207) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, ʿAmr ibn Murrah informed me, he said: I heard Hilāl ibn Yasāf say, I heard ʿAmr ibn Rāshid narrate from Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw a man praying behind the row alone, so he commanded him to repeat the prayer. Thus narrated ʿAmr ibn Murrah. And Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān contradicted him, for he narrated it from Hilāl ibn Yasāf as well. [Authentic through other chains. Abū Dāwūd 682]"
"5208","(5208) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī informed us, Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat informed us, Khallād ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Ḥuṣayn, from Hilāl ibn Yasāf, from Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw a man praying behind the row alone, so he commanded him, and he repeated the prayer. [Authentic through other chains]"
"5209","(5209) Abū Saʿīd — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfarāʾinī — informed us, Abū Baḥr al-Barbahārī informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us, al-Ḥumaydī informed us, Sufyān — that is, ibn ʿUyaynah — informed us, Ḥuṣayn narrated from Hilāl ibn Yasāf, who said: Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd took my hand and stood me before a man in al-Raqqah and said: This man narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw a man praying behind the row alone, so he commanded him to repeat it. And his name is Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad al-Asadī. And a group narrated it from Ḥuṣayn. And it was narrated from another chain from Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd. [Authentic]"
"5210","(5210) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ — informed him, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Musaddad informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Dāwūd informed us, Yazīd — meaning Ibn Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd — narrated from ʿUbayd ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Wābiṣah that a man prayed behind the rows alone, so the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded him to repeat the prayer. And it was narrated through a weak chain from al-Shaʿbī from Wābiṣah. [Authentic through other chains]"
"5211","(5211) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed him, Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī — informed us, Mālik ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, al-Sarī ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from al-Shaʿbī, from Wābiṣah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw a man praying behind the rows alone. He said: \O you who prays alone"," why did you not join the row or drag a man to yourself so that he could stand with you? Repeat the prayer.\"" Al-Sarī ibn Ismāʿīl is the sole narrator of it"," and he is weak. And Abū Dāwūd narrated it in al-Marāsīl. [Very weak. Abū Yaʿlā 1588]"""
"5212","(5212) From al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Ḥassān, from Muqātil ibn Ḥayyān, who raised it (to the Prophet), he said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \If a man comes and finds no one"," let him pull a man from the row to stand with him"," and how great is the reward of the one who is pulled!\"" Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed him"," Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī informed us"," Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī informed us"," Abū Dāwūd narrated it"," and it is disconnected (munqaṭiʿ). And it was narrated from another chain from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the command to repeat. [Very weak]"""
"5213","(5213) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed him, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, Abū al-Nuʿmān, and al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ said: Mulāzim ibn ʿAmr informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Badr narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAlī ibn Shaybān, from his father ʿAlī ibn Shaybān, who was one of the delegation that came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from Banī Suḥaym, who said: We prayed with the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) a prayer, and he glanced with the back of his eyes and saw a man who was not straightening his back in rukūʿ and sujūd. When he gave salām, he said: \O people"," there is no prayer for the one who does not straighten his back in rukūʿ and sujūd.\"" And I prayed with the Prophet (ﷺ) another day"," and when he gave salām"," behold"," there was a man behind the row praying alone. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood until he completed his prayer"," and when he gave salām"," he said: \""Repeat your prayer; there is no prayer for the one who is alone behind the row.\"" [Authentic. Ibn Mājah 1003]"""
"5214","(5214) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us, Abū al-Ḥusayn Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb al-ʿUkhlī informed us, Sharīk narrated from Mughīrah, from Ibrāhīm, regarding the man who prays behind the row alone, he said: His prayer is valid, but there is no doubling (of reward) for him. The Shaykh said: By this he means that there will be no doubling of reward through the congregation (jamāʿah), as though the Prophet (ﷺ) negated the virtue of the congregation and commanded him to repeat so that he might attain the increased reward, and he would not return to abandoning the Sunnah. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"5215","(5215) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us, Ḥammād informed us, Ziyād al-Aʿlam informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Ziyād al-Aʿlam, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he came while the people were in rukūʿ, so he performed rukūʿ before (reaching) the row, then he walked to the row. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) completed his prayer, he said: \Which of you performed rukūʿ before the row and then walked to the row?\"" Abū Bakrah said: \""I did"," O Messenger of Allah.\"" He said: \""May Allah increase your eagerness"," but do not do it again.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Muqriʾ. And in the ḥadīth of al-Rūdhbārī: that Abū Bakrah came while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in rukūʿ"," and the rest is similar. [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī 750]"""
"5216","(5216) And Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Ziyād al-Aʿlam, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah that he entered the mosque while the Prophet (ﷺ) was in rukūʿ, so he performed rukūʿ before reaching the row. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \May Allah increase your eagerness"," but do not do it again.\"" ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed him"," Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us"," Tamtām — meaning Muḥammad ibn Ghālib — informed us"," Abū ʿUmar informed us"," Hammām narrated it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl from Hammām. [Authentic. See the preceding]"""
"5217","(5217) It was narrated through Yazid ibn Zurai' from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Aruba from Ziyad al-A'lam: al-Hasan informed us that Abu Bakrah narrated to him: He entered the mosque while the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) was in rukū'. He said: \So I performed rukū' without joining the row.\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \""May Allah increase your eagerness"," but do not repeat it.\""\n\nIt was reported to us by Abu al-Rudhbari"," who reported from Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah"," who reported from Abu Dawud"," who reported from Humayd ibn Mas'adah"," that Yazid ibn Zurai' informed them"," saying: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Aruba narrated it. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"""
"5218","(5218) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu al-Husayn 'Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Balkhi al-Tajir in Baghdad informed us, who said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sulami narrated to us, who said: Sa'id ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, who said: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed me, who said: Ibn Jurayj informed me from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, who said: He heard 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr saying to the people from the minbar: \When one of you enters the mosque and the people are in rukū'"," let him perform rukū' upon entering"," then let him walk in a bowing posture until he joins the row"," for that is the Sunnah.\""\n\n'Ata' said: \""And I saw him doing that.\"" [Sahih]"""
"5219","(5219) Abu al-Rudhbari informed us, who said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us, who said: Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us, who said: Shadhan narrated to us, who said: Sufyan ibn Sa'id narrated to us from Ma'mar and al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunaif, who said: Zayd ibn Thabit entered the mosque while the imam was in rukū'. He performed rukū' — that is, without the row — until he straightened up and joined the row.\n\nAnd we have narrated this previously from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq and 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both), and also the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, where he stood to the left of the Prophet (ﷺ) and the Prophet turned him around from behind until he placed him to his right, which serves as evidence in this matter, because during the turning he remained standing alone behind the Prophet and his prayer was not invalidated. [Sahih. Al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Kabir, 242.]"
"5220","(5220) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us, who said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, who said: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, who said: Nasr ibn 'Ali narrated to us, who said: Sufyan narrated to us from Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah, from his paternal uncle Anas ibn Malik, who said: \I prayed behind the Prophet (ﷺ)"," I and an orphan who was in our care"," and Umm Sulaym was behind us.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of Sufyan"," and both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Malik from Ishaq"," and it has passed previously. [Sahih. It was narrated previously under no. 5160.]"""
"5221","(5221) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, who said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, who said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, who said: Hajjaj narrated to us. Ibn Jurayj said: Ziyad informed me that Qaza'ah, a freedman of 'Abd al-Qays, informed him that he heard 'Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, say that Ibn 'Abbas said: \I prayed beside the Prophet (ﷺ) and 'A'ishah was behind us praying with us"," while I was beside the Prophet (ﷺ) praying with him.\"" [Da'if. Al-Nasa'i 804.]"""
"5222","(5222) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us, who said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us, who said: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us, who said: Hajjaj al-A'war narrated it with its like. [Da'if. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5223","(5223) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and others informed us, who said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, who said: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us, who said: al-Shafi'i informed us, who said: Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray his night prayer while I was lying across between him and the qiblah"," like a funeral bier lying across.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyaynah"," and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of al-Zuhri. [Sahih. Muslim 512.]"""
"5224","(5224) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu al-Nadr Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us, who said: Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us, who said: Musaddad narrated to us, who said: Khalid narrated to us, who said: al-Shaybani narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray while I was beside him and I was menstruating. Sometimes his garment would touch me when he prostrated.\"" She said: \""And he used to pray on a khumrah (a small mat).\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 496.]"""
"5225","(5225) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us, who said: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, who said: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us, who said: Khalid ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us from al-Shaybani, from 'Abd Allah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, who said: Maymunah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), narrated to me, and he mentioned it with its like.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5226","(5226) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Khālid ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us, from al-Shaybānī, from ʿAbdillāh ibn Shaddād ibn al-Hād, who said: Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated it to me in a similar manner.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5227","(5227) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — and Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk — both said: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us: Mālik ibn Mighwal informed us from ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah from his father, who said:\n\nI came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at al-Abṭaḥ, and he was in a tent. Bilāl came out and gave the adhān, then he went in and came out with a small vessel or a water-skin. When the people saw the wuḍūʾ of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), they hastened to take from it. Then he went in and came out with a spear (ʿanazah), and the iqāmah for the prayer was given. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed Ẓuhr as two rakʿahs and ʿAṣr as two rakʿahs, facing the spear, while a woman and a donkey passed behind it.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Mālik ibn Mighwal. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3373]"
"5228","(5228) Abū Saʿīd informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said:\n\nIf a woman does not invalidate the prayer of the one praying by being in front of him, then if she is to his right or to his left, it is even more appropriate that she should not invalidate it.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Kitāb al-Umm 1/299]"
"5229","(5229) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated to us from Abū Ḥāzim, who said:\n\nThey asked Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him) from what material the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was made. He said: \There is no one among the people who knows it better than me. It was made from the wood of the forest. So-and-so"," the freed slave of so-and-so"," made it for him. I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he ascended it"," he faced the qiblah and said the takbīr"," then he recited"," then he performed rukūʿ"," then he descended backwards and performed sajdah"," then he ascended and recited"," then he performed rukūʿ"," then he descended backwards and performed sajdah.\""\n\nBoth al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 875]"""
"5230","(5230) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Warrāq informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Rajāʾ Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd ibn Jamīl ibn Ṭarīf al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī al-Qurashī al-Iskandarānī narrated to us: Abū Ḥāzim ibn Dīnār narrated that:\n\nA group of men came to Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī and they disputed regarding the minbar — from what wood was it made? So they asked him about that. He said: \By Allah"," I know from what it is made. I saw it the first day it was placed and the first day the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat upon it. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent to a woman — whom Sahl named — saying: 'Tell your carpenter slave to make for me some planks upon which I may sit when I address the people.' So she commanded him"," and he made it from the wood of the forest. Then he brought it"," and she sent it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," and he ordered for it to be placed here. Then I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray upon it and say the takbīr while he was upon it"," then he performed rukūʿ while he was upon it"," then he descended backwards and performed sajdah at the base of the minbar"," then he returned. When he finished"," he turned to the people and said: 'O people"," I have only done this so that you may follow me and learn my prayer.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd"," and they also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī Ḥāzim from his father"," and in it: \""He said the takbīr and the people said the takbīr with him.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5231","(5231) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim informed us from his father that a group of men came to Sahl ibn Saʿd, and he mentioned its meaning.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said — as Abū Saʿīd informed us from Abū al-ʿAbbās from al-Rabīʿ from him:\n\nI prefer for the imām who is teaching those behind him to pray upon an elevated object so that those behind him may see him. But once he has taught the people one time, I would prefer that he pray on the same level as the congregation.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"5232","(5232) He cited as evidence what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated to us from Ibrāhīm from Hammām, that:\n\nḤudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer in al-Madāʾin upon a raised platform (dukkān). Abū Masʿūd took hold of his shirt and pulled him down. When he finished his prayer, he said: \Did you not know that they used to forbid that?\"" — or he said: \""Did you not know that it was forbidden?\"" He said: \""Yes"," I remembered when you pulled me.\""\n\nZiyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bakkāʾī narrated it from al-Aʿmash with the same meaning as the narration of Yaʿlā"," except that he said: Abū Masʿūd said to him: \""Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade that the imām stand above while the people remain behind him?\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 597]"""
"5233","(5233) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us: Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated it.\n\nAnd it was narrated through another chain as a musnad ḥadīth with a variation therein for this."
"5234","(5234) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Makhzūmī al-Ghaḍāʾirī informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us orally: Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Abī Naṣr al-Qūmisī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr narrated to me: al-Layth narrated to us from Zayd ibn Jubayrah from Abī Ṭuwalah from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), that:\n\nḤudhayfah ibn al-Yamān led them in prayer in al-Madāʾin upon a raised platform (dukkān). Salmān pulled him and then said to him: \I do not know whether it has been a long time or you have forgotten. Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'The imām should not pray upon an elevated place while his companions are below him'?\""\n\nHe said: \""Salmān\"" instead of \""Abū Masʿūd.\"" And it was narrated through another chain as a musnad ḥadīth with a variation therein from what preceded. [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi]"""
"5235","(5235) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ḥajjāj narrated from Ibn Jurayj: Abū Khālid informed me from ʿAdī ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī, who said:\n\nA man narrated to me — that he was with ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir in al-Madāʾin. The prayer was established, and ʿAmmār came forward and stood upon a raised platform (dukkān) to pray while the people were below him. Ḥudhayfah came forward and took hold of his hands. ʿAmmār followed him until Ḥudhayfah brought him down. When ʿAmmār finished his prayer, Ḥudhayfah said to him: \Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'When a man leads the people in prayer"," he should not stand in a place higher than their place'\"" — or something to that effect.\n\nʿAmmār said: \""That is why I followed you when you took hold of my hands.\"" [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Abū Dāwūd 598]"""
"5236","(5236) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Muthannā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us: ʿAffān narrated to us: Wuhayb narrated to us: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us. He said: I heard Abā al-Naḍr narrate from Busr ibn Saʿīd from Zayd ibn Thābit, that:\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) made a chamber in the mosque from matting, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed in it for several nights until a group of people gathered to him. Then they lost track of his voice and thought that he had fallen asleep. Some of them began to clear their throats so that he would come out to them. He said: \What I have seen of your conduct has continued until I feared that it might be prescribed upon you. If it were prescribed upon you"," you would not be able to perform it. So pray"," O people"," in your homes. For the best prayer of a person is in his home"," except the obligatory prayer.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq from ʿAffān"," and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Wuhayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6860]"""
"5237","(5237) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah narrated from Sa'id al-Maqburi from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: \The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," had a mat which he would use as a partition at night to pray behind. The people began praying behind him in his prayer"," and he would spread it out during the day. Then one night the people gathered in large numbers. He said: 'O people"," take upon yourselves only as much of deeds as you are able"," for Allah does not grow weary until you grow weary"," and the most beloved of deeds to Allah are those upon which consistency is maintained"," even if they are few.'\"" And the family of Muhammad"," when they performed a deed"," would persist upon it.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna. [Sahih. Bukhari 5523]"""
"5238","(5238) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: Mu'tamir narrated from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id from Abu Salamah from 'A'ishah: \The Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him"," would use a mat as a partition at night to pray behind"," and he would spread it out during the day and sit upon it. She said: The people began coming to the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," and praying behind him in his prayer until their numbers increased greatly. He turned to them and said: 'O people"," take upon yourselves only as much of deeds as you are able"," for Allah does not grow weary until you grow weary"," and the most beloved of deeds to Allah are those that are done consistently"," even if they are few.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"""
"5239","(5239) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Sa'd informed me: Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Harashi narrated to us: 'Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id.\n\n(And) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me: Abu Yahya Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Ruwayani narrated to us: Ibrahim, who is ibn Musa al-Farra', informed us: 'Isa, who is ibn Yunus, narrated from Yahya from 'Amrah from 'A'ishah: \The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," used to pray in his room"," and the wall of the room was short. The people saw the figure of the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," so a group of people stood and prayed behind him in his prayer. In the morning they spoke about it. The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," stood the second night to pray"," and a group of people stood and prayed behind him in his prayer. They did this for two or three nights. Then after that"," the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," sat and did not come out. When morning came"," the people mentioned it"," and he said: 'I feared that the night prayer might be made obligatory upon you.'\""\n\nThe wording is that of the hadith of Abu 'Amr. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad from 'Abdah.\n\nAnd in the context of these hadiths there is evidence that what is meant by \""the room\"" in the narration of Hushaym from Yahya ibn Sa'id is the unrestricted sense"," and in the hadith of Anas ibn Malik there is clarification of that. And in the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit there is evidence that the room was in the mosque. [Sahih. Bukhari 696]"""
"5240","(5240) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us with the hadith of Hushaym: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us: Abu 'Abdullah Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Sufi narrated to us: Khalaf ibn Salim narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Yahya narrated from 'Amrah from 'A'ishah, who said: \The Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him"," prayed in his room"," and the people followed him from behind the room"," praying behind him in his prayer.\"" [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"""
"5241","(5241) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Tahir al-Muhammadabadi informed us: Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah al-Sa'di informed us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Humayd al-Tawil narrated from Anas ibn Malik: \The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," was praying one night in his room when some of his Companions came to him and prayed behind him in his prayer. He shortened it and entered the house"," then came out again. He did this several times—each time he would pray and then leave and enter. When morning came"," they said: 'O Messenger of Allah"," we prayed with you last night and we would love for you to prolong your prayer.' He said: 'I knew of your presence; I did that deliberately.'\""\n\n[Sahih. Ahmad 3/103]"""
"5242","(5242) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Amr ibn Matar narrated to us: Yahya ibn Muhammad narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Husayn from 'Amir ibn Dhu'ayb, who said: It was said to Ibn 'Abbas: \Do you pray behind these people in the maqsurah?\"" He said: \""Yes"," they feared that we might cause them hardship.\"" [Da'if]"""
"5243","(5243) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muqri' in Kufah narrated to us: 'Abbad ibn Ya'qub al-Asadi informed us: Ibn Abi Yahya, that is Ibrahim, narrated from Dawud ibn al-Husayn from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: \There is no harm in praying in the courtyard of the mosque and on the paved area behind the Imam in prayer.\"" [Very weak]"""
"5244","(5244) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari informed us: My grandfather Abu Muhammad Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us: Abu 'Ali Muhammad ibn 'Amr informed us: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated from Salih, the freed slave of al-Taw'amah, who said: \Abu Hurayrah and I used to pray on top of the mosque"," praying the obligatory prayer behind the Imam.\"" [Sahih. Ibn Abi Shaybah 6159]"""
"5245","(5245) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated to me: Salih, the freed slave of al-Taw'amah, informed me: \He saw Abu Hurayrah praying on top of the mosque behind the Imam in the mosque.\"" [Very weak. 'Abd al-Razzaq 4888]"""
"5246","(5246) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' said: al-Shafi'i said: \Some women prayed with 'A'ishah"," the wife of the Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him"," in her room"," and she said: 'Do not pray behind the Imam"," for you are behind a barrier from him.'\""\n\nAl-Shafi'i"," may Allah the Most High have mercy upon him"," said: \""And just as 'A'ishah said regarding her room—if she said it—we say the same.\""\n\nThe Shaykh said: \""And it has been narrated to us from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib"," may Allah be pleased with him"," that he said: 'There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque.' And that has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him"," in a marfu' narration.\"" [I could not find it.]"""
"5247","(5247) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me: ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated from Ṣāliḥ ibn Ibrāhīm, who said: I saw Anas ibn Mālik pray Jumuʿah in the houses of Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, following the prayer of the Imam in the mosque, and between the houses of Ḥumayd and the mosque there was a path. [Very weak. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī, 242]"
"5248","(5248) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh informed us: Yazīd ibn al-Haytham informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth informed us: al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Sufyān, from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from ʿAbd Rabbih, who said: I saw Anas ibn Mālik pray Jumuʿah following the Imam in a room near the courtyard of the mosque of Baṣrah. [Very weak]"
"5249","(5249) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father: that he used to pray Jumuʿah in the houses of Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān in the year al-Walīd performed the Ḥajj, when the people were numerous, and between them and the mosque there was a path. [Very weak]"
"5250","(5250) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us: it was read to Ibn Wahb: Mālik ibn Anas narrated to you, he said: more than one person in whom I have confidence narrated to me.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mahrjānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Ibn Bukayr informed us: Mālik narrated from the trustworthy person in his view: that the people used to enter the chambers of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ after the death of the Prophet ﷺ and pray Jumuʿah therein. He said: the mosque had become too narrow for its people, so they expanded by means of them, and the chambers of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ were not part of the mosque, but their doors opened into the mosque.\n\nMālik said: Whoever prays in any part of the mosque's courtyards adjoining it or in its open spaces that are adjacent to it, that suffices him, and this has always been the practice of the people; no one among the people of fiqh has ever objected to it. Mālik said: As for a locked house that cannot be entered without permission, it is not appropriate for anyone to pray the Jumuʿah prayer with the Imam in it, even if it is close, because it is not part of the mosque. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5251","(5251) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfaraʾinī informed us: Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Barbahārī informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us: al-Ḥumaydī informed us: Sufyān informed us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār and Abū al-Zubayr, as Allah willed, both heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh say: Muʿādh ibn Jabal used to pray ʿIshāʾ with the Prophet ﷺ, then return to his people, Banū Salamah, and lead them in it. One night the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the ʿIshāʾ. Muʿādh prayed it with him, then returned and led his people in prayer. He began Sūrat al-Baqarah. A man from behind him stepped aside and prayed alone. When they finished, they said: \You have become a hypocrite"," O so-and-so!\"" He said: \""I have not become a hypocrite"," but I will go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and inform him.\"" He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," you delayed the ʿIshāʾ last night"," and Muʿādh prayed it with you"," then returned and led us in prayer. He began Sūrat al-Baqarah"," so I stepped aside and prayed by myself. We are people of water-drawers who work with our hands.\"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to Muʿādh and said: \""O Muʿādh"," you are a trial! You are a trial! Recite with such-and-such sūrah and such-and-such sūrah.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously reported as no. 5100]"""
"5252","(5252) ʿAmr said: \He enumerated sūrahs.\"" Sufyān said: Abū al-Zubayr said: the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \""Recite Sabbiḥi isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā"," wa al-Samāʾi wa al-Ṭāriq"," wa al-Samāʾi dhāt al-Burūj"," wa al-Shamsi wa ḍuḥāhā"," wa al-Layli idhā yaghshā"," and the like of these.\"" I said to ʿAmr: \""Abū al-Zubayr used to say that the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Recite Sabbiḥi isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā"," wa al-Samāʾi wa al-Ṭāriq"," wa al-Samāʾi dhāt al-Burūj"," wa al-Shamsi wa ḍuḥāhā"," wa al-Layli idhā yaghshā.'\"" ʿAmr said: \""These or the like of these.\""\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us: al-Ḥumaydī informed us: Sufyān informed us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated from Jābir"," and he mentioned it. Then he mentioned the addition of Abū al-Zubayr. And with this meaning"," a group narrated it from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5253","(5253) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī narrated it from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, and said in the hadith: \A man turned away"," then gave the salām"," then prayed alone"," and departed.\"" Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Yaʿlā informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād informed us: Sufyān informed us"," and he mentioned it.\n\nMuslim reported it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād."""
"5254","(5254) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Sufyān narrated from Abū Ḥāzim, from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī, who was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: A dispute arose between the Aws and the Khazraj, and some of them reviled others. The Prophet ﷺ came and was informed, so he came to them and delayed. Bilāl gave the adhān, and the Prophet ﷺ delayed. When he delayed, the iqāmah for the prayer was given. Abū Bakr stepped forward to lead the people in prayer. The Prophet ﷺ came on his return from that. He said: the people made way for him until he reached the row next to Abū Bakr. The people clapped, and Abū Bakr would not turn during the prayer. When he heard the clapping, he turned, and there was the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ signaled to him to remain in his place. Abū Bakr raised his head toward the sky and stepped backward. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stepped forward and led them in prayer. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished the prayer, he said: \What prevented you from remaining in your place?\"" He said: \""It was not fitting for Allah to see the son of Abī Quḥāfah standing before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""What is the matter with you that when something befalls you in your prayer"," you clap? That is for women. When something befalls a man in his prayer"," let him say 'Subḥān Allāh.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim reported it from various routes from Abū Ḥāzim. The hadiths regarding his takbīr"," then his going out for ghusl"," his return"," and the one who had already said the takbīr following him before his return have been mentioned in the section on the junub. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2544]"""
"5255","(5255) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Ḥuṣayn, from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, who said: I saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned part of the hadith. He said: When he passed between the two rows, he would stand. If he saw a gap, he said: \Straighten up!\"" until he saw no gap among them. Then he stepped forward and said the takbīr. He said: Sometimes he would recite Sūrat Yūsuf"," or Sūrat al-Naḥl"," or the like of that"," in the first rakʿah until the people gathered. He said: He had only said the takbīr when I heard him say: \""The dog has killed me","\"" or \""The dog has eaten me","\"" when he stabbed him. The ruffian flew with the double-edged dagger; he did not pass anyone to his right or left without stabbing him"," until he stabbed thirteen men. Nine of them died. When a Muslim man saw that"," he threw a cloak over him. When the ruffian thought he was caught"," he killed himself. He said: ʿUmar took the hand of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and put him forward. He said: Whoever was next to ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," saw what he saw. As for the sides of the mosque"," they did not know"," except that they had lost the voice of ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," and they were saying: \""Subḥān Allāh"," Subḥān Allāh.\"" He said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf led them in a light prayer"," and he mentioned the hadith. Al-Bukhārī reported it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl.\n\nIn this there is evidence for the permissibility of appointment of a deputy"," as al-Shāfiʿī"," may Allah have mercy on him"," permitted it in his later view. In his earlier view"," he did not permit it. He would say to those who cited this against him: \""You narrate that from Ḥuṣayn"," while Abū Isḥāq reports from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn that he did not say the takbīr.\"" He said: \""And likewise the hadith of our companions. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān only stepped forward in the morning after ʿUmar had been stabbed by an hour"," and he recited two short sūrahs"," hastening before sunrise.\"" This was the statement of al-Shāfiʿī in his earlier view. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3497]"""
"5256","(5256) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Bāqillānī informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr informed us: Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah informed us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn al-Awdī, who said: I was present with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, when he was stabbed. He said: Abū Luʾluʾah came to him while he was straightening the rows. He stabbed him and stabbed twelve other men. He said: I saw ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, stretching out his hand, saying: \Seize the dog"," for he has killed me!\"" A man came from behind him and seized him. He said: ʿUmar was carried to his house. The physician came to him and said: \""Which drink do you prefer?\"" He said: \""Nabīdh.\"" He said: Nabīdh was brought"," and he drank from it"," and it came out from one of his wounds. He said: \""This is only pus"," the pus of blood.\"" He said: Milk was called for"," and he drank it. The physician said: \""Make a will"," O Amīr al-Muʾminīn"," for by Allah"," I do not think you will survive until evening.\"" Kaʿb came to him and said: \""Did I not tell you that you would only die as a martyr?\"" And you used to say: \""How"," when I am in the island of Arabia?\"" He said: A man said: \""The prayer"," O servants of Allah! The sun is about to rise!\"" He said: They pushed forward until they put ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf forward. He recited the two shortest sūrahs in the Qurʾān: {Wa al-ʿAṣr} and {Innā aʿṭaynāka al-Kawthar}. Thus said Abū Isḥāq.\n\nAnd likewise Maimūn ibn Mihrān narrated it from Ibn ʿUmar. And we have narrated it from Abū Rāfiʿ"," similar to the narration of Ḥuṣayn from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn. And Ḥuṣayn has the best narration of the hadith compared to others. Al-Bukhārī reported it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ"," and it is as though he was the most precise. We have also narrated the appointment of a deputy from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"," may Allah be pleased with him"," on another occasion. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5257","(5257) Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya — and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both narrated, saying: Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakim informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us. And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Amr ibn al-Harith informed you, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Zur'ah ibn Ibrahim, from Khalid ibn al-Lajlaj, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, one day led the people in prayer. When he sat in the first two rak'ahs, he prolonged the sitting. When he rose to stand, he stepped backward, took hold of a man from among the people, and placed him in his position. When the time for 'Asr came, he led the people in prayer. When he finished, he took hold of the side of the minbar, praised Allah, and extolled Him, then said: \To proceed"," O people"," I performed wudu for prayer and passed by a woman from among my family"," and there occurred between me and her what Allah willed to occur. When I was in my prayer"," I found moisture (i.e."," ritual impurity)"," so I gave myself a choice between two matters: either I should feel shy before you and be audacious toward Allah"," or I should feel shy before Allah and be audacious before you. And feeling shy before Allah and being audacious before you was more beloved to me. So I went out"," performed wudu"," and repeated my prayer. Whoever does as I did should do as I did.\""\n\nIt was narrated regarding the permissibility of appointing a substitute (istikhlaf) from Ali ibn Abi Talib"," may Allah be pleased with him. [Weak. Ibn Asakir in his Tarikh"," 19/4]"""
"5258","(5258) Abu al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Ma'ruf al-Faqih al-Mihrajani narrated to us in it: Bishr ibn Ahmad ibn Bishr narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Qattan narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Sumay'i narrated to us: Abu Razin said: I prayed behind Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and he had a nosebleed. He turned back, took hold of a man's hand, and placed him forward. The man led the prayer, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, went out. [Sahih]"
"5259","(5259) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad: Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu Sa'id — Abd al-Rahman — and Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman both said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Namir narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: Khalid ibn Abdullah ibn Rabah al-Sulami informed me that he prayed with Mu'awiyah on the day he was stabbed in Aelia (Jerusalem) one rak'ah, and Mu'awiyah was stabbed when he completed it. He wanted to raise his head from prostration, so Mu'awiyah said to the people: \Complete your prayers.\"" Each person stood and completed his own prayer"," and no one appointed a successor"," nor did the people appoint anyone for him. [Weak. al-Bukhari in his Tarikh"," 3/159]"""
"5260","(5260) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir, from Anas, who said: \I never prayed behind any imam whose prayer was lighter and more complete than that of the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya"," and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Muslim 469]"""
"5261","(5261) Abu Muhammad — Janah ibn Nadhir al-Qadi narrated to us in Kufa: Abu Ja'far ibn Duha'im narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Hunayn al-Qazzaz narrated to us: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: \The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," used to shorten the prayer and perfect it.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Ma'mar"," from Abd al-Warith. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 674]"""
"5262","(5262) Abu Nasr — Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Muhammad al-Shirazi al-Faqih narrated to us: Abu Abdullah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Yahya ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' —\n\n(And) Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: \The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," used to shorten the prayer and perfect it.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Rabi'. [Sahih. Muslim 469]"""
"5263","(5263) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-Adl and Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan both informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Shakir informed us: Abu Usamah informed us: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah —\n\n(And) Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq and Ali ibn Hamshadh both narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, who said: \The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," was the lightest of people in prayer while maintaining its perfection.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. al-Nasa'i 824]"""
"5264","(5264) Abu Muhammad — Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us —\n\n(And) Abu Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us in Nishapur, and Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-Adl informed us in Baghdad, both saying: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — all saying: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Qays, from Abu Mas'ud, who said: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: \O Messenger of Allah"," I miss the morning prayer because of the length so-and-so makes with us.\"" The Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," said: \""Among you there are those who cause aversion. Whoever leads the people in prayer should lighten it"," for among them are the elderly"," the infirm"," and those with a need.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi Umar"," from Suf ibn Uyaynah. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 90]"""
"5265","(5265) Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar informed us: Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: \O Messenger of Allah"," I can hardly ever catch the prayer because of the length so-and-so makes with us.\"" I never saw the Messenger of Allah"," peace and blessings be upon him"," more angry in a sermon than he was that day. He said: \""O people"," among you there are those who cause aversion. Whoever prays with the people should lighten it"," for among them are the sick"," the weak"," and those with a need.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Kathir. [Sahih. Same meaning as the one before it]"""
"5266","(5266) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Abdullah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Husayn ibn Hasan ibn Muhajir and Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Mihran both narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. My father said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, who narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \When one of you leads the people in prayer"," he should lighten it"," for among them are the weak"," the elderly"," and those with a need.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abd al-Malik ibn Shu'ayb.\n\nAnd Ibn Wahb narrated it from Yunus"," from Ibn Shihab"," from Abu Salamah"," from Abu Hurayrah. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 671]"""
"5267","(5267) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Isma'il ibn Ahmad al-Jurjani informed us: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah informed us: Harmalah ibn Yahya informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yunus narrated from Ibn Shihab: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When one of you leads the people in prayer"," let him shorten it"," for among the people are the weak"," the sick"," and those who have pressing needs.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Harmalah ibn Yahya. [Sahih. See the hadith before it.]"""
"5268","(5268) And Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us: Abu Muslim Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah informed us: Abu al-Walid and Sulayman ibn Harb both said: Shu'bah narrated from 'Amr ibn Murrah from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: I heard 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: \When you lead a people in prayer"," lighten the prayer for them.\""\n\n[Sahih. Muslim 468]"""
"5269","(5269) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn Faris informed us: Yunus ibn Habib informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Shu'bah informed me: 'Amr ibn Murrah said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab say: 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As narrated: The last injunction the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave me was: \When you lead a people in prayer"," lighten the prayer for them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ghundar from Shu'bah. [Sahih. See the hadith before it.]"""
"5270","(5270) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastuyah informed us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us: al-Humaydi informed us: Sufyan informed us: Isma'il — that is, Ibn Abi Khalid — narrated from his father: I came to Madinah and stayed with Abu Hurayrah, and there was a kinship between him and my freedmen. He used to lead the people in prayer and would shorten it. I said: \O Abu Hurayrah"," was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) like this?\"" He said: \""Yes"," and even more concise.\""\n\n[Da'if. Abu Ya'la 6422]"""
"5271","(5271) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us: 'Ali ibn Dawud informed us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas informed us.\n\n(Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi in Hamadhan informed us: Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn informed us: Adam informed us:) Shu'bah informed us: Muharib ibn Dithar informed me: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abdillah al-Ansari say: A man came with two of his water-carrying camels, and the night had set in. He came upon Mu'adh ibn Jabal praying Maghrib. He left his camels and went to Mu'adh to pray with him. Mu'adh recited al-Baqarah, or al-Nisa'. The man departed, and it reached him that Mu'adh had spoken ill of him. So he came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and complained to him about Mu'adh. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \Are you a source of tribulation?\"" — or he said: \""Are you a trial?\"" — three times. \""Had you not recited Sabbihisma Rabbika al-A'la"," wa al-Shamsi wa duhaha"," wa al-Layli idha yaghsha"," for behind you pray the elderly"," those with pressing needs"," and the weak.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas. Thus said Muharib ibn Dithar from Jabir: Maghrib. And 'Amr ibn Dinari"," Abu al-Zubayr"," and 'Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam said from Jabir: 'Isha'. As for the hadith of 'Amr"," it has already passed in this book in two places. And as for the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr — [Sahih. Previously cited as no. 5100]"""
"5272","(5272) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakka and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Bahr ibn Nasr said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Ibn Lahi'ah and al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed you from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, who informed him: Mu'adh ibn Jabal prayed 'Isha' and lengthened it for his companions. The Prophet (ﷺ) was informed of that, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to Mu'adh: \Are you a source of tribulation? Lighten the prayer for the people and recite wa al-Shamsi wa duhaha"," wa Sabbihisma Rabbika al-A'la"," and the like of that"," and do not impose hardship upon the people.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah and others from al-Layth ibn Sa'd"," except that he added: \""Recite Iqra' bismi Rabbika [al-'Alaq: 1] and wa al-Layli idha yaghsha","\"" and he did not say: \""and do not impose hardship upon the people.\"" As for the hadith of 'Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam — [Sahih. Previously cited as no. 5100]"""
"5273","(5273) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Yahya ibn Habib informed us: Khalid ibn al-Harith informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated from 'Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam from Jabir: He mentioned the story of Mu'adh and that account. He said: Mu'adh used to pray 'Isha' with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then return and pray with his companions. One night he returned and prayed with them, and a young man from his people prayed behind him. When the prayer became long for the young man, he prayed and left. He took hold of the reins of his camel and departed. When Mu'adh finished the prayer, that was mentioned to him, and he said: \Indeed this one is a hypocrite. I shall inform the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) of what he did.\"" The young man said: \""And I too shall inform the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) of what he did.\"" They went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the morning"," and Mu'adh informed him of what the young man had done. The young man said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," he stays long with you"," then returns and lengthens the prayer for us.\"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""Are you a source of tribulation"," O Mu'adh?\"" And he said to the young man: \""How do you pray"," O my brother's son?\"" He said: \""I recite the Opening of the Book"," I ask Allah for Paradise"," and I seek refuge in Him from the Fire. And I do not know what your murmuring and Mu'adh's murmuring are.\"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""Mu'adh and I are both concerning these two\"" — or something to that effect. The young man said: \""But Mu'adh will know when the people arrive and have been informed that the enemy has drawn near.\"" They arrived"," and the young man had been martyred. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to Mu'adh after that: \""What happened to my opponent and your opponent?\"" He said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," Allah spoke the truth and I lied — he was martyred.\"""""
"5274","(5274) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Musa ibn Isma'il informed us: Talib ibn Habib said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir narrating from Hazm ibn Abi Ka'b: He came to Mu'adh while he was leading a people in the Maghrib prayer in this hadith. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \O Mu'adh"," do not be a source of tribulation. For behind you pray the elderly"," the weak"," those with pressing needs"," and the traveler.\""\n\nThus he said. And the earlier narrations regarding 'Isha' are more accurate"," and Allah knows best. [Sahih in other than this chain. Abu Dawud 791]"""
"5275","(5275) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik informed us.\n\n(Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi informed us: 'Abdillah — meaning al-Qa'nabi — narrated from Malik from Abu al-Zinad from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: \When one of you leads the people in prayer"," let him shorten it"," for among them are the sick"," the weak"," and the elderly. And when one of you prays alone"," let him lengthen as he wishes.\""\n\nIn the narration of al-Shafi'i: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""When one of you leads the people in prayer"," let him shorten it"," for among them are the sick and the weak. And when he prays alone"," let him lengthen as he wishes.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdillah ibn Yusuf from Malik"," and Muslim narrated it from another source from Abu al-Zinad"," and some of them added \""the young child\"" in it. [Sahih. Previously cited as no. 5266]"""
"5276","(5276) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Nasir al-Khuldi informed us: Musa ibn Harun informed us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us: al-Mughirah narrated from Abu al-Zinad from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \When one of you leads the people in prayer"," let him shorten it"," for among them are the young"," the elderly"," the weak"," and the sick. And when he prays alone"," let him pray however he wishes.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah. [Sahih. Muslim 467]"""
"5277","(5277) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar reported from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When one of you leads the people in prayer"," let him shorten the Salah"," for among them are the elderly"," the weak"," and the sick. And if he prays alone"," let him lengthen his Salah as he wishes.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 467]"""
"5278","(5278) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated. And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us by dictation, and the wording is his; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us; my father narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ al-Thaqafī narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \Lead your people in prayer.\"" He said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," I find something within myself.\"" He said: \""Come near.\"" So he seated me before him and placed his hand on my chest between my breasts. Then he said: \""Turn around.\"" So he placed both hands on my back between my shoulders. Then he said: \""Lead your people in prayer. Whoever leads a people"," let him shorten it"," for among them are the elderly"," among them are the young"," among them are the sick"," and among them is the one with a need. When one of you prays alone"," let him pray as he wishes.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 468]"""
"5279","(5279) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Ḥajjāj narrated; Ibn Jurayj informed me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym from Nāfiʿ ibn Sarjis, who said: \We visited Abū Wāqid al-Laythī during the illness in which he died"," and we heard him say: 'The Prophet ﷺ was the briefest of people in leading the people in Salah"," and the longest of people in praying for himself.'\""\n\n[Ḥasan — Aḥmad 5/218]"""
"5280","(5280) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us; al-Manīʿī and Abū Yaʿlā both narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us; al-Awzāʿī informed us.\n\nAnd Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid and Bishr ibn Bakr narrated from al-Awzāʿī; from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr; from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah; from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \I stand up for Salah intending to lengthen it"," but I hear the crying of a child"," so I shorten it"," not wishing to cause distress to its mother.\""\n\nThe wording is that of al-Rūdhbārī. In the hadith of al-Adīb"," it says: \""in the Salah","\"" and he said: \""so I shorten my Salah.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of al-Walīd ibn Muslim from al-Awzāʿī"," then he said: \""It was corroborated by Bishr ibn Bakr and Ibn al-Mubārak.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 675]"""
"5281","(5281) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū Salamah, meaning Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, narrated to us.\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb informed us from al-Ḥārith, meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; from Sālim; from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: \I stand up for Salah intending to lengthen it"," but I hear the crying of a child"," so I shorten my Salah"," because of what I know of the distress of his mother caused by his crying.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ"," saying: \""And Mūsā said: Abān narrated to us.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 677]"""
"5282","(5282) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb informed us.\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb reported from al-Ḥārith, meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; from Sālim; from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to shorten the Salah"," and he would lead us in prayer reciting al-Ṣāffāt.\""\n\n[Ḥasan — al-Nasāʾī 826]"""
"5283","(5283) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Sukayn ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; al-Muthannā al-Aḥmar narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qays narrated, saying: \I asked Anas about the extent of the Prophet's ﷺ recitation in Salah.\"" He said: \""He instructed al-Naḍr ibn Anas"," or one of his sons"," so he led us in the Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr prayer"," reciting {Wa al-Mursalāt} and {ʿAmma yatasāʾalūn}.\""\n\n[Ḍaʿīf]"""
"5284","(5284) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī al-Ṭūsī informed us there; Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Ṣamad, meaning Ibn Ḥassān, narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb that he heard Jābir ibn Samurah say: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform the prayers in a manner similar to your prayers that you perform today"," but he used to shorten them. His Salah was lighter than your Salah. He would recite in the Fajr prayer al-Wāqiʿah and the like of it from the surahs.\""\n\n[Ḥasan — Aḥmad 5/104]"""
"5285","(5285) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān said: \We memorized it from al-Aʿmash"," but we did not find it here in Makkah.\"" He said: \""I heard Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ narrating from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj al-Ḥaḍramī from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī"," who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The one who leads the people in prayer should be the one most proficient in reciting the Book of Allah. If they are equal in recitation"," then the one most knowledgeable of the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah"," then the one who emigrated earliest. If they are equal in emigration"," then the oldest among them. And no man should lead another in his domain of authority"," and no one should sit upon his place of honour in his house"," except with his permission.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 673]"""
"5286","(5286) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād, and Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Rūḥ ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to me; al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim from Sulaymān al-Aʿmash from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj from ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmr, Abī Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The one who leads the people in prayer should be the one who has memorized the most Qurʾān. If they are equal in Qurʾān"," then the one who emigrated earliest. If the emigration is equal"," then the one most versed in understanding (fiqh). If understanding is equal"," then the oldest among them. And no man should be led in prayer by another in his domain of authority"," and no one should sit upon his place of honour in his house unless he gives him permission.\"" Thus did Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrate it from al-Aʿmash.\n\nAnd the group narrated it from al-Aʿmash with the first wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ — see the preceding]"""
"5287","(5287) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; Saʿīd —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us; Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated to us; Saʿīd — from Qatādah, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When there are three of them"," let one of them lead them in prayer"," and the most deserving of them to lead the prayer is the one who has most memorized the Qur'an.\"" The wording of both is the same.\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 672]"""
"5288","(5288) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hishām — from Qatādah, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \When there are three of them on a journey"," let one of them lead them in prayer"," and the most deserving of them to lead the prayer is the one who has most memorized the Qur'an.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Muʿādh ibn Hishām from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5289","(5289) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Shādhān al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us; Sharīk narrated to us — from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿAbdullāh, who said: \When we learned ten verses of the Qur'an from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," we would not learn the next ten verses that were revealed after them until we understood what was in them.\"" It was said to Sharīk: \""In terms of action?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Ḥākim 1/743]"""
"5290","(5290) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh al-Mahrajānī narrated to us therein; Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad ibn Bishr informed us; Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Junād al-Ḥalabī narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated to us — from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah, from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAwf, who said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar say: \We lived for a period of our time"," and one of us would be given faith (īmān) before the Qur'an. A sūrah would be revealed upon Muḥammad (ﷺ)"," and he would learn its lawful and its unlawful"," its commands and its prohibitions"," and what one should pause at from it — just as you learn the Qur'an today. Then I have seen today men to whom the Qur'an is given before faith. He reads from its opening to its closing"," not knowing what its command is"," nor its prohibition"," nor what one should pause at from it"," and he scatters it like scattered dates.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Ḥākim 1/91]"""
"5291","(5291) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us — from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Abū al-Safar, who said: Ḥudhayfah said: \We are a people who were given faith before the Qur'an. And you are a people who were given the Qur'an before faith.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5292","(5292) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥurayth narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us — from Ḥammād ibn Najīḥ, from Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī, from Jundub, who said: \We were young men"," sturdy lads"," in the company of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," and we learned faith before the Qur'an. Then we learned the Qur'an"," and it increased us in faith. But today you learn the Qur'an before faith.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Mājah 61]"""
"5293","(5293) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb al-Faqīh informed us — dictating; Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated to us — from Abū Qilābah; Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith narrated to us, who said: \We came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while we were young men of similar age"," and we stayed with him for twenty nights. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was merciful and gentle. When he thought that we had begun to long for our families and missed them"," he asked us about those we had left behind"," and we informed him. He said: 'Return to your families"," establish prayer among them"," teach them"," and command them.'\"" And he mentioned some things which I remember and some which I do not remember: \""And pray as you have seen me pray. When the time for prayer comes"," let one of you give the adhān and let the oldest of you lead you in prayer.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā"," and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Abī ʿUmar"," both from al-Thaqafī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 602]"""
"5294","(5294) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Ismāʿīl and Maslamah ibn Muḥammad — the meaning is the same — narrated to us, from Khālid, from Abū Qilābah, from Mālik ibn Ḥuwayrith, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him or to a companion of his: \When the time for prayer comes"," give the adhān"," then pronounce the iqāmah"," and let the older of you two lead you in prayer.\""\n\nIn the ḥadīth of Maslamah"," he said: \""We were on that day close to one another in knowledge.\"" And in the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl"," Khālid said: \""I said to Abū Qilābah: 'Where is the recitation (qirā'ah)?' He said: 'They were close to one another in that.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See 4998]"""
"5295","(5295) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālūyah al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated to us — from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: \This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to me.\"" He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""The people are followers of Quraysh in this matter: their Muslims follow their Muslims"," and their disbelievers follow their disbelievers.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3305]"""
"5296","(5296) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; ʿĀṣim ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — from his father, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \This matter (authority) will remain among Quraysh as long as there remain two people on earth.\""\n\nal-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3310]"""
"5297","(5297) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Adami in Makkah informed us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar narrated from al-Zuhri from Ibn Abi Hathmah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Do not teach Quraysh"," but learn from them. Do not go ahead of them"," and do not lag behind them. For a Qurayshi possesses the strength of two men among others in judgment.\"" (i.e."," in opinion.) This is a mursal narration"," and it has been narrated as mawṣūl but is not strong. [Ṣaḥīgh for its other routes. Ibn Abī Shaybah 32386]"""
"5298","(5298) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated; 'Ubaydullah narrated from Shaybān from al-A'mash from Sahl, who was known as Abu Asad, from Bukayr al-Jazari from Anas that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \The leaders (al-a'immah) are from Quraysh.\"" [Ṣaḥīgh for its other routes. Aḥmad 3/129]"""
"5299","(5299) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Yazid al-Hafiz informed us — and I asked him — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah al-'Asqalani, who was among the notables of al-Shām, narrated; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, Abu Khalid al-Qāḍī, a descendant of 'Attab ibn Usayd, informed us; Abu 'Asim informed us; 'Azrah ibn Thābit narrated from Alba' ibn Ahmar from Abu Zayd al-Anṣāri, who was 'Amr ibn Akhṭab, from the Prophet (ﷺ) who said: \When there are three of them"," let the one who has most memorized the Book of Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," lead them in prayer. If they are equal in recitation"," then the eldest among them. If they are equal in age"," then the one with the most handsome face.\"" [Munkar. Narrated by al-Dalimi as in al-Fawa'id al-Majmu'ah 1/32]"""
"5300","(5300) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated; Abu Dawud narrated; Ahmad ibn Ṣalih narrated; Ibn Wahb narrated; Mu'awiyah ibn Ṣalih narrated from al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith from Makhul from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Jihād is obligatory upon you with every commander (amir)"," whether he is righteous or wicked. And prayer is obligatory upon you behind every Muslim"," whether he is righteous or wicked"," even if he commits major sins. And prayer is obligatory upon every Muslim"," whether he is righteous or wicked"," even if he commits major sins.\"" [Da'if. Abu Dawud 2533]"""
"5301","(5301) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Muslim narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Nafi' that Ibn 'Umar withdrew during the fighting between Ibn al-Zubayr and al-Hajjaj at Mina, and he prayed behind al-Hajjaj."
"5302","(5302) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffar narrated; Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn 'Asim narrated; Muhammad ibn Muṣaffa narrated; al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated; Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated from 'Umayr ibn Hani' who said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan sent me with letters to al-Hajjaj. I came to him and he had set up forty catapults against the House (the Ka'bah). I saw Ibn 'Umar — when the prayer time came with al-Hajjaj, he prayed with him, and when Ibn al-Zubayr came, he prayed with him. I said to him: \O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman"," do you pray with these people despite their deeds?\"" He said: \""O brother of the people of al-Sham"," I am not one to praise them"," nor do I obey any created being in disobedience to the Creator.\"" He said: I said: \""What do you say about the people of al-Sham?\"" He said: \""I am not one to praise them.\"" I said: \""What is your view about the people of Makkah?\"" He said: \""I am not one to excuse them. They fight for the world and hurl themselves into the Fire like flies plunging into broth.\"" I said: \""What is your view about this pledge of allegiance that Marwan has taken from us?\"" Ibn 'Umar said: \""When we used to give our pledge of allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) upon hearing and obedience"," he would instruct us: '(Do so) to the best of your ability.'\"" [For its other routes. Ibn Abī Shaybah 2/84]"""
"5303","(5303) Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father that al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to pray behind Marwan. He (the narrator) said: \Did they not pray when they returned to their homes?\"" He said: \""No"," by Allah"," they did not do more than the prayer of the imams.\"" [Da'if. Ibn Abī Shaybah 7560]"""
"5304","(5304) Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi informed us; Ibrahim ibn Abdullah al-Aṣbahani informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn Faris narrated; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari narrated; he said, 'Abdullah told us from Mu'awiyah ibn Ṣalih from 'Abd al-Karim al-Bakka'i who said: \I met ten of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ)"," all of them praying behind the unjust imams (a'immat al-jawr).\"" [Da'if. al-Bukhari in his Tarikh 6/90]"""
"5305","(5305) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Dawud al-Munadi al-Mukharrami in Baghdad narrated; Yunus, who was Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addib, narrated; Abu Shihab narrated; Yunus ibn 'Ubayd narrated from Nafi' who said: Ibn 'Umar used to greet the Khashabiyyah and the Khawarij with salam while they were fighting. He (Ibn 'Umar) said: \Whoever calls: 'Come to prayer","' I respond to him. Whoever calls: 'Come to success (falah)","' I respond to him. But whoever calls: 'Come to kill your Muslim brother and seize his wealth","' I say: No.\"" [Hasan. Abu Nu'aym in al-'Ulyah 1/309]"""
"5306","(5306) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated; al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated; 'Abdullah, meaning Ibn Maslamah, narrated. And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us, and the wording is his; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated; Abu Dawud narrated; al-Qa'nabi narrated from Malik from Abu Hazim ibn Dinar from Sahl ibn Sa'd that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went to Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf to reconcile between them. The time for prayer came, and the mu'adhdhin came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: \Will you lead the people in prayer so that I may pronounce the iqamah?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" So Abu Bakr led the prayer. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came while the people were in prayer. He made his way through until he stood in the row. The people began clapping. Abu Bakr"," may Allah be pleased with him"," would not turn around during the prayer. When the people increased in clapping"," he turned and saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) signaled to him to remain in his place. Abu Bakr raised his hands and praised Allah for what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had commanded him to do. Then Abu Bakr stepped back until he stood in the row"," and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came forward and led the prayer. When he finished"," he said: \""O Abu Bakr"," what prevented you from remaining in place when I commanded you?\"" Abu Bakr"," may Allah be pleased with him"," said: \""It was not fitting for the son of Abu Quhafah to pray in front of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""Why is it that I see you clapping so much? Whoever is afflicted by something in his prayer"," let him say 'SubhanAllah","' for when he says 'SubhanAllah","' attention is turned to him. Clapping is for women.\"" Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their two Sahihs from the hadith of Malik and others from Abu Hazim. [Sahih. Previously mentioned under no. 5254]"""
"5307","(5307) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; 'Amr ibn 'Awn informed us; Hammad ibn Zayd informed us from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: There was a fight between Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf, and that reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he came to them to reconcile between them after Dhuhr. He said to Bilal: \If the time for 'Asr prayer arrives and I have not come to you"," then command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\"" When the time for 'Asr arrived"," Bilal gave the adhan"," then he gave the iqamah"," then he commanded Abu Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — and he stepped forward"," and he mentioned the hadith. He said at its end: \""If anything befalls you in the prayer"," then let the men say 'Subhan Allah' and let the women clap.\""\n\nThe Sheikh said: His statement to Bilal in this hadith is an addition preserved by Hammad ibn Zayd"," and an addition from one such as him is acceptable. And Allah knows best."""
"5308","(5308) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; 'Ubayd ibn Sharik al-Bazzar informed us; Yahya ibn Bukayr informed us; al-Layth informed us from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Abbad ibn Ziyad narrated to me from 'Urwah ibn al-Mughirah and Hamzah ibn al-Mughirah.\n\n(And) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us; Abu Salih informed me; al-Layth informed me; Yunus narrated to me from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Abbad ibn Ziyad narrated to me from 'Urwah and Hamzah, the two sons of al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, that they both heard al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah narrating: He traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of Tabuk. When Fajr drew near, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned aside, and I turned aside with him. He made his mount kneel, and he went to relieve himself, and I had with me a vessel containing water. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me, I was commanded, so I poured water over his hand from the vessel three times. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ washed his face. Then he went to pull out his forearms, but the sleeves of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ's jubbah were tight, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ inserted his hands into his jubbah and brought them out from beneath the jubbah, and washed them up to the elbows. Then he wiped his head, and he performed wudu over his leather socks. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came forward, and al-Mughirah came forward with him, and he found the people had already stood for prayer and had put forward 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf to lead them in prayer. 'Abd al-Rahman led them in one rak'ah of the Fajr prayer before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and stood with the people behind 'Abd al-Rahman in the second rak'ah. When 'Abd al-Rahman said the salam, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to complete his prayer. The people were alarmed by that and increased in saying 'Subhan Allah.' When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed his prayer, he said to the people: \You have done rightly\"" — or he said — \""You have done well.\""\n\nBoth of them said in their isnad: from 'Abbad"," from 'Urwah and Hamzah. [Sahih. Bukhari 356]"""
"5309","(5309) Ibn Wahb narrated it from Yunus ibn Yazid, and he said: from 'Urwah only. And Ibn Jurayj narrated it, and he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me from the hadith of 'Abbad ibn Ziyad, that 'Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah narrated to him that al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah informed him, and he mentioned the hadith up to his saying: \When the Prophet ﷺ completed his prayer"," he turned to them and said: 'You have done well' — or he said — 'You have done rightly.'\"" He envied them for having prayed the prayer at its proper time.\n\nIbn Jurayj said: Ibn Shihab said"," from Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd"," from Hamzah ibn al-Mughirah"," something similar to the hadith of 'Abbad. Al-Mughirah said: I wanted to delay 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf"," but the Prophet ﷺ said: \""Leave him.\"" Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us of that; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah and Abu Ibrahim ibn Abu Talib both said: Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi'. [Sahih. In Muslim 421]"""
"5310","(5310) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us; Ibn Qa'nab and Ibn Bukayr informed us from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Abu 'Ubayd, the mawla of Ibn Azhar, who said: I attended the 'Id prayer with 'Umar — may Allah be pleased with him — and I attended the 'Id prayer with 'Ali, while 'Uthman was besieged. [Sahih. Malik 429]"
"5311","(5311) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari in Baghdad informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar informed us from al-Zuhri from 'Urwah from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Adi, meaning Ibn al-Khiyar, that he entered upon 'Uthman — may Allah be pleased with him — while he was besieged, and 'Ali — may Allah be pleased with him — was leading the people in prayer. He said: \I would be constrained to pray with these people while you are the imam.\"" 'Uthman said to him: \""Prayer is the best of what the people do. When you see them doing good"," then do good with them"," and when you see them doing evil"," then avoid their evil.\"" [Sahih. Bukhari 663]"""
"5312","(5312) Abu Ahmad al-'Adl informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far informed us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Malik informed us from Abu Ja'far al-Qari, who said: He saw the owner of the maqsura during the fitnah; when the time for prayer arrived, he would go out following the people, saying: \Who will lead the people in prayer?\"" Until he reached 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar. 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar said to him: \""Then you step forward and lead the people in prayer.\"" [Sahih. Ibn Qudamah al-Mughni 2/90]"""
"5313","(5313) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us; Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Baghdadi informed us; Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr informed us; Dawud ibn 'Abd al-Rahmi al-Makki informed us; 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaym informed us from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that his father informed him: al-Walid ibn 'Uqbah delayed the prayer in Kufah, and I was sitting with my father in the mosque. 'Abdullah stood and called out the takbir for prayer and led the people in prayer. Al-Walid sent to him: \What compelled you to what you did? Did a command come to you from the Commander of the Faithful"," so it is hearing and obedience"," or did you innovate what you did?\"" He said: \""No command came to us from the Commander of the Faithful"," and Allah forbid that I should innovate. Allah and His Messenger have forbidden us to wait for you in our prayers while you attend to your needs.\"" [Hasan. Ahmad 1/450]"""
"5314","(5314) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf informed us by dictation; Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Fakihi in Makkah informed us; Abu Yahya ibn Abi Masrrah informed us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Azraqi informed us; Dawud ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed us from Ibn Khuthaym from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud from his father from his grandfather, that the Prophet ﷺ said: \There will be after me commanders who will delay the prayers from their appointed times and will introduce innovations (bid'ah).\""\n\nIbn Mas'ud said: \""So what should I do if I encounter them?\"" He ﷺ said: \""You ask me"," Ibn Umm 'Abd"," what you should do? There is no obedience to one who disobeys Allah.\""\n\nIsma'il ibn Zakariyya followed him from Ibn Khuthaym and added in it: \""They will extinguish the Sunnah.\"" [Da'if. Ibn Majah 2865]"""
"5315","(5315) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us; Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi informed us; Abu al-Rabi' informed us; Hammad ibn Zayd informed us from Abu 'Imran al-Jawni from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: \How will you be when there are over you commanders who delay the prayer from its time\"" — or he said — \""who cause the prayer to die from its time\""?\n\nHe said: I said: \""So what do you command me?\""\n\nHe ﷺ said: \""Pray the prayer at its time. If you catch it with them"," then pray"," for it will be a supererogatory prayer for you.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Rabi'. [Sahih. Muslim 648]"""
"5316","(5316) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim al-Dimashqi, who is Duhaym, informed us. And Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us; Duhaym informed us; al-Walid, who is Ibn Muslim, informed us; al-Awza'i informed us; Hassan ibn 'Atiyyah informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit informed us; 'Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi informed us, who said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal — may Allah be pleased with him — came to us in Yemen as a messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to us. He said: I heard his takbir with Fajr — a man with a deep voice. He said: My love was cast upon him, and I did not part from him until I buried him dead in Syria. Then I looked to the most learned of the people after him, so I came to Ibn Mas'ud. I stayed with him until he died. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: \How will it be with you when there come over you commanders who pray the prayer at other than its time?\""\n\nI said: \""So what do you command me"," O Messenger of Allah"," if I encounter that?\""\n\nHe ﷺ said: \""Pray the prayer at its appointed time"," and make your prayer with them a supererogatory prayer (subhah).\"" [Sahih in other than it. Ibn Hibban 1481]"""
"5317","(5317) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh al-Naḥwī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Dāwūd al-Munādī narrated to us: Abū Badr narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed me: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ, from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj, from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The one who leads the people in prayer is the one most proficient in the recitation of the Book of Allah. If they are equal in recitation"," then the one most knowledgeable of the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah"," then the one who emigrated earliest. If they are equal in emigration"," then the one who is oldest in age. And let not a man lead another in his domain"," nor sit in his house upon his seat of honour"," except with his permission.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth from Abū ʿAmr.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced as no. 5285]"""
"5318","(5318) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ al-Zubaydī. He said: I heard Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj narrating from Abū Masʿūd al-Badrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The one who leads the people in prayer is the one most proficient in the recitation of the Book of Allah and the foremost in emigration. If they are equal in recitation"," then the one who emigrated earliest. If they are equal in emigration"," then the one who is oldest in age. And let not a man lead another in his house"," nor in his domain"," nor sit upon his seat of honour except with his permission"," or he said: unless he grants you permission.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced as no. 5285]"""
"5319","(5319) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated: Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ informed me. He mentioned it with its chain and text identically, except that he said: \The one most proficient in the recitation of the Book of Allah"," and the foremost in recitation. If they are equal in recitation"," then let the one oldest among you lead them.\"" Then he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth. Shuʿbah said: I said to Ismāʿīl: \""What is his seat of honour (takrimah)?\"" He said: \""His bed (firāsh).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 582]"""
"5320","(5320) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ, from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj, who said: I heard Abū Masʿūd say — and he mentioned the ḥadīth similar to the ḥadīth of Ibn Fūrak, except that he said: \And let not a man lead another in his domain"," nor in his household"," nor sit upon his seat of honour in his house except with his permission"," or unless he grants him permission.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah"," with the wording of the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb"," except that he said: \""The one most proficient in the recitation of the Book of Allah and the foremost in recitation.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced as no. 5285]"""
"5321","(5321) And the Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān narrated to us by dictation: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad al-Harawī informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā al-Asadī narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: al-Masʿūdī narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ, from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj, from Abū Masʿūd ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmr al-Badrī, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \The one who leads you in prayer is the one most proficient in the recitation of the Book of Allah and the foremost in the recitation of the Qurʾān. If your recitations are equal"," then the one who emigrated earliest. If your emigrations are equal"," then the one who is oldest in age. And let not a man lead another in his domain"," nor in his household"," and let him not sit upon his seat of honour except with his permission.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced as no. 5285]"""
"5322","(5322) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Khurrādhādh narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated from Isḥāq ibn Yaḥyya ibn Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh. He said: al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ and Maʿbad ibn Khālid narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Khaṭmī, who was the governor of al-Kūfah. He said: We came to Qays ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah in his house, and he called the adhān for prayer. We said to Qays: \Stand and lead us in prayer.\"" He said: \""I would not lead a people over whom I am not the governor.\"" Then a man who was not inferior to him"," called ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥanẓalah al-Ghasīl"," said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \""A man has the greatest right over the front of his mount and the front of his bed"," and he has the greatest right to lead the prayer in his household.\"" Qays then said to one of his freedmen: \""Stand and lead them in prayer.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. al-Ḥākim 2/73]"""
"5323","(5323) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūh al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Saʿīd ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us: Qatādah narrated from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd, the freedman of Abī Usayd, who said: Ḥudhayfah, Abū Dharr, and Ibn Masʿūd visited me, and the time of prayer arrived. Abū Dharr wished to step forward, but Ḥudhayfah said to him: \The lord of the house has the greater right.\"" ʿAbdullāh said: \""Yes"," O Abū Dharr.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 3818]"""
"5324","(5324) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Abān narrated from Budayl, who said: Abū ʿAṭiyyah, a freedman of ours, narrated to me. He said: Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith used to come to us at this place of prayer of ours. The iqāmah for prayer was called, and we said to him: \Step forward and lead us in prayer.\"" He said to us: \""Appoint a man from among you to lead you in prayer"," and I shall tell you why I do not lead you.\"" I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \""Whoever visits a people"," let him not lead them in prayer; rather"," let a man from among them lead them.\"" [Ḥasan in meaning. Aḥmad 5/53]"""
"5325","(5325) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated from Ibn Jurayj. He said: Nāfiʿ informed me. He said: The iqāmah for prayer was called in a mosque in a quarter of al-Madīnah, and Ibn ʿUmar had nearby land which he worked. The imām of that mosque was his freedman, and the residence of that freedman and his companions was there. When ʿAbdullāh heard them, he came to join them in prayer. The freedman, the owner of the mosque, said to him: \Step forward and lead the prayer.\"" ʿAbdullāh said: \""You have a greater right to lead the prayer in your mosque than I.\"" So the freedman led the prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 3850]"""
"5326","(5326) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyya ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Qays, who said: I heard Huzayl ibn Sharḥbīl say: Ibn Masʿūd came to our mosque, and the iqāmah for prayer was called. We said to him: \Step forward.\"" He said: \""Let your imām step forward.\"" We said: \""Our imām is not here.\"" He said: \""Then let a man from among you step forward.\"" So a man stood upon a raised platform in the mosque. ʿAbdullāh forbade him from that. And something to the same effect has been narrated from Ibn ʿUmar. [Ḥasan. al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 9560]"""
"5327","(5327) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us that Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us that Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us that 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us that Ma'mar narrated from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn 'Umar, who said: \I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ at Mina two rak'ahs"," and with Abu Bakr two rak'ahs"," and with 'Umar two rak'ahs"," and with 'Uthman at the beginning of his caliphate two rak'ahs; then he prayed four.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih. Its takhrij will follow.]"""
"5328","(5328) Abu Ahmad — 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrajanai informed us that Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkai informed us that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Bushanjai informed us that Ibn Bukair informed us that Malik narrated from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn 'Abdullah that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when he came to Makkah, would pray for them two rak'ahs, then say: \O people of Makkah"," complete your prayer"," for we are people travelling.\"" [Sahih. Its takhrij will follow.]"""
"5329","(5329) And with his chain of narration, he said: Malik narrated from Zaid ibn Aslam from his father from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, something similar to that."
"5330","(5330) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Ja'far informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal informed me that my father informed me that Hasan ibn Musa informed us that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated from Zaid ibn Aslam from 'Ata' ibn Yasar from Abu Hurairah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \They (the imam) pray for you; if they are correct"," it is for you and for them"," and if they err"," it is for you and upon them.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Fadl ibn Sahl from Hasan ibn Musa. [Sahih. Bukhari 662]"""
"5331","(5331) Abu al-Husayn — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastawaih informed us that Ya'qub ibn Sufyan al-Farisi informed me that Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam informed us that Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah that Abu 'Ali al-Hamdani, who resided in Alexandria, informed me. He said: \I went out on a journey and with us was 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir. We said to him: 'Lead us in prayer.' He said: 'I will not do so. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever leads the people in prayer and gets the time right and completes the prayer"," it is for him and for them. And whoever falls short in any of that"," it is upon him and not upon them.'\"" [Sahih. Ibn Hibban 2221]"""
"5332","(5332) And 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us that Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad ibn 'Uqail informed us that Abu Shu'aib al-Harrani informed us that 'Ali ibn al-Madini informed us that Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us that Sufyan informed us that Sulaiman — al-A'mash — narrated from Abu Salih, and I do not think he heard it directly from him, from Abu Hurairah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The imam is a guarantor"," and the mu'adhdhin is a trustee. May Allah guide the imams and forgive the mu'adhdhins.\"" [Sahih. Its takhrij is found in the Book of the Adhan.]"""
"5333","(5333) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us that Isma'il al-Saffar informed us that Sa'dan ibn Nasr informed us that Sufyan narrated from Mughirah from Ibrahim from Abu Ma'mar, who said: \The prayer was established and they pushed one another forward"," so Hudhaifah stepped forward and prayed with them. Then he said: 'You will surely be tested with an imam other than me"," or you will pray alone.'\"" Mughirah said: \""I narrated this hadith to Mujahid while Ibrahim was present"," and Mujahid said: 'Furada (individually).' He said: 'Furada and wahdana (alone) are the same.'\"" And it has been narrated from Abu Talhah al-Ansari that he disliked that. [Sahih through other chains. Ibn Abi Shaybah 4114]"""
"5334","(5334) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us that Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us that Ibn Numair informed us that my father informed us that 'Ubaidullah narrated from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Hearing and obedience are incumbent upon the Muslim person in what he likes and dislikes"," unless he is commanded to disobey (Allah). If he is commanded to disobey"," then there is no hearing and no obedience.\"" [Sahih. Bukhari 2796]"""
"5335","(5335) And Abu Ishaq — Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Isfara'ini informed us that Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Yazdad ibn Mas'ud informed us that Muhammad ibn Ayyub al-Razi informed us that Musaddad ibn Musarhad informed us that Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated from 'Ubaidullah, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: \So long as he is not commanded to disobey (Allah).\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad"," and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numair. [Sahih. See the preceding.]"""
"5336","(5336) Abu Sa'id — Muhammad ibn Musa ibn al-Fadl informed us that Abu 'Abdullah — Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Saffar informed us that Abu Ja'far — Ahmad ibn Mihran al-Isbahani informed us that Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah informed us that Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaim from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from his father from 'Abdullah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \There will soon be placed in charge of your affairs a people who will extinguish the Sunnah"," introduce innovations"," and delay the prayers from their prescribed times.\"" Ibn Mas'ud said: \""How should I act"," O Messenger of Allah"," if I reach them?\"" He said: \""O son of Umm 'Abd"," there is no obedience to one who disobeys Allah.\"" He said it three times. [Da'if. Previously referenced under number 5314]"""
"5337","(5337) And it was narrated to us by Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūṣuf al-Aṣbahānī, by dictation and recitation; he informed us that Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī informed us in Makkah; he narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥajjāj ibn Iyāṣ al-Ḍabbī narrated to us; he narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from ʿĀṣim, from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Perhaps you will encounter people who perform the Ṣalāh outside its prescribed time. If you encounter them"," then perform the Ṣalāh in your homes at the time you know"," then pray with them and count it as a supererogatory prayer (Sunnah).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Muslim 534]"""
"5338","(5338) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us; he informed us that Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us; he narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; he narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us; he narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Ayyūb and Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ṣāmit, from Abū Dharr, who attributed it (marfūʿan) and said: He struck my thigh and said: \How will you be when you remain among a people who delay the Ṣalāh from its prescribed time?\"" Then he said: \""Perform the Ṣalāh at its time"," then leave. And if you are in the mosque and the Iqāmah is called"," then pray with them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah from Budayl"," as a musnad narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 5315]"""
"5339","(5339) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; he informed us that Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; he informed us that Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; he informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād narrated —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, and the wording is his; he informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; he narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; he narrated to us that Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād ibn Anʿum, who said: ʿImrān ibn ʿAbd al-Maʿāfirī narrated to me, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \Three persons — Allah does not accept Ṣalāh from them: one who leads a people in prayer while they dislike him; a man who comes to the Ṣalāh late (dibāran)\"" — and he said: dibārah means coming after the time has elapsed; \""and a man who enslaves a freed person.\""\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said in the book of al-Imāmah"," regarding this topic: It is said that the Ṣalāh of one who leads a people in prayer while they dislike him is not accepted"," nor the Ṣalāh of a woman whose husband is displeased with her"," nor the Ṣalāh of a runaway slave until he returns. And I do not preserve it from a chain that the scholars of ḥadīth would consider authentic.\n\nAl-Shaykh said: This ḥadīth with this meaning is only narrated through two weak chains"," one of which is mursal and the other mawṣūl.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 593]"""
"5340","(5340) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he narrated to us that Abū ʿUtbah narrated to us; he narrated to us that Baqiyyah narrated to us; he narrated to us that Ismāʿīl narrated from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Three persons — their Ṣalāh does not pass beyond their heads: a man who leads a people in prayer while they dislike him; a woman who spends the night while her husband is angry with her; and a slave who flees from his master.\"" [Ḥasan by other than him. Tirmidhī 360]"""
"5341","(5341) Through their chain, Baqiyyah narrated to us; he narrated to us that Ismāʿīl narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd, from the Prophet (ﷺ), with its like. And the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād is more complete than this, although it is also not strong. And the preserved narration from the ḥadīth of Qatādah is what was mentioned. [Ḥasan by other than him]"
"5342","(5342) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; he informed us that Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; he narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; he narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; he informed us that Maʿmar narrated from Qatādah, who said: I do not know except that he attributed it (marfūʿan), saying: \Three persons — their Ṣalāh does not pass beyond their ears: a slave who has fled from his master until he comes and places his hand in his master's hand; a woman whose husband spends the night angry with her; and a man who leads a people in prayer while they dislike him.\""\n\nIt was also narrated from Abū Ghālib from Abū Umāmah"," but it is not strong. And it was narrated regarding the Imāmah and the woman from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Dīnār from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration"," and from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from ʿAmr ibn al-Walīd from Anas ibn Mālik"," who attributed it (rafʿahu).\n\n[Ḥasan by other than him. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 20449]"""
"5343","(5343) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he informed us that Abū Muḥammad Jaʿfar ibn Hārūn al-Naḥwī narrated to us in Baghdad; he narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Ṣadaqah ibn Ṣubayḥ narrated to us; he narrated to us that Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us; he narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār narrated to me, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) dispatched an expedition and appointed Usāmah ibn Zayd as its commander. Some people criticized his command, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \If you criticize his command"," you had previously criticized the command of his father. By Allah"," he was worthy of command"," and his father was among the most beloved of people to me"," and this one is among the most beloved of people to me after him.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Khālid ibn Makhlad"," and Muslim narrated it through another chain from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3524]"""
"5344","(5344) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sayyārī narrated to us in Marw; he narrated to us that Abū al-Muwajjah narrated to us; he narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; he narrated to us that Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \You will covet leadership"," and it will be a source of regret and remorse on the Day of Resurrection. How excellent is the nursing mother"," and how terrible is the weaning mother.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6729]"""
"5345","(5345) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; he informed us that Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; he narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated to us; he narrated to us that Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Ibn ʿAjlān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \There is no commander of ten people but that he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection in bonds"," until justice frees him or oppression destroys him.\"" Some of them said: \""Yūbiqahu al-jawr\"" (oppression will destroy him).\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Dārimī 2515]"""
"5346","(5346) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad; he informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; he narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; he narrated to us that Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; he narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar, from Sālim ibn Abī Sālim al-Jayshānī, from his father, from Abū Dharr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: \O Abū Dharr"," I see you as weak"," and I love for you what I love for myself. Do not take command over even two people"," and do not assume guardianship over the property of an orphan.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others from al-Muqriʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1826]"""
"5347","(5347) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hārūn ibn ʿAbbād al-Azdī narrated to us; Marwān narrated to me; Ṭalḥah, Umm Ghurāb, informed me from ʿAqīlah, a woman from Banī Fazārah, a freedwoman of them, from Salāmah bint al-Ḥur, sister of Kharashah bint al-Ḥur al-Fazārī, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \Indeed"," among the signs of the Hour is that the people of the mosque will push one another away [from leading the prayer]"," finding no imām to lead them in prayer.\"" [Weak. Abū Dāwūd 581]"""
"5348","(5348) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Zayd ibn Ḥubāb al-ʿUkulī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; al-Safar ibn Nusayr al-Azdī narrated to me from Yazīd ibn Shuraīḥ al-Ḥaḍramī from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \When a man leads a people in prayer"," he should not make duʿāʾ exclusively for himself"," excluding them. If he does so"," he has betrayed them. And he should not let his gaze enter a house without the permission of its people. If he does so"," he has betrayed them.\""\n\nThis is a hadith over which there have been differing narrations from Yazīd ibn Shuraīḥ from various routes"," this being one of them. And the second is what… [Good. Abū Dāwūd 90]"""
"5349","(5349) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād in Baghdād informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Aṣbugh ibn Zayd narrated to us; Manṣūr narrated to us from Thawr ibn Yazīd from Yazīd ibn Shuraīḥ from Abī Ḥayy al-Mu'adhdhin from Abī Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \It is not permissible for a man to pray while suppressing his need [to relieve himself] until he relieves himself. And it is not permissible for a Muslim man to lead a people in prayer except with their permission. And he should not single himself out with duʿāʾ to the exclusion of them. If he does so"," he has betrayed them. And it is not permissible for a Muslim man to look into the interior of a house. If he looks"," he has entered [without permission]—or he said: he has intruded.\""\n\nAnd others narrated it similarly from Thawr. His statement \""damara\"" means he entered without their permission. [Good. See the preceding]"""
"5350","(5350) Abū Zakariyyā informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Baqiyyah said: Shuʿbah said to me: \How did Ḥabīb ibn Ṣāliḥ narrate to you? Repeat it to me so I may verify it.\"" I said: Ḥabīb ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me from Yazīd ibn Shuraīḥ from Abī Ḥayy al-Mu'adhdhin from Thawbān from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," similarly. And likewise Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated it from Ḥabīb ibn Ṣāliḥ. [Good. See the preceding]"""
"5351","(5351) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj narrated to us; Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us; al-ʿIshī narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us; Thābit narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb that the Prophet (ﷺ) came upon ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) as he had gone out for the Fajr prayer, and ʿAlī was saying: \O Allah"," forgive me; O Allah"," have mercy on me; O Allah"," accept my repentance.\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) struck his shoulder and said: \""Make it general"," for the virtue of generality over specificity is like that of the heavens over the earth.\"" Abū Dāwūd narrated it in al-Marāsīl. [Very Weak. Abū Dāwūd in al-Marāsīl 3259]"""
"5352","(5352) ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us; Ḥumayd ibn al-Aswad, Abū al-Aswad, narrated to us; Muṣʿab ibn Thābit ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn Muslim ibn Khabbāb, who said: Anas ibn Mālik came and sat in your place. He said: \Do you know what this staff is?\"" We said: \""No.\"" He said: \""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood for prayer"," he would take it in his right hand and say: 'Straighten up"," align your rows.' Then he would take it in his left hand and say: 'Straighten up"," align your rows.'\"" When the mosque was demolished"," it was lost. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) searched for it and found that the Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf had taken it and placed it in their mosque. He took it and restored it.\n\nAnd we have narrated in the chapters on actions during prayer from Umm Qays ibn Miḥṣan that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) grew old and his body became heavy"," he took a pillar in his place of prayer to lean upon. [Weak. Abū Dāwūd 670]"""
"5353","(5353) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥummāmī (may Allah have mercy on him) in Baghdād informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Jumayʿ narrated to us; my grandmother narrated to me from Umm Waraqah bint ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Ḥārith. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to visit her and call her \the Martyr.\"" She had compiled the Qurʾān. When the Prophet (ﷺ) set out for the Battle of Badr"," she said: \""Give me permission to go out with you"," so that I may treat your wounded and nurse your sick; perhaps Allah"," the Most High"," will grant me martyrdom.\"" He said: \""Indeed"," Allah"," the Most High"," will grant you martyrdom.\"" So he used to call her \""the Martyr.\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) had commanded her to lead the people of her household in prayer. A slave girl and a boy of hers"," whom she had manumitted"," killed her during the caliphate of ʿUmar. It was said: \""Umm Waraqah has been killed by her slave girl and her boy"," and they have fled.\"" They were brought"," and they were crucified"," being the first two to be crucified in Madīnah. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) spoke the truth. He used to say: 'Let us go and visit the Martyr.'\"" [Weak. Aḥmad 6/405]"""
"5354","(5354) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus al-Ḍabbī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Dāwūd al-Khuraybī narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Jumayʿ narrated to us from Laylā ibn Mālik and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, meaning Ibn Khallād al-Anṣārī, from Umm Waraqah al-Anṣāriyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: \Let us go to the Martyr and visit her.\"" And he commanded that the adhān and iqāmah be called for her and that she lead the people of her household in the obligatory prayers.\n\nAnd Wakīʿ narrated it from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Jumayʿ from his grandmother and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khallād al-Anṣārī from Umm Waraqah"," with the same meaning as the narration of Abū Nuʿaym. [Weak. See the preceding]"""
"5355","(5355) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us from Maysarah, Abī Ḥāzim, from Rāʾiṭah al-Ḥanafiyyah that ʿĀʾishah led women in the obligatory prayer and stood among them in the middle. [Good for other than its chain. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5086]"
"5356","(5356) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Idrīs narrated to us from Layth from ʿAṭāʾ from ʿĀʾishah that she used to give the adhān, call the iqāmah, lead the women in prayer, and stand in their midst."
"5357","(5357) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'quq informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Ibn Uyyaynah narrated from Ammar al-Duhni from a woman of his people called Hujayrah, from Umm Salamah, that she led them in prayer and stood in their midst. [Weak. Abd al-Razzaq 5082]"
"5358","(5358) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Sa'id al-Muqri' in Kufah narrated to us; Abbad ibn Ya'qub al-Asadi narrated to us; Ibn Abi Yahya, meaning Ibrahim, narrated from Dawud, meaning ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: \A woman may lead women in prayer"," standing in their midst.\"" And we have narrated regarding it a musnad hadith in the chapter of the Adhan"," though it contains weakness. [Very Weak. Abd al-Razzaq 5083]"""
"5359","(5359) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi in Marw narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mas'ud narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us; al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us; Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Do not prevent your women from [going to] the mosques"," though their homes are better for them.\"" [Sahih in other chains. Ahmad 2/72]"""
"5360","(5360) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Amr ibn al-Harith informed us that Darraj, Abu al-Samh, narrated to him from al-Sa'ib, the freed slave of Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (s), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \The best mosques for women are the innermost parts of their homes.\"" [Hasan in other chains. Ahmad 6/297]"""
"5361","(5361) Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Ahmad al-Zahid narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Mahdi ibn Rustum al-Isbahani narrated to us; Amr ibn Asim al-Kalabi narrated to us; Hammam narrated from Qatadah, from Muwarriq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \A woman's prayer in her house is better than her prayer in her chamber"," and her prayer in her private room is better than her prayer in her house.\"" [Sahih in other chains. Ibn Khuzaymah 1688]"""
"5362","(5362) Abu Ishaq al-Isfara'ini al-Imam narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Yazdad ibn Mas'ud narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub al-Razi informed us; Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us; Ali ibn Mushir narrated from Ibrahim ibn Muslim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \A woman does not pray any prayer more beloved to Allah than the prayer she offers in the darkest part of her house.\"" And Ja'far ibn Aw narrated it from Ibrahim al-Hajari"," but he stopped it at Abd Allah. [Weak. Ibn Khuzaymah 1691/1692]"""
"5363","(5363) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu Abd Allah ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Ja'far narrated it as a mawquf narration, except that he said: \In the darkest place in her house.\"" [Weak]"""
"5364","(5364) Abu Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us; Abu al-Mundhir Isma'il ibn Umar narrated to us; al-Mas'udi narrated from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, who said: \By the One besides whom there is no deity"," a woman does not pray any prayer better for her than a prayer she offers in her house"," except the Masjid al-Haram or the Masjid of the Messenger (ﷺ)"," unless she is an elderly woman who prays in her socks.\"" Ja'far ibn Aw and others followed him in narrating from al-Mas'udi. [Sahih. Ibn Abi Shaybah 7614]"""
"5365","(5365) Abu al-Qasim Abd al-Khaliq ibn Ali ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khanb informed us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Tirmidhi narrated to us; Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me; Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated from Sharik, from Yahya ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Labibah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \That a woman should pray in her house is better for her than that she should pray in her chamber; and that she should pray in her chamber is better for her than that she should pray in the [open] house; and that she should pray in the house is better for her than that she should pray in the mosque.\"" [Sahih in other chains. al-Bukhari in his Tarikh 8/265]"""
"5366","(5366) Abu al-Hasan al-Hasani narrated to us orally, and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us by recitation; they said: Hajib ibn Ahmad informed us; Abd al-Rahim ibn Munib narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \When any one of you's wife asks his permission to go to the mosque"," he should not prevent her.\"" Al-Alawi added in his narration: Sufyan said: \""That is"," if it is at night.\"" [Sahih. al-Bukhari 4940]"""
"5367","(5367) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — 'Ali ibn Hamshadh informed us — Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us — 'Ali ibn al-Madini informed us — Sufyan informed us — al-Zuhri mentioned it with its like, except that he said: \Indeed"," the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said…\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madini. And Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Harb and others from Sufyan"," except that he said: \""Yablughu bihi.\"" [Sahih. Its meaning is found in the hadith preceding it.]"""
"5368","(5368) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Tusi in Nishapur, in the year three hundred and thirty-three — (h) — and Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Ayyub informed us — Abu Hatim al-Razi informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us — Hanzhalah informed us — from Salim — from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **\When your women ask your permission to go to the mosque — or to the mosques — then grant them permission.\""**\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa and said: \""To the mosque\"" — without doubt. And Muslim narrated it from another face from Hanzhalah ibn Abi Sufyan. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 827.]"""
"5369","(5369) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Shu'bah informed us — from al-A'mash — from Mujahid — from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: **\Do not prevent the women from the mosques at night.\""**\n\nHis son said: \""By Allah"," we shall prevent them"," lest they take it as a means of deception.\"" So he raised his hand and struck him. He said: \""I narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and you say this?!\""\n\nAl-Bukhari commented"," and Shu'bah narrated from al-A'mash. And Muslim narrated it from two other faces from al-A'mash. [Sahih. Muslim 442.]"""
"5370","(5370) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sadiq ibn Abi al-Fawaris informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi informed us — Abu Usamah informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar informed us — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, who said: There was a wife of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who used to attend the Fajr and 'Isha prayers in congregation in the mosque. It was said to her: \Why do you go out when you know that 'Umar dislikes that and is jealous?\"" She said: \""What prevents him from forbidding me?\"" It was said: \""He is prevented by the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: **'Do not prevent the female servants of Allah from the mosques of Allah.'**\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Yusuf ibn Musa from Abu Usamah. And Muslim narrated the hadith without the story of 'Umar from the hadith of 'Ubayd Allah. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 858.]"""
"5371","(5371) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Misri informed us — Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih informed us — Ibrahim ibn Marwan Abu Bakr informed us — 'Abd al-Mu'min ibn 'Abd Allah al-Kinani informed us — 'Abd al-Hamid ibn al-Mundhir ibn Abi Humayd al-Sa'idi informed us — from his father — from his grandmother Umm Humayd, that she said: \O Messenger of Allah"," we love the prayer — meaning prayer with you — but our husbands prevent us.\"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **\""Your prayer in your houses is better for you than your prayer in your tribal mosques"," and your prayer in your tribal mosques is better for you than your prayer in the congregational mosque.\""**\n\nAbu Zakariyya said: \""I asked Abu Bakr about this 'Abd al-Mu'min — where did he hear it from him?\"" He said: \""In Wadan"," and 'Abd al-Mu'min was there at that time.\""\n\nThe Sheikh said: And Ibn Luhay'ah also narrated it from 'Abd al-Hamid. And in it is evidence that the command not to prevent them is a matter of recommendation and preference"," not a matter of obligation and compulsion"," and this is the position of the generality of the scholars. [Hasan in light of other narrations. Al-Tabarani in al-Kabir 356.]"""
"5372","(5372) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — Sa'dan ibn Nasr al-Makhrami informed us — Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah informed us — Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us — from 'Amrah bint 'Abd al-Rahman — from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \If the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had seen what the women innovated after him"," he would have prevented them from the mosque"," just as the women of the Children of Israel were prevented.\"" We said: \""O this one\"" — meaning 'Amrah — \""Were the women of the Children of Israel prevented?\"" She said: \""Yes.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Amr al-Naqid from Sufyan. And al-Bukhari narrated it from another face from Yahya. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 831.]"""
"5373","(5373) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — Abu 'Umar ibn al-Sammak informed us — Muhammad ibn Ghalib informed us — Umayt ibn Bisṭam informed us — Yazid ibn Zurai' informed us — Ruh ibn al-Qasim informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Ajlān — (h) — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu 'Umar ibn Abi Ja'far informed me — al-Husayn ibn Sufyan informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us — Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us — from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlān — from Bukayr ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj — from Busr ibn Sa'id — that Zaynab al-Thaqafiyyah used to narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: **\When one of you attends the late 'Isha prayer"," let her not apply perfume.\""**\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Muslim 443.]"""
"5374","(5374) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah informed us — (h) — and the Qadi Abu al-A'la and Abu Ja'far al-'Azayimi informed us — they said: Abu Sahl al-Isfara'ini informed us — Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi — they both said: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah informed me — Yazid ibn Khusayfah informed me — from Busr ibn Sa'id — from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **\Any woman who has applied incense"," let her not attend the late 'Isha prayer with us.\""**\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. See the hadith preceding it.]"""
"5375","(5375) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Misri informed us — Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani informed us — Bishr ibn Bakr informed us — al-Awza'i informed us — Musa ibn Yasar informed me — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: He met a woman whose fragrance was wafting, and he said: \O servant of the Almighty"," do you wish to go to the mosque?\"" She said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""And you have applied perfume?\"" She said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Then go back"," for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: **'No woman goes out to the mosque and her fragrance wafts"," and Allah accepts any prayer from her until she returns and bathes.'**\""\n\nAnd similarly al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazyad narrated it from his father from al-Awza'i. [Hasan in light of other narrations. 'Abd al-Razzaq 8109.]"""
"5376","(5376) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri informed us — Khalid ibn Makhlad informed us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abi 'Ubayd, from the shaykhus of Kutha, a mawla of Abu Rahm al-Ghifari — from his grandfather, who said: I went out with my father, Abu Hurayrah, from the mosque at forenoon, and we met a woman with a fragrance the like of which I had never smelled with my nose. Abu Hurayrah said to her: \Alayki al-salam.\"" She said: \""Wa 'alayka.\"" He said: \""Where do you wish to go?\"" She said: \""The mosque.\"" He said: \""And for what purpose have you applied this perfume?\"" She said: \""For the mosque.\"" He said: \""By Allah?\"" She said: \""By Allah.\"" He said: \""By Allah?\"" She said: \""By Allah.\"" He said: \""Then indeed my beloved"," Abu al-Qasim ﷺ"," informed me that no prayer is accepted from a woman who applies perfume for other than her husband until she bathes from it as she would bathe from janabah. So go and bathe from it"," then come back and pray.\""\n\nHis grandfather was Abu al-Harith 'Ubayd ibn Abi 'Ubayd"," who was 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn al-Harith ibn Abi 'Ubayd.\n\nAnd 'Asim ibn 'Ubayd Allah narrated it from 'Ubayd"," a mawla of Abu Rahm. [Hasan in light of other narrations.]"""
"5377","(5377) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Do not prevent the female servants of Allah from the mosques of Allah"," and let them go out (to the mosques) without applying perfume.\"""""
"5378","(5378) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl in Baghdād informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū ʀāṣim narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmmār.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muʿāwiyah informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abī ʿAmmār, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bābawayh, from Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah, who said: I said to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him: \There is no blame upon you that you shorten the prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve may put you to trial\"" [al-Nisāʾ: 101]"," yet the people are now safe. He said: \""I was astonished by the same thing that astonished you"," so I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and he said: 'It is a charity with which Allah has bestowed charity upon you"," so accept His charity.'\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Idrīs.\n\nAnd in the ḥadīth of Abū ʀāṣim"," he said: I said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"," may Allah be pleased with him: Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," said: \""If you shorten the prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve may put you to trial\"" [al-Nisāʾ: 101]. And at the end of it he said: \""It is a charity of Allah upon you"," so accept it.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others"," from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs."""
"5379","(5379) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and Musaddad said: Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī ʿAmmār narrated to me, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bābawayh, from Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah, who said: I said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him: \The people are shortening the prayer today"," yet Allah"," exalted be His praise"," said: 'If you fear that those who disbelieve may put you to trial'\"" [al-Nisāʾ: 101]. That fear has passed today. He said: \""I was astonished by the same thing that astonished you"," so I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and he said: 'It is a charity with which Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," has bestowed charity upon you"," so accept His charity.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr."""
"5380","(5380) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdād informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Naysābūrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, Abū al-Walīd informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, Abū Isḥāq narrated, who said: I heard Ḥārithah ibn Wahb, a man from Khuzāʿah, say: We prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Minā when we were at our most numerous and at our most secure, two rakʿahs.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd."
"5381","(5381) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us, Zuhayr narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Ḥārithah ibn Wahb, who said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Minā when the people were at their most numerous. He prayed two rakʿahs during the Farewell Pilgrimage.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus."
"5382","(5382) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzzakī, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, it was read to Ibn Wahb while I was listening: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: Allah obligated the prayer when He obligated it as two rakʿahs, then He completed it in residence, and the prayer of travel was confirmed upon the original obligation.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā and others, from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another chain from Ibn Shihāb."
"5383","(5383) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-Sulamī informed us, Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Baṣrī informed us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, obligated the prayer upon the tongue of your Prophet ﷺ: four rakʿahs in residence, two rakʿahs in travel, and one rakʿah in fear. The wording of both their ḥadīths is the same.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, Abū al-Rabīʿ, and others."
"5384","(5384) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī informed us, Ayyūb narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet ﷺ used to travel from Madīnah to Makkah, fearing none but Allah, and he would pray two rakʿahs."
"5385","(5385) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Farrāʾ, and Qaṭn ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, they said: Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated to me, from Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to travel between Makkah and Madīnah, fearing none but Allah, and he would shorten the prayer."
"5386","(5386) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us, Ḥajjāj and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us, they both said: Yazīd ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated, who said: I was informed that Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Prophet ﷺ used to travel between Makkah and Madīnah, fearing none but Allah, and he would shorten the prayer."
"5387","(5387) Abu Nadrah reported that a young man asked 'Imran ibn Husayn about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during journey. He said: \This young man is asking me about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during journey"," so memorize it from me. I never undertook a journey with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except that he prayed two rak'ahs until he returned. I witnessed with him at Hunayn and at Ta'if"," and he would pray two rak'ahs. Then I performed Hajj and 'Umrah with him"," and he prayed two rak'ahs. Then he said: 'O people of Makkah"," complete the prayer"," for we are a people on journey.' Then I performed Hajj and 'Umrah with Abu Bakr"," and he prayed two rak'ahs. Then he said: 'O people of Makkah"," complete the prayer"," for we are a people on journey.' Then I performed Hajj and 'Umrah with 'Umar"," and he prayed two rak'ahs and said: 'Complete the prayer"," for we are a people on journey.' Then I performed Hajj and 'Umrah with 'Uthman"," and he prayed two rak'ahs. Then 'Uthman completed [the prayer] — may Allah be pleased with them.\""\n\n[Weak. Ibn Khuzaymah 1643]"""
"5388","(5388) Umayyah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Khalid ibn Asid reported that he asked Ibn 'Umar: \What do you say about shortening the prayer during journey? We do not find it in the Book [of Allah]. We only find mention of the prayer of fear.\"" Umayyah said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar replied: \""O son of my brother"," Allah"," the Almighty and Majestic"," sent Muhammad ﷺ and we knew nothing. We would only do what we saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ doing. And the shortening of the prayer during journey is a sunnah established by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\""\n\n[Authentic. Ibn Majah 1066]"""
"5389","(5389) Anas ibn Malik said: \We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Madinah to Makkah. He would pray two rak'ahs"," two rak'ahs"," until we returned to Madinah.\"" He said: \""We asked: 'Did you stay in Makkah for anything?' He replied: 'We stayed there for ten [days].'\""\n\n[Authentic. Al-Bukhari 1031]"""
"5390","(5390) Zayd ibn Aslam reported from his father that 'Umar shortened the prayer on the way to Khaybar.\n\n[Authentic. 'Abd al-Razzaq 4294]"
"5391","(5391) Nafi' reported from Ibn 'Umar that he shortened the prayer on the way to Khaybar and said: \That is a distance of three qawa'id\"" — meaning three nights [of travel].\n\n[Authentic. 'Abd al-Razzaq 5174]"""
"5392","(5392) Salim ibn 'Abd Allah reported that his father, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, set out toward Dhat al-Nusub and shortened the prayer during that journey. Malik said: \And between Dhat al-Nusub and Madinah there are four barid.\""\n\n[Authentic. Malik 338]"""
"5393","(5393) Salim ibn 'Abd Allah reported from his father, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, that he set out toward Rim and shortened the prayer during that journey. Malik said: \And that is approximately four barid.\""\n\n[Authentic. See the preceding narration]"""
"5394","(5394) Salim ibn 'Abd Allah reported that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar would shorten the prayer during a full day's journey.\n\n[Authentic. Malik 340]"
"5395","(5395) Malik reported that he was informed that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas used to say: \One shortens the prayer in a distance like that between Makkah and Ta'if"," and in a distance like that between Makkah and Judda"," and in a distance like that between Makkah and 'Usfan.\"" Malik said: \""And that is four barid.\""\n\n[Authentic in other respects. Malik 342]"""
"5396","(5396) Mujahid reported from Ibn 'Abbas that he said: \If you travel a day into the night"," then shorten the prayer.\""\n\n[Authentic. Ibn Abi Shaybah 8135]"""
"5397","(5397) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Rāzī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Zāhir ibn Aḥmad informed us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us; Yūsuf ibn Saʿīd ibn Muslim informed us; Ḥajjāj informed us; Layth informed us; Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb informed me from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās used to pray two rakʿahs and break their fast at a distance of four barīd and beyond. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn al-Mundhir as mentioned in al-Fatḥ 2/466]"
"5398","(5398) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Sufyān informed us from Yūnus from al-Ḥasan regarding the shortening of prayer. He said: \At a distance of two nights' travel.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 8124]"""
"5399","(5399) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān informed us from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās that he was asked: \Do you shorten the prayer when travelling to ʿArafah?\"" He said: \""No"," but one may shorten it when travelling to ʿUsfān"," Juddah"," and al-Ṭāʾif.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī 94]"""
"5400","(5400) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us; Ādam informed us; Shuʿbah informed us; Shubayl al-Ḍubaʿī informed us. He said: I heard Abū Ḥabrah say: I said to Ibn ʿAbbās, \May I shorten the prayer when travelling to al-Ubullah?\"" He said: \""Can you return in one day?\"" I said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Then do not shorten.\"" [Jayyid]"""
"5401","(5401) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik ibn Anas informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mahrjānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik informed us from Nāfiʿ that he used to travel with Ibn ʿUmar a distance of one barīd, and he would not shorten the prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Muwaṭṭāʾ 341]"
"5402","(5402) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us. He said: Abū ʿUbayd said regarding the ḥadīth of ʿUthmān that he said: \It has reached me that some of you go out to their lands"," whether for trade"," tax collection"," or gathering livestock"," and they shorten the prayer. Do not do so. For the prayer is only shortened by one who is setting out on a journey or is in the presence of an enemy.\"" Abū ʿUbayd said: Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated it to us from Ayyūb from Abū Qilābah"," who said: \""Someone who read the letter of ʿUthmān to me"," or it was read to him"," narrated it to me.\""\n\n(Ḡ) Abū ʿUbayd said: His word \""al-ḥashr\"" refers to people who take their animals out to the pasture.\n\nThe fiqh point in this narration is that he did not consider the shortening of prayer valid unless the person's absence reaches the distance of a journey. [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4285]"""
"5403","(5403) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Miʿsar informed us from Qays ibn Muslim from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — said: \Let not this crowd of yours deceive you"," for it is merely from your Kūfah.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 81542]"""
"5404","(5404) Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Mujāhid from his father, and from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \O people of Makkah"," do not shorten the prayer at a distance of less than four barīd from Makkah to ʿUsfān.\""\n\nAbū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh narrated it to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed us; Abū Ismāʿīl al-Tirmidhī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated it.\n\nThis is a weak ḥadīth. Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh is not used as a proof"," and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Mujāhid is thoroughly weak. The correct position is that this statement is from Ibn ʿAbbās himself"," as was mentioned previously."""
"5405","(5405) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us from ʿUbaydullāh, who said: Nāfiʿ informed me from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \A woman must not travel for three days except with a maḥram.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1036]"""
"5406","(5406) Abū al-Fawāris al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Fawāris in Baghdād informed us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ṣawwāf informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; al-Aʿmash informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Zāhid informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-ʿAbsī informed us; Wakīʿ informed us from al-Aʿmash from Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \A woman must not travel a journey of three days or more except with her father"," her son"," her brother"," her husband"," or a maḥram.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ.\n\nIn the narration of Abū Nuʿaym: \""Except with her husband"," her father"," her brother"," or a maḥram.\"" And he said: \""The woman.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Wakīʿ.\n\nHe also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah from al-Aʿmash"," and in it he said: \""A journey that is three days or more.\""\n\nQazaʿah ibn Yaḥyā narrated it from Abū Saʿīd"," and in one of the two narrations from him he said: \""More than three.\"" And in the other narration from him he said: \""Two days.\""\n\nAbū Hurayrah narrated it from the Prophet ﷺ"," and in one of the narrations from him he said: \""A day and a night.\"" And in some of them he said: \""A day.\"" And in some of them he said: \""A night.\"" And in some of them he said: \""A barīd.\""\n\nAs for the first narration from Qazaʿah — [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1340]"""
"5407","(5407) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah and Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbdillāh narrated from Qatādah, from Qazaʿah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade a woman from travelling for more than three days except with a maḥram.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿArūbah and Hishām al-Dastawāʾī.\n\nAs for the other narration from him: [Authentic. Previously cited as no. 5405]"
"5408","(5408) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Tamtām narrated to us: ʿAffān, Abū al-Walīd, Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, and ʿAmr ibn Ḥakkām all said: Shuʿbah narrated to us. ʿAbd al-Malik informed me, saying: I heard Qazaʿah, the freedman of Ziyād, say: I heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: \A woman shall not travel a distance of two days and two nights except with her husband or a maḥram of hers.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar"," Abū al-Walīd"," and others"," and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah.\n\nAs for the narrations concerning this from Abū Hurayrah: [Authentic. Bukhārī 1893]"""
"5409","(5409) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \It is not permissible for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to travel a distance of a day and a night except with a maḥram.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik"," and al-Bukhārī alluded to it. Similarly"," al-Qaʿnabī"," Ibn Bukayr"," and a group narrated it from Mālik. And Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated it from Mālik"," from Saʿīd"," from his father"," from Abū Hurayrah. And Ibn Abī Dhiʾb and al-Layth ibn Saʿd likewise narrated it from Saʿīd.\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī Dhiʾb: [Authentic. Bukhārī 1038]"""
"5410","(5410) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \It is not permissible for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to travel for a day except with a maḥram.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb"," and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb. And Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr likewise narrated it from Saʿīd.\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of al-Layth: [Authentic. See the preceding]"""
"5411","(5411) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from Saʿīd, from his father, that Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \It is not permissible for a Muslim woman to travel a distance of a night except with a man who is her maḥram.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd from al-Layth.\n\nThese narrations from Abū Hurayrah are all in agreement regarding the text of the ḥadīth"," because the one who said \""a day\"" intended thereby its night"," and the one who said \""a night\"" intended thereby its day. [Authentic. Muslim 1339]"""
"5412","(5412) And Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \A woman shall not travel a distance of a barīd (approximately twelve miles) except with a maḥram.\""\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ"," and he mentioned it.\n\nThese narrations regarding three days"," two days"," and one day are all authentic"," and it is as though the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked about a woman travelling for three days without a maḥram"," and he said: \""No.\"" And he was asked about her travelling for two days without a maḥram"," and he said: \""No.\"" And about one day"," and he said: \""No.\"" Each narrator conveyed what he had memorized"," and none of these numbers constitutes a specific limit for travel. And with Allah is success. [Authentic. Ibn Khuzaymah 2526]"""
"5413","(5413) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us orally: Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Marwazī narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Ādam al-Marwazī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah al-Hilālī narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abū Maʿbad, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) say: \Let no man be alone with a woman"," and let no woman travel except with a maḥram.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Authentic. Bukhārī 2844]"""
"5414","(5414) Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū al-Ḍuḥā, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did something and granted a concession regarding it, but some of his Companions came to know of it, and it was as though they disliked it and kept away from it. He said: \What is the matter with certain men that news reaches them of something in which I have granted a concession"," and they dislike it and keep away from it? By Allah"," I am the most knowledgeable of them about Allah"," and the most fearful of Him among them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth from al-Aʿmash. [Authentic. Bukhārī 5750]"""
"5415","(5415) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Haytham narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf narrated to us: Ibn al-Duraysrī narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Ḥarb ibn Qays, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Indeed"," Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," loves that His concessions be accepted just as He loves that His binding commands be fulfilled.\"" [Authentic in other than this chain. Ibn Ḥibbān 354]"""
"5416","(5416) And Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah similarly narrated it from Ibn al-Duraysrī, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, except that he said: \Just as He dislikes that His disobediences be committed.\""\n\nAbū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Qutaybah Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Adamī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated it.\n\nAnd Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: ʿAbdān ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥalīm al-Bayhaqī narrated to us: Abū Muṣʿab narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from ʿUmārah ibn Ghaziyyah"," from Ḥarb ibn Qays"," from Nāfiʿ"," from Ibn ʿUmar"," that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \""Indeed"," Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," loves that His concessions be accepted just as He dislikes that His disobediences be committed.\""\n\nAnd ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī"," Qutaybah"," and others narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz from ʿUmārah"," and it is as though he heard it from both of them together. And we have narrated it with its meaning from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd"," ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar"," and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās as their own statement"," except that they said: \""Just as He loves that His binding commands be fulfilled.\"" [Authentic in other than this chain. Aḥmad 2/108]"""
"5417","(5417) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Abspahani informed us — Ibn Mani' narrated to us — Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us — Abd al-Warith narrated to us — Abu al-Tayyah narrated from Muwarriq al-'Ijli — from Safwan ibn Muhriz, who said: I asked Ibn Umar about the prayer of travel. He said: \Two rak'ahs. Whoever contravenes the Sunnah has committed kufr.\"" [Sahih. Abd al-Razzaq 4281]"""
"5418","(5418) Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us — Abu Musa narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us — al-A'mash narrated from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi' — from Ali ibn Mudrik, who said: I saw Abu Ayyub removing his leather socks. The people looked at him, and he said: \Verily"," I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) performing masḥ over them. However"," wuḍū' is more beloved to me.\"" Muhammad ibn Ubayd said: \""Ali ibn Mudrik — not the one from whom Shu'bah narrated. And perhaps the correct name is ibn al-Salt"," and Allah knows best.\"" We have also reported it in the Book of Purification from the ḥadīth of Aflaḥ"," the mawlā of Abu Ayyub"," from Abu Ayyub. [Sahih by other than him. Aḥmad 5/421]"""
"5419","(5419) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said — narrated to us Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Abu Asim narrated — from Ibn Jurayj — Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Ammar narrated to us — from Abd Allah ibn Babay — from Ya'la, who said: I said to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): \What do you say regarding the words of Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic: {And there is no blame upon you that you shorten [your] prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve will put you in trial} [al-Nisa': 101]?\"" He said: \""I was astonished by the very thing that astonished you"," so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)"," and he said: 'It is a ṣadaqah that Allah has bestowed upon you"," so accept it.'\"" [Sahih. Previously cited as no. 5378]"""
"5420","(5420) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri narrated to us — Ubayd Allah ibn Sa'id ibn Kathir narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to me — al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me — Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated — from Ibn Jurayj, that he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar narrated to me — from Abd Allah ibn Babah — from Ya'la ibn Munayyah, that he said: I said to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): \What do you say regarding the words of Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic: {There is no blame upon you that you shorten [your] prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve will put you in trial} [al-Nisa': 101]"," when the people are safe? What"," then"," is the matter with shortening?\"" Umar said: \""I was astonished by the very thing that astonished you"," so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about it"," and he said: 'It is a ṣadaqah that Allah has bestowed upon you"," so accept His ṣadaqah.'\"" Thus said Ibn Babah. And thus said al-Shafi'i"," narrating from Abd al-Majid and Muslim ibn Khalid"," from Ibn Jurayj. And Muslim included it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj"," as has preceded. And Abd Allah ibn Babay said so"," and a group said so from Ibn Jurayj regarding this ḥadīth. And Yahya ibn Ma'īn claimed that they were three: Ibn Babay"," Ibn Babah"," and Ibn Babayh — and the one from whom Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar narrated is Abd Allah ibn Babayh. And Ya'qub ibn Sufyan was of the opinion that they were a single person"," and he was from Makkah"," and the statement of al-Bukhari (may Allah have mercy upon him) points to the same. [Sahih. Previously cited as no. 5378]"""
"5421","(5421) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' said: al-Shafi'i said: \Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) indicated that shortening the prayer in travel without fear is a ṣadaqah from Allah"," and a ṣadaqah is a concession (ruḥṣah)"," not an obligation (ḥatm)"," upon Allah that they should shorten. And He indicated that they may shorten in travel without fear if the traveler wishes. And A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) did all of that — he performed the complete prayer in travel and he shortened.'\"" [Sahih. Kitab al-Umm 3/141]"""
"5422","(5422) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us — al-Mahamili narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn Thawwab narrated to us — Abu Asim narrated to us — Umar ibn Sa'id narrated — from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah — from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace be upon him) used to shorten in travel and complete it, and he used to break his fast and fast. Ali said: \This is a sound isnad.\"" The Shaykh said: \""And for this there is a corroborating ḥadīth from Dalham ibn Salih"," and al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad"," and Talhah ibn 'Amr — all of them being weak (ḍa'īf).\"" As for the ḥadīth of Dalham ibn Salih: [Sahih. Al-Daraqutni 2/189]"""
"5423","(5423) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said — narrated to us Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Affan narrated to us — Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us — Dalham ibn Salih al-Kindi narrated — from Ata' — from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \We used to pray with the Prophet (peace be upon him) when we set out for Makkah — four rak'ahs — until we returned.\"" And as for the ḥadīth of al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad: [Weak]"""
"5424","(5424) Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — al-Kudaymi narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us — al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad narrated — from Ata' — from A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) used to shorten in travel and complete it. And thus narrated Waki' and others from al-Mughirah. And as for the ḥadīth of Talhah: [Sahih by other than him. Previously cited as no. 5432]"
"5425","(5425) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us — Ya'la ibn Ubayd and Abu Nu'aym said — narrated to us Talhah ibn 'Amr — from Ata' — from A'ishah, who said: \All of that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) did — he completed and shortened"," and he fasted and broke his fast in travel.\"" And Umar ibn Dharr al-Murhabi al-Kufi was a trustworthy narrator. [Sahih by other than him]"""
"5426","(5426) Ata' ibn Abi Rabah informed us — that A'ishah used to pray the prescribed prayer in travel as four rak'ahs — and this is in what Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Shayban in Harat informed us — Mu'adh ibn Najdah narrated to us — Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us — Umar ibn Dharr narrated, and he mentioned it. And it is in agreement with the narration of Dalham ibn Salih, even though in Dalham's narration there is an additional chain. And for its chain there is a strong corroborating narrator with a sound isnad."
"5427","(5427) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī — informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Marwazī both said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Kathīr al-Ṣūrī narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ghazzī narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ḥāmid — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Rāzī al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Zāhir ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Kathīr al-Ṣūrī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Ghazzī both said: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for an ʿUmrah in Ramaḍān. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ broke his fast and I kept it; he shortened the prayer and I performed it in full. I said: \O Messenger of Allah"," may my father and mother be sacrificed for you"," you broke your fast and I fasted"," and you shortened the prayer and I performed it in full.\"" He said: \""You have done well"," O ʿľʾishah.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī 1456]"""
"5428","(5428) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Tubbāʿī narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, who said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ʿUmrah and I was with him. He shortened the prayer and I performed it in full; he broke his fast and I fasted. When I arrived in Makkah"," I said: 'May my father and mother be sacrificed for you"," O Messenger of Allah"," you shortened the prayer and I performed it in full"," and you broke your fast and I fasted.' He said: 'You have done well"," O ʿľʾishah.' And he did not censure me.\""\n\nʿAlī said: The first narration is connected (muttaṣil)"," and it is a ḥasan chain. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān had reached ʿľʾishah and visited her while he was on the threshold of adolescence.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5429","(5429) And Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Rāzī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Zāhir ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Zuhayr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she performed ʿUmrah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from al-Madīnah to Makkah. When she arrived in Makkah, she said: \O Messenger of Allah"," may my father and mother be sacrificed for you"," you shortened the prayer and I performed it in full"," and you broke your fast and I fasted.\"" He said: \""You have done well"," O ʿľʾishah.\"" And he did not censure her.\n\nAbū Bakr al-Naysābūrī said: This is what Abū Nuʿaym said — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān"," from ʿľʾishah directly. And whoever said \""from his father\"" in this hadith has erred.\n\nThe Sheikh said: And it is ṣaḥīḥ from ʿľʾishah that she used to perform the prayer in full based on her statement: \""The prayer was ordained as two rakʿahs.\"""""
"5430","(5430) Abū Ḥāmid — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Rāzī al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Zāhir ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq, and Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh all said: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she used to pray four rakʿahs while travelling. I said to her: \Why do you not pray two rakʿahs?\"" She said: \""O son of my sister"," it is not burdensome for me.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5431","(5431) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Ilyās ibn Ṣadaqah narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to me: Khālid ibn Yazīd narrated to me: Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl narrated to me: Rabīʿah narrated to me, from Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān, that ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him from ʿľʾishah that when the prayer was first ordained, it was two rakʿahs both in residence and in travel. The prayer of travel was confirmed at two rakʿahs, and the prayer of residence was completed at four. He (the narrator) said: \I informed ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz of this"," and he said: 'ʿUrwah has informed me that ʿľʾishah used to pray four rakʿahs while travelling.'\"" He said: \""So I found ʿUrwah one day with him and I said: 'How is it that you informed me about ʿľʾishah?' So he narrated to me what ʿUmar had narrated.\"" ʿUmar said: \""Did you not narrate to me that she used to pray four rakʿahs while travelling?\"" He said: \""Yes.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi. See the preceding.]"""
"5432","(5432) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Khashram narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿľʾishah, who said: \The prayer was first ordained as two rakʿahs"," two rakʿahs. Then the prayer of residence was increased"," and the prayer of travel was confirmed.\"" I said: \""What was the matter with ʿľʾishah that she used to perform the prayer in full?\"" He said: \""She interpreted it as ʿUthmān"," may Allah be pleased with him"," interpreted it.\"" The wording is from the hadith of al-Shāfiʿī.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Khashram. And al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad from Sufyān.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1040]"""
"5433","(5433) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash; Ibrāhīm narrated to us: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd say: \ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān"," may Allah be pleased with him"," led us in prayer at Minā with four rakʿahs.\"" This was mentioned to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd"," so he said: 'Innā lillāhi wa innā ilayhi rājiʿūn' (To Allah we belong and to Him we shall return). He said: \""I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Minā two rakʿahs"," and I prayed with Abū Bakr"," may Allah be pleased with him"," at Minā two rakʿahs"," and I prayed with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"," may Allah be pleased with him"," at Minā two rakʿahs. Would that my share of four rakʿahs were two accepted rakʿahs!\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd"," and likewise Muslim.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1034]"""
"5434","(5434) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us that Abū Muʿāwiyah and Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to them — and the hadith of Abū Muʿāwiyah is more complete — from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, who said: \ʿUthmān"," may Allah be pleased with him"," prayed at Minā with four rakʿahs.\"" ʿAbd Allāh said: \""I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two rakʿahs"," and with Abū Bakr two rakʿahs"," and with ʿUmar two rakʿahs.\"" Ḥafṣ added: \""And with ʿUthmān at the beginning of his caliphate"," then he completed it.\"" Abū Muʿāwiyah added here: \""Then the paths diverged for you. I wish that for me"," out of four rakʿahs"," there were two accepted rakʿahs.\""\n\nAl-Aʿmash said: \""Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah narrated to me from his elders that ʿAbd Allāh prayed four rakʿahs. It was said to him: 'You censured ʿUthmān"," then you prayed four rakʿahs?' He said: 'Divergence (al-khilāf) is evil.'\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi. Abū Dāwūd 1960]"""
"5435","(5435) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mūsā ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah narrated to us in Wāsiṭ, from the elders of the tribe, who said: \ʿUthmān prayed Ẓuhr at Minā with four rakʿahs. This reached ʿAbd Allāh"," so he censured him. Then he prayed ʿAṣr with his companions in his camp with four rakʿahs. I said to him: 'You censured ʿUthmān and you prayed four rakʿahs?' He said: 'Indeed"," I detest divergence (al-khilāf).'\"" This has been narrated with a connected chain.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi. See the preceding.]"""
"5436","(5436) ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī in Makkah narrated to us: Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrrah narrated to us: Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, who said: \We were with ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd at Jamʿ (Muzdalifah). When he entered the mosque of Minā"," he asked: 'How many rakʿahs did the Commander of the Faithful pray?' They said: 'Four.' So he prayed four. We said to him: 'Did you not narrate to us that the Prophet ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs and that Abū Bakr prayed two rakʿahs?' He said: 'Yes"," and I narrate it to you now. However"," ʿUthmān is the imam"," and I would not oppose him"," for divergence (al-khilāf) is evil.'\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi]"""
"5437","(5437) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that Musa ibn Isma'il informed us that Hammad narrated from Ayyub from al-Zurhi that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) performed the prayer in full at Mina on account of the Bedouins, for they had increased in number that year, so he prayed with the people performing four rak'ahs in order to teach them that the prayer consists of four rak'ahs. [Weak — Abu Dawud 1964]"
"5438","(5438) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us that Musa ibn Ishaq al-Qadi informed us that Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib informed us that Sulayman ibn Salim, the freedman of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd from his father from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan that he performed the prayer in full at Mina, then he addressed the people and said: \O people"," the Sunnah is the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and the Sunnah of his two companions. However"," there occurred among the people this year something new"," and I feared that they might adopt it as a practice.\"" The Sheikh said: And it has been said otherwise"," and the more likely view is that he considered it a concession (rukhsah) and deemed performing in full to be permissible"," as 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) also held"," and this has been narrated from more than one of the Companions despite their preference for shortening. [Weak — Tarikh Ibn 'Asakir 39/255]"""
"5439","(5439) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari in Baghdad informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us that Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi informed us that 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us that Isra'il informed us that Abu Ishaq from Abu Layla al-Kindi said: Salman arrived with twelve riders from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him), and when the time of prayer came, they said: \Come forward"," O 'Abd Allah"," and lead us in prayer.\"" He said: \""We shall not lead you in prayer"," nor shall we marry your women. Indeed"," Allah has guided us through you.\"" So a man from among the people came forward and prayed with them performing four rak'ahs. Salman said: \""What concern have we with the four-rak'ah prayer? Half of the four-rak'ah prayer would have sufficed us"," and we are more in need of the concession.\"" Thus Salman al-Farsi"," in the presence of these Companions"," made clear that shortening the prayer is a concession. And from Allah alone comes success. And we have narrated from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn 'Abd Yaghuth that both of them used to perform the prayer in full while traveling and also fast"," and we have narrated the permissibility of both positions from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Qilabah. [Authenticated by others — al-Tabrani in al-Kabir 6053]"""
"5440","(5440) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us that Abu Muslim informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Raja' informed us that 'Imran ibn Zayd al-Taghlibi narrated from Zayd al-'Ammi from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: \We"," the company of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)"," used to travel"," and among us would be the fasting one and the non-fasting one"," and the one performing in full and the one shortening. The fasting one would not censure the non-fasting one"," nor would the non-fasting one censure the fasting one"," nor would the one shortening censure the one performing in full"," nor would the one performing in full censure the one shortening.\"" [Weak"," but its origin is in Muslim 1119]"""
"5441","(5441) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Hafiz and Isma'il ibn Ahmad said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah informed us that Harmalah informed us that Ibn Wahb informed me that Yunus narrated from Ibn Shihab that 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar informed him that his father said: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined the Maghrib and 'Isha prayers at Jam' (Muzdalifah) without any prostration between them. He prayed Maghrib with three rak'ahs and 'Isha with two rak'ahs. And 'Abd Allah used to pray at Jam' in the same manner until he was reunited with Allah"," the Almighty and Majestic.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Harmalah ibn Yahya"," and al-Bukhari alluded to its meaning from another narration in his book. [Authentic — Muslim 1288]"""
"5442","(5442) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bilal al-Bazzaz informed us that Muhammad ibn al-Munkhal informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Asim narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq who informed me that Anas ibn Malik said: \We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) from Madinah to Makkah"," and he prayed with us two rak'ahs"," two rak'ahs"," except for Maghrib"," until we returned to Madinah.\"" I said to Anas: \""How long did you stay in Makkah?\"" He said: \""We stayed for ten days.\"" [Authentic]"""
"5443","(5443) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sab'i both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us that Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us that 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' informed us that Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated from 'Amir al-Sha'bi from 'A'ishah that she said: \The prayer was prescribed as two rak'ahs"," two rak'ahs"," except for Maghrib"," which was prescribed as three. And the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)"," when he traveled"," would pray the first prayer (i.e."," the shortened form)"," and when he resided"," he would add two rak'ahs to every two rak'ahs"," except for Maghrib"," for it is odd (witr)"," and except for Fajr"," for the recitation in it is made lengthy.\"" Thus did 'Abd al-Wahhab narrate it. And we have narrated it in the first book of prayer from the hadith of Bakkar ibn 'Abd Allah from Dawud from 'Amir al-Sha'bi from Masruq from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) with part of its meaning. And Mahbub ibn al-Hasan similarly related it from Dawud ibn Abi Hind. [Hasan for others — Ibn Abi Shaybah 36004]"""
"5444","(5444) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isfahani informed us that Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us that Sa'dan ibn Nasr informed us — and 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us that Sa'dan informed us — that Sufyan narrated from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: \I prayed the Zuhr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in Madinah performing four rak'ahs"," and at Dhu al-Hulayfah performing two rak'ahs.\"" He said: And Sufyan narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from Anas and Ibrahim ibn Mayyasarah that he heard from Anas something similar to that. [Authentic — al-Bukhari 1039]"""
"5445","(5445) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Hamid ibn Bilal informed us that Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki informed us that Sufyan narrated from Ibn al-Munkadir and Ibrahim ibn Mayyasarah from Anas who said: \I prayed the Zuhr prayer with the Prophet (peace be upon him) in Madinah performing four rak'ahs"," and the 'Asr prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah performing two rak'ahs.\"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Nu'aym"," and Muslim narrated it from Sa'id ibn Mansur"," both from Sufyan from them"," and they both narrated the hadith of Ayyub from another source. [Authentic — see the preceding hadith]"""
"5446","(5446) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that Ibn Bashshar informed us — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed us that Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us that Muhammad ibn Bashshar al-'Abdi informed us that Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us that Shu'bah narrated from Yahya ibn Yazid al-Huna'i who said: \I asked Anas ibn Malik about shortening the prayer"," as I used to go out to Kufah and pray two rak'ahs until I returned. Anas said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)"," when he set out on a journey of three miles or three fursakhs' — Shu'bah was uncertain — 'would shorten the prayer.'\"" This is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Salamah. And in the narration of Abu Dawud: \""He would pray two rak'ahs","\"" without his mentioning the words \""I used to go out to Kufah and pray two rak'ahs.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Bashshar. [Authentic — Muslim 691]"""
"5447","(5447) Abū Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us — al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Yazīd ibn Khumayr, who said: I heard Ḥabīb ibn ʿUbayd narrate from Jubayr ibn Nufayr from Ibn al-Simṭ: that he came to a village in Ḥimṣ at a distance of thirteen miles and prayed two rakʿahs. I said: \Do you pray two rakʿahs?\"" He said: \""I saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh"," may Allah be pleased with him"," at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah praying two rakʿahs"," so I asked him about that"," and he said: 'I only do as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ doing.'\"" This is the wording of al-Naḍr's hadith.\n\nIn the narration of Abū Dāwūd"," he said: from Ibn al-Simṭ that he heard ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," say: \""I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah two rakʿahs.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī from Shuʿbah"," and all of that refers to the meaning of what Ibn al-Munkadir and others narrated from Anas ibn Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 692]"""
"5448","(5448) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us — Yazīd, meaning Ibn Hārūn, narrated to us — Wiqāʾ ibn Iyās, Abū Yazīd, narrated to us — from ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿah, who said: We went out with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, heading in this direction — and he pointed with his hand towards al-Shām — and he prayed two rakʿahs by two rakʿahs until, when we returned and saw al-Kūfah, the time for prayer came. They said: \O Commander of the Faithful"," this is al-Kūfah — shall we complete the prayer?\"" He said: \""No"," until we enter it.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4321]"""
"5449","(5449) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us — al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us — from Sufyān, from Wiqāʾ ibn Iyās al-Asadī, who narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿah said: We went out with ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and we shortened the prayer while we could still see the houses. Then we returned and shortened the prayer while we could still see the houses. We said to him about that, and ʿAlī said: \We shorten until we enter it.\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"5450","(5450) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us from the original of his book — ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqayl narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Hāshim al-Baghawī narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ said: My uncle Juwayriyah ibn Asmāʾ narrated to me from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn ʿUmar, when he resolved to stay in a town, he would complete the prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5451","(5451) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us — Ibn Jurayj narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿd narrated to me that he heard Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān say: al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd narrated to me that he heard al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The muḥājir may remain in Makkah after completing his rites for three days.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1352]"""
"5452","(5452) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Naḍr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us — al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd that he heard ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ask al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd: \Did you hear anything regarding staying in Makkah?\"" Al-Sāʾib said: \""I heard al-Ḥaḍramī say: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \""For the muḥājir"," a stay of three (days) after entering Makkah.\""'\"" It is as if he said: \""He should not exceed that.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī. Similarly"," in this chain al-Ḥaḍramī is al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī"," and Ḥumayd and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd both heard that from al-Sāʾib. Al-Bukhārī also narrated it through another chain from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5453","(5453) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us — al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Zāhid narrated to us — Abū Saʿīd al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd, who said: I heard ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz say to his companions: \What have you heard regarding dwelling in Makkah?\"" Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd said: \""I heard al-ʿAlāʾ — or he said"," al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī — say: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""The muḥājir shall remain in Makkah after completing his rites for three days.\""'\"" This is the wording of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5454","(5454) ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, set for the Jews, the Christians, and the Magians in Madīnah a stay of three nights during which they could trade and fulfill their needs, and none of them could remain beyond three nights.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said in the older view, and he gave a similar answer in the newer one: \Whoever resolves to stay four (days)"," he completes the prayer.\"" He said: \""Narrations have been reported regarding this"," among them from Qatādah from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān"," may Allah be pleased with him"," similar to that. And likewise Mālik narrated to us from ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib that he said: 'Whoever resolves to stay four (days)"," he completes the prayer.'\""\n\nAs for the hadith of ʿUthmān"," may Allah be pleased with him"," I did not find its chain of narration. And as for the hadith of Ibn al-Musayyib. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 872]"""
"5455","(5455) Abū Aḥmad al-ʿAdl narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Khurāsānī that he heard Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib say: \Whoever resolves to stay four nights while he is a traveler"," he completes the prayer.\""\n\nMālik said: \""And that is the matter upon which the people of knowledge among us have always been.\""\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said: \""We found that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'The muḥājir shall remain after completing his rites for three (days).' And we found that ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," expelled the Jews from the Arabian Peninsula and set them a term of three (days). So we considered three (days) to be within the scope of what a traveler may stay"," and four (days) to be more akin to that of a resident"," because if it were permissible for a traveler to stay more than three"," it would be fitting that the Prophet ﷺ would command the muḥājir to do so and that ʿUmar"," may Allah be pleased with him"," would permit it for the Jews.\""\n\nThe Sheikh said: \""As for the hadith which…\"" [Jayyid. Mālik 345]"""
"5456","(5456) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī narrated to us in al-Kūfah — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayn al-Qazzāz narrated to us — al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, who said: I heard Anas say: \We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he shortened (the prayer) until he came to Makkah. We stayed there for ten (days)"," and he continued to shorten until he returned.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn"," and Muslim narrated it through another route from al-Thawrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 5389]"""
"5457","(5457) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥmmad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq from Anas who said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and performed the Ḥajj with him. He used to pray two rakʿahs at a time until he returned. Anas said: I asked, \How long did you stay in Makkah?\"" He said: \""Ten days.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah"," and this is a ṣaḥīḥ ḥadīth.\n\nWhat Anas ibn Mālik meant by his statement \""we stayed there for ten days\"" is the combined stay in Makkah"," Minā"," and ʿArafāt. This is because the established reports indicate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Makkah on the fourth of Dhū al-Ḥijjah during his Ḥajj and stayed there three days"," shortening his prayers"," without counting the day on which he arrived in Makkah"," for he was travelling on that day"," nor the day of Tarwiyah (8th Dhū al-Ḥijjah)"," for he had departed on that day to Minā"," where he prayed Ẓuhr"," ʿAṣr"," Maghrib"," ʿIshāʾ"," and Fajr. When the sun rose"," he departed from there to ʿArafāt. Then he departed from ʿArafāt when the sun set and came to al-Muzdalifah"," where he spent that night until morning. Then he departed from there until he came to Minā"," where he completed his rites. Then he proceeded to Makkah and performed his ṭawāf there. Then he returned to Minā and stayed there three days"," shortening his prayers. Then he departed from there and stopped at al-Muḥaṣṣab"," announced to his Companions the order to depart"," and set out. He passed by the House and performed ṭawāf before the Fajr prayer"," then departed for Madīnah. Thus the Prophet ﷺ did not stay in any single place for four days while shortening his prayers.\n\nAll of this is found collectively in the narrations of Ibn ʿAbbās"," ʿĀʾishah"," Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh"," Anas ibn Mālik"," and others regarding the account of the Ḥajj. Those narrations with their full chains will be cited"," by the will of Allah"," in the Book of Ḥajj.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the detailed extraction.]"""
"5458","(5458) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān — and he is ʿAbdān —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī informed us in Marw, Abū al-Muwajjih informed us, ʿAbdān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh informed us, ʿĀṣim narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Makkah for nineteen days, praying two rakʿahs [i.e., shortening].\n\nAbū al-Muwajjih added in his narration: Ibn ʿAbbās said: \So we pray two rakʿahs for nineteen days; if we stay more than that"," we complete [the full prayer].\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān. Similarly"," Ḥibbān narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak: nineteen days.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1030.]"""
"5459","(5459) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Manīʿī informed us, Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr informed us, Abū Shihāb narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: We stayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey for nineteen days, and he shortened the prayer. Ibn ʿAbbās said: \If we exceed [that]"," we complete [the prayer].\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus from Abū Shihāb. And Khalaf ibn Hishām narrated it from Abū Shihāb and said: \""Seventeen.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"5460","(5460) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed it to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad informed us, Khalaf ibn Hishām informed us, Abū Shihāb narrated it, and at the end of it Ibn ʿAbbās said: \And we shorten for seventeen; if we exceed [that]"," we complete [the prayer].\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as the extraction above.]"""
"5461","(5461) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, Abū ʿUmar al-Namarī informed us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal and Ḥuṣayn from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ travelled and stayed nineteen days, shortening the prayer. So when we travel and stay nineteen days, we shorten; and when we exceed that, we complete the prayer.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl from Abū ʿAwanah. And Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Ḥabīb, Luwayn, narrated it from Abū ʿAwanah from both of them and said: \We travelled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he stayed seventeen days"," shortening the prayer","\"" and he also mentioned the statement of Ibn ʿAbbās regarding seventeen.\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed it to us"," Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us"," Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ṣaffār informed us"," Luwayn narrated it.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 5458.]"""
"5462","(5462) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Muʿallā ibn Asad informed us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: I travelled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he stayed seventeen days, shortening the prayer.\n\nAbū Muʿāwiyah narrated it from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, and in most of the narrations from him he said: \Nineteen.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Ḥibbān 2750.]"""
"5463","(5463) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bisṭāmī informed it to us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Abū Yaʿlā informed me, Abū Khaythamah and Surayj ibn Yūnus both said: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad informed us, Abū Bakr informed me, al-Jawzī informed us, Mujāhid ibn Mūsā, Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī, al-Faḍl ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, and Yūsuf said: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated — and this is the ḥadīth of al-Jawzī — from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ undertook a journey and stayed nineteen days, praying two rakʿahs at a time. And Ibn ʿAbbās said: \So when we travel and stay nineteen days"," we pray two rakʿahs at a time; and when we stay more than that"," we pray four [i.e."," complete the prayer].\""\n\nAnd likewise in the ḥadīth of Abū Khaythamah and Surayj: nineteen.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 5458.]"""
"5464","(5464) ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it from Abū Muʿāwiyah and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ travelled and stayed nineteen days, praying two rakʿahs at a time.\n\nAbū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed it to us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, ʿImrān informed us, ʿUthmān informed us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated it.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"5465","(5465) Ḥafṣ ibn Ghayyāth narrated it from ʿĀṣim and said: \Seventeen — he shortened the prayer. Whoever stays seventeen days"," he shortens; and whoever stays more than that"," he completes [the prayer].\""\n\nAbū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī al-Adīb informed it to us"," Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me"," al-Ḥasan — that is"," ibn Sufyān — informed us"," Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr informed us"," Ḥafṣ narrated it.\n\nAnd ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr narrated it from ʿIkrimah: \""Nineteen.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 5458.]"""
"5466","(5466) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed it to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū ʿImrān informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith informed us, ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed at the time of the Conquest (Fath) for nineteen nights, praying two rakʿahs.\n\nAnd ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aṣbahānī narrated it from ʿIkrimah: \Seventeen.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"""
"5467","(5467) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī informed me; my father narrated to us; Sharīk narrated from Ibn al-Aṣbahānī from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet ﷺ stayed in Makkah for seventeen days, praying two rakʿahs.\n\nThese narrations differ regarding whether it was nineteen or seventeen, as you see. The most authentic in my view—and Allah knows best—is the narration of those who reported nineteen, which is the narration that Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī included in al-Jāmiʿ al-Ṣaḥīḥ. Those who narrated it did not differ upon it, to my knowledge, including ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak, who is the most precise of those who narrated it from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal. And Allah knows best.\n\n[Ṣaḥīgh for other than itself. Abū Dāwūd 1232]"
"5468","(5468) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥātim narrated to us; Abū Saʿīd al-Ashajj narrated to us; Ibn Idrīs narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from al-Zuhrī from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Prophet ﷺ stayed during the Year of the Conquest—the Conquest of Makkah—for fifteen days, shortening the prayer, until he departed for Ḥunayn.\n\nThis is how he narrated it, and I do not consider it preserved. [Ḍaʿīf. al-Nasāʾī 1911]"
"5469","(5469) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us of it; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ibn Idrīs narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: And Muḥammad ibn Muslim narrated to me: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Makkah for fifteen nights, shortening the prayer, until he departed for Ḥunayn. This is the authentic version, though it is mursal.\n\nIt was also narrated by ʿAbadah ibn Sulaymān, Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī, and Salamah ibn al-Faḍl from Ibn Isḥāq, and they did not mention Ibn ʿAbbās in it. [Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding]"
"5470","(5470) Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from al-Zuhrī from ʿUbaydullāh from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed during the Year of the Conquest for fifteen days, shortening the prayer.\n\nAbū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us of it; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Nufaylī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, and he mentioned it.\n\nIt was also narrated by ʿIrik ibn Mālik from the Prophet ﷺ in a mursal form. And the narration of ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās is more authentic than all of that. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding]"
"5471","(5471) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; al-Ḥajjāj and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb both said: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us.\n\n(H) Yaʿqūb said: And Abū Maʿmar narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us—all of them—from ʿAlī ibn Zayd from Abū Naḍrah from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Makkah at the time of the Conquest for eighteen nights, praying two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs. [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 4/431]"
"5472","(5472) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed you from Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl from a man from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed for seventeen days, praying two rakʿahs, while besieging al-Ṭāʾif.\n\nThe Sheikh said: It is possible to reconcile between the narration of those who reported nineteen, the narration of those who reported seventeen, and the narration of those who reported eighteen, by saying that those who reported nineteen counted the day of entry and the day of departure; those who reported eighteen did not count one of the two days; and those who reported seventeen did not count either of them. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than itself. Previously cited as no. 5462]"
"5473","(5473) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Tabūk for twenty days, shortening the prayer. Maʿmar alone narrated this as a musnad narration.\n\nIt was also narrated by ʿAlī ibn al-Mubārak and others from Yaḥyā from Ibn Thawbān from the Prophet ﷺ in a mursal form. It was narrated from al-Awzāʿī from Yaḥyā from Anas, who said: \Some thirteen","\"" and I do not consider it preserved. It has also been narrated from another chain from Jābir as \""some thirteen.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Ḥibbān 2749]"""
"5474","(5474) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah—meaning ibn ʿAmr—narrated from Abū Isḥāq—meaning al-Fazārī—from Abī Unaysah from Abī al-Zubayr from Jābir, who said: I went on campaign with the Prophet ﷺ, the expedition of Tabūk, and he stayed there for some thirteen days, and he did not exceed two rakʿahs until he returned."
"5475","(5475) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Rajāʾ al-Adīb, Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Khālid ibn Khalī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan—meaning ibn ʿUmarah al-Bajalī—narrated from al-Ḥakam from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Khaybar for forty days, praying two rakʿahs. al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmarah alone narrated it, and he is not relied upon as a ḥujjah. [Munkar]"
"5476","(5476) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: Snow overwhelmed us while we were in Adharbījān for six months during a campaign. Ibn ʿUmar said: And we used to pray two rakʿahs. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 2/83]"
"5477","(5477) Abu al-Mihrājānī informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, that he used to say: \I shall continue to pray the prayer of the traveller so long as I do not resolve to stay"," even if that keeps me detained for twelve nights.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 343]"""
"5478","(5478) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Usayd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān, from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah, who said: \We were with him for two winters — meaning with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — and we did not resolve to stay"," and we shortened the prayer.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5099]"""
"5479","(5479) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Hishām narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Anas, that Anas resided in al-Shām with ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān for two months, praying the prayer of the traveller. [Ḥasan]"
"5480","(5480) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed us, Abū Bakr al-Sadūsī informed us, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated — and Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd al-Imām said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from Anas, that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) resided in Rāmahurmuz for nine months, shortening the prayer. [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil 5/274]"
"5481","(5481) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿImrān narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to me, Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated that Ibn Shihāb narrated to him that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Miswar ibn Makhramah said: \I set out with my father"," Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ"," and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī in the year of al-Adhraḥ. The epidemic struck al-Shām"," so we stayed in al-Sargh for fifty nights. Ramadān entered upon us"," so al-Miswar and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad fasted"," while Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and my father refused to fast. I said to Saʿd: 'O Abū Isḥāq"," you are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and you witnessed Badr"," yet al-Miswar and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān are fasting and you are not fasting?' Saʿd replied: 'I have greater understanding than they do.'\"" [Ḥasan]"""
"5482","(5482) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Hushaym narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: \We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from Madīnah to Makkah"," and he prayed two rakʿahs at a time until he returned.\"" I asked: \""How long did he stay in Makkah?\"" He said: \""Ten days.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as no. 5389]"""
"5483","(5483) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq, who said: \I heard Abū al-Safar narrate from Saʿid ibn Shufayy"," from Ibn ʿAbbās"," who said: 'Whenever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out from his house as a traveller"," he would pray two rakʿahs at a time until he returned.'\"" And the hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās and ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn regarding the Prophet's (ﷺ) shortening of the prayer in Makkah during the Year of the Conquest has already been cited. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as no. 5384]"""
"5484","(5484) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Muslim informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from ʿAlī ibn Zayd, from Abī Naḍrah, that a man asked ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during travel. He said: \Come to our gathering.\"" Then he said: \""This man has asked me about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during travel"," so memorize it from me: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never undertook a journey except that he prayed two rakʿahs until he returned"," and he would say: 'O people of Makkah"," stand and pray two rakʿahs"," for we are travellers.' He conquered al-Ṭāʾif and Ḥunayn and prayed two rakʿahs"," and he came to al-Jiʿrānah and performed ʿUmrah therefrom. I performed Hajj and ʿUmrah with Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and he used to pray two rakʿahs; and with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and he used to pray two rakʿahs; and with ʿUthmān"," and he prayed two rakʿahs at the beginning of his caliphate"," then ʿUthmān prayed four rakʿahs in Minā.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Previously cited as no. 5471]"""
"5485","(5485) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Abī ʿUthmān al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, ʿAffān ibn Muslim, Abū al-Walīd Hishām ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, Abū ʿUmar Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq, and al-Rabīʿ ibn Yaḥyā al-Ushnānī said: Shuʿbah narrated — and this is the wording of ʿAffān's hadith — he said: \Qatādah informed me"," saying: 'I heard Mūsā ibn Salamah say: \""I asked Ibn ʿAbbās and said: 'I am in Makkah"," so how should I pray?' He said: 'Two rakʿahs — that is the Sunnah of Abū al-Qāsim (ﷺ).'\""'\"" And ʿAmr ibn Marzūq said in his hadith: \""I asked Ibn ʿAbbās: 'How many rakʿahs should I pray if I miss the prayer in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām?' He said: 'Two rakʿahs — that is the Sunnah of Abū al-Qāsim (ﷺ).'\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Shuʿbah and others from Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 688]"""
"5486","(5486) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Wuhayb narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sawādah al-Qushayrī narrated from his father, from Anas ibn Mālik — a man from among them — that he came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while the Prophet (ﷺ) was taking his midday meal. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \Come"," join the meal.\"" He said: \""O Prophet of Allah"," I am fasting.\"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \""Allah has relieved the traveller of fasting and half of the prayer"," and the pregnant and the nursing mother.\"""""
"5487","(5487) Yahyā ibn Naṣr ibn Ḥājib narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shubrumah from Nāʿim from Ibn ʿUmar that Tamīm al-Dārī asked ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, about travelling by sea, as he was heavily engaged in maritime trade. He instructed him to shorten the prayer. He said: Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, says: \It is He who enables you to travel on land and sea\"" [Yūnus: 22].\n\nAbū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported it in al-Tārīkh; Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Mudhakkir narrated to us; Abū Naṣr Fatḥ ibn Nūḥ al-Shāhanbarī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Naṣr ibn Ḥājib al-Qurashī narrated to us"," and he mentioned it. [Weak]"""
"5488","(5488) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb reported to us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī reported to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed me; he said: Abū Hammām narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭaḥmān from Ḥusayn al-Muktab from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: \I was suffering from haemorrhoids"," so I asked the Prophet ﷺ. He said: 'Pray standing; if you are unable"," then pray sitting; and if you are unable"," then on your side.'\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān from Ibn al-Mubārak. A specific hadith has also been narrated in this chapter. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1066]"""
"5489","(5489) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported it to us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Shaybānī reported to us in Kūfah; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayd narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \The Prophet ﷺ was asked about prayer on a ship. He said: 'How should I pray on a ship?' He said: 'Pray on it standing"," unless you fear drowning.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 1/409]"""
"5490","(5490) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥurḍī reported to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd ibn Suhayl al-Mawṣilī reported to us; Ḥāmid ibn Shuʿayb al-Balkhī narrated to us; al-Ṣalt ibn Masʿūd narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Dāwūd narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded his companions when they set out for Abyssinia to pray on the ship standing"," so long as they did not fear drowning.\"" Thus he said. There is a difference of opinion regarding this narration from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dāwūd"," and it is said that he did not hear it from Jaʿfar. The hadith of Abū Nuʿaym"," al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn"," is sound. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains]"""
"5491","(5491) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥurḍī reported to us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd reported to us; Ḥāmid al-Balkhī narrated to us; al-Ṣalt ibn Masʿūd narrated to us; ʿAmr — I think he is ibn ʿAbd al-Ghaffār al-Fuqaymī — narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib"," may Allah be pleased with him"," and his companions"," when they set out for Abyssinia"," used to pray on the ship standing.\"" [Munkar]"""
"5492","(5492) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh reported to us; Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdaws ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Simsār narrated to us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us; he said: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us; he said: \Anas ibn Mālik was asked about prayer on a ship. ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī ʿUtbah"," the freedman of Anas"," who was with us in the assembly"," said: 'I travelled with Abū al-Dardāʾ"," Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī"," and Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh"," and he led us in prayer standing on the ship"," and we prayed behind him standing. Had we wished"," we could have disembarked.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5493","(5493) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr reported to us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muḥammad al-Muʾaddib narrated to us; Ḥarb ibn Maymūn narrated from al-Naḍr ibn Anas from Anas: \When he boarded a ship and the prayer time came while the ship was stationary"," he prayed standing; and when it was moving"," he prayed sitting in congregation.\"" [Strong]"""
"5494","(5494) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb reported to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; al-Shāfiʿī reported to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, who said: \I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: 'Should I shorten the prayer when going to ʿArafah?' He said: 'No"," but to Jiddah"," ʿUsfān"," and al-Ṭāʾif. And if you arrive at your family or your livestock"," then pray the full prayer.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously narrated under no. 5495]"""
"5495","(5495) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh reported to us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Najīd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Umayyah narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us; Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim narrated from ʿAmr from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ from Ibn ʿAbbās, that a man came to him and said: \Should I shorten the prayer from Marr?\"" He said: \""No.\"" He said: \""From ʿArafāt?\"" He said: \""No.\"" He said: \""From Jiddah?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""From al-Ṭāʾif?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""And when you reach your family or your livestock"," then complete the prayer.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding narration]"""
"5496","(5496) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī reported to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us; Warqāʾ narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid regarding His statement, \without being a transgressor or aggressor\"" [al-Baqarah: 173]: he says: \""Without being a highway robber"," or one who abandons the Imāms"," or one who goes out in disobedience to Allah.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarī narrated it in his Tafsīr 2/88]"""
"5497","(5497) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, he said: (ḥ) And Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us, and the wording is his; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad narrated to us; my father narrated to us, both saying: Wakīʿ narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah narrated to us from his father, who said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah, and he was in al-Abṭaḥ in a red leather tent. He said: Bilāl came out with his ablution water, and people were taking from it—some receiving a share and some sprinkling it. He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ came out, wearing a red ḥullah (garment), and it was as though I could see the whiteness of his shins. He said: He performed ablution, and Bilāl called the adhān. He said: I kept following his mouth here and there, as he turned right and left, saying, \Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh"," ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ\"" (Come to prayer"," come to success). He said: Then a short spear was planted for him"," and he stepped forward and prayed Ẓuhr as two rakʿahs. A donkey and a dog passed in front of him"," and they were not prevented. Then he prayed ʿAṣr as two rakʿahs. Then he continued to pray two rakʿahs until he returned to al-Madīnah.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 503]"""
"5498","(5498) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us; ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿAbd al-Malik Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us; ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah narrated to us from his father, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ in a red tent, and I saw Bilāl bring out his ablution water. I saw the people hastening toward it and wiping themselves with it. Whoever did not obtain any of it took from the moisture of his companion. And I saw Bilāl bring out a short spear; he planted it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out. I saw him in a red ḥullah, with his garment rolled up, praying toward the short spear with the people, two rakʿahs.\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿUmar ibn Abī Zāʾidah, and the aḥādīth on this meaning are numerous. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5499","(5499) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us; Ibn ʿUlayyah informed us; ʿAlī ibn Zayd narrated to us from Abū Naḍrah from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: I went on military expeditions with the Prophet ﷺ, and I witnessed the Conquest (of Makkah) with him. He stayed in Makkah for eighteen nights, praying only two rakʿahs. He would say: \O people of the city"," pray four"," for we are travellers.\""\n\nAnd we have narrated before this"," in this ḥadīth"," from Abū Bakr and ʿUmar"," something similar. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1229]"""
"5500","(5500) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmrūyah al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī narrated to us; Shaybān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us from Zayd ibn Aslam: that his father informed him that he witnessed ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) pray with the people of Makkah during the Ḥajj as two rakʿahs. Then, after he had said the salām, he said to them: \Complete your prayer"," O people of Makkah"," for we are travellers.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 346]"""
"5501","(5501) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us from Ibn Shihāb from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣafwān, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar came to visit ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣafwān, and he led us in two rakʿahs, then he turned away (said salām), so we stood and completed (the prayer).\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 349]"
"5502","(5502) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that when he prayed with the imām, he prayed four (rakʿahs), and when he prayed alone, he prayed two rakʿahs.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 694]"
"5503","(5503) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated to us from Abī Mijlaz, who said: I said to Ibn ʿUmar: \A traveller catches two rakʿahs of the prayer of the people\""—meaning the residents—\""are the two rakʿahs sufficient for him"," or should he pray with their prayer?\"" He laughed and said: \""He should pray with their prayer.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it. Ibn Abī Shaybah 13978]"""
"5504","(5504) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī informed us in Baghdād, in the Mosque of Ḥarbiyyah; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us, reciting to him; Abū Ismāʿīl Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Muʿallā ibn Asad, the brother of Bahz ibn Asad, narrated to us; Wuhaib ibn Khālid narrated to us from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from Abū Murrah, the freedman of ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib, from Umm Hānīʾ: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in her house during the year of the Conquest eight rakʿahs in a single garment, the two ends of which were crossed over each other.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir from Muʿallā ibn Asad, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū al-Naḍr from Abū Murrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 4902]"
"5505","(5505) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; he said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: al-Layth ibn Saʿd and Abū Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān informed you from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym from Abū Bustrah al-Ghifārī from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: I travelled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on eighteen journeys, and I never saw him omit the two rakʿahs before Ẓuhr.\n\nAnd there have preceded aḥādīth regarding the Prophet's ﷺ voluntary prayers during his journeys while mounted on his riding animal. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Tirmidhī 550]"
"5506","(5506) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Sūsī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd informed me; my father narrated to me; al-Awzāʿī narrated to me; Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī narrated to me; Ḥasan ibn Muslim narrated me; Ṭāwūs al-Yamānī narrated to me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ established as Sunnah—meaning the prayer of travel—two rakʿahs, and he established the prayer of residence as four rakʿahs. And just as the prayer before and after the prayer of residence is good, likewise the prayer before and after the prayer of travel (is good).\n\n[Ḥasan. Ibn Mājah 1072]"
"5507","(5507) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, ʿĪsā ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated from his father, who said: I accompanied Ibn ʿUmar on a road—meaning the road to Makkah. He led us in two rakʿahs, then he turned and saw people standing. He said: \What are these people doing?\"" I said: \""They are performing supererogatory prayers.\"" He said: \""Had I been performing supererogatory prayers"," I would have completed my prayer"," O son of my brother. I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey"," and he did not increase beyond two rakʿahs until Allah took his soul. I accompanied Abū Bakr—may Allah be pleased with him—and he did not increase beyond two rakʿahs until Allah took his soul. I accompanied ʿUmar"," and he did not increase beyond two rakʿahs until Allah took his soul. I accompanied ʿUthmān"," and he did not increase beyond two rakʿahs until Allah took his soul.\"" And Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," has said: {Indeed"," in the Messenger of Allah you have an excellent example} [al-Aḥzāb: 21].\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī"," and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from ʿĪsā ibn Ḥafṣ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 689]"""
"5508","(5508) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, that he used not to pray anything alongside the obligatory prayer on a journey, neither before it nor after it, except during the depths of the night, for he would pray upon his camel—or upon his mount—whichever direction it faced. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 350]"
"5509","(5509) Abū Jaʿfar—Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī—in Naysābūr, and Abū Ṭāhir al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Salamah al-Hamadhānī in Hamadhān, both said: Abū Sahl—Bishr ibn Aḥmad ibn Bishr al-Isfarāʾīnī—informed us, Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUqayl ibn Saʿīd al-Bayhaqī, Abū Sulaymān, narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Tamīmī al-Naysābūrī informed us, Abū Khaythamah narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and it rained upon us. He said: \Let whoever wishes among you pray in his dwelling.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as no. 5022]"""
"5510","(5510) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī—may Allah have mercy on him—informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us, Abū ʿAwn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, ʿAbdān informed us, my father narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey on a dark and muddy night—or a dark and cold night—or a dark and rainy night, and his caller called out: \Pray in your dwellings.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as no. 5020]"""
"5511","(5511) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ, when the journey became urgent for him, would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1055]"
"5512","(5512) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when the journey became urgent for him"," would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us"," Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us"," Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us"," Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar"," from Nāfiʿ"," that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar hastened the journey and combined Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. I asked Nāfiʿ"," and he said: \""After the twilight had departed"," by an hour.\"" And he said: \""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do that when the journey became urgent for him.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 703]"""
"5513","(5513) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā both said: Yaḥyā narrated to us—and this is the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Muthannā—Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh informed us, Nāfiʿ informed me, from Ibn ʿUmar, that when the journey became urgent for him, he would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ after the twilight had departed. And he mentioned that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when the journey became urgent for him, would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"5514","(5514) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, that Ibn ʿUmar was called to the aid of Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd while he was in Makkah and she was in Madīnah. He set out and travelled until the sun had set and the stars had appeared. A man who was accompanying him said: \The prayer! The prayer!\"" But Ibn ʿUmar continued travelling. Sālim said to him: \""The prayer!\"" He said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when something urgent arose for him on a journey"," would combine these two prayers.\"" Then he travelled on until"," when the twilight had departed"," he combined the two prayers. And he travelled between Makkah and Madīnah for three days.\n\nMaʿmar narrated it from Ayyūb"," and Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Nāfiʿ"," and he said in the ḥadīth: \""He delayed Maghrib after the departure of the twilight until a portion of the night had passed"," then he dismounted and prayed Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\"" And he said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that when the journey became urgent for him or something pressing arose.\""\n\nYazīd ibn Hārūn narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī"," from Nāfiʿ"," and he mentioned that he travelled close to a quarter of the night"," then dismounted and prayed. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 2/51]"""
"5515","(5515) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid and Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī both said: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd al-ʿUdhri in Beirut narrated to us, my father informed me, ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Zayd narrated to me, Nāfiʿ, the mawlā of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, narrated from Ibn ʿUmar, that he came from Makkah and news of Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd reached him, so he hastened the journey. When the sun had set, one of his companions said: \The prayer!\"" He remained silent and continued travelling. His companion said again: \""The prayer!\"" He remained silent. The one who had said \""The prayer\"" said: \""He certainly knows something about this that I do not know.\"" He continued travelling until"," after the twilight had departed by about an hour"," he dismounted and established the prayer. He would not give the call to prayer for any of the prayers during travel. He stood and prayed Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together"," combining the two. Then he said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when the journey became urgent for him"," would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ after the twilight had departed by about an hour. And he would pray upon the back of his mount in whichever direction it faced during travel for the supererogatory prayer"," and he would inform them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that.\""\n\nAl-Naysābūrī said: \""…for any of the prayers during travel.\"" The narration of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī"," Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah"," ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar"," Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī"," and ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Zayd from Nāfiʿ are in agreement that Ibn ʿUmar's combining of the two prayers was after the setting of the twilight. Those who do not match them in the preservation of Nāfiʿ's aḥādīth opposed them. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5516","(5516) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd informed us, my father informed me. He said: I heard Ibn Jābir say: Nāfiʿ narrated to me, saying: I went out with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar while he was heading to a piece of land of his. He stopped at a place, and a man came to him and said: \Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd is in a state [of illness]"," and I do not think you will reach her\""—and that was after ʿAṣr. He set out quickly"," and a man from Quraysh was with him. We travelled until the sun had set"," and he did not say \""the prayer\"" to me. It was my experience with my companion that he was vigilant about the prayer. When he delayed"," I said: \""The prayer"," may Allah have mercy on you!\"" He did not turn to me. He continued as he was until"," when it was the last part of the twilight"," he dismounted and prayed Maghrib. Then he established the prayer when the twilight had disappeared"," and he led us in prayer. Then he turned to us and said: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when something became urgent for him"," would do likewise.\""\n\nFuḍayl ibn Ghazwān and ʬāṭaf ibn Khālid narrated something similar from Nāfiʿ. The narration of the ḥuffāẓ among the companions of Nāfiʿ is more deserving of being correct. Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh"," Aslam the mawlā of ʿUmar"," ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār"," and Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhʾayb—or it is said Ibn Dhʾayb—narrated from Ibn ʿUmar something similar to their narration.\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of Sālim"," ʿĀṣim ibn Muḥammad narrated it from his brother ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad"," from Sālim. As for the ḥadīth of Aslam… [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the meaning above]"""
"5517","(5517) He was informed by Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb, who was informed by Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī, who was informed by Mūsā ibn al-ʿAbbās, who narrated from al-Saghānī and ʿAlī ibn al-Mughīrah, who both said: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, who was informed by Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, who was informed by Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, who said: I was with Ibn ʿUmar on the road to Makkah when news reached him of Ṣafiyyah's severe illness. He hastened the journey until after the twilight had set, then he dismounted and prayed Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ, combining the two. He said: \I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) when the journey pressed him"," he would delay Maghrib and combine the two prayers.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam. As for the ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār… [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1711]"""
"5518","(5518) Al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, who was informed by ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh, who narrated from Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān, who narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ and Ibn Bukayr, who both said: Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us. Rabīʿah ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār — who was among the righteous Muslims in truthfulness and piety — narrated to me, saying: \The sun set while we were with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar"," so we continued traveling. When we saw that evening had come"," we said to him: 'The prayer!' He remained silent and traveled on until the twilight disappeared and the stars became prominent. Then he dismounted and prayed the two prayers together. Then he said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) when the journey pressed him"," he would pray these two prayers — meaning he would combine the two after nightfall.'\""\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān… [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1217]"""
"5519","(5519) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us, who narrated from Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, who was informed by al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān, who was informed by al-Shāfiʿī, who was informed by Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī informed us in Kūfah, who was informed by Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym, who narrated from Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim, who narrated from al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn, who narrated from Ibn ʿUyaynah, from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Dhūʾayb, who said: \I accompanied Ibn ʿUmar. When the sun set"," we were hesitant to say to him: 'Stand for the prayer.' When the whiteness of the horizon and the darkness of the evening disappeared"," he dismounted and prayed three rakʿahs and two rakʿahs. Then he turned to us and said: 'Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) do.'\""\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn.\n\nAnd the ḥadīth of al-Shāfiʿī is more complete. He said: \""We set out with Ibn ʿUmar towards al-Ḥimā. The sun set"," and we were hesitant to say to him: 'Dismount and pray.' When the whiteness of the horizon and the darkness of the evening disappeared"," he dismounted and prayed three rakʿahs"," then gave salām"," then prayed two rakʿahs"," then gave salām. Then he turned to us and said: 'Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) do.'\"" He narrated it from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Asadī.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 2/12]"""
"5520","(5520) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who narrated from Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, who narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Saghānī, who was informed by Ḥassān ibn ʿAbdullāh, who narrated from al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, who was informed by Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah, who narrated from Abū Dāwūd, who narrated from Qutaybah and Ibn Mawhab, who both said: al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to us from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) departed before the sun declined"," he would delay Ẓuhr until the time of ʿAṣr"," then dismount and combine the two. If the sun declined before he departed"," he would pray Ẓuhr and then ride.\""\n\nIn the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdullāh: \""That the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would…\"" and the remainder is the same.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥassān ibn ʿAbdullāh and Qutaybah. And Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1060]"""
"5521","(5521) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, who narrated from Baḥr ibn Naṣr, who said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: \Jābir ibn Ismāʿīl informed you from ʿUqayl"," from Ibn Shihāb"," from Anas ibn Mālik"," from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)"," that when the journey pressed him"," he would delay Ẓuhr to the beginning of the time of ʿAṣr and combine the two"," and he would delay Maghrib until he combined it with ʿIshāʾ when the twilight disappeared.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and others from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 704]"""
"5522","(5522) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who was informed by ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar ibn Mahdī al-Ḥāfiẓ, who narrated from al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl, who narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, who narrated from Shabābah, who narrated from Layth ibn Saʿd, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Anas, who said: \When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) intended to combine Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr during travel"," he would delay Ẓuhr until the beginning of the time of ʿAṣr.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid from Shabābah"," and he added: \""Then he would combine the two.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 704]"""
"5523","(5523) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, who was informed by Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī, who was informed by Jaʿfar al-Firyābī, who narrated from Isḥāq ibn Rāhawayh, who narrated from Shabābah ibn Sawār, from Layth ibn Saʿd, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Anas ibn Mālik: \When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was on a journey and the sun declined"," he would pray Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together"," then depart.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5524","(5524) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, who was informed by Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Adamī, who narrated from al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad, who narrated from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar ibn Fāris.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī informed us in Baghdad, who narrated from Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Daqqāq, known as Ibn al-Sammāk, by dictation in the year 339 AH, who narrated from Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram ibn Ḥassān al-Bazzāz, who narrated from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar, who narrated from Sufyān, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abū al-Ṭufayl, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined Ẓuhr"," ʿAṣr"," Maghrib"," and ʿIshāʾ during the year of Tabūk.\""\n\nʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar is alone in narrating it thus.\n\nAnd others narrated it from al-Thawrī"," from Abū al-Zubayr"," from Abū al-Ṭufayl.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 706]"""
"5525","(5525) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us, who narrated from Usayd ibn ʿĀṣim, who narrated from al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ, from Sufyān, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Abū al-Ṭufayl, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, who said: \The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined during the expedition of Tabūk between Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr"," and between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\""\n\n[See the preceding]"""
"5526","(5526) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us, who narrated from Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, who was informed by al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān, who was informed by al-Shāfiʿī, who was informed by Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, who was informed by Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah, who narrated from Abū Dāwūd, who narrated from al-Qaʿnabī, from Mālik, from Abū al-Zubayr al-Makkī, from Abū al-Ṭufayl ʿĀmir ibn Wāthilah: \Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) informed them that they went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) on the expedition of Tabūk"," and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would combine Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr"," and Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. He delayed the prayer one day"," then went out and prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr"," then went in"," then went out and prayed Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together.\""\n\nThe wording of both their ḥadīths is the same.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥanafī from Mālik. He also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah and Qurrah ibn Khālid"," from Abū al-Zubayr"," from Abū al-Ṭufayl.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 706]"""
"5527","(5527) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Mawhab al-Ramlī narrated to us that al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah narrated to us from al-Layth ibn Saʿd from Hishām ibn Saʿd from Abū al-Zubayr from Abū al-Ṭufayl from Muʿādh ibn Jabal that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the expedition of Tabūk, when the sun had declined before he departed, he combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together; and if he departed before the sun had declined, he delayed Ẓuhr until he dismounted for ʿAṣr. And regarding Maghrib he did likewise: if the sun set before he departed, he combined Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together; and if he departed before the sun had set, he delayed Maghrib until he dismounted for ʿIshāʾ and then combined the two. [Ṣaḥīh. Abū Dāwūd 1208]"
"5528","(5528) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us that Qutaybah narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Kaʿbī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us that Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī in al-Rayy narrated to us that al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Ṭufayl from Muʿādh ibn Jabal that the Prophet (ﷺ) during the expedition of Tabūk, when he departed before the sun's decline, he delayed Ẓuhr until he combined it with ʿAṣr, praying them both together; and when he departed after the sun's decline, he prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together and then proceeded. And when he departed before Maghrib, he delayed Maghrib until he prayed it with ʿIshāʾ; and when he departed after Maghrib, he hastened ʿIshāʾ and prayed it with Maghrib. Qutaybah ibn Saʿd was alone in narrating this from al-Layth from Yazīd. [Ṣaḥīh. Abū Dāwūd 1220]"
"5529","(5529) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: I heard Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn ʿImrān al-Faqīh al-Ṣaydalānī say: I heard Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah say: I heard Ṣāliḥ ibn Ḥafṣawayh, a Naysābūrī who was a scholar of ḥadīth, say: I heard Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī say: I said to Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd, \With whom did you write from al-Layth ibn Saʿd the ḥadīth of Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Ṭufayl?\"" He said"," \""I wrote it alongside Khālid al-Madāʾinī.\"" Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl said: And this Khālid al-Madāʾinī used to interpolate ḥadīths into the narrations of the shaykhs. The Shaykh said: What they objected to in this narration is specifically the report of Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Ṭufayl. As for the report of Abū al-Zubayr from Abū al-Ṭufayl"," it is preserved and authentic."""
"5530","(5530) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Rūḥ narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us that Ibn Jurayj narrated to us from Ḥusayn from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when the sun declined while he was at his camp, he combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr; and if it did not decline until he departed, he traveled until the time of ʿAṣr entered, then he dismounted and combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr. And when the sun set while he was at his camp, he combined Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ; and if it did not set until he departed, he traveled until the time of ʿIshāʾ arrived, then he dismounted and combined Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated it from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ḥusayn informed me from Kurayb from Ibn ʿAbbās, and it is as though Ḥusayn heard it from both of them. [Ṣaḥīh through other chains. Aḥmad 1/367]"
"5531","(5531) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn Yaḥyā al-Jurjānī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbbās informed me from ʿIkrimah and from Kurayb, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, that Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \Shall I not inform you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during travel?\"" We said"," \""Yes.\"" He said: \""When the sun declined for him at his camp"," he combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr before mounting; and if it did not decline for him at his camp"," he traveled until the time of ʿAṣr arrived"," then he dismounted and combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr. And when Maghrib arrived for him at his camp"," he combined it with ʿIshāʾ; and if it did not arrive at his camp"," he mounted until the time of ʿIshāʾ arrived"," then he dismounted and combined the two.\"" ʿAlī said: ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated it from Ibn Jurayj from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from Ḥusayn from Kurayb from Ibn ʿAbbās"," so it is possible that Ibn Jurayj first heard it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from Ḥusayn"," as ʿAbd al-Majīd reported from him"," and then Ibn Jurayj met Ḥusayn and heard it directly from him"," as ʿAbd al-Razzāq and Ḥajjāj reported from Ibn Jurayj. The Shaykh said: It was also narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān"," Yazīd ibn al-Hād"," and Abū Uways al-Madanī from Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbdullāh from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās"," and it is strengthened by the preceding supporting narrations. And with Allah is the success. [Ṣaḥīh through other chains. See what precedes]"""
"5532","(5532) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs al-Naysābūrī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to me that my father narrated to me that Ibrāhīm narrated to me from al-Ḥusayn from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr during travel when he was upon his mount, and he combined Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: And Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān mentioned it. And Ayyūb narrated from Abū Qilābah from Ibn ʿAbbās, and he did not know it except as marfūʿ, with the same meaning as the narration of Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbdullāh. [Ṣaḥīh through other chains. See what precedes]"
"5533","(5533) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyūb from Abū Qilābah from Ibn ʿAbbās, and I do not know it except as marfūʿ; otherwise it is from Ibn ʿAbbās: that when he dismounted at a place during travel and the place pleased him, he stayed there until he combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, then he departed. And if the place was not suitable for him, he continued traveling and delayed Ẓuhr until he reached the place where he intended to combine Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr. He said: And Ismāʿīl narrated to us that ʿĀrim narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyūb from Abū Qilābah from Ibn ʿAbbās, and I do not know it except as marfūʿ. ʿĀrim said: Thus Ḥammād narrated it, saying: When he traveled and dismounted at a place that pleased him, he stayed there until he combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"5534","(5534) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us from Ayyūb from Abū Qilābah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \When you are traveling and a campsite presents itself to you"," continue until you reach a place where you can combine the two prayers. And if you are encamped and something urgent arises"," combine the two prayers and then depart.\"" [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"5535","(5535) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-Jārī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from Mālik from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) experienced the setting of the sun in Makkah and combined the two prayers at Saraf. And we narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Ḥimyānī from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and al-Ajlaḥ narrated it from Abū al-Zubayr likewise. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1215]"
"5536","(5536) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Hishām, a neighbor of Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us from Hishām ibn Saʿd, who said: \Between them—meaning between Makkah and Saraf—there are ten miles. And combining the two prayers due to the excuse of travel is a well-known matter practiced among the Companions and the Successors (may Allah be pleased with them all)"," established from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," then from his Companions"," and then from what the Muslims have unanimously agreed upon regarding the combining of prayers at ʿArafah and then at al-Muzdalifah.\"" [Abū Dāwūd 1216]"""
"5537","(5537) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān narrated to us; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, who said: Sālim narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: \I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when the journey pressed him during travel"," he would delay the Maghrib prayer until he combined it with the ʿIshāʾ.\"" Sālim said: \""And ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to do that when the journey pressed him — he would commence the Maghrib prayer and pray three rakʿahs"," then give salām. Then he would scarcely wait before commencing the ʿIshāʾ prayer and praying two rakʿahs"," then giving salām. He would not perform any supererogatory prayer between them with a rakʿah"," nor would he perform any supererogatory prostration after the ʿIshāʾ until he rose in the middle of the night.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See 5512]"""
"5538","(5538) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, that he said to Sālm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar: \How greatly have I seen your father ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar delay the Maghrib during travel?\"" He said: \""The sun set for him at Dhāt al-Jaysh"," and he prayed it at al-ʿAqīq.\""\n\nAl-Thawrī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and added in it: \""Eight miles.\"" And Ibn Jurayj narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and added in it: he said"," \""I said: 'At what time was that?' He said: 'A third or a quarter of the night had passed.'\""\n\nAnd Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Nāfiʿ"," who said: \""He travelled some miles"," then dismounted and prayed.\"" Yaḥyā said: \""And Nāfiʿ narrated this ḥadīth to me another time and said: 'He travelled close to a quarter of the night"," then dismounted and prayed.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 336]"""
"5539","(5539) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us; Saʿīd narrated to us from Qatādah, from Jābir ibn Zayd, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he used to combine the two prayers during travel and would say: \It is a Sunnah.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4408]"""
"5540","(5540) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Jurayrī and Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated to us from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī, who said: \Saʿīd ibn Zayd and Usāmah ibn Zayd"," when the journey pressed them"," would combine Ẓuhr with ʿAṣr and Maghrib with ʿIshāʾ.\""\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us concerning that from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Anas ibn Mālik"," and it has been narrated from ʿUmar and ʿUthmān"," may Allah be pleased with them. [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"5541","(5541) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us from Ibn Shihāb, that he said: \I asked Sālm ibn ʿAbd Allāh: 'Is Ẓuhr combined with ʿAṣr during travel?' He said: 'Yes"," there is no harm in that. Have you not seen the prayer of the people at ʿArafah?'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 332]"""
"5542","(5542) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Salamah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī narrated to us from Zayd ibn Aslam, and Rabīʿah ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, and Abū al-Zinād, among their peers, who went out to al-Walīd — he had sent for them to seek their legal opinion on a matter — and they would combine Ẓuhr with ʿAṣr when the sun declined. [Ḥasan]"
"5543","(5543) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah ibn Qaʿnab narrated to us from Mālik ibn Anas, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together"," and Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together"," without fear and without travel.\"" Mālik said: \""I think that was due to rain.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 330]"""
"5544","(5544) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: \I read it back to Mālik"," and he mentioned it similarly"," except that he did not mention the statement of Mālik.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Likewise"," Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah and Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it from Abū al-Zubayr — without fear and without travel — except that they did not mention Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ"," and they said: \""In Madīnah.\"" And it was also narrated by Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah and Hishām ibn Saʿd from Abū al-Zubayr with the meaning of Mālik's narration. However"," Qurrah ibn Khālid contradicted them from Abū al-Zubayr"," and said in the ḥadīth: \""During a journey he undertook to Tabūk.\""\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of Zuhayr: [Ṣaḥīḥ. See what precedes it]"""
"5545","(5545) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Abū al-Zubayr narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together in Madīnah"," without fear and without travel.\"" Abū al-Zubayr said: \""So I asked Saʿīd: 'Why did he do that?' He said: 'I asked Ibn ʿAbbās just as you asked me"," and he said: He intended that he should not cause hardship upon anyone of his Ummah.'\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus.\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of Ḥammād ibn Salamah: [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 705]"""
"5546","(5546) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Ḥajjāj, meaning Ibn Minhāl, narrated to us; he said: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us from Abū al-Zubayr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr in Madīnah, without fear and without travel.\n\nAs for the ḥadīth of Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah: [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as above]"
"5547","(5547) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us: Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Ali, that is Ibn al-Madini, narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Amr, who heard Jabir ibn Zayd say: I heard Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I prayed with the Prophet (peace be upon him) eight rak'ahs combined and seven rak'ahs combined.\n\n'Ali said: Sufyan narrated it to us from Abu al-Zubayr from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas, similarly. I said to Ibn 'Abbas: Why did he do that? He said: He intended not to place hardship upon his Ummah. Sufyan added once in the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr: without fear and without travel.\n\nAs for the hadith of Hisham ibn Sa'd… [Its meaning is sound as previously stated.]"
"5548","(5548) Abu al-Qasim al-Harfi in Baghdad informed us: 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Kufi narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated from Hisham ibn Sa'd. Abu al-Zubayr narrated from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined between Zuhr and 'Asr, and Maghrib and 'Isha' in Madinah, without fear and without travel. I said: What do you think, O Abu 'Abbas? He said: He intended not to place hardship upon his Ummah.\n\nAs for the hadith of Qurrah ibn Khalid, it differs from these. [Its meaning is sound; its chain has been previously cited.]"
"5549","(5549) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu al-Walid al-Faqih narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn Nasr al-Hafiz narrated to us: Yahya ibn Habib ibn 'Arabi narrated to us: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us: Qurrah.\n\n(And) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Umari al-Muqri' in Baghdad informed us: Abu Muhammad Isma'il ibn 'Ali al-Khattabi narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad narrated to me: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Qurrah narrated from Abu al-Zubayr from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined, during a journey he made during the expedition to Tabuk, between Zuhr and 'Asr, and Maghrib and 'Isha'. I said to Ibn 'Abbas: What motivated him to do that? He said: He intended not to place hardship upon his Ummah.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Habib. It is as though Qurrah ibn Khalid intended the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr from Abu al-Tufayl from Mu'adh; this is the wording of his hadith. Or Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated both hadiths together, and Qurrah heard one of them, while those mentioned earlier heard the other, and this is more likely. For Qurrah also narrated the hadith of Abu al-Tufayl. Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated it from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, but he contradicted Abu al-Zubayr in its text. [Its meaning is sound; previously cited under no. 5545.]"
"5550","(5550) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us: Hajib ibn Ahmad al-Tusi narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Hashim narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us: al-A'mash.\n\n(And) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Abu Musa narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah.\n\n(And) Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us. They said: al-A'mash narrated from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined between Zuhr and 'Asr, and Maghrib and 'Isha' in Madinah, without fear and without rain. It was said to him: What did he intend by that? He said: He intended not to place hardship upon his Ummah. Waki' said in his hadith: Sa'id said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: Why did the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) do that? He said: So that he would not place hardship upon his Ummah.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others from Abu Mu'awiyah, and from Abu Kurayb and others from Waki'. Al-Bukhari did not narrate it, despite Habib ibn Abi Thabit being from his criteria. Perhaps he refrained from it—and Allah knows best—due to the difference of opinion regarding its text from Sa'id ibn Jubayr. The narration of the group from Abu al-Zubayr is more deserving of being preserved. 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated it from Jabir ibn Zayd Abu al-Sha'tha' from Ibn 'Abbas, close in meaning to the narration of Malik from Abu al-Zubayr. [Its meaning is sound; previously cited under no. 5545.]"
"5551","(5551) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us: Musaddad and Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us. They said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar from Jabir ibn Zayd from Ibn 'Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed in Madinah seven and eight—Zuhr and 'Asr, and Maghrib and 'Isha'.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Rabi'. Al-Bukhari narrated it from Abu al-Nu'man from Hammad ibn Zayd, and he added at its end: Ayyub said: Perhaps it was on a rainy night. He said: Perhaps. It has also been narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar that he interpreted it as delaying Zuhr to the end of its time and hastening 'Asr at the beginning of its time. [Its meaning is sound; al-Bukhari 518.]"
"5552","(5552) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us: Ja'far al-Faryabi narrated to us: Abu Bakr and 'Uthman said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-'Adl narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Mukram al-Birti narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us. He said: I heard Jabir ibn Zayd say: I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) eight combined and seven combined. He said: I said: O Abu al-Sha'tha', I think he delayed Zuhr and hastened 'Asr, and delayed Maghrib and hastened 'Isha'. He said: And I think so too.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madini, and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Its meaning is sound; al-Bukhari 1120.]"
"5553","(5553) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' and Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr—the wording being that of Abu al-Rabi'—said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khiriyt from 'Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, who said: Ibn 'Abbas delivered a sermon to us one day after 'Asr until the sun set and the stars appeared. The people began to say: The prayer! The prayer! A man from Banu Tamim came, ceaselessly saying: The prayer! The prayer! He said: Would you teach me the Sunnah? May you have no mother! Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combine between Zuhr and 'Asr, and Maghrib and 'Isha'. 'Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq said: Something of that troubled my chest, so I went to Abu Hurayrah and asked him, and he confirmed his statement.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani. [Its meaning is sound; Muslim 705.]"
"5554","(5554) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us: Hajib ibn Ahmad narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Hashim narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Mansur narrated to us: Harun ibn Yusuf ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Ibn Abi 'Umar narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us: 'Imran ibn Hudayr narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq al-'Uqayli, who said: A man said to Ibn 'Abbas: The prayer! And he remained silent. Then he said: The prayer! And he remained silent. Then he said: The prayer! And he remained silent. Then he said: May you have no mother! Would you teach us about the prayer? We used to combine between the two prayers during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar. In the narration of 'Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq from Ibn 'Abbas from the Prophet (peace be upon him), from these two established routes, there is neither negation of rain nor negation of travel. Thus, it is interpreted according to one of the two, or according to what 'Amr ibn Dinar interpreted it as. There is nothing in their narration that prevents this interpretation. We have narrated from Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar the combining of prayers during rain, and this strengthens the interpretation of those who interpret it as referring to rain. And Allah knows best. [Its meaning is sound; see what precedes it.]"
"5555","(5555) As for the narration from Ibn 'Abbas regarding it, al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said in the older recension: One of our companions informed us from Usamah ibn Zayd from Mu'adh ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Khubayb that Ibn 'Abbas combined between them during rain before the disappearance of twilight.\n\nAs for the narration from Ibn 'Umar… [Its chain is weak.]"
"5556","(5556) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajan informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Nafi': When the commanders combined between Maghrib and 'Isha', 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar would combine with them on a rainy night.\n\nIt was narrated by al-'Umari from Nafi', and he said: before the disappearance of twilight. [Its meaning is sound; Malik 331.]"
"5557","(5557) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Bundār narrated to us, Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated that his father ʿUrwah, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, and Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām ibn al-Mughīrah al-Makhzūmī used to combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ on a rainy night when they combined the two prayers, and they did not disapprove of that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5558","(5558) And with his chain of narration, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz used to combine Maghrib and the later ʿIshāʾ when it rained, and that Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and the elders of that era used to pray with them and did not disapprove of that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5559","(5559) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Usayd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Saʿīd, from Qatādah, from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \Combining the two prayers without an excuse is among the major sins.\""\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said in Sunan Ḥarmalah: The excuse is constituted by travel and rain. However"," this is not established from ʿUmar; it is mursal.\n\nAl-Shaykh said: It is as al-Shāfiʿī said. The well-known chain of narration for this athar is what we have mentioned"," and it is mursal. Abū al-ʿĀliyah did not hear from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him).\n\nAnd it has been narrated through another chain"," to whose text al-Shāfiʿī alluded in some of his books. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 8253]"""
"5560","(5560) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed him, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ramjārī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Ṣubayḥ, who said: Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl narrated to me, from Abū Qatādah — meaning al-ʿAdawī — that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to one of his governors: \Three things are among the major sins: combining the two prayers except with an excuse"," fleeing from the battlefield"," and highway robbery.\""\n\nAbū Qatādah al-ʿAdawī contemporized ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him); thus"," if he witnessed his writing"," the narration is mawṣūl (connected). Otherwise"," when joined with the first narration"," it becomes strong. A ḥadīth has also been narrated in this regard from the Prophet (peace be upon him) through a chain containing someone who cannot be used as a proof. [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 2035]"""
"5561","(5561) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed him, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā al-Ḥīrī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. They both said: al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, from his father, from Ḥanash, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \Combining the two prayers without an excuse is among the major sins.\"" This is the wording of Nuʿaym's ḥadīth.\n\nAnd in the narration of Yaʿqūb: \""Whoever combines the two prayers without an excuse has come upon one of the gates of the major sins.\""\n\nThis was narrated uniquely by Ḥusayn ibn Qays"," Abū ʿAlī al-Raḥabī"," known as Ḥanash"," and he is weak (ḍaʿīf) according to the scholars of transmission; his report cannot be used as a proof. [Bāṭil. Abū Yaʿlā 2751]"""
"5562","(5562) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Abū Qilābah narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from ʿAmmār, the freedman of Banī Hāshim, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the verse {And by the witness and that which is witnessed} [al-Burūj: 3], he said: \The witness (al-shāhid) is the day of Jumuʿah"," and the witnessed (al-mashhūd) is the day of ʿArafah.\"" [Strong. Narrated by Ibn Jarīr in his Tafsīr 12/520]"""
"5563","(5563) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us by dictation, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me, my father narrated to us, Muḥammad — meaning ibn Jaʿfar — narrated to us, from Shuʿbah, who said: I heard ʿAlī ibn Zayd and Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd narrating from ʿAmmār, the freedman of Banī Hāshim, from Abū Hurayrah.\n\nAs for ʿAlī, he raised it to the Prophet (peace be upon him), and as for Yūnus, he did not go beyond Abū Hurayrah in this verse {And by the witness and that which is witnessed} [al-Burūj: 3]. He said: \The witness (al-shāhid) is the day of ʿArafah and the day of Jumuʿah"," and the witnessed (al-mashhūd) is the promised day — the Day of Resurrection.\"" [Ḥasan for other than it. Aḥmad 2/298]"""
"5564","(5564) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bazzāz in Ṭabarān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah informed me, Ayyūb ibn Khālid narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rāfiʿ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \The promised day (al-yawm al-mawʿūd) is the Day of Resurrection"," the witness (al-shāhid) is the day of Jumuʿah"," and the witnessed (al-mashhūd) is the day of ʿArafah.\"" [For other than it. See what precedes it]"""
"5565","(5565) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Khālid ibn Khalī al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us, Bishr ibn Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated to us, my father Shuʿayb narrated to me, from Abū al-Zinād ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dhakwān al-Madanī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz al-Aʿraj, the freedman of Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith, from what he mentioned that he heard Abū Hurayrah narrating from Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: \We are the last (in the world) and the foremost on the Day of Resurrection"," notwithstanding that they were given the Book before us and we were given it after them. Then this is their day which was made obligatory upon them"," but they differed concerning it"," so Allah guided us to it. The people are followers of us in it: the Jews tomorrow and the Christians the day after tomorrow.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān from Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 236]"""
"5566","(5566) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, Abū al-Zinād narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \We are the last and we are the foremost on the Day of Resurrection"," notwithstanding that every nation was given the Book before us and we were given it after them. Then this is the day which Allah ordained for us; Allah guided us to it. The people are followers of us in it: the Jews tomorrow and the Christians the day after tomorrow.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid from Sufyān with this wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See what precedes it]"""
"5567","(5567) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn Sahl ibn Baḥr narrated to me; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, and Ibn Ṭāwūs from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \We are the last [in the world] and the foremost on the Day of Resurrection.\"" One of them said \""bayda\"" and the other said \""bayda\"" — meaning that they [the previous nations] were given the Book before us"," and we were given it after them. Then this day which Allah prescribed for them"," and they differed concerning it. So Allah guided us to it"," and the people follow us in it: the Jews [follow] tomorrow and the Christians [follow] the day after tomorrow.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar"," by way of reference to what preceded it. In the one before it he said \""ʿalaynā\"" (upon us)"," and in this one he said \""ʿalayhim\"" (upon them)"," as we have narrated"," and he did not distinguish between the two. Perhaps \""ʿalayhim\"" is more correct"," in agreement with Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah and Mālik ibn Anas on that.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its takhrīj.]"""
"5568","(5568) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us; Maḥmūd ibn Hishām narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me; Warqāʾ ibn ʿUmar narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said — and he mentioned it with the same meaning as the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah, except that he said: \So this is their day which was made obligatory upon them.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its takhrīj.]"""
"5569","(5569) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us from Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \We are the last"," the foremost on the Day of Resurrection — bayda that they were given the Book before us and we were given it after them. So this is their day which was prescribed for them"," and they differed concerning it. So Allah guided us to it"," and they follow us in it. So the Jews [follow] tomorrow and the Christians [follow] the day after tomorrow.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its takhrīj.]"""
"5570","(5570) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Daqīqī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Fuḍayl ibn Marzūq narrated to me; al-Walīd ibn Bukayr narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated from ʿAlī ibn Zayd, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) upon his minbar saying: \O people"," repent to Allah"," Mighty and Majestic"," before you die"," and hasten to righteous deeds"," and maintain the bond between you and your Lord through abundant remembrance of Him and abundant charity in secret and in public — you will be rewarded"," praised"," and provided for. And know that Allah"," Mighty and Majestic"," has made Friday obligatory upon you — an obligatory duty prescribed — in my place"," in this month of mine"," in this year of mine"," until the Day of Resurrection"," for whoever finds the means to it. So whoever abandons it during my lifetime or after me"," out of denial of it and belittling it — whether he has a just or unjust imam — may Allah not gather his affairs"," and may He not bless his matter. Beware"," he shall have no prayer; beware"," he shall have no wuḍūʾ; beware"," he shall have no zakāh; beware"," he shall have no Ḥajj; beware"," he shall have no righteousness — until he repents. And if he repents"," Allah will accept his repentance. Beware"," let no woman lead a man [in prayer]; beware"," let no Bedouin lead a Muhājir [in prayer]; and beware"," let no sinful person lead a believer [in prayer] — unless he overpowers him by authority"," fearing his sword and his whip.\""\n\nʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad is al-ʿAdawī — a narrator of rejected ḥadīth"," not followed in his narrations. This was stated by Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī.\n\nAnd the scribe of al-Layth narrated from Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd"," and Abū Yaḥyā al-Waqār from Khālid ibn ʿAbd al-Dāʾim"," from Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd"," from Zuhrah ibn Maʿbad"," from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib"," from Abū Hurayrah"," from the Prophet (ﷺ)"," the meaning of this regarding Friday — and it is also weak.\n\n[Very weak. Ibn Mājah 1081.]"""
"5571","(5571) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī informed us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us; Abū Tawbah narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām narrated from his brother Zayd ibn Salām, that he heard Abū Salām say: al-Ḥakam ibn Mināʾ narrated to me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and Abū Hurayrah narrated to him that they both heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say while he was upon the planks of his minbar: \Let people desist from neglecting the Fridays"," or Allah will seal their hearts"," and then they will be among the heedless.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī from Abū Tawbah al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 865.]"""
"5572","(5572) And it was narrated by Abān from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Zayd ibn Salām, from al-Ḥaḍramī ibn Lāḥiq, from al-Ḥakam ibn Mināʾ, that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn ʿUmar narrating that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said — and he mentioned it with the same meaning, except that he said: \or Allah will seal their hearts.\""\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us"," saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Umayyah narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us; Abān al-Aṭṭār narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr — and he mentioned it.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See what precedes it.]"""
"5573","(5573) And Hishām al-Dastawāʾī contradicted him, narrating it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, that Abū Salām narrated that al-Ḥakam ibn Mināʾ narrated that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated that they heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See what precedes it.]"
"5574","(5574) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hishām narrated it with the same wording as the narration of Abān al-Aṭṭār. And the narration of Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām from his brother Zayd is more deserving of being preserved. And Allah knows best."
"5575","(5575) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Uṣfāṭī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Zuḥayr narrated —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓafar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghurzah narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Zuḥayr narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to a people who were staying away from Friday [prayer]: \I have considered commanding a man to lead the people in prayer"," and then burning the houses of those men who stay away from Friday [prayer].\""\n\nThe word \""buyūtahum\"" (their houses) is not in the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 652.]"""
"5576","(5576) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAlqamah narrated from ʿAbīdah ibn Sufyān al-Ḥaḍramī, from Abū al-Jaʿd al-Ḍamrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever abandons Friday [prayer] three times out of negligence"," Allah will seal his heart.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. Tirmidhī 500.]"""
"5577","(5577) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us. Abū al-Aḥraz Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn Jamīl al-Azdī informed us. Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Naṣr al-Tirmidhī narrated to us in Baghdad. Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr al-Miṣrī, the client of Banī Makhzūm, narrated to us. Al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd al-Qitbānī narrated to me. ʿAyyāsh ibn ʿAbbās narrated from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ashajj, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from Ḥafṣah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \Upon every person who has reached puberty is the obligation of attending Jumuʿah"," and upon whoever comes to Jumuʿah is the obligation of performing ghusl (ritual bath).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 1721]"""
"5578","(5578) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm narrated to us. Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us. Huraym, meaning Ibn Sufyān, narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \Jumuʿah is an obligatory right upon every Muslim in congregation"," except for four: a slave"," a woman"," a child"," or a sick person.\"" Abū Dāwūd said: \""Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb saw the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) but did not hear anything from him.\"" The Shaykh said: \""It was narrated by ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad al-ʿIjlī from ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm"," connecting it by mentioning Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī in it"," but this is not preserved. Others besides ʿAbbās also narrated it from Isḥāq without mentioning Abū Mūsā in it.\"" [Qawī. Abū Dāwūd 1067]"""
"5579","(5579) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbāṣ al-Aṣamm informed us. Al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us. Al-Shāfiʿī informed us. Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me. Salamah ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Khaṭmī narrated from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb that he heard a man from Banī Wāʾil say: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Jumuʿah is obligatory upon every Muslim except a woman"," a child"," or a slave.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī 258]"""
"5580","(5580) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me. ʿAmr, meaning ibn al-Ḥārith, narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar that Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to him from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \The people used to come to Jumuʿah from their homes and from the outskirts (al-ʿAwālī).\"" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 860]"""
"5581","(5581) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Fāris narrated to us. Qabīṣah narrated to us. Sufyān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd, from Abū Salamah ibn Nabīh, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hārūn, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \Jumuʿah is obligatory upon whoever hears the call (al-nidāʾ).\"" Abū Dāwūd said: \""A group narrated this hadith from Sufyān"," stopping it at ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr without mentioning the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). Only Qabīṣah made it marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet).\"" The Shaykh said: \""Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah is among the trustworthy narrators"," and this Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd is al-Ṭāʾifī"," who is trustworthy. It has a corroborating witness from the hadith of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather.\"" [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd 1056]"""
"5582","(5582) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us. ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath narrated to us. Hishām ibn Khālid narrated to us. Al-Walīd narrated from Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \Jumuʿah is only obligatory upon whoever hears the call (al-nidāʾ).\"" Thus al-Dāraquṭnī (may Allah have mercy on him) mentioned it in his book with this chain as marfūʿ. It was also narrated from Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh from ʿAmr"," likewise as marfūʿ. [Munkar. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/6]"""
"5583","(5583) Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us. Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us. Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us. Abū ʿĀmir Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us. Al-Walīd, meaning ibn Muslim, said: Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad informed me from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: \Jumuʿah is only obligatory upon whoever hears the call. So whoever hears it and does not come has disobeyed his Lord.\"" This is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Tārīkh 1/93]"""
"5584","(5584) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālūyah narrated to us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us. Abū Maʿmar narrated to us. Hushaym narrated to us. Shuʿbah informed us from ʿAdī ibn Thābit, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \Whoever hears the call and does not respond"," there is no prayer for him.\"" Qurād"," Abū Nūḥ"," followed him in raising it (as marfūʿ) from Shuʿbah"," and its mention has passed. However"," other trustworthy narrators besides them contradicted them. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Tārīkh 1/93] [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 4940]"""
"5585","(5585) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhabārī informed us in Ṭūs. Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us. Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad. Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us. Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us. Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb both narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdī ibn Thābit, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \Whoever hears the call and does not respond"," there is no prayer for him except due to an excuse.\"" They narrated it as mawqūf (stopped) from Ibn ʿAbbās. It was narrated as marfūʿ (raised) by Maghrāʾ al-ʿAbdī from ʿAdī ibn Thābit. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 12344]"""
"5586","(5586) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad from the original of his book. Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated to us. Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us. Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: (5580) \Whoever hears the call and does not respond"," there is no prayer for him.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 4940]"""
"5587","(5587) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated it to us, and he mentioned it similarly, marfūʿ (elevated to the Prophet). It has also been narrated from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with him, both marfūʿ and mawqūf (stopped at the Companion)."
"5588","(5588) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from Abū Ḥuṣayn, from Abū Burdah, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \Whoever hears the caller (adhān) while being free and healthy and does not respond"," there is no prayer for him.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — see the preceding narration]"""
"5589","(5589) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rabbāḥ al-Bazzāz informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; Misʿar narrated from Abū Ḥuṣayn, from Abū Burdah, from his father, who said: \Whoever hears the caller (adhān) and does not respond"," there is no prayer for him"," except due to an excuse.\"" Mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5590","(5590) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān informed us; Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb informed us; Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah informed us; Abū Ḥuṣayn narrated from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Burdah, from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, who said: \Whoever hears the adhān while being free and healthy and then does not respond"," there is no prayer for him.\"" He (the narrator) said: \""Thus he narrated from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Burdah"," and I do not consider it to be anything other than an error (wahm).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5591","(5591) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Abū Ḥayyān narrated from his father, who said: ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque.\"" It was asked: \""And who is the neighbor of the mosque?\"" He said: \""The one whom the caller (muezzin) can hear.\"" [Previously referenced under no. 4942]"""
"5592","(5592) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: \The Jumuʿah (Friday prayer) is obligatory upon whoever hears the caller (adhān).\"" [Ḍaʿīf — Kitāb al-Um"," 1/330]"""
"5593","(5593) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us, who said: al-Shāfiʿī said: \Saʿīd ibn Zayd and Abū Hurayrah used to be at al-Shajarah"," at a distance of less than six miles"," and they would attend the Jumuʿah and sometimes leave it.\"" He said: \""And it is narrated that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ was at a distance of two miles from al-Ṭāʾif"," and he would attend the Jumuʿah and leave it.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Kitāb al-Um"," 1/330]"""
"5594","(5594) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me; Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Ibn Abī Jaʿfar, from al-Aʿraj, that Abū Hurayrah used to come for the Jumuʿah from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, walking, while it was at a distance of six miles from Madīnah. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than this — Ibn Abī Shaybah, 5086]"
"5595","(5595) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū ʿĀmir informed us; al-Walīd, who is Ibn Muslim, informed me; Sabrah ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated from al-Zuhrī, that the people of Dhū al-Ḥulayfah used to perform the Jumuʿah with the Prophet ﷺ, and that was at a distance of several miles from Madīnah. [Ḍaʿīf — ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 5160]"
"5596","(5596) He said: And al-Walīd narrated to me; al-Awzāʿī narrated from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, who said: \The people of Minā used to attend the Jumuʿah in Makkah.\"" [Ḥasan]"""
"5597","(5597) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī — informed us; Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Aṣbahānī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāliḥ — narrated to us; Fulayḥ, from Thābit ibn Mishḥal, the freedman of Abū Hurayrah, who said: Abū Hurayrah used to be at al-Shajarah, and when Jumuʿah (Friday) arrived, he would not descend from it (his place) to attend it, despite having riding animals at his disposal.\n\nThe Sheikh said: This indicates that attending (the Jumuʿah) was a matter of choice. A group (of scholars) held the view that whoever spends the night returning to his family upon departing (from a journey), then attendance (of Jumuʿah) is obligatory upon him. [Weak. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Tārīkh, 2/167]"
"5598","(5598) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Khiḍr al-Shāfiʿī — narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, from his book, at the last session he held before he died, said: Ibn Mahdī — informed us, from Khālid ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: Ghusl (the ritual bath) is only obligatory upon the one upon whom Jumuʿah is obligatory, and Jumuʿah is obligatory upon the one who is at his home (i.e., resident, not a traveler). [Ḥasan]"
"5599","(5599) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān — narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan — narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir — narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim — narrated to us; Abū ʿAmr, who is al-Awzāʿī, informed me, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, that Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm commanded the people of Dhū al-Ḥulayfah to attend the Jumuʿah in al-Madīnah, and so they used to perform Jumuʿah there. [Ḥasan]"
"5600","(5600) He said: And Abū ʿAmr informed me, from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, something similar to that. Al-Walīd said: I said to Abū ʿAmr, \Upon whom is Jumuʿah obligatory?\"" He said: \""Upon the one who spends the night returning to his family upon departing from it (his journey).\"" ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5601","(5601) Al-Walīd said: And Ismāʿīl informed me, from ʿAmr ibn Muhājir, from his father, that he heard Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān say: \Jumuʿah is obligatory upon the one who returns to his family.\"" And he used to say in his khutbah: \""O people of Qurdā! O people of Rākiyah! O people of the outskirts of al-Ghuṭah! And those near al-Thaniyyah: Jumuʿah! Jumuʿah!\""\n\nAnd it has been narrated in a musnad hadith"," except that it is extremely weak. We have mentioned it so that its chain of narration may be known. [Weak]"""
"5602","(5602) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh — informed us; Abū Saʿīd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb — narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm — narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī — informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī — informed us; Abū Yaʿlā — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr — narrated to us; Muslim, from al-Muʿārik ibn ʿAbbād, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \Whoever knows that the night will bring him to his family"," let him attend the Jumuʿah.\""\n\nAl-Muʿārik ibn ʿAbbād is alone in narrating this from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd. And Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal"," may Allah have mercy on him"," said: \""I do not know al-Muʿārik.\"" And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd is Abū ʿAbbād"," a rejected narrator whose hadith is abandoned. [Munkar]"""
"5603","(5603) Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq — in Baghdād, informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān — said: It was read to Abū Qilābah ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī while I was listening; Rajāʾ ibn Salamah — narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubārak — narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān — narrated to us; Abū Jamrah — narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \The first Jumuʿah that was held after the Jumuʿah that was held in al-Madīnah was the Jumuʿah of al-Baḥrayn"," in Juwāthā"," a village of the villages of ʿAbd al-Qays.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī"," 852]"""
"5604","(5604) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū Aḥmad — Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath — narrated to us; Bundār — narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir — narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān — narrated to us, and he mentioned something similar to it, except that he said: \After a Jumuʿah in the Masjid of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," (it was held) in the Masjid of ʿAbd al-Qays in Juwāthā"," which is in al-Baḥrayn.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā"," from Abū ʿĀmir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5605","(5605) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us by dictation and recitation; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī — narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr — narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl — narrated to me, from his father, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik — narrated to me, who said: \I used to lead my father after his eyesight had been lost. When I would take him out to Jumuʿah and he would hear the adhān for it"," he would seek forgiveness for Abū Umāmah Asʿad ibn Zurārah. I remained for some time hearing this from him"," and I said: 'It is a failing on my part that I do not ask him about this.' So I took him out as I used to"," and when he heard the adhān for Jumuʿah"," he sought forgiveness for him. I said: 'O father"," what do you think of your seeking forgiveness for Asʿad ibn Zurārah every time you hear the adhān for Jumuʿah?' He said: 'O my son"," Asʿad was the first one to lead us in Jumuʿah in al-Madīnah before the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," in a place called Hazm"," in the Ḥarrah of Banī Bayāḍah"," in a depression called al-Khaḍamāt.' I said: 'How many of you were there that day?' He said: 'Forty men.'\"" [Ḥasan. Ibn Mājah"," 1082]"""
"5606","(5606) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah — narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd — narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us; Ibn Idrīs — narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, from his father, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik — and he used to lead his father after his eyesight had been lost — from his father Kaʿb ibn Mālik, that whenever he heard the call on the day of Jumuʿah, he would invoke mercy upon Asʿad ibn Zurārah. So I said to him: \When you hear the call"," you invoke mercy upon Asʿad ibn Zurārah?\"" He said: \""Because he was the first one to lead us in Jumuʿah in Hazm al-Nabiyy"," in the Ḥarrah of Banī Bayāḍah"," in a depression called al-Khaḍimāt.\"" I said: \""How many were you that day?\"" He said: \""Forty.\""\n\nAnd Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim and Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq"," who said: Muḥammad ibn Abī Umāmah narrated to me"," as Yūnus ibn Bukayr said.\n\nAnd when Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq mentions his direct hearing in the narration"," and the narrator is trustworthy"," the chain is sound. And this is a hadith with a ḥasan chain of narration"," ṣaḥīḥ. And another hadith has been narrated concerning it"," but one cannot use it as a proof. [Ḥasan. See the preceding]"""
"5607","(5607) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith the jurist — informed us. Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, known as Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī, informed us. Isḥāq ibn Ḥakīm narrated to us. Isḥāq ibn Khālid al-Bālisī narrated to us. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Qurashī narrated to us. Khuṣayf narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir, who said: \The established practice (al-sunnah) is that for every group of three"," there shall be an imām; and for every forty or more"," there shall be Jumuʿah"," Fiṭr"," and Aḍḥā — because they constitute a congregation (jamāʿah).\"" (T) Similarly"," Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated it from al-Zuhrī. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Qurashī is the sole narrator of this"," and he is weak (ḍaʿīf). Reliance is placed upon what has been established and what will follow"," in shāʾ Allāh Taʿālā. [Weak]"""
"5608","(5608) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us. al-Shāfiʿī informed us. Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to me. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from his father, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, who said: \Every village in which there are forty men"," the Jumuʿah is obligatory upon them.\"" [Very weak. al-Shāfiʿī narrated it"," 259]"""
"5609","(5609) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us. Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us. al-Rabīʿ informed us. al-Shāfiʿī said: \A trustworthy authority informed me from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote to the people of the coastal regions between Shām and Makkah: 'Perform the Jumuʿah when you reach forty (in number).'\"" [Weak. Kitāb al-Um"," 1/328]"""
"5610","(5610) Abū Ḥāzim al-ʿAbdawī informed us. Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Ḥalabī narrated to us. Abū Nuʿaym al-Ḥalabī, that is ʿUbayd ibn Hishām, narrated to us. Abū al-Malīḥ, that is al-Raqqī, said: \A letter from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz reached us: 'When the people of a village reach forty men"," let them perform the Jumuʿah.'\"" [Weak]"""
"5611","(5611) Abū Ḥāzim informed us. Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us. Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us. al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Muʿāwiyah, that is ibn Ṣāliḥ, who said: \ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote: 'Any village in which fifty men gather"," let one of them lead them in prayer"," deliver the sermon for them"," and lead them in the Jumuʿah.'\"" [Sound]"""
"5612","(5612) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith the jurist informed us. Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us. Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us. Abū ʿĀmir Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us. al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, saying: \I asked al-Layth ibn Saʿd"," and he said: 'Every city or village in which there is a congregation and over whom there is an amīr who has been commanded regarding the Jumuʿah — let him lead them in the Jumuʿah. For indeed the people of Alexandria"," the cities of Egypt"," and the cities of its coasts used to perform the Jumuʿah during the era of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān — may Allāh be pleased with both of them — by their command"," and among them were men from the Companions (Ṣaḥābah).'\"" [Good]"""
"5613","(5613) With his chain of narration, al-Walīd narrated to us, saying: \Shaybān informed me. A client of the family of Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated to me that he asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allāh be pleased with him — about the villages between Makkah and Madīnah: 'What do you think regarding the Jumuʿah?' He said: 'Yes"," if they have an amīr"," let him lead them in the Jumuʿah.'\""\n\nAnd it has been narrated from ʿAṭāʾ that he said: \""When villages are contiguous to one another"," they should perform the Jumuʿah.\"" [Weak]"""
"5614","(5614) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ardistānī informed us. Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us. Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us. ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated from Sufyān, from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān, who said: \ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz — may Allāh be pleased with him — wrote to ʿAdī ibn ʿAdī al-Kindī: 'Look to every village whose inhabitants are settled people"," not among those who are nomadic and move from place to place. Appoint an amīr over them"," and then command him to lead them in the Jumuʿah.'\""\n\nThe Shaykh said: \""What is most consistent with the statements of the early predecessors (al-salaf) and their actions regarding the establishment of the Jumuʿah in villages whose inhabitants are settled people and not nomadic is that this is the intent of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allāh be pleased with him — in what…\"" [Sound. Ibn Abī Shaybah"," 5069]"""
"5615","(5615) Abū Ṭāhir the jurist informed us. Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us. Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us. Sufyān narrated from Zubayd, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ʿAlī — may Allāh be pleased with him — said: \There is no Jumuʿah and no Tashrīq except in a major city (miṣr jāmiʿ).\"" [Sound. ʿAbd al-Razzāq"," 5175]"""
"5616","(5616) As for what Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith the jurist informed us of: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Wahb ibn ʿAṭiyyah narrated to us. Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated to us. Muʿāwiyah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. Muʿāwiyah ibn Saʿīd al-Tujībī narrated to us. al-Zuhrī narrated from Umm ʿAbd Allāh al-Dawsiyyah, who said: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The Jumuʿah is obligatory upon every village"," even if there are only four (people) in it.'\"" (He means by \""villages\"" the cities.)\n\nSimilarly"," it has been narrated from al-Muwaqqarī and al-Ḥakam al-Aylī from al-Zuhrī.\n\nAl-Dāraquṭnī said: \""This is not sound from al-Zuhrī. Everyone who narrated it from him is abandoned (matrūk)"," and al-Zuhrī's hearing (samāʿ) from al-Dawsiyyah is not established.\""\n\nImām Aḥmad — may Allāh have mercy upon him — said: \""It has also been reported from al-Tujībī"," from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Aylī"," from al-Zuhrī"," similarly. Muḥammad ibn al-Muṣaffā said it from Baqiyyah.\"" [Baseless (bāṭil). al-Dāraquṭnī"," 2/7]"""
"5617","(5617) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdiyy narrated to us; Ibn Salm narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muṣaffá narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated to us; Muʿāwiya ibn Yaḥyá narrated to us; Muʿāwiya ibn Saʿīd al-Tujībī narrated from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from al-Zuhrī, from Umm ʿAbd Allāh al-Dawsiyyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Friday prayer is obligatory upon every village in which there is an Imam"," even if they are only four.\"" Until the Prophet (ﷺ) mentioned three.\n\nAl-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd Allāh is abandoned (matrūk)"," and Muʿāwiya ibn Yaḥyá is weak (ḍaʿīf)"," and this is not authentically transmitted from al-Zuhrī. A hadith has been narrated in this chapter regarding fifty"," whose chain is not sound. It is also narrated from al-Zuhrī that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr to Madīnah"," he gathered them for Friday prayer and they were twelve men. [Bāṭil (False). See the preceding entry.]"""
"5618","(5618) Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī narrated to us; Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Nufaylī said: I recited to Maʿqil ibn ʿUbayd Allāh from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned it.\n\nThis chain is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). Even if it were sound, what is intended by the assistance of the twelve is the chiefs (nuqabāʾ) whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent in their company or in their trail to Madīnah to recite to the Muslims and lead them in prayer. The number of those who prayed with them among the Muslims is mentioned in the ḥadīth of Kaʿb ibn Mālik, when Muṣʿab established it by the instruction and support of Asʿad ibn Zurārah. [Ḍaʿīf (Weak). Ibn Saʿd in al-Ṭabaqāt 3/118.]"
"5619","(5619) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal al-Māsarjisī narrated to us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Masʿūdī narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gathered us for Friday prayer, and I was the last of those who came to him, and we were forty men. He said: \You shall attain your objectives"," you shall be granted victory"," and conquest shall be opened for you. Whoever lives to see that"," let him fear Allah"," command what is right"," forbid what is wrong"," and maintain the ties of kinship. Whoever lies about me deliberately"," let him take his seat in the Fire.\""\n\nIt was also narrated by al-Thawrī and Misʿar ibn Kidām from Simāk. In the narration of Misʿar"," it states: \""He gathered us"," approximately forty.\"" [Ḍaʿīf (Weak). Abū Yaʿlā 5304.]"""
"5620","(5620) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to me; Abū Isḥāq narrated from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in a tent, approximately forty men, and he said: \Would you be pleased to be a quarter of the people of Paradise?\"" They said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Would you be pleased to be a third of the people of Paradise?\"" They said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""By the One in whose Hand is my soul"," I hope that you will be half of the people of Paradise. That is because no soul shall enter Paradise except a Muslim soul"," and you are no more in polytheism than a white hair on the hide of a black bull"," or a black hair on the hide of a red bull.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar"," from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Bukhārī 6163.]"""
"5621","(5621) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn Ḥasan ibn Muhājir and Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān narrated to us; they both said: Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; Abū Ṣakhr narrated from Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Namir, from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, that a son of his died at Qudayd or ʿUsfān, and he said: \O Kurayb"," see how many people have gathered for him.\"" So I went out and found that people had gathered"," and I informed him. He said: \""Are they forty?\"" I said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""Take him out"," for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'No Muslim man dies and forty men who associate nothing with Allah stand over his funeral"," but Allah will accept their intercession for him.'\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Saʿīd and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Muslim 948.]"""
"5622","(5622) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; Abū al-ʿUmays narrated from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, who said: A man from the Jews came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: \O Commander of the Faithful"," there is a verse in your Book which you recite. Had it been revealed to us"," the Jews"," we would have taken that day as a festival.\"" He said: \""Which verse?\"" He said: {This day I have perfected for you your religion"," completed My favor upon you"," and have chosen for you Islam as your religion} [Al-Māʾidah: 3]. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \""I know the day on which it was revealed and the place in which it was revealed. It was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at ʿArafāt on a Friday.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ"," and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd"," both from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn. We have also narrated from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh that which indicates that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed it as Ẓuhr"," not Jumuʿah"," on that day. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Bukhārī 45.]"""
"5623","(5623) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī and a group whom he mentioned said: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated from his father, from Jābir, and he mentioned the long ḥadīth regarding the Ḥajj, in which is: \Then Bilāl gave the adhān"," then he gave the iqāmah"," and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed Ẓuhr. Then he gave the iqāmah and prayed ʿAṣr"," without praying anything between them.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Bukhārī 894.]"""
"5624","(5624) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from Ḥuṣayn, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to deliver the sermon on Friday standing. A caravan came from al-Shām, and the people turned toward it until only twelve men remained with him. Then this verse was revealed which is in Sūrat al-Jumuʿah: {And when they see a trade or amusement, they disperse toward it and leave you standing} [Al-Jumuʿah: 11].\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. Likewise, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs narrated it from Ḥuṣayn. Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah and Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated it from Ḥuṣayn, and they both mentioned that this occurred while they were in prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Bukhārī 894.]"
"5625","(5625) As for the ḥadīth of Zāʾidah: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Maymūn narrated to us; Muʿāwiya ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; Zāʾidah narrated from Ḥuṣayn, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, and Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, who said: \While we were praying Jumuʿah with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," a caravan carrying food approached.\"" He said: \""They turned and dispersed"," until only twelve men remained with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," and then this verse was revealed: {And when they see a trade or amusement"," they disperse toward it and leave you standing} [Al-Jumuʿah: 11].\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muʿāwiya ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). See the preceding entry.]"""
"5626","(5626) As for the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl: Abū ʿAbd Allāh and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both narrated to us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \A caravan approached while we were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) praying Jumuʿah"," and the people poured toward it"," until only twelve men remained"," and then this verse was revealed: {And when they see a trade or amusement"," they disperse toward it} [Al-Jumuʿah: 11].\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad"," from Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl.\n\nLikewise"," Sulaymān ibn Kathīr narrated it from Ḥuṣayn. Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṭaḥḥān and Hushaym ibn Bashīr narrated it from Ḥuṣayn"," from Abū Sufyān and Sālim"," from Jābir"," without the detailed explanation"," and it has been said that it occurred during the sermon. And Allah knows best.\n\nʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim narrated it from Ḥuṣayn"," but he contradicted the group regarding the number of those who remained with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Bukhārī 894.]"""
"5627","(5627) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Adamī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ḥasanī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim narrated from Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said:\n\nWhile the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the Friday sermon to us, a caravan carrying food arrived until they alighted at al-Baqīʿ. The people turned towards it and dispersed towards it, leaving the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) with no one remaining with him except forty men, among whom I was. He said: Then Allah revealed to the Prophet (ﷺ): {And when they see some merchandise or amusement, they break away to it and leave you standing} [al-Jumuʿah: 11].\n\nʿAlī said: In this chain of narration, no one other than ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim from Ḥuṣayn mentioned \forty men","\"" and the other students of Ḥuṣayn contradicted him"," saying that no one remained with the Prophet (ﷺ) except twelve men.\n\nThe Sheikh said: What is most likely is that the correct narration is from the one who reported that this occurred during the khutbah (sermon)"," and the one who said \""we prayed Jumuʿah with him\"" intended by it the khutbah"," as though he expressed the prayer to mean the sermon. The ḥadīth of Kaʿb ibn ʿUrah also indicates that"," and that will be presented"," if Allah the Most High wills. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this narration. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/4]"""
"5628","(5628) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Tamtām narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Muṣʿab ibn Thābit from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited Sūrat al-Najm and performed prostration with us, and he prolonged the prostration. The people were numerous, so some of them prostrated upon the backs of others. [Munkar]"
"5629","(5629) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Salām, meaning Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb from Sayyār ibn al-Maʿrūr, who said:\n\nI heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābl (may Allah be pleased with him) delivering a sermon and saying: O people, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) built this mosque and we were with him, along with the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār. When the crowding becomes intense, let a man among you prostrate upon the back of his brother. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this narration. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 1556]"
"5630","(5630) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān from al-Aʿmash from al-Musayyab from Zayd ibn Wahb that ʿUmar said:\n\nWhen the heat becomes intense, let a man prostrate upon his garment, and when the crowding becomes intense, let one of you prostrate upon the back of his brother. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5631","(5631) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed him; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik narrated; ʿAṭāʾ narrated from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh:\n\nThat he prayed the Fear Prayer (Ṣalāt al-Khawf) with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he mentioned that the enemy was between him and the qiblah. He (the Prophet ﷺ) said the takbīr and we said the takbīr. He performed rukūʿ and we all performed rukūʿ together. When he raised his head from rukūʿ, he prostrated and the row that was next to him prostrated with him, while the rear row stood facing the enemy. When he stood up and the row next to him stood up, the rear row went down in prostration. Then the rear row moved forward and the front row moved back. He performed rukūʿ and we all performed rukūʿ together. Then he prostrated, and when he raised his head, he prostrated and the row next to him prostrated with him, while the rear row stood facing the enemy. When the row next to him prostrated and sat, the rear row went down in prostration. Then he (the Prophet ﷺ) gave salām and we all gave salām together. Jābir said: It is just as your guards do this with their commanders.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān. As for the standing in rows (istikhlāf), there are already reports and narrations concerning it in the chapters of Imāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5632","(5632) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maʿdān narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq ibn Abī al-ʿAnbas al-Kūfī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Huraym ibn Sufyān narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir from Qays ibn Muslim from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\Jumuʿah is obligatory upon every Muslim"," except upon a slave"," a woman"," a child"," or a sick person.\""\n\nAlthough this ḥadīth contains an irsāl (broken chain)"," it is a good mursal ḥadīth"," for Ṭāriq is among the great Tābiʿūn and among those who saw the Prophet (ﷺ)"," even if he did not hear directly from him. This ḥadīth has corroborating narrations"," among which is what… [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under number 5578]"""
"5633","(5633) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Bayān narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ, meaning al-Naysābūrī, informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris narrated to us; Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī, narrated to me; Ismāʿīl ibn Abān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah narrated from al-Ḥakam, Abī ʿAmr, from Ḍirār ibn ʿAmr from Abī ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāmī from Tamīm al-Dārī from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\Jumuʿah is obligatory"," except upon a woman"," a child"," a sick person"," or a traveler.\""\n\nIn the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān: \""Indeed"," Jumuʿah is obligatory"," except upon a child"," a slave"," or a traveler.\"" [Ḥasan in other than this narration. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 1257]"""
"5634","(5634) Among them is what Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us; al-Baghawī narrated to us; Kāmil ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us; Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Muḥammad al-Anṣārī narrated from Abī al-Zubayr from Jābir that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day"," then upon him is Jumuʿah on the day of Jumuʿah"," except upon a sick person"," a traveler"," a child"," or a slave. And whoever is indifferent to it due to amusement or trade"," Allah is indifferent to him"," and Allah is Self-Sufficient"," Praiseworthy.\""\n\nSaʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated it from Ibn Lahīʿah and added among them: \""or a woman.\"" [Ḥasan in other than this narration. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/3]"""
"5635","(5635) Among them is what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Faṣīl narrated to us; Ḥasan, meaning Ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Ḥayy, narrated to me; my father narrated to me; Abū Ḥāzim narrated from a mawlā of the family of al-Zubayr, raising it to the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said:\n\n\Jumuʿah is obligatory upon every adult (ḥālim)"," except upon four: the child"," the slave"," the woman"," and the sick person.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this narration. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5148]"""
"5636","(5636) Among them is what Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Asīd ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ḥulw ibn al-Sarī narrated from Abī al-Bilād from Ibn ʿUmar, who said:\n\nI heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \Jumuʿah is obligatory"," except upon those whom your right hands possess (slaves) or one who is ill.\"" [Very weak (Ḍaʿīf jiddan)]"""
"5637","(5637) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — al-Asfāṭī narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn ʿUthmān narrated to me — Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAṭiyyah narrated to me from his grandmother, Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: When Rasūlullāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) arrived in al-Madīnah, he gathered the women of the Anṣār in a house and sent ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb to them. He stood at the door and greeted us, and we returned the greeting. He said: \I am the messenger of the Messenger of Rasūlullāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) to you.\"" She said: We said: \""Welcome to the Messenger of Rasūlullāh and to the messenger of the Messenger of Rasūlullāh.\"" He said: \""Pledge allegiance to me that you shall not associate anything with Allah"," nor steal"," nor commit adultery.\"" She said: We said: \""Yes.\"" He then extended his hand from outside the house"," and we extended our hands from inside the house. Then he said: \""O Allah"," bear witness.\"" And we were commanded regarding the two ʿĪds that the menstruated women and the elderly women should attend them"," but that there is no Jumuʿah upon us. And he forbade us from following funeral processions. Ismāʿīl said: I asked my grandmother about His statement {wa lā yaʿṣīnaka fī maʿrūf} [al-Mumtaḥanah: 12]"," and she said: \""He forbade us from wailing (al-niyāḥah).\"" [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 5/85]"""
"5638","(5638) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us — my father narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays — from his father Qays, who said: I heard him saying: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) saw a man who had tied his mount. He said: \What is keeping you back?\"" He said: \""The Jumuʿah.\"" He said: \""The Jumuʿah does not detain a traveler. Go.\"" [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq"," previously cited as no. 5537]"""
"5639","(5639) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Yaʿqūb Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb al-Faqīh in Wāsiṭ narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Saʿd al-Zuhrī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān al-Juʿfī narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to me — ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me — ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ — from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \There is no Jumuʿah upon a traveler.\"" This is the correct version"," it is mawqūf. (T) And ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from his father"," raising it to the Nabī (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam). And it was narrated to us from al-Ḥasan"," who said: We were with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah in Khurāsān — we would shorten the ṣalāh and not perform Jumuʿah. [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"5640","(5640) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us — they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, that is, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī — from Sufyān — from Yūnus — from al-Ḥasan, and he mentioned it. Thus I found it in my book: wa lā nujammuʿ with tashdīd and rafʿ of the nūn."
"5641","(5641) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Faqīh in Bukhārā narrated to us — Qays ibn Unayf narrated to us — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Jarīr narrated from Abū Janāb — from Maghrāʾ al-ʿAbdī — from ʿAdī ibn Thābit — from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr — from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: Rasūlullāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: \Whoever hears the muezzin and is not prevented by an excuse from following him"," then there is no ṣalāh for him.\"" They said: \""And what is the excuse?\"" He said: \""Fear or illness.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as no. 4940]"""
"5642","(5642) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shīrūyah narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Bayān narrated to us — Hushaym narrated — from Shuʿbah — from ʿAdī ibn Thābit — from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr — from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Nabī (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: \Whoever hears the call and does not respond"," then there is no ṣalāh for him"," except due to an excuse.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as no. 4940]"""
"5643","(5643) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us — Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us — Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ — from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that Ibn ʿUmar was called on the day of Jumuʿah while he was applying perfume for Jumuʿah, to Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl, who was dying. He went to him and left the Jumuʿah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5108]"
"5644","(5644) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated to us — al-Layth narrated from Yaḥyā — from Nāfiʿ, that Ibn ʿUmar was told that Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl — who was a Badrī companion — was ill on the day of Jumuʿah. So he went to him after the day had advanced and the Jumuʿah had drawn near, and he left the Jumuʿah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3769]"
"5645","(5645) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Musaddad informed us — Ismāʿīl informed me — ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, the companion of al-Ziyādī, narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith, the paternal uncle of Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, narrated that Ibn ʿAbbās said to his muezzin on a rainy day: \When you say 'Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh","' do not say 'Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh'; rather say"," 'Ṣallū fī buyūtikum' (Pray in your homes).\"" He said: The people seemed to disapprove of that"," so he said: \""One who is better than me has done this. Indeed"," the Jumuʿah is an obligation (ʿazīmah)"," and I disliked that I should bring you out and you should walk through mud and rain.\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad"," and Muslim narrated it from ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr — both from Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 591]"""
"5646","(5646) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, and ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, and ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, the companion of al-Ziyādī — from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith, who said: Ibn ʿAbbās addressed us on a day of deep mud. When the muezzin reached \Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh","\"" he commanded him to announce \""Al-ṣalāt fī al-riḥāl\"" (Prayer in the homes). The people looked at one another"," and he said: \""It is as though you have disapproved of this. One who is better than me has done this"," and indeed it is an obligation (ʿazīmah).\"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad"," and he said: \""on a day of razgh\"" — which is deep al-waḥl (mire) — and likewise al-radgh. And Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5647","(5647) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us, al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, companion of al-Ziyādī, said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith say: The mu'adhdhin of Ibn ʿAbbās called the adhān on a rainy Friday. He said: \Allāhu Akbar"," Allāhu Akbar"," I bear witness that there is no god but Allāh"," I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allāh","\"" and then said: \""Pray in your homes.\"" He said: \""I disliked putting you through hardship"," for one better than me did it"," and I disliked that you walk through slippery and muddy ground.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr. It was also narrated by Maʿmar from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith"," who mentioned that it was a Friday. It was also narrated by Wuhaib from Ayyūb from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 699]"""
"5648","(5648) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Saʿīd — Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — informed us, from Qatādah, from Abū al-Malīḥ, from his father, that he witnessed the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) on a rainy day, and he ordered his caller to announce that the prayer was to be performed in their homes. Saʿīd said: \A companion of ours narrated to us that he heard Abū al-Malīḥ say: 'That was a Friday.'\"" As for Qatādah"," he did not mention Friday in his hadith.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. al-Nasāʾī 854]"""
"5649","(5649) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Naṣr ibn ʿAlī informed us, Sufyān ibn Ḥabīb informed us, from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Malīḥ, from his father, that he witnessed the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) on a Friday during the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, when rain had fallen but the bottoms of their sandals had not yet become wet. The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded them to pray in their homes.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"5650","(5650) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to me, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to me, Abū Isḥāq narrated to me, from Saʿd ibn Iyās, who said: I saw Ḥumayd al-Fazārī narrating from a woman among them, who said: Ibn Masʿūd came to us on a Friday and said: \How do you pray?\"" Then he said: \""If you pray with the imām"," then [your prayer is] according to his prayer"," and if you pray alone"," then you pray four [rakʿahs].\""\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5237]"""
"5651","(5651) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to me, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to me, Abū Isḥāq narrated to me, from Saʿd ibn Iyās, who said: I saw ʿAbd Allāh [ibn Masʿūd] expelling the women from the mosque on a Friday, saying: \Go out"," for this is not for you.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5201]"""
"5652","(5652) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Raffāʾ al-Baghdādī informed us, Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and ʿĪsā ibn Mīnāʾ — and the wording is that of Ismāʿīl — both said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to us that his father said: \Among the jurists whom I encountered"," whose opinions are followed...\"" He then mentioned the seven jurists from among the Tābiʿūn"," along with other eminent scholars of their calibre"," people of jurisprudence and virtue. He said: \""They used to say: If a woman attends the Jumuʿah or any of the Eids"," it suffices for you [to know that].\"" They said: \""Boys"," slaves"," travellers"," and the sick — likewise"," there is no Jumuʿah nor Eid upon them. Whoever among them attends a Jumuʿah or an Eid"," it suffices him.\""\n\n[Ḥasan]"""
"5653","(5653) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr al-Miṣrī narrated to us, al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah informed us, from ʿAyyāsh ibn ʿAbbās, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ashajj, from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), that he said: \Attendance at Jumuʿah is obligatory upon every pubescent person"," and upon whoever attends the Jumuʿah"," a bath [ghusl] is required.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited as 5577]"""
"5654","(5654) Abū Zakariyyāʾ ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, along with others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from his father, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāllāh (may Allāh be pleased with him) saw a man in the appearance of a traveller and heard him say: \Were it not a Friday"," I would have set out.\"" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: \""Set out"," for the Jumuʿah does not prevent one from travelling.\""\n\nIt was also narrated by al-Thawrī from al-Aswad"," and he said in it: \""He saw a man intending to travel on a Friday"," waiting for the Jumuʿah"," and ʿUmar said what he said.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5537]"""
"5655","(5655) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn ʿUbaydah narrated to us, Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān informed us, from his father, from Mughīrah, from al-Ḥārith al-ʿUklī, from Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr al-Bajalī, who said: ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) dispatched an army in which was Muʿādh ibn Jabal. They set out on a Friday. Muʿādh stayed behind until he prayed, and then ʿUmar passed by him and said: \Were you not in this army?\"" He said: \""Yes.\"" He said: \""What is the matter with you?\"" He said: \""I wanted to attend the Jumuʿah and then join them.\"" He said: \""Have you not heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say: 'A morning expedition or an evening expedition in the cause of Allāh is better than the world and all that is in it'?\""\n\nA musnad hadith has been narrated on this subject with a weak chain.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain.]"""
"5656","(5656) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz informed us, Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us, al-Ḥajjāj informed us, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) sent Zayd ibn Ḥārihah, Jaʿfar, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah (may Allāh be pleased with them), and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah lagged behind. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: \What held you back from your companions?\"" He said: \""I wished to attend the Jumuʿah with you and then catch up with them.\"" He said: \""Even if you spent all that is on earth"," you would not attain the reward of their morning departure.\"" They had set out on a Friday.\n\nIt was also narrated by Ḥammād ibn Salamah and Abū Muʿāwiyah from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh"," and al-Ḥajjāj is alone in narrating it. And Allāh knows best.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 1/224]"""
"5657","(5657) Abu Dawud narrated from Qutaybah, from Abu Safwan, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Salih ibn Kathir — who was a companion of Ibn Shahab al-Zuhri — that Ibn Shahab set out for a journey on Friday at the beginning of the day. Salih said: I said to him regarding that, and he said: \Indeed"," the Prophet (ﷺ) set out for a journey on Friday at the beginning of the day.\"" It was reported to us by Muhammad ibn Muhammad; it was reported to us by Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi; it was narrated to us by Abu al-Lu'lu'i; it was narrated to us by Abu Dawud"," and he mentioned it. This chain is munqati' (discontinuous). [Da'if. Abu Dawud in al-Marasil"," 2/66]"""
"5658","(5658) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu Muhammad Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Muzani informed me — I read it to him — 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa informed us; Abu al-Yaman informed us; Shu'aib informed us from al-Zuhri, who said: Salim ibn 'Abd Allah informed me that he heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \Whoever among you comes to the Friday prayer"," let him perform ghusl (ritual bath).\"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter (Jami' Abwab al-Fasl li al-Jumu'ah wa al-A'yan)]"""
"5659","(5659) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi informed us; Abu Hamid ibn al-Sharqi informed us; Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhli informed us; Yahya ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar. And from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) upon this minbar — meaning the minbar of al-Madinah — say: \Whoever among you comes to the Friday prayer"," let him perform ghusl.\"" [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter indicated to it]"""
"5660","(5660) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Zakariyya informed us; Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah informed us; my grandfather informed us; Muhammad ibn Rafi' informed us; Zayd ibn Hubab informed me; 'Uthman ibn Waqid al-'Umari informed me; Nafi' informed me from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Whoever attends the Friday prayer"," men and women"," let him perform ghusl; and whoever does not attend it"," there is no obligation of ghusl upon him"," men and women.\"" [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter indicated to it]"""
"5661","(5661) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki and others said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to me; 'Ali ibn Hamshadh al-'Adl narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi and Muhammad ibn Ghalib ibn Harb narrated to us, saying: 'Abd Allah — meaning Ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi — narrated to us from Malik.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to me; 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhawayh narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, saying: I read to Malik ibn Anas, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \Ghusl on Friday is obligatory upon every one who has reached puberty (muhtalim).\"" In the narration of Yahya: \""Ghusl on Friday (is obligatory).\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from al-Qa'nabi"," and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter (al-Ghusl 'ala man arada al-Jumu'ah)]"""
"5662","(5662) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan and al-Hasan ibn al-Tayyib al-Shuja'i said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghayyath narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us; Ibn Tawus narrated to us from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \We are the last"," yet the preceding. Every nation was given the Book before us"," and we were given it after them. This is the day concerning which they differed"," so Allah guided us to it. Tomorrow is for the Jews"," and the day after tomorrow is for the Christians.\""\n\nThen he fell silent"," then said: \""It is a right upon every Muslim that he perform ghusl once every seven days"," a day in which he washes his head and his body.\""\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musa ibn Isma'il from Wuhaib. [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter (al-Dalalah 'ala anna al-Ghusl yawm al-Jumu'ah)]"""
"5663","(5663) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Tamim ibn Muhammad and al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said: Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Yunus informed me from Ibn Shahab, who said: Salim ibn 'Abd Allah informed me from his father that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was addressing the people on Friday when a man from the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) entered. 'Umar called out to him: \What time is this?\"" He said: \""I was occupied today and did not return to my family until I heard the call to prayer"," so I did no more than perform wudu'.\"" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \""And wudu' as well? You know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command ghusl.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Harmalah ibn Yahya"," and they both reported it from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah from 'Umar"," and they named the one who entered as 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him). It has already been mentioned in the book of Purification. [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter indicated to it]"""
"5664","(5664) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us; 'Amr, the freed slave of al-Muttalib, narrated to us from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that two men from the people of Iraq came to him and asked him about ghusl on Friday: \Is it obligatory?\"" Ibn 'Abbas said to them: \""Whoever performs ghusl"," it is better and purer. And I shall inform you of how ghusl began. The people during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) were in need; they wore wool and carried dates on their backs. The mosque was narrow"," with a low ceiling. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out on Friday on a hot summer day"," and his minbar was short — it was only three steps. He addressed the people"," and the people sweated in their wool garments"," and the odors of sweat and wool arose"," until some of them were nearly harming others"," until their odors reached the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was on the minbar. He said: 'O people"," when this day comes"," perform ghusl"," and let one of you apply what he finds of perfume or oil.'\"" [Hasan. Ahmad"," 1/268]"""
"5665","(5665) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq said: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us; Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah, who said: \The people were laborers for themselves"," and they would come to the Friday prayer in their working condition. It was said to them: 'If only you would perform ghusl.'\""\n\nThey both reported it in al-Sahih from the hadith of Yahya al-Ansari. [Sahih. Its meaning is referenced in the chapter (al-Dalalah 'ala anna al-Ghusl yawm al-Jumu'ah)]"""
"5666","(5666) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhabari informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; 'Imran ibn Musa narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Isa al-Misri narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to me; 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far that Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to him from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), that she said: \The people used to attend the Friday prayer from their homes and from al-'Awali (the outskirts)"," coming in the dust"," with sweat upon them"," and odor would emanate from them. A man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) who had a foul odor"," and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'If only you would purify yourselves for this day of yours.'\"" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari"," 860]"""
"5667","(5667) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Munayiʿī informed me; Aḥmad b. ʿĪsā narrated it with its chain of narration, similar to it, except that he said: \They come amidst the dust"," and dust and sweat befall them.\"" And he said: \""Some of them came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was with me"," and he said…\""\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad b. ʿĪsā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"""
"5668","(5668) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad b. Aḥmad b. Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdād informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad b. Salmān al-Faqīh informed us, saying: It was read to Abū Qilābah ʿAbd al-Malik b. Muḥammad; Bishr b. ʿUmar, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad b. ʿAbd al-Wārith, Abū al-Walīd, and Ḥafṣ b. ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī narrated; they said: Hammām narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; Aḥmad b. Salmān informed us; Jaʿfar b. Muḥammad b. Shākir al-Ṣāʾigh narrated; ʿAffān b. Muslim narrated; Hammām narrated from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever performs wuḍūʾ on Friday"," it is sufficient and excellent"," and whoever performs ghusl"," then ghusl is superior.\""\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. Its extraction is referenced in the chapter indicating the virtue of ghusl on Friday.]"""
"5669","(5669) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr b. al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated; Abū Bakr Muḥammad b. Isḥāq al-Shaghānī narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī in Baghdād narrated; Muḥammad b. ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated; Isḥāq b. al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated; they both said: Surayj b. al-Nuʿmān narrated; Fulayḥ narrated from ʿUthmān b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that Anas b. Mālik informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray Jumuʿah when the sun declined.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Surayj b. al-Nuʿmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 762.]"
"5670","(5670) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr b. Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl b. Qutaybah narrated; Yaḥyā b. Yaḥyā narrated; Wakīʿ narrated from Yaʿlā b. al-Ḥārith.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ b. Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā b. Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated; Aḥmad b. Salamah narrated; Isḥāq b. Ibrāhīm and Hannād b. al-Sarī narrated. Isḥāq said: \informed us","\"" and Hannād said: \""narrated.\"" Wakīʿ narrated; Yaʿlā b. al-Ḥārith narrated from Iyās b. Salamah b. al-Akwaʿ"," from his father"," who said: We would pray Jumuʿah with the Prophet ﷺ when the sun had declined"," then we would return seeking the shade.\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā b. Yaḥyā and Isḥāq b. Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 860.]"""
"5671","(5671) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad b. Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq b. Abī al-Fawāris al-Ṣaydalānī all informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated; al-Rabīʿ b. Sulaymān narrated; ʿAbd Allāh b. Wahb narrated; Sulaymān b. Bilāl narrated; Jaʿfar narrated to me, from his father, that he asked Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh: \When would the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray Jumuʿah?\"" Jābir said: \""He would pray"," then we would go to our camels to give them rest\"" — meaning the water-drawing camels.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 858.]"""
"5672","(5672) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated; Muḥammad b. al-Ḍir b. Salamah b. al-Jārūd informed us; ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dārimī narrated; Yaḥyā b. Ḥassān narrated; Sulaymān b. Bilāl narrated; Jaʿfar b. Muḥammad narrated the hadith with a similar chain, and added in it: \when the sun declines.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning"," like the preceding one.]"""
"5673","(5673) Muḥammad b. ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated; al-Ḥasan b. Sufyān narrated; Abū Bakr b. Abī Shaybah narrated; Yaḥyā b. Ādam narrated; Ḥasan b. ʿAyyāsh narrated; Jaʿfar b. Muḥammad narrated from his father, from Jābir, who said: We would pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return and give our water-drawing camels rest. Ḥasan said: I said to Jaʿfar b. Muḥammad: \At what time was that?\"" He said: \""The decline of the sun.\""\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr b. Abī Shaybah. And this statement is attributed to ʿUmar"," ʿAlī"," Muʿādh b. Jabal"," al-Nuʿmān b. Bashīr"," and ʿAmr b. Ḥurayth — meaning regarding the time of Jumuʿah: when the sun declines. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its meaning is in the preceding hadith.]"""
"5674","(5674) Abū Zakariyyā b. Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad b. Isḥāq informed us; Abū Khalīfah al-Faḍl b. al-Ḥubāb narrated; Abū al-Walīd narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī b. Aḥmad b. ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad b. ʿUbayd narrated; al-Asfaṭī narrated; Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated; Yaʿlā b. al-Ḥārith al-Muḥāribī narrated; Iyās b. Salamah b. al-Akwaʿ narrated from his father, who said: We would pray Jumuʿah with the Prophet ﷺ, and there would be no shade from the walls for us to seek shelter in.\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq b. Ibrāhīm from Abū al-Walīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 860.]"
"5675","(5675) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh b. Yaḥyā b. ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl b. Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated; ʿAbbās b. ʿAbd Allāh al-Tarqafī narrated; Yaḥyā b. Yaʿlā narrated; my father narrated; Iyās b. Salamah b. al-Akwaʿ narrated from his father — and his father was among the Companions of the Tree — who said: We would pray Jumuʿah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then depart, and there would be no shade from the walls to seek shelter in. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5676","(5676) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh b. Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd b. al-Aʿrābī narrated; al-Ḥasan b. Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated; Abū Muʿāwiyah al-Ḍarīr narrated; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from—\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr b. Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh b. Jaʿfar narrated; Yūnus b. Ḥabīb narrated; Abū Dāwūd narrated; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated; Muslim b. Jundub narrated from al-Zubayr b. al-ʿAwwām, who said: We would pray Jumuʿah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then hasten to the shade, and there would be no more than the space of a foot or two. In the narration of Abū Muʿāwiyah: \Then we would return and find no shade on the ground except the space of our feet.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 1840.]"""
"5677","(5677) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Munayiʿī narrated to me; Hārūn ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us; Ḥarmiyyah ibn ʿUmarah ibn Abī Ḥafṣah narrated to me; Abū Khaldah narrated to me. He said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik, and Yazīd al-Ḍabbī called out to him on a Friday, \O Abū Ḥamzah"," you witnessed the prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and you have witnessed the prayer with us"," so how did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray the Jumuʿah?\"" He said: \""When the cold was severe"," he would hasten the prayer"," and when the heat was severe"," he would delay the prayer.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 864]"""
"5678","(5678) And Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb narrated to us in Bukhārā; Abū Isḥāq Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; al-Muqaddamī narrated to us.\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Ḥarmiyyah ibn ʿUmarah narrated to us; Abū Khaldah narrated to us. He said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when the cold was severe"," would hasten the prayer"," and when the heat was severe"," would delay the prayer\"" — meaning the Jumuʿah.\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī. al-Bukhārī said: And Yūnus ibn Bukayr said: Abū Khaldah informed us"," and he said \""the prayer\"" and did not mention \""the Jumuʿah.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5679","(5679) Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb narrated to us; Abū Isḥāq Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Yaʿīsh narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Khālid ibn Dīnār Abū Khaldah informed us. He said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik, while he was sitting with al-Ḥakam, the governor of Baṣrah, on the couch, saying: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when it was hot"," would delay the prayer"," and when it was cold"," would hasten the prayer.\""\nAnd it was narrated by Bishr ibn Thābit al-Bazzār"," who said: Abū Khaldah Khālid ibn Dīnār narrated to us from Anas ibn Mālik: \""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," when it was winter"," would hasten the Ẓuhr"," and when it was summer"," would delay it"," and he would pray ʿAṣr while the sun was bright and clear.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the meaning]"""
"5680","(5680) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Farāʾiḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Kuzburānī narrated to us; Bishr ibn Thābit al-Bazzār narrated it, and al-Bukhārī had alluded to it.\n[The preceding ḥadīth is also narrated through this chain.]"
"5681","(5681) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from al-Zuhrī, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd: \The first call on the day of Jumuʿah was when the imām came out"," in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ"," and in the time of Abū Bakr"," and in the time of ʿUmar — when the imām came out"," and when the prayer was established. Then in the time of ʿUthmān"," the people increased in number"," so he added the third call at al-Zawrāʾ"," and it has remained so until the present.\""\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 873]"""
"5682","(5682) Abū ʿAmr al-Razjāhī informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me; Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us; Ṣāliḥ ibn Mālik narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to me; al-Zuhrī narrated to us.\n(Ḥ) And Ibn Nājiyah said: Muḥammad ibn Bazīʿ narrated to us; Bishr ibn Mufaḍḍal narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Mājishūn narrated from al-Zuhrī, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, who said: \It was only ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān who ordered the third adhān on the day of Jumuʿah"," when the people of Madīnah increased in number. The adhān on the day of Jumuʿah was only when the imām sat on the minbar.\""\nAbū Bakr said: This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Munayiʿī. And Ibn Nājiyah said: \""It was only ʿUthmān who ordered the third call on the day of Jumuʿah"," and the call was only when the imām sat"," and the Prophet ﷺ had only one mu'adhdhin.\""\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 874]"""
"5683","(5683) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd: \The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever performs ghusl on the day of Jumuʿah"," uses the siwāk"," wears his best garments"," and applies perfume if he has it"," then comes and does not step over the people"," and prays as much as Allah wills him to pray"," and when the imām comes out"," remains silent — that is an expiation until the next Jumuʿah.'\""\n[Ḥasan. Ibn Khuzaymah 1762]"""
"5684","(5684) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and others informed us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik: \He informed them that in the time of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh"," may Allah be pleased with him"," they would pray on the day of Jumuʿah until ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came out. When he came out and sat on the minbar and the mu'adhdhins gave the adhān"," they would sit and converse. When the mu'adhdhins fell silent and ʿUmar stood"," they would fall silent"," and no one would speak.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 233]"""
"5685","(5685) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānjānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: \When the mu'adhdhin fell silent.\"" And he added from Ibn Shihāb that he said: \""The emergence of the imām cuts off the prayer"," and his speech cuts off speech.\""\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"""
"5686","(5686) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; al-Shāfiʿī narrated to me; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to me from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb from Ibn Shihāb. He said: Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik narrated to me: \The sitting of the imām cuts off supererogatory prayer"," and his speech cuts off speech. They used to converse on the day of Jumuʿah while ʿUmar was sitting on the minbar. When the mu'adhdhin fell silent"," ʿUmar would stand"," and no one would speak until he had delivered both khutbahs. When the prayer was established and ʿUmar descended"," they would speak.\""\n[Jayyid. al-Shāfiʿī narrated it 271]"""
"5687","(5687) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sukkari narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sahm narrated to us — Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī narrated to us — Maʿmar narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr — from Ḍamḍam ibn Jaws — from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \The emergence of the Imām on Friday for the prayer — meaning it interrupts the prayer — and his speech interrupts speech.\""\n\nAnd this is a grievous error. It was only narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib as his own statement"," not marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet). And it was narrated by Ibn Abī Dhiʾb and Yūnus from al-Zuhrī from Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik. And Mālik narrated from al-Zuhrī"," distinguishing the words of al-Zuhrī from the words of Thaʿlabah"," as we have mentioned"," and this is the preserved version according to Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī.\n\n[Weak]"""
"5688","(5688) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us — Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us — Ḥassān ibn Ibrāhīm al-Kirmānī narrated to us — from Layth — from Mujāhid — from Abū al-Khalīl — from Abū Qatādah, that the Prophet ﷺ forbade praying at midday except on Friday, because Jahannam is heated every day except Friday.\n\n[Weak. Abū Dāwūd 1083]"
"5689","(5689) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Bazzāz narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr — from Jābir, who said: A man entered while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon and said: \Have you prayed?\"" He said: \""No.\"" He ﷺ said: \""Pray two rakʿahs.\""\n\nHe said: And Sufyān narrated to us from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir"," and he was Sulayk al-Ghaṭafānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 888]"""
"5690","(5690) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Shādhān narrated to us — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Sufyān — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shīrūyah narrated us — Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh saying: A man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the sermon on Friday, and he said: \O so-and-so"," have you prayed?\"" He said: \""No.\"" He ﷺ said: \""Pray two rakʿahs.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī al-Madīnī from Sufyān"," and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah and Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding]"""
"5691","(5691) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Makhlad ibn Jaʿfar al-Daqqāq narrated to us — Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — al-Layth from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, that he said: Sulayk al-Ghaṭafānī came on Friday while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated on the minbar. Sulayk sat down before praying, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \Have you prayed two rakʿahs?\"" He said: \""No.\"" He ﷺ said: \""Stand and pray them.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 875]"""
"5692","(5692) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah from al-Aʿmash — (ḥ) and Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us — my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us — al-Aʿmash from Abū Sufyān from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: Sulayk al-Ghaṭafānī came while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the sermon on Friday, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Have you prayed two rakʿahs?\"" He said: \""No.\"" He ﷺ said: \""Stand and pray two rakʿahs and be brief in them.\"" And he ﷺ said: \""When one of you comes and the Imām is delivering the sermon"," let him pray two rakʿahs and be brief in them.\"" These are the words of the ḥadīth of ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 113]"""
"5693","(5693) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfārāyinī informed us — Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Barbahārī narrated to us — Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us — al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us — Sufyān narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, that he heard ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh saying: I saw Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī enter the mosque on Friday while Marwān was delivering the sermon, and he stood and prayed two rakʿahs. The guards came to make him sit, but he refused until he had prayed the two rakʿahs. When we finished, we came to him and said: \O Abū Saʿīd"," those people nearly attacked you!\"" Abū Saʿīd said: \""I would not leave them for anything after something I saw from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I saw a man enter the mosque in a dishevelled state while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon on Friday"," and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Have you prayed"," O so-and-so?' He said: 'No.' He ﷺ said: 'Pray two rakʿahs.' Then that man entered on the second Friday while the Prophet ﷺ was standing delivering the sermon"," and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'O so-and-so"," have you prayed?' He said: 'No.' He ﷺ said: 'Pray two rakʿahs.'\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Tirmidhī 511]"""
"5694","(5694) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us — Abū Qilābah narrated to us — Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Hind narrated to us — from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr — from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym — from Abū Qatādah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \When one of you enters the mosque"," let him not sit until he has prayed two rakʿahs.\"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see 5692 above]"""
"5695","(5695) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated to us — from Zāʾidah, who said: ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā al-Anṣārī narrated to me — Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān narrated to me — from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym ibn Khaldah al-Anṣārī — from Abū Qatādah, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: I entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting among the people. He said: I sat down, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \What prevented you from praying two rakʿahs before sitting?\"" He said: I said: \""O Messenger of Allah"," I saw you sitting and the people were sitting.\"" He ﷺ said: \""When one of you enters the mosque"," let him not sit until he has prayed two rakʿahs.\"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 714]"""
"5696","(5696) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Muʾadhdhin informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb al-Bukhārī informed us — Abū Ismāʿīl Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Tirmidhī narrated to us — Ayyūb ibn Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Uways narrated to me — from Sulaymān, meaning Ibn Bilāl, who said: Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Saʿīd, informed me — Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Anas ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī informed me — that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh saying: The mosque in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was roofed with palm trunks, and when the Prophet ﷺ delivered the sermon, he would stand by a trunk. When the minbar was made, he used to sit upon it, and we heard from that trunk a sound like the groaning of she-camels, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and placed his hand upon it, and it became still. al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways from his brother Abū Bakr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 3392]"
"5697","(5697) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, they both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim informed us, from his father, that a group of people came to Sahl ibn Saʿd, disputing about the minbar — from what type of wood it was. He said: \By Allah"," I know from what wood it was made"," and who crafted it"," and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the first day he sat upon it.\"" He said: I said to him: \""O Abū ʿAbbās"," then narrate to us.\"" He said: \""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent a message to a woman\"" — Abū Ḥāzim said: \""He even named her at that time\"" — saying: \""Tell your carpenter slave to prepare for me pieces of wood upon which I may address the people.\"" So he made these three steps. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered it to be placed"," and it was set in this position. It was made from tamarisk wood of al-Ghābah. And I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stand upon it and say the takbīr"," and the people behind him said the takbīr while he was upon the minbar — meaning"," then he performed rukūʿ"," then he raised his head"," then descended backwards until he prostrated at the base of the minbar"," then he returned until he completed the end of his prayer. Then he turned to the people and said: \""O people"," I have done this only so that you may follow my example and learn my prayer.\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Authentic. See no. 5229]"""
"5698","(5698) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Faḍl ibn al-Samḥ narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad al-Harawī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ayman narrated to us: My father narrated to me, from Jābir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to stand on Fridays by a tree or a palm trunk. A woman from the Anṣār — or a man — said: \O Messenger of Allah"," shall we not make for you a minbar?\"" He said: \""If you wish"," then make it.\"" So they made a minbar for him. When Friday came"," he went to the minbar"," and the palm trunk cried out like the crying of a child. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) descended and embraced it; it moaned like the moaning of a child that is being comforted. He said: \""It was weeping over what it used to hear of the remembrance of Allah (dhikr) near it.\"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym. [Authentic. Bukhārī 3391]"""
"5699","(5699) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Jahm Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qurashī narrated to us: Shuʿayb ibn ʿAmr al-Ḍubaʿī narrated to us: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us: Ibn Abī Rawwād narrated to me: Nāfiʿ narrated to me, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, that Tamīm al-Dārī said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) when he had grown old and become heavy: \Shall I not make for you a minbar upon which you may sit — or gather — or a word similar to these two — to support your bones?\"" So he had made for him two or three steps"," and he sat upon them. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ascended"," and a palm trunk that was in the mosque — which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to lean against when delivering the sermon — moaned. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) descended and embraced it"," and said something to him — I do not know what it was. Then he ascended the minbar. At that time"," the pillars of the mosque were trunks and its roof was made of palm fronds. al-Bukhārī said: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Ibn Abī Rawwād"," and he mentioned it. [Strong. Abū Dāwūd 1081]"""
"5700","(5700) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Muʿādh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to deliver the sermon leaning against a palm trunk. When the minbar was made, the palm trunk moaned, so he came to it and embraced it (peace and blessings be upon him). al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ, and he said: \And ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd said: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us.\"" [Authentic. Bukhārī 3390]"""
"5701","(5701) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr and Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, they said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar informed us, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to deliver two sermons on Friday, with a sitting between them. [Authentic. Ibn Mājah 1103]"
"5702","(5702) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḍabbī narrated to us: al-Qāsim — who is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mahdī, Abū al-Ṭāhir — in Egypt, narrated to us: My uncle, meaning Muḥammad ibn Mahdī, narrated to us: Yazīd, meaning Ibn Yūnus ibn Yazīd al-Aylī, narrated to us, from his father Yūnus, from al-Zuhrī, who said: \It has reached us that the first Jumuʿah prayer was established in Madīnah before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived there"," and Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmir led the Muslims in Jumuʿah. And it has reached us that there is no Jumuʿah without a sermon; whoever does not deliver a sermon should pray four rakʿahs.\"" [Weak]"""
"5703","(5703) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Saʿīd informed us, from Abū Maʿshar, from Ibrāhīm, who said: \If the imām does not deliver a sermon on Friday"," he should pray four rakʿahs.\"" And something similar has been narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ and others"," and from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr"," who said: \""Jumuʿah was originally four rakʿahs"," and the sermon was made in place of the two rakʿahs.\"" [Weak. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5335]"""
"5704","(5704) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from Abū ʿUbaydah, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah: He entered the mosque while ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥakam was delivering the sermon sitting down. He said: \Look at this wicked man delivering the sermon while sitting"," when Allah"," the Mighty and Majestic"," has said: {And when they see some merchandise or amusement"," they rush toward it and leave you standing} [al-Jumuʿah: 11].\"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and others"," except that he said: \""ʿAbd al-Rāḥmān"," the son of Umm al-Ḥakam.\"" [Authentic. Muslim 864]"""
"5705","(5705) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Ḥuṣayn, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to deliver the Friday sermon standing. A caravan arrived from al-Shām, and the people rushed toward it until only twelve men remained with him. Then this verse from Sūrat al-Jumuʿah was revealed: {And when they see some merchandise or amusement, they rush toward it and leave you standing} [al-Jumuʿah: 11]. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm."
"5706","(5706) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū Khaythamah narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Simāk, who said: Jābir ibn Samurah informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to deliver the sermon standing, then sit, then stand and deliver the sermon standing. So whoever informs you that he used to deliver the sermon sitting has lied. By Allah, I prayed with him more than two thousand prayers. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Good. Muslim 862]"
"5707","(5707) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbīyy informed us; Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us; al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us; Shuʿbah informed us from Ḥuṣayn, who said: I heard al-Shaʿbī say: The first one to introduce the practice of sitting upon the pulpit (minbar) was Muʿāwiyah.\n\nThe Shaykh Aḥmad said: It is possible that he did so due to weakness from old age or illness. And Allāh knows best.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn ʿAsākir in Tārīkh Dimashq 59/202]"
"5708","(5708) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād both said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawārīrīyy narrated to us; Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us. And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿImrān ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Abū Kāmil narrated to us; Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to deliver the Friday sermon standing, then he would sit, then he would stand and deliver the sermon, just as they do today.\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh al-Qawārīrīyy, and Muslim also narrated it from Abū Kāmil.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 886]"
"5709","(5709) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū al-Qāsim Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Mūsāʾīyy in Makkah narrated to us; Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Ḥanẓalīyy narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Farwīyy narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who informed him: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to deliver two sermons on Friday, sitting between them, and he would deliver both of them while standing.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than his own narration. Al-Shāfiʿī cited it 279]"
"5710","(5710) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Aṣbahānīyy narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisīyy narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Hilāl ibn Abī Maymūnah from ʿAṭāʾ from Abū Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) sat upon the pulpit and we sat around him, and he said: \Indeed"," I fear for you"," after me"," that which will be opened unto you of the splendour and adornment of the worldly life.\"" A man said: \""Does good come with evil?\"" He (ﷺ) fell silent. It was said to the man: \""What is the matter with you? You speak to the Prophet (ﷺ) and he does not speak to you","\"" and we saw that revelation was descending upon him. He (ﷺ) then recovered and began wiping the sweat"," and said: \""Where is the questioner?\"" — as though praising him. He (ﷺ) said: \""Good does not come with evil. Indeed"," that which the spring produces may kill or cause illness"," except the eater of green vegetation (ākilat al-khaḍir)"," for it eats until its flanks are full"," then it faces the sun"," urinates"," defecates"," and grazes again. Indeed"," this wealth is green and sweet"," and how excellent is the wealth of a Muslim — that from which he gives to the orphan"," the destitute"," and the wayfarer\"" — or as the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: \""And indeed"," whoever takes it without right is like one who eats but is never satisfied"," and it will be a witness against him on the Day of Resurrection.\""\n\nBoth al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hishām al-Dastawāʾīyy.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6063]"""
"5711","(5711) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq — originally from Kūfah, in Fusṭāṭ — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlīyy ibn Ghurāb narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Abān ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bajalīyy from ʿAdīyy ibn Thābit from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) ascended the pulpit — or he said: sat upon the pulpit — we would turn our faces toward him.\n[Ḍaʿīf]"
"5712","(5712) He said: And Abū Bakr ibn Khuzaymah informed us, saying: This report is, in my view, defective (maʿlūl). ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd al-Ashajj narrated to us; al-Naḍr ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us from Abān ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bajalīyy, who said: I saw ʿAdīyy ibn Thābit turning his face toward the imām when he stood to deliver the sermon. I said to him: \I saw you turning your face toward the imām.\"" He said: \""I saw the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) doing so.\""\n\nThe Shaykh said: And similarly"," Ibn al-Mubārak narrated it from Abān ibn ʿAbd Allāh from ʿAdīyy ibn Thābit"," except that he said: \""Thus the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to do with the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).\"" Abū Dāwūd mentioned it in al-Marāsīl from Abū Tawbah from Ibn al-Mubārak.\n[Ḥasan in other than his own narration]"""
"5713","(5713) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūh al-Faqīh al-Mihrajānīyy narrated to us there; Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾīnīyy narrated to us; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad al-Kātib narrated to us; Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī, who said: When the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) began his sermon, they would turn their faces toward him until he finished it.\n[Ḍaʿīf]"
"5714","(5714) He said: And Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Juwayriyyah said: I saw Anas ibn Mālik, the servant of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), when the imām began the sermon on Friday, turning his face toward him until the imām finished his sermon.\n[Ḍaʿīf]"
"5715","(5715) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānīyy narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to me; Ismāʿīl and others narrated to me from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārīyy, who said: The Sunnah is that when the imām sits upon the pulpit on Friday, the people should all turn their faces toward him.\n[Ḍaʿīf]"
"5716","(5716) And with his chain of narration, al-Walīd narrated to us, saying: I mentioned that to Layth ibn Saʿd, and he informed me from Ibn ʿAjlān, who informed him from Nāfiʿ: that Ibn ʿUmar used to finish his supererogatory prayers on Friday before the imām emerged, and when the imām came out, he would turn his face toward him before the imām sat down.\n[Ḥasan]"
"5717","(5717) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān informed us: Shuʿayb reported from al-Zuhrī, who said: Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik al-Quraẓī — who had met ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) — informed me, saying: We used to converse when ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) would sit upon the minbar until the mu'adhdhin completed his adhān, and ʿUmar would speak. When ʿUmar spoke, our conversation would cease, and we would fall silent. None of us would speak until the Imam completed his khutbah. [Ṣaḥīh. Previously referenced as 5686]"
"5718","(5718) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad informed us: Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd narrated from Zubayd al-Ayamī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, who said: ʿUmar (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: The prayer of al-Aḍḥā is two rakʿahs, the prayer of al-Fiṭr is two rakʿahs, the prayer of al-Jumuʿah is two rakʿahs, and the prayer of the traveller is two rakʿahs — complete, not shortened.\n\nAl-Thawrī narrated it from Zubayd but did not mention Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah in his isnād, except that he raised it (marfūʿ) at its end. [Ṣaḥīh. Previously referenced in al-Nasāʾī as 1440]"
"5719","(5719) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbbād informed us: Abū Nuʿaym informed us: Sufyān narrated from Zubayd, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from ʿUmar, who said: The prayer of al-Jumuʿah is two rakʿahs, the prayer of al-Aḍḥā is two rakʿahs, and the prayer of travel is two rakʿahs — complete, not shortened — upon the tongue of your Prophet (ﷺ).\n\nYaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān narrated it from Sufyān, from Zubayd, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Thiqah, from ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"5720","(5720) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd al-Thaqafī narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam appointed Abū Hurayrah as his deputy. So Abū Hurayrah led them in the Jumuʿah prayer, reciting Sūrat al-Jumuʿah in the first rakʿah and in the second rakʿah: {Idhā jāʾaka al-munāfiqūn} [al-Munāfiqūn: 1]. ʿUbaydullāh said: When Abū Hurayrah finished, I walked alongside him and said: You have recited two sūrahs that I heard ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib recite in the prayer. Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite them. [Ṣaḥīh. Muslim 1877]"
"5721","(5721) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Jaʿfar, from his father, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, who said: Marwān appointed Abū Hurayrah as his deputy over al-Madīnah and departed for Makkah. Abū Hurayrah led us in the Jumuʿah prayer, reciting Sūrat al-Jumuʿah in the first sajdah (rakʿah), and in the last (second rakʿah): {Idhā jāʾaka al-munāfiqūn} [al-Munāfiqūn: 1]. ʿUbaydullāh said: I caught up with Abū Hurayrah when he had finished and said: You have recited two sūrahs that ʿAlī (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) used to recite in al-Kūfah. Abū Hurayrah said: Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite them.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. He also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Bilāl and ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad. In the ḥadīth of Sulaymān, at the end of the ḥadīth, it states: \He would recite them in the Jumuʿah.\"" [Ṣaḥīh. Previously referenced in the one before it]"""
"5722","(5722) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Makhūl, from Muslim, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to recite in the Jumuʿah prayer Sūrat al-Jumuʿah and Sūrat al-Munāfiqīn, and he used to recite in the morning prayer on Friday: {Alif-Lām-Mīm Tanzīl} [al-Sajdah: 1-2] and {Hal atā}. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīh. Muslim 879]"
"5723","(5723) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us. He said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Mālik ibn Anas informed you from Ḍamrah ibn Saʿīd al-Māzinī, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUtbah, that al-Ḍaḥḥāq ibn Qays asked al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr: What did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite on Friday after Sūrat al-Jumuʿah? He said: He used to recite {Hal atāka ḥadīth al-ghāshiyah}. [Ṣaḥīh. Muslim 878]"
"5724","(5724) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sahl ibn Baḥr narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ḍamrah ibn Saʿīd, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: Al-Ḍaḥḥāq ibn Qays wrote to al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr asking him: What did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recite on Friday besides Sūrat al-Jumuʿah? He said: He used to recite {Hal atāka}. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīh. See the one before it]"
"5725","(5725) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Jarīr informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from Ḥabīb ibn Sālim, the mawlā of al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to recite on Friday in the Jumuʿah prayer: {Sabbih isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā} and {Hal atāka ḥadīth al-ghāshiyah}. And if Jumuʿah and ʿĪd fell on the same day, he would recite both of them in both the Jumuʿah and the ʿĪd prayers.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīh. Meaning as in what preceded]"
"5726","(5726) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated from Miʿsar, from Maʿbad ibn Khālid, from Zayd ibn ʿUqbah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to recite in the Jumuʿah prayer: {Sabbih isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā} and {Hal atāka ḥadīth al-ghāshiyah}.\n\nAl-Masʿūdī narrated it from Maʿbad regarding the two ʿĪds. [Ṣaḥīh. al-Nasāʾī 1422]"
"5727","(5727) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, who narrated to me: Mikhwāl narrated to me, from Muslim al-Baṭīn, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday {Alif, Lām, Mīm, Tanzīl} [Sūrat al-Sajdah] and {Hal atā ʿalā al-insān} [Sūrat al-Insān, 1], and on Friday he used to recite Sūrat al-Jumuʿah and Sūrat al-Munāfiqīn.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Sufyān al-Thawrī. [Authenticated. Previously referenced under no. 5765.]"
"5728","(5728) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bazzāz narrated to us in Ṭabarān, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, saying: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, saying: al-Thawrī narrated to us, saying: I heard Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday {Tanzīl} [Sūrat al-Sajdah] and {Hal atā ʿalā al-insān} [Sūrat al-Insān].\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī. [Authentic. Muslim 880.]"
"5729","(5729) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sharqī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah, from Abū Wāʾil, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the morning prayer on Friday {Alif, Lām, Mīm, Tanzīl} [Sūrat al-Sajdah] and {Hal atā ʿalā al-insān} [Sūrat al-Insān]."
"5730","(5730) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk and Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Abū Qalābah ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Raqāshī narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, saying: Saʿīd ibn Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated to me — and I do not know him except from Jābir ibn Samurah — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Maghrib prayer on the night of Friday {Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn} [Sūrat al-Kāfirūn] and {Qul huwa Allāhu aḥad} [Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ], and he used to recite in the ʿIshāʾ prayer on the night of Friday Sūrat al-Jumuʿah and Sūrat al-Munāfiqīn. [Very weak. Ibn Ḥibbān 1841.]"
"5731","(5731) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us among others, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Ziyad narrated to me, saying: My father narrated to me, saying: al-Awzāʿī narrated to me, saying: al-Zuhrī narrated to me.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd al-Narsī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, saying: Maʿmar, Yūnus, al-Awzāʿī, and Mālik — all of them — narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever catches a rakʿah of the prayer has caught the prayer.\""\n\nIn the narration of al-Qaʿnabī from Mālik"," and the narration of Sufyān: \""Whoever catches a rakʿah of the prayer has caught the prayer.\""\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik.\n\nAnd Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik"," and from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Sufyān"," and from Abū Kurayb from Ibn al-Mubārak. Muslim also narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from al-Zuhrī. It has already been mentioned at the beginning of the Book of Prayer"," and in it is the additional wording: \""He has caught it entirely.\"" [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī 555.]"""
"5732","(5732) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Yamān narrated to me, saying: Shuʿayb narrated to me, from al-Zuhrī, who narrated to me: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me that Abū Hurayrah said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \Whoever catches a rakʿah of the prayer has caught it.\"" [Authentic. Same meaning as the preceding.]"""
"5733","(5733) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Tamīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Yūnus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever catches a rakʿah of the prayer with the imām has caught the prayer.\""\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā. [Authentic. Muslim 607.]"""
"5734","(5734) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: \Whoever catches a rakʿah of the prayer has caught it.\""\n\nAl-Zuhrī said: \""And al-Jumuʿah is of the prayer; this is the correct view"," and it is the narration of the group from al-Zuhrī. In the narration of Maʿmar there is an indication that the wording of the ḥadīth regarding prayer is absolute"," and by its generality it encompasses al-Jumuʿah as it encompasses other prayers. And Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated the ḥadīth from al-Zuhrī specifically regarding al-Jumuʿah.\"" [Authentic. See the meaning above.]"""
"5735","(5735) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us, saying: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, saying: Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: \Whoever catches a rakʿah of al-Jumuʿah"," let him add another one to it.\"" And similarly it was narrated from Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar from al-Zuhrī. [Authentic in other than this chain. Ibn Mājah 1121.]"""
"5736","(5736) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq ibn al-Bahlūl narrated to us, saying: My grandfather narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us, from Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever catches a rakʿah of al-Jumuʿah"," let him add another one to it. If he finds them sitting"," he should pray four [rakʿahs].\""\n\nAnd that was narrated through other chains from al-Zuhrī"," which we have mentioned under the section of differing opinions.\n\nAnd it was narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ"," from Abū Hurayrah"," as his own statement"," stopped at him. [Denounced. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/10.]"""
"5737","(5737) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd reported from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \Whoever catches one rakʿah of Jumuʿah has indeed caught it"," except that he must make up what he missed.\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
"5738","(5738) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ reported from Sufyān from al-Ashʿath from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \If you catch one rakʿah of Jumuʿah"," then add another one to it. But if you find them sitting (in tashahhud)"," then pray four (rakʿahs).\"" Ayyūb corroborated it from Nāfiʿ. [Ḍaʿīf]"""
"5739","(5739) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ reported that al-Shāfiʿī narrated as a report from Abū Muʿāwiyah from al-Aʿmash from Abī Isḥāq from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: \If you catch one rakʿah of Jumuʿah"," then add another one to it. But if the rakʿūʿ escapes you"," then pray four (rakʿahs).\"" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. al-Shāfiʿī narrated it in al-Umm 7/296]"""
"5740","(5740) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Khaṭīb informed us; Abū Baḥr al-Barbahārī informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us; al-Ḥumaydī informed us; Wakīʿ informed us; Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah reported from Abī Isḥāq from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ and Hubayrah, both of whom said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said: \Whoever catches one rakʿah of Jumuʿah"," let him pray another one alongside it. And whoever misses both rakʿahs"," let him pray four.\"" ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated it from Zakariyyā"," adding: \""And if he finds the people sitting"," let him pray four.\"" Al-Aʿmash narrated it from Abī Isḥāq: \""If the rakʿūʿ escapes you"," then pray four","\"" and he did not mention Hubayrah in the isnād. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"""
